window.__JAZZ_DATA__ = {"genresFile":{"meta":{"present":2027,"generated":"hand-authored; see research/notes.md"},"families":[{"id":"trad-mainstream","name":"Traditional & Mainstream","short":"Mainstream","blurb":"The acoustic through-line: New Orleans to swing to bebop to hard bop to the modern mainstream."},{"id":"avant","name":"Avant-Garde & Composed","short":"Avant-Garde","blurb":"The experimental and composed wing — free jazz, the collectives, European improvisation, chamber jazz."},{"id":"electric","name":"Electric & Crossover","short":"Electric","blurb":"Amplified, groove-first and producer-shaped: fusion, funk, and everything downstream of the sampler."},{"id":"global","name":"Global Currents","short":"Global","blurb":"Jazz built on rhythmic systems from outside the United States, and the scenes that grew around them."}],"eras":[{"id":"trad","name":"Trad","start":1890,"end":1929},{"id":"swing","name":"Swing","start":1929,"end":1945},{"id":"modern","name":"Modern","start":1945,"end":1959},{"id":"the-break","name":"The Break","start":1959,"end":1969},{"id":"electric","name":"Electric","start":1969,"end":1980},{"id":"postmodern","name":"Postmodern","start":1980,"end":2027}],"genres":[{"id":"ragtime","name":"Ragtime","aka":["Rag","Classic ragtime"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1895,"peak":[1899,1917],"end":1919},"origin":{"city":"Sedalia / St. Louis / Missouri river towns","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Written-out piano music where the left hand marches and the right hand refuses to land on the beat.","summary":"Put your foot on the floor with the left hand — steady, even, oom-pah, like a march. Now listen to what the right hand does over the top: it keeps arriving a half-beat early, tugging against that floor. That tug is the whole idea, and 'ragged time' is where the name comes from. Ragtime is composed music, published as sheet music and sold by the ton between 1899 and 1917, mostly in multi-strain march form: four separate 16-bar tunes played in sequence, each repeated, often with a key change for the third. It is not improvised, which is why some writers refuse to call it jazz at all. But every early jazz pianist learned it, the New Orleans repertoire was built out of it, and the syncopation habit it drilled into American ears is the precondition for everything downstream. Scott Joplin wanted it treated as concert music and wrote an opera to prove the point; the market wanted novelty numbers and cakewalks. Both things happened.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Straightforward European parlour harmony — tonic, dominant, secondary dominants, brisk circle-of-fifths runs. Blues notes are rare; this is not blues-derived music.","rhythm":"An unvarying duple left hand under a right hand full of tied-over, off-beat accents. The syncopation is written, not felt in the moment.","form":"Multi-strain march form: AABBACCDD or similar, 16 bars per strain, with the C strain ('the trio') usually in the subdominant.","instrumentation":"Solo piano first and foremost; also banjo, brass band, and the ragtime orchestras that played from stock arrangements.","improvisation":"Essentially none in the classic repertoire. Performers were expected to play the printed notes, and Joplin famously instructed that it never be played fast.","timbre":"Bright upright piano, often slightly out of tune, in a room with hard surfaces. Cleanness and evenness of touch were the prized virtues."},"earMarkers":["A steady oom-pah left hand that never varies while the melody keeps landing between the beats","Four distinctly different tunes inside one piece, each played twice before moving on","A key change roughly halfway through, usually up a fourth, when the 'trio' section arrives","No drums, no bass, no horns — one piano doing all of it"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Scott Joplin","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Wrote the rags that defined the classic style and fought to have it heard as art music rather than novelty."},{"name":"James Scott","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"The most rhythmically intricate of the classic ragtime composers; his right-hand writing is harder than Joplin's."},{"name":"Joseph Lamb","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"The third of the 'big three' and the only white composer among them; wrote the style's most harmonically ambitious pieces."},{"name":"Jelly Roll Morton","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"The hinge figure — took rag form and loosened it with swing, blues and what he called the 'Spanish tinge'."}],"keyLabels":["John Stark & Son (sheet music)","Victor","Nonesuch (1970s revival)"],"contested":"Whether ragtime is jazz at all. It has no improvisation and no blues content, and Gunther Schuller and others treat it strictly as a precursor. Ragtime scholars often resent its absorption into jazz history entirely. This guide treats it as a root rather than a branch.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"new-orleans","name":"New Orleans Jazz","aka":["Dixieland","Trad jazz","Hot jazz"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1900,"peak":[1917,1928],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New Orleans","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Three horns improvising different melodies at the same time over a marching-band rhythm section, and somehow it works.","summary":"The sound is a polite pile-up. Cornet states the tune in the middle register, clarinet weaves a busy countermelody above it, trombone slides around underneath filling the gaps — all three at once, all the time, nobody waiting their turn. This collective improvisation is the defining feature and the thing that disappeared from jazz almost immediately afterward. Underneath, banjo and tuba or bass mark the beat with the rhythm section still audibly descended from brass bands and street parades. The repertoire came from marches, rags, hymns, blues, and popular songs, and the ensemble had a fixed division of labour that everyone understood, which is what kept the counterpoint from becoming noise. It was recorded first in 1917 by a white band, the Original Dixieland Jass Band, which is a fact worth sitting with: the music was Black in origin and the record industry got there in the wrong order. King Oliver's 1923 sessions and Armstrong's Hot Fives are where you actually hear what the style could do.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Simple and functional — I, IV, V with passing diminished chords. Blues form sits alongside 32-bar song and multi-strain rag form.","rhythm":"Two-beat, with the accent on 1 and 3 and a distinct march inheritance. The 'stomp' feel is heavier and squarer than later swing.","form":"Head arrangements over the tune's chorus structure, with breaks, stop-time passages, and a final out-chorus where everyone plays louder.","instrumentation":"Front line of cornet/trumpet, clarinet, trombone; rhythm section of banjo or guitar, tuba or string bass, drums, sometimes piano.","improvisation":"Collective and simultaneous rather than one soloist at a time. Individual paraphrase of the melody rather than running the changes.","timbre":"Vibrato-heavy, vocalised brass — growls, smears, plunger mutes. Nobody is trying to sound clean."},"earMarkers":["Three horns playing three different melodies at once, and you can pick out each one","The clarinet noodling high and fast above everything else","Trombone smearing between notes rather than hitting them squarely","A two-beat oom-pah bass line, not the even four of later jazz","Everyone getting louder and busier for the last chorus"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Louis Armstrong","instrument":"trumpet, vocals","why":"Turned the ensemble style inside out by proving a single soloist could carry a record; the pivot from collective to individual runs through him."},{"name":"King Oliver","instrument":"cornet","why":"Led the Creole Jazz Band, the best-documented example of the two-cornet New Orleans ensemble working properly."},{"name":"Sidney Bechet","instrument":"soprano saxophone, clarinet","why":"The first great jazz soloist on record alongside Armstrong; his wide vibrato is the sound of the idiom's clarinet lineage."},{"name":"Jelly Roll Morton","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Arranged the style rather than just playing it — his Red Hot Peppers sides are composed frameworks with improvisation slotted in."}],"keyLabels":["Gennett","OKeh","Victor","Columbia","Good Time Jazz (revival)"],"contested":"'Dixieland' and 'New Orleans jazz' are often used interchangeably, but many musicians and writers use 'Dixieland' specifically for the later white revivalist style and consider the term loaded. There is also a live argument about whether the 1940s revival was preservation or pastiche.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"chicago-jazz","name":"Chicago Jazz","aka":["Chicago style","Austin High Gang style"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1923,"peak":[1925,1930],"end":1938},"origin":{"city":"Chicago","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"New Orleans jazz after the Great Migration, played hotter and faster by kids who learned it off records.","summary":"When the New Orleans players moved north during the Great Migration, a generation of young white Chicagoans — the Austin High Gang among them — learned the music by wearing out King Oliver records and then playing it their own way. What came out was tenser than the original. The collective front line is still there but thinner, the tempos are up, the saxophone starts elbowing into the front line where it never belonged in New Orleans, and there is a nervous, driving quality to the rhythm that the older players did not have. The signature device is the 'explosion' — a hard accented break, often on the last beat before a chorus. It is transitional music: still ensemble-based, but with individual solos taking up more and more space until, by about 1930, the format simply became the small swing band. Its historical importance is partly demographic — this is where jazz stopped being regional and became a national commercial music.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Same functional harmony as New Orleans, with more use of the 32-bar popular song form and slightly richer passing chords.","rhythm":"Faster and more forward-leaning than New Orleans, with a shift away from two-beat towards an even four. Tense rather than rolling.","form":"Head–solos–head. The out-chorus survives, but the sequence of individual solos is now the centre of the record.","instrumentation":"Cornet, clarinet, trombone, plus tenor or C-melody saxophone; guitar replacing banjo, string bass replacing tuba.","improvisation":"Individual solo choruses become the point. Bix Beiderbecke's cool, placed lyricism and Frank Teschemacher's jagged clarinet are the two poles.","timbre":"Brighter and drier than New Orleans; Beiderbecke's bell-like cornet tone is deliberately un-vibrato'd and became a model for cool jazz twenty years later."},"earMarkers":["Faster tempos and a jumpier, more anxious feel than New Orleans records","A hard accented 'explosion' hit just before a new chorus starts","A saxophone in the front line, which New Orleans bands mostly did not have","Long individual solos rather than continuous group weaving"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Bix Beiderbecke","instrument":"cornet","why":"Played with a round, unforced tone and unusual interval choices; the first widely copied alternative to Armstrong's approach."},{"name":"Eddie Condon","instrument":"guitar, banjo, bandleading","why":"Organiser and lifelong advocate; his sessions kept the style documented for four decades."},{"name":"Frank Teschemacher","instrument":"clarinet","why":"The jagged, harmonically restless clarinet voice of the style, killed in a car crash at 26."},{"name":"Gene Krupa","instrument":"drums","why":"Brought the full drum kit forward in the mix and carried the Chicago drive into the swing era."}],"keyLabels":["OKeh","Brunswick","Gennett","Commodore"],"contested":"Whether 'Chicago style' names a genuine musical style or just a place and a cohort. Some writers treat it as a white derivative of New Orleans jazz rather than a development; others point to the rhythmic shift toward four-beat as a real innovation that fed the swing era.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"stride","name":"Stride Piano","aka":["Harlem stride","Harlem piano"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1919,"peak":[1923,1938],"end":1945},"origin":{"city":"New York (Harlem)","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Ragtime's left hand taught to swing, played by men competing to physically overwhelm each other at rent parties.","summary":"The left hand leaps: bass note on 1 and 3, chord up in the middle of the keyboard on 2 and 4, back and forth across a tenth or more. That leap is the stride, and it is genuinely hard — the reach alone excluded a lot of players. Where ragtime's left hand was even and metronomic, stride's is springy and slightly ahead or behind, and the right hand is no longer bound to the printed page. This was competitive music. Harlem rent parties in the 1920s ran on cutting contests, where pianists took turns trying to demolish each other, and the style's showmanship — the tempo escalations, the interpolated classical quotations, the sheer density — comes directly from that. James P. Johnson is the founding figure, Willie 'The Lion' Smith the great character, and Fats Waller the one who took it to a mass audience by adding comedy and a hit-making instinct. Art Tatum then took the technique somewhere nobody has followed since. Ellington, Basie and Monk all played stride, and you can hear it surfacing in their playing decades later.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Richer than ragtime — sixths, ninths, chromatic passing chords, and a growing use of blues inflection. Tatum pushed it into substitutions that anticipated bebop.","rhythm":"Alternating bass-note/chord left hand at speed, with rhythmic elasticity the right hand plays against. Swing eighth notes replace ragtime's even ones.","form":"Popular song forms and rag strains, with the arrangement built as a series of escalating choruses.","instrumentation":"Solo piano. The whole point is that one player covers bass, chords, and melody without help.","improvisation":"Extensive right-hand variation over a memorised structure, plus reharmonisation and interpolated quotation.","timbre":"Full-range, orchestral use of the piano — deep bass, ringing treble, heavy pedalling on ballads."},"earMarkers":["The left hand physically jumping between a low bass note and a mid-range chord, over and over","One pianist producing a whole band's worth of sound with no rhythm section","Tempos that ratchet up as the performance goes on, showing off","Sudden quotations from classical pieces or other songs dropped in mid-solo"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"James P. Johnson","instrument":"piano","why":"The founder; 'Carolina Shout' was the test piece every aspiring Harlem pianist had to master."},{"name":"Fats Waller","instrument":"piano, organ, vocals","why":"Johnson's student, and the one who made the style commercially enormous without diluting the left hand."},{"name":"Willie 'The Lion' Smith","instrument":"piano","why":"The most harmonically impressionistic of the strider; Ellington cited him directly as an influence."},{"name":"Art Tatum","instrument":"piano","why":"Took stride's mechanics and applied a harmonic imagination and technique that reset what the instrument could do."}],"keyLabels":["OKeh","Victor","Decca","Commodore"],"contested":"Whether Art Tatum belongs inside stride or outside it. His left hand is stride-derived, but his harmonic language points forward to bebop; some historians file him as a category of one.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"kansas-city","name":"Kansas City Jazz","aka":["KC swing","Territory band style"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1928,"peak":[1935,1942],"end":1946},"origin":{"city":"Kansas City, Missouri","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Blues-soaked big band jazz built on riffs, with a rhythm section so relaxed it seems to float.","summary":"Kansas City under the Pendergast machine had wide-open nightlife and clubs that stayed open all night, which meant jam sessions ran for hours and a musician's reputation was made in them. Two things came out of that. First, riff-based arranging: instead of writing intricate charts, sections trade short repeated phrases, building layer on layer, and the arrangement can be assembled on the bandstand by ear. Second, a new rhythm section feel — Basie's band with Walter Page on bass, Freddie Green on guitar and Jo Jones on drums shifted the pulse to an even, buoyant four beats to the bar, moved timekeeping to the hi-hat and ride, and left enormous space. Basie's own piano playing reduced to a few notes, which was a radical act of subtraction. Blues form is everywhere, far more than in eastern swing bands. And the marathon jam sessions turned tenor saxophone into a combat sport, which produced Lester Young — whose light, behind-the-beat lyricism became the direct model for Charlie Parker's generation.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Blues-dominant. Twelve-bar form is the default rather than an occasional feature, with simple changes that leave room for the soloist.","rhythm":"Even four-to-the-bar with a light, springing feel. Bass walks, guitar chunks quarter notes, drums move time to the hi-hat and use the bass drum sparingly.","form":"Head arrangements built from riffs, with call-and-response between brass and reeds, and long strings of solo choruses.","instrumentation":"Full big band, but also the small jam-session combo. Guitar is a rhythm instrument, not a solo voice.","improvisation":"Chorus after chorus over the blues, with the expectation that a soloist builds across many choruses rather than saying it all in one.","timbre":"Lester Young's dry, vibratoless tenor against Coleman Hawkins' heavier eastern sound — the two poles of saxophone tone for the next twenty years."},"earMarkers":["Short repeated phrases stacked in layers, brass answering reeds","Twelve-bar blues far more often than in other big band music","A rhythm section that feels like it's gliding rather than pushing","Piano that plays almost nothing — a few notes, then silence","Solos that go on for four, six, eight choruses and keep building"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Count Basie","instrument":"piano, bandleading","why":"Built the rhythm section that redefined swing feel, and made minimalism at the piano a style rather than an absence."},{"name":"Lester Young","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"Played light, behind the beat, with long asymmetric lines — the direct precursor to bebop phrasing and to cool jazz tone."},{"name":"Bennie Moten","instrument":"piano, bandleading","why":"Led the band that became Basie's; the 1932 Victor sessions are where the KC rhythmic shift first lands on record."},{"name":"Mary Lou Williams","instrument":"piano, arranging","why":"Chief arranger for Andy Kirk's Clouds of Joy and the KC scene's most important compositional mind; later mentored the bebop generation."},{"name":"Jay McShann","instrument":"piano, bandleading","why":"Ran the band where Charlie Parker got his first recorded solos, making the KC-to-bebop link literal."}],"keyLabels":["Decca","Victor","Vocalion","Commodore"],"contested":"Whether Kansas City is a separate genre or a regional dialect of swing. This guide treats it separately because the rhythmic and blues-form innovations there are specific, traceable, and fed bebop more directly than the New York swing mainstream did.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"swing","name":"Swing & Big Band","aka":["Big band jazz","The Swing Era"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1930,"peak":[1935,1946],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York / Kansas City / Chicago","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Jazz as America's pop music: written arrangements for fourteen-plus players, built for dancing.","summary":"For about a decade, this was simply what popular music sounded like. The instrument is the orchestra: reeds and brass as blocks of sound answering each other, a rhythm section keeping an even four, and arrangements written down so a large band could execute complicated material precisely night after night. The arranger becomes as important as any soloist — Fletcher Henderson, Sy Oliver, Eddie Sauter, Billy Strayhorn. What separates swing from the dance music before it is the rhythmic feel: uneven eighth notes with a forward lean, and a rhythm section that propels rather than merely marks time. Within that commercial frame there was enormous range, from Glenn Miller's tightly controlled hits through Benny Goodman's virtuoso precision to Ellington's orchestra, which was really a composer's laboratory with an unusually stable roster. Its commercial dominance ended after 1946 — economics, a wartime recording ban, changing dance habits, the entertainment tax — but big band writing never stopped, and the repertory and contemporary large-ensemble scenes are direct continuations.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Popular song harmony of the era, richly voiced — added sixths and ninths, sectional block voicings, and increasingly adventurous writing as the 1940s approach.","rhythm":"Even four-to-the-bar with swung eighths. The rhythm section is a unit whose job is lift.","form":"Written arrangements on 32-bar AABA popular songs and blues, with solos slotted into designated choruses and shout choruses near the end.","instrumentation":"Standardised big band: four or five reeds, three or four trumpets, three or four trombones, piano, guitar, bass, drums. Often a featured vocalist.","improvisation":"Present but rationed — usually one or two choruses inside an otherwise written chart.","timbre":"Section blend as the primary colour: saxophones as a single voice, brass with and without mutes, and the deliberate contrast between the two."},"earMarkers":["Whole sections of horns moving together in harmony, like one huge instrument","Brass and reeds trading phrases back and forth in call-and-response","A hard-hitting climactic 'shout chorus' near the end where the whole band goes up a gear","Danceable, steady tempos — this was functional music for a ballroom floor"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Duke Ellington","instrument":"piano, composition, bandleading","why":"Wrote for the specific players in his band rather than for generic parts, which is why his music does not transfer well to other orchestras."},{"name":"Fletcher Henderson","instrument":"arranging, bandleading","why":"Codified the reed-versus-brass call-and-response template that the entire era used, then sold his charts to Goodman."},{"name":"Benny Goodman","instrument":"clarinet, bandleading","why":"Made swing a mass phenomenon in 1935 and led racially integrated small groups in public when that was a professional risk."},{"name":"Billy Strayhorn","instrument":"composition, arranging, piano","why":"Ellington's writing partner for nearly thirty years; the harmonic sophistication of the later Ellington book is substantially his."},{"name":"Coleman Hawkins","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"His 1939 'Body and Soul' is a full chorus of improvisation on the harmony rather than the melody — a bebop premise, two years early."}],"keyLabels":["Victor","Decca","Columbia","Capitol"],"contested":"Where swing ends. Some treat 1946 as the hard stop; others point to Basie's second band, Kenton, Herman's Herds and the entire repertory movement as evidence that it simply stopped being pop music without ceasing to exist. This guide takes the latter view.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"manouche","name":"Jazz Manouche","aka":["Gypsy jazz","Gypsy swing","Hot club jazz"],"family":"global","era":{"start":1934,"peak":[1936,1949],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"Paris","country":"France"},"oneLine":"Swing played on acoustic guitars with no drums, where the rhythm guitars are the drum kit.","summary":"No drums, no horns, no piano — two or three acoustic guitars, a violin, and a double bass. The rhythm guitars play 'la pompe', a percussive chunk-chunk on every beat with the chord damped immediately, and that sound does the entire job of a drum kit. Over it, a lead guitar plays fast, ornamented single-note lines full of arpeggios, chromatic runs, and a wide fingered vibrato that comes out of Romani string tradition rather than American jazz. Django Reinhardt built the style with violinist Stéphane Grappelli in the Quintette du Hot Club de France, formed in Paris in 1934, and he did it with two working fingers on his fretting hand after a caravan fire — the resulting chord voicings and octave-heavy lines are partly a workaround that became a vocabulary. The repertoire mixes American standards with French musette waltzes and Django's own compositions. It is one of the very few jazz styles born outside the United States that fed back into the American mainstream, and it remains a living tradition with festivals, dynasties and a defined technique.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Swing-era changes with a strong minor-key and diminished flavour, plus musette-derived chromaticism. Sixth and diminished chords everywhere.","rhythm":"'La pompe' — a damped, percussive four-to-the-bar guitar stroke with a hard accent on 2 and 4 that replaces drums entirely.","form":"Standards and originals in 32-bar and 16-bar forms, often taken at extreme tempos, with breaks and unaccompanied intros.","instrumentation":"Lead guitar, one or two rhythm guitars, violin, double bass. Selmer-Maccaferri guitars with a distinctive cutting tone.","improvisation":"Fast arpeggio-based lines, chromatic approach notes, tremolo and octave passages. More ornamental and less blues-inflected than American jazz of the same era.","timbre":"All-acoustic, bright and nasal. Wide, fast vibrato on both guitar and violin."},"earMarkers":["Guitars chopping out every beat with the chord cut short — that's the drum kit","Absolutely no drums and no horns anywhere","Lead guitar lines running up and down arpeggios at improbable speed","Violin as a front-line solo instrument, which is rare elsewhere in jazz","A wide, fast shimmer of vibrato at the end of held notes"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Django Reinhardt","instrument":"guitar","why":"Invented the idiom's guitar language, with a fretting hand damaged by fire that forced a distinctive approach to voicings and octaves."},{"name":"Stéphane Grappelli","instrument":"violin","why":"Made violin a credible jazz lead voice and carried the style forward for fifty years after Django's death."},{"name":"Biréli Lagrène","instrument":"guitar","why":"The most prominent of the modern inheritors; recorded as a teenage prodigy and later moved between manouche and fusion."},{"name":"Baro Ferret","instrument":"guitar","why":"Key figure in the Parisian Romani guitar scene whose musette-inflected playing shows the style's non-American roots."}],"keyLabels":["Ultraphone","Swing (French label)","Decca (France)","Frémeaux & Associés (reissues)"],"contested":"The name. 'Gypsy jazz' is standard in English-language usage but 'Gypsy' is widely considered a slur by Romani people; 'jazz manouche' is the French term and is preferred by many players. This guide leads with the latter. There is also debate over whether Django's postwar electric bebop-influenced work is the same genre at all.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"vocal-jazz","name":"Vocal Jazz & the Songbook","aka":["Jazz singing","Great American Songbook"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1926,"peak":[1938,1962],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York / Chicago","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Singers treating a written song as raw material — bending the time, the pitch and the meaning of the lyric.","summary":"The difference between a jazz singer and a good singer of the same songs is what they do to the tune. Billie Holiday takes a mediocre Tin Pan Alley number, strips the melody down to a few notes, drags every phrase behind the beat, and makes the lyric mean something the writer never intended. Ella Fitzgerald keeps the melody gleaming and then improvises whole choruses of wordless scat with the accuracy of a horn player. Sarah Vaughan uses an operatically trained instrument to reharmonise on the fly. All three are doing the same underlying thing — improvising, in the specific sense that they are making choices about time and pitch that are not in the sheet music. The repertoire is largely the Great American Songbook: Gershwin, Porter, Berlin, Kern, Rodgers, Arlen, written mostly for Broadway and Hollywood between the 1920s and 1950s, whose 32-bar AABA forms became the harmonic playing field for jazz instrumentalists too. Scat singing is the most obvious marker but far from the most important one; phrasing is.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Songbook harmony — 32-bar AABA and ABAC forms with rich ii–V movement. Singers often introduce their own passing notes and blue thirds against it.","rhythm":"Radical flexibility with the beat. Holiday sits far behind it, Sinatra stretches phrases across bar lines, Fitzgerald floats on top of it.","form":"Verse (often omitted) then chorus, with instrumental solo sections and a final chorus that departs furthest from the written melody.","instrumentation":"Voice with anything from solo piano to full string orchestra; the small-group-with-horns setting is where the interplay is most audible.","improvisation":"Melodic paraphrase first, scat second. The best singers vary the melody so continuously that the written line never returns intact.","timbre":"Enormous range, from Holiday's small, frayed sound to Vaughan's four-octave control. Vibrato use is a stylistic signature in itself."},"earMarkers":["The singer arriving noticeably late on words, letting the band go ahead","The written melody getting further from itself with each chorus","Wordless syllables used as an instrumental solo (scat)","Conversational give-and-take between the voice and one instrument — Holiday and Lester Young are the model"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Billie Holiday","instrument":"voice","why":"Rewrote what phrasing could do; her interpretive approach to a lyric is the reference point for everyone who followed."},{"name":"Ella Fitzgerald","instrument":"voice","why":"The most technically complete scat improviser, and her Songbook series fixed the canon of American popular song."},{"name":"Sarah Vaughan","instrument":"voice","why":"Brought bebop harmony and operatic technique to the same phrase; the most harmonically daring of the major singers."},{"name":"Louis Armstrong","instrument":"voice, trumpet","why":"Established that a jazz singer phrases like a horn player, and popularised scat in the 1920s."},{"name":"Betty Carter","instrument":"voice","why":"Took the abstraction furthest — extreme tempos, melodies dismantled beyond recognition, and a band she ran like a bebop leader."}],"keyLabels":["Columbia","Decca","Verve","Capitol","Commodore"],"contested":"Whether 'vocal jazz' is a genre or a role that cuts across every genre. Purists argue that Sinatra and much of the Songbook repertoire is popular singing with jazz accompaniment rather than jazz. This guide treats it as a genre-shaped tradition with a defined repertoire and a distinct set of techniques, while acknowledging the boundary is porous — an interpretive call, not a settled fact.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"bebop","name":"Bebop","aka":["Bop","Rebop"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1944,"peak":[1945,1953],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York (Harlem / 52nd Street)","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Small-group jazz played fast and dense enough that it stopped being dance music and became something you sit down for.","summary":"Bebop is what happens when musicians decide the audience is other musicians. A small group — trumpet, alto or tenor, piano, bass, drums — plays a fast, angular unison melody, then everyone solos at length over the chord changes, then the melody returns. The tunes are often new heads written over the harmony of existing standards, so the players get fresh material without paying royalties or relearning forms. The lines are long, asymmetric, and full of chromatic approach notes and upper-structure intervals — flattened fifths and ninths that sounded wrong to swing-trained ears. Rhythmically the whole timekeeping apparatus moves: the ride cymbal carries the pulse, the bass drum stops marking every beat and instead 'drops bombs' as accents, and the pianist stops stride-style timekeeping and comps in irregular stabs. It developed at after-hours sessions in Harlem, notably Minton's Playhouse, during the early 1940s, and the 1942–44 recording ban means the crucial transition is largely undocumented — the records pick up when the style is already formed.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Fast-moving functional harmony with extensions and substitutions: flat nines, sharp elevens, tritone substitutions, and ii–V chains inserted where the original song had none.","rhythm":"Ride cymbal as primary timekeeper, walking bass in even four, bass drum used for irregular accents rather than pulse, piano comping in syncopated punctuation.","form":"Head–solos–head over 32-bar AABA, rhythm changes, and 12-bar blues. Contrafacts — new melodies on borrowed changes — are everywhere.","instrumentation":"Quintet: trumpet, saxophone, piano, bass, drums. Guitar drops out of the rhythm section.","improvisation":"The central activity. Long eighth-note lines that outline the harmony precisely, with chromatic enclosures and deliberate targeting of the upper extensions.","timbre":"Dry, hard, minimal vibrato. Parker's alto tone and Gillespie's high, brilliant trumpet are deliberately unsentimental."},"earMarkers":["A fast, twisting melody played in unison by two horns with no harmony underneath","Drums keeping time on a cymbal while the bass drum punches at unpredictable moments","Solos that run in continuous eighth notes for a very long time without obvious pause","Tunes you half-recognise — the chords are from a standard but the melody is new","Tempos that are simply too fast to dance to"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Charlie Parker","instrument":"alto saxophone","why":"Built the melodic vocabulary. Almost every jazz improviser since has absorbed his phrasing, whether or not they play his style."},{"name":"Dizzy Gillespie","instrument":"trumpet, composition","why":"Parker's counterpart and the style's theorist and teacher; also the one who brought Afro-Cuban rhythm into it."},{"name":"Bud Powell","instrument":"piano","why":"Translated Parker's horn lines to the keyboard and invented the modern jazz piano role — right-hand lines over sparse left-hand chords."},{"name":"Thelonious Monk","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Present at the creation but never really a bebop player; his compositions and use of space run at an angle to the whole style."},{"name":"Max Roach","instrument":"drums","why":"Moved the drum kit from timekeeping to conversation, playing melodically against the soloist."},{"name":"Kenny Clarke","instrument":"drums","why":"The Minton's house drummer who first shifted the pulse to the ride cymbal — the technical change that made bebop possible."}],"keyLabels":["Savoy","Dial","Blue Note","Clef/Norgran (later Verve)","Prestige"],"contested":"Whether bebop was a revolution or an evolution. Musicians involved often described it as a natural extension of what Lester Young, Charlie Christian and Coleman Hawkins were already doing; the press framed it as a rupture. Both accounts have evidence behind them.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"cool-jazz","name":"Cool Jazz","aka":["The cool school","Birth of the Cool style"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1948,"peak":[1949,1958],"end":1964},"origin":{"city":"New York","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Bebop's harmony at half the temperature — soft attack, written counterpoint, and space where the heat used to be.","summary":"Take bebop's harmonic sophistication and remove its aggression. Cool jazz keeps the extended chords and the fast changes but plays them with a light, vibrato-free tone, at moderate tempos, with far more written material and much more silence. The Miles Davis nonet sessions of 1949–50 are the founding document: nine pieces including French horn and tuba, arranged by Gil Evans, Gerry Mulligan and John Lewis into something closer to chamber music than to a jam session. The other root is Lennie Tristano's circle in New York, which pursued long, seamless improvised lines with almost no rhythmic emphasis, deliberately suppressing the drama. Lester Young is the shared ancestor of both — his dry tone and behind-the-beat placement is what 'cool' means as a sound. Critics have often written the style off as bloodless or as a whitening of bebop, and the racial politics of its reception are genuinely ugly, but the actual music of Tristano, Konitz, Evans and Lewis is intellectually demanding and unlike anything else.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Bebop's extended harmony plus impressionist colouring — quartal voicings, whole-tone passages, and polytonal writing in the arranged pieces.","rhythm":"Even, unemphatic, deliberately undramatic. Drummers use brushes often and avoid punctuation; the pulse is stated rather than driven.","form":"Arranged ensemble passages framing solos, with contrapuntal writing and unusual section lengths rather than straight head–solos–head.","instrumentation":"Unusual for jazz: French horn, tuba, baritone saxophone, flute, cello. Piano-less groups also appear.","improvisation":"Long, evenly phrased melodic lines with little rhythmic display. Tristano's students practised eliminating cliché and accent entirely.","timbre":"Soft attack, minimal vibrato, blended ensemble sound. Nothing is played loudly for effect."},"earMarkers":["Horns playing with a soft, breathy tone and almost no vibrato","Two or more melody lines moving independently in a written arrangement, not just chords","Instruments you don't expect in jazz — French horn, tuba, cello","Drums using brushes and staying out of the way","Medium tempos with noticeable space between phrases"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Miles Davis","instrument":"trumpet, bandleading","why":"Assembled the nonet and provided its middle-register, unhurried lead voice; then left the style behind within a few years."},{"name":"Gil Evans","instrument":"arranging","why":"The orchestral imagination behind the nonet and later the Davis–Evans collaborations; treated the ensemble as a single instrument with variable colour."},{"name":"Lennie Tristano","instrument":"piano, teaching","why":"Ran a rigorous school of improvisation aimed at pure line, and cut two free improvisations in 1949, years before Ornette Coleman."},{"name":"Lee Konitz","instrument":"alto saxophone","why":"The one major altoist of his generation who did not sound like Charlie Parker — a genuine alternative line of descent."},{"name":"John Lewis","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Nonet arranger who then built the Modern Jazz Quartet around baroque counterpoint and blues."}],"keyLabels":["Capitol","Prestige","Atlantic","Verve"],"contested":"Whether cool jazz and West Coast jazz are one thing. This guide separates them: cool jazz is the New York nonet/Tristano lineage, West Coast is the Los Angeles studio-musician scene that grew partly out of it. Many writers merge the two, and the overlap in personnel is real.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"west-coast","name":"West Coast Jazz","aka":["California jazz","Cool West Coast"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1952,"peak":[1953,1959],"end":1963},"origin":{"city":"Los Angeles","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Cool jazz relocated to LA studios: light, arranged, lyrical, and made by musicians with day jobs scoring films.","summary":"Los Angeles in the 1950s had a large population of superb reading musicians working in film and television studios, and a club scene — the Lighthouse in Hermosa Beach, Zardi's, the Haig — where they played jazz at night. The result is a music that prizes arrangement, clarity and craft: contrapuntal writing, unusual instrumentation, tightly rehearsed ensembles, and solos that stay lyrical rather than combative. The Gerry Mulligan Quartet with Chet Baker dropped the piano entirely, which forced the baritone and trumpet to imply the harmony through counterpoint, and produced the style's defining sound. Shorty Rogers, Shelly Manne, Jimmy Giuffre and Bud Shank are the other central figures. The style has been criticised, then and since, as polite and racially coded — the LA studio system was overwhelmingly white and the Black LA scene around Central Avenue was a distinct thing that got far less attention. Both criticisms have force. The music itself, taken on its own terms, is more rhythmically inventive than its reputation suggests.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Clean bebop-derived changes with a taste for counterpoint over vertical density; some pieces borrow directly from fugue and canon procedures.","rhythm":"Light and buoyant rather than driving. Shelly Manne's playing is detailed and quiet, closer to a percussionist's approach than a swing drummer's.","form":"Heavily arranged: written introductions, interludes, backgrounds behind solos, and shaped endings. Less head–solos–head than anywhere else in 1950s jazz.","instrumentation":"Piano-less quartets, flute and oboe doubling, baritone saxophone as a lead voice, occasional strings and woodwind sections.","improvisation":"Melodic and thematic, often developing a motif from the written material rather than running changes.","timbre":"Dry, close-recorded, transparent. Chet Baker's trumpet and Mulligan's baritone define the palette."},"earMarkers":["No piano at all in many groups — just horns, bass and drums","Two horns weaving separate lines around each other like a written duet","Flute, oboe or baritone saxophone carrying the melody","Everything sounding rehearsed rather than jammed","Quiet, detailed drumming with a lot of brushwork and rim sounds"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Gerry Mulligan","instrument":"baritone saxophone, arranging","why":"The piano-less quartet was his idea and it defined the style's texture; also a first-rate contrapuntal writer."},{"name":"Chet Baker","instrument":"trumpet, vocals","why":"The style's lyrical voice — middle register, no vibrato, melodically simple and rhythmically subtle."},{"name":"Shorty Rogers","instrument":"trumpet, arranging","why":"The scene's most prolific arranger; his Giants sessions codified the West Coast small-band sound."},{"name":"Shelly Manne","instrument":"drums","why":"Redefined what a drummer could contribute texturally, and co-led the LA scene's most exploratory sessions."},{"name":"Jimmy Giuffre","instrument":"clarinet, saxophones, composition","why":"Pushed furthest out — drumless trios, folk-derived material, and by 1961 a chamber-free-jazz that predates most European improv."}],"keyLabels":["Pacific Jazz","Contemporary","Atlantic","World Pacific"],"contested":"Whether it is a real style or a marketing geography. Critics including many at the time argued 'West Coast jazz' described where records were made, not how they sounded — Ornette Coleman and Eric Dolphy were also in Los Angeles. This guide keeps it because the piano-less contrapuntal small group and the arranger-forward approach are a genuinely coherent set of choices.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"hard-bop","name":"Hard Bop","aka":["Funky hard bop","East Coast jazz"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1954,"peak":[1956,1965],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York / Detroit / Philadelphia","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Bebop with the blues and the church put back in — heavier, slower, and built to hit you in the chest.","summary":"By 1954 bebop had become a specialist's music and cool jazz had gone pale. Hard bop is the correction: the same harmonic language and the same quintet format, but slowed down, made bluesier, and reconnected to gospel and rhythm-and-blues. The tunes are catchier — often written by the band members themselves, with singable heads and memorable bass figures rather than abstract contrafacts. The drums get heavier, with a backbeat on 2 and 4 appearing regularly where swing drummers would have kept it on the cymbal. Piano comping picks up gospel voicings and repeated amen cadences. Art Blakey's Jazz Messengers, running from 1955 until his death in 1990, functioned as both the style's flagship and its university — Horace Silver, Benny Golson, Lee Morgan, Wayne Shorter, Freddie Hubbard, Keith Jarrett and dozens more came through it. Blue Note under Alfred Lion, with Rudy Van Gelder engineering and paid rehearsal time, gave the style a house sound that is still what most people mean when they picture a jazz record.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Bebop changes plus explicit blues and gospel material — minor blues, modal vamps, IV–I plagal cadences lifted from church music.","rhythm":"Heavier and more grounded than bebop, with backbeats, Latin-tinged intros, and Blakey's press rolls and hard shuffle feels.","form":"Head–solos–head, but with composed introductions, interludes and shout choruses that make the records feel arranged.","instrumentation":"Trumpet–tenor quintet is the default; sextets with trombone or alto are common. Organ and vibes appear at the edges.","improvisation":"Blues-saturated and rhythmically emphatic. Motivic repetition and worrying at a phrase are valued over pure line.","timbre":"Big, fat horn tones with more vibrato than bebop; Van Gelder's close-miked recording gives the drums and piano unusual weight."},"earMarkers":["Drums hitting hard on beats 2 and 4, not just riding the cymbal","Gospel-style church chords in the piano comping, with repeated amen-like cadences","Heads you can actually hum, often with a strong bass riff underneath","A drum roll that builds and crashes into the next soloist's entrance","Minor-key tunes with a Latin feel on the head that switches to swing for the solos"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Art Blakey","instrument":"drums, bandleading","why":"Ran the Jazz Messengers for 35 years as both a band and a training system; his press roll and backbeat are the style's rhythmic signature."},{"name":"Horace Silver","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Wrote the tunes that defined the funky side of the style and made the piano a percussive, blues-drenched instrument."},{"name":"Clifford Brown","instrument":"trumpet","why":"The technical and lyrical model for every hard bop trumpeter; dead at 25 in 1956, which is why the recordings are so concentrated."},{"name":"Cannonball Adderley","instrument":"alto saxophone","why":"Carried the blues-and-joy side of the style to a large audience without softening the playing."},{"name":"Lee Morgan","instrument":"trumpet","why":"Blakey's most quotable soloist and the maker of the style's biggest commercial hit."},{"name":"Sonny Rollins","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"Built solos by developing motifs across whole choruses — thematic improvisation as an explicit method."}],"keyLabels":["Blue Note","Prestige","Riverside","Savoy","Columbia"],"contested":"Some writers treat hard bop and soul jazz as a single continuum rather than two genres, and there is no clean line — Horace Silver sits on both sides of it. This guide splits them at the point where the organ trio and the R&B backbeat become the format rather than a flavour. The label 'East Coast jazz' as an answer to West Coast is a critics' construction more than a musicians' one.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"soul-jazz","name":"Soul Jazz & Organ Trio","aka":["Funky jazz","Groove jazz","Organ combo"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1956,"peak":[1960,1969],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"Philadelphia / Newark / Chicago","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Jazz for the neighbourhood bar: Hammond organ, guitar, drums, a hard groove, and no interest in impressing critics.","summary":"The Hammond B-3 organ with a Leslie rotating speaker is the whole sound. One player covers bass with the pedals or the left hand, chords and melody with the right, and can swell the volume with a foot pedal in a way no piano can — which is why the organ trio needs no bassist and can fill a room. Jimmy Smith worked out the vocabulary in the mid-1950s and effectively created a job market: organ, guitar, drums, sometimes tenor, working the chitlin' circuit and neighbourhood taverns rather than concert halls. The music is groove-first, blues-based, often built on simple vamps and 12-bar forms, with long solos and an audience that responds out loud. It was hugely popular and largely dismissed by the jazz press at the time as commercial, which says more about the press than the music. Everything in later jazz-funk, acid jazz and hip-hop-sampled jazz runs back through here — a huge proportion of sampled jazz breaks come from soul jazz records.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Blues and simple functional changes, gospel plagal cadences, and long single-chord vamps. Harmonic ambition is deliberately not the point.","rhythm":"Shuffle and backbeat feels drawn from rhythm and blues, with the drummer often playing a boogaloo or a hard 12/8.","form":"12-bar blues, simple riff tunes, and modal vamps stretched out for extended solos. Head arrangements, minimal writing.","instrumentation":"Hammond B-3 through a Leslie cabinet, electric guitar, drums, and often tenor saxophone. No upright bass — the organ covers it.","improvisation":"Long, riff-driven, and openly crowd-pleasing. Repetition and building intensity matter more than harmonic navigation.","timbre":"The Leslie speaker's swirl is the defining colour, plus hollow-body guitar with a warm, unhurried attack and a big-toned tenor."},"earMarkers":["Hammond organ with that swirling, rotating speaker sound","No double bass — the bass line is being played by the organist's left hand or feet","A hard backbeat you could dance to, closer to R&B than to swing","Long stretches sitting on one chord while the solo builds","Audible crowd noise — a lot of these were recorded live in bars"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Jimmy Smith","instrument":"Hammond organ","why":"Invented the modern jazz organ vocabulary — bass lines in the left hand, bebop lines in the right — and created the format."},{"name":"Grant Green","instrument":"guitar","why":"The most rhythmically pure single-note guitarist of the style; almost never played chords in solos."},{"name":"Stanley Turrentine","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"The definitive soul jazz tenor tone — huge, warm, blues-first."},{"name":"Jack McDuff","instrument":"Hammond organ","why":"Ran the hardest-grooving of the working organ bands and gave George Benson his first job."},{"name":"Ramsey Lewis","instrument":"piano","why":"Proved the piano trio could do the same job, and took soul jazz into the pop charts in 1965."}],"keyLabels":["Blue Note","Prestige","Argo/Cadet","Verve"],"contested":"Where hard bop ends and soul jazz begins. Horace Silver, Cannonball Adderley and Lee Morgan sit on both sides. Some writers also fold Ramsey Lewis and the piano-based groove records into a separate 'crossover' category. The genre's low critical standing at the time has been substantially revised.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"modal","name":"Modal Jazz","aka":["Modal improvisation"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1958,"peak":[1959,1967],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Take away most of the chords and see what a soloist does with the space.","summary":"Bebop and hard bop hand the improviser a chord every two beats; modal jazz might hand them one scale for sixteen bars. The reduction is the innovation. With the harmony slowed almost to a stop, a soloist can no longer succeed by outlining changes accurately, and has to build a line out of melodic shape, motif and colour instead. George Russell's Lydian Chromatic Concept, published in 1953, gave the idea a theoretical frame; Miles Davis's 'Milestones' in 1958 and Kind of Blue in 1959 gave it a sound. The consequences show up everywhere: quartal voicings in the piano (stacked fourths, which are ambiguous about what key you are in), pedal points in the bass, drummers freed from marking chord changes and able to play texture, and modes borrowed from outside the major/minor system. Coltrane pushed it further and harder than Davis did, turning single-chord vamps into ecstatic marathons, and by way of that the style opens directly into spiritual jazz and free playing.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Static or very slow-moving. One scale or mode held for long stretches, with quartal and suspended voicings that avoid stating a clear tonal centre.","rhythm":"Freed from chord-marking. Bass often plays pedal points or ostinatos; drums play colour and wave-shape rather than navigation.","form":"Long sections over a single mode, sometimes with a second mode as contrast. Forms can be irregular lengths because they are not tied to song structure.","instrumentation":"Standard quintet or sextet, but the piano's role changes fundamentally — texture and colour over functional comping.","improvisation":"Melodic and motivic. With no changes to navigate, the soloist must generate interest from shape, repetition, register and intensity.","timbre":"Space becomes audible. Bill Evans' voicings and Coltrane's overtone-rich upper register are the two ends of the style's colour range."},"earMarkers":["The harmony sits still — you can wait a long time for the chord to change","Piano chords built from stacked fourths, which sound open and unresolved","A bass note held or repeated under everything (a pedal point)","Melodies that sound slightly foreign because the scale isn't ordinary major or minor","A soloist building intensity by repetition rather than by moving through chords"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Miles Davis","instrument":"trumpet, bandleading","why":"Made the theoretical idea audible and commercially enormous with Kind of Blue in 1959."},{"name":"John Coltrane","instrument":"tenor and soprano saxophone","why":"Took modal frameworks to their limit — long vamps played at maximum intensity, which is where free jazz starts for him."},{"name":"Bill Evans","instrument":"piano","why":"Supplied the voicing language, borrowed partly from Ravel and Debussy, that makes modal harmony sound the way it does."},{"name":"George Russell","instrument":"composition, theory","why":"Wrote the Lydian Chromatic Concept, the theoretical framework that gave modal thinking a vocabulary."},{"name":"McCoy Tyner","instrument":"piano","why":"Turned quartal voicings into a percussive, pentatonic-driven style that became the default sound of modal piano."},{"name":"Herbie Hancock","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Wrote modal pieces with ambiguous, floating harmony that avoid both functional resolution and static vamps."}],"keyLabels":["Columbia","Impulse!","Blue Note","Atlantic"],"contested":"Whether modal jazz is a genre or a technique. Almost every post-1960 style uses modal devices, and albums like Kind of Blue get filed as modal, cool, or post-bop depending on the writer. Whether Kind of Blue is modal jazz or a modal-flavoured hard bop record is a genuinely live argument.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"third-stream","name":"Third Stream","aka":["Jazz-classical fusion"],"family":"avant","era":{"start":1957,"peak":[1957,1963],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York / Boston","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Composers trying to get classical form and jazz improvisation into the same piece without either one winning.","summary":"Gunther Schuller coined the term in a 1957 lecture at Brandeis University, describing a third stream flowing between the classical and jazz mainstreams. The premise: use the compositional resources of Western art music — twelve-tone rows, fugue, extended form, orchestral instrumentation — while keeping improvisation as a live element rather than decoration. The Modern Jazz Quartet under John Lewis had been circling the idea since 1952 with baroque counterpoint over blues; Schuller, Charles Mingus, George Russell and Jimmy Giuffre pushed it further. The problems are obvious in hindsight and were obvious then: classical players could not improvise convincingly, jazz players found the written material stiff, and a lot of the results sound like two musics politely taking turns. But the successful examples are genuinely strange and good, and the idea did not die — it re-emerges in ECM's chamber jazz, in Anthony Braxton's compositional systems, and in almost every contemporary large-ensemble writer. Schuller later expanded the term to mean any fusion of an improvised and a notated tradition, not only European ones.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Twelve-tone and freely atonal writing alongside jazz harmony; polytonality and unresolved dissonance used structurally.","rhythm":"Often deliberately un-swung in the composed sections, with metric modulation and irregular meters, then a swing feel entering with the improvisation.","form":"Extended, through-composed structures — sonata-like development, fugue, theme and variations — with improvised sections written into the architecture.","instrumentation":"Jazz group plus string quartet, chamber orchestra, or full symphony; also the Modern Jazz Quartet's austere vibes-piano-bass-drums.","improvisation":"Constrained and located — the soloist improvises within a specified section, sometimes on a row or a set of pitches rather than changes.","timbre":"Concert-hall clarity and dynamic range. Strings, woodwinds and percussion used for colour in ways rare elsewhere in jazz."},"earMarkers":["Strings or woodwinds that are clearly a classical section, not a pop string sweetening","Passages of counterpoint that sound like Bach, followed by a swinging solo","Dissonance that never resolves, used on purpose","Long pieces in several distinct movements rather than head–solos–head","An audible seam where the written part stops and improvisation begins"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Gunther Schuller","instrument":"composition, French horn, criticism","why":"Named the movement, wrote much of its repertoire, and later wrote the two-volume history of jazz that shaped how it is taught."},{"name":"John Lewis","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"The Modern Jazz Quartet applied fugal and rondo procedures to blues material for four decades without gimmickry."},{"name":"Charles Mingus","instrument":"bass, composition","why":"His extended works use classical scale and ambition while remaining rooted in blues and gospel; he rejected the label but fits the description."},{"name":"George Russell","instrument":"composition, theory","why":"Combined a rigorous theoretical system with big-band writing that neither classical nor jazz orthodoxy could claim."},{"name":"Jimmy Giuffre","instrument":"clarinet, composition","why":"His 1961 trio with Paul Bley and Steve Swallow is chamber music and free improvisation at the same time."}],"keyLabels":["Atlantic","Columbia","Verve","ECM (later)"],"contested":"Whether it is a genre, a project, or a failure. Many musicians it describes — Mingus above all — rejected the label. Schuller himself later widened the definition so far that it arguably stopped naming anything specific. Critics of the term argue it presumes classical music is a separate, higher stream that jazz must be married into.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"post-bop","name":"Post-Bop","aka":["Modern mainstream","The Blue Note new thing"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1963,"peak":[1964,1969],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"The music of players who absorbed free jazz without abandoning form — ambiguous, floating, still structured.","summary":"By the mid-1960s a group of musicians had heard Ornette Coleman and Cecil Taylor and did not want to go all the way, but could not go back either. Post-bop is what they built. The forms are still there but stretched — 22 bars instead of 32, sections of irregular length, tunes that never quite resolve. The harmony floats: chords with ambiguous function, key centres that shift without announcing themselves, melodies that imply several possible tonalities. The rhythm section becomes genuinely interactive rather than supportive — the Miles Davis Second Great Quintet with Hancock, Carter and Williams played 'time, no changes', keeping a pulse while treating the harmony as negotiable, and Williams could imply three tempos at once. Wayne Shorter's compositions are the central body of writing: short, enigmatic heads with harmony that resists analysis. Andrew Hill, Bobby Hutcherson, Joe Henderson and Herbie Hancock make up most of the rest. It is still the default language of conservatory-trained modern jazz, which is why the term also gets used loosely for anything mainstream and post-1960.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Functionally ambiguous. Slash chords, unresolved suspensions, modal interchange, and progressions that move by step or third rather than by fifth.","rhythm":"Elastic. The pulse is present but the group can imply double time, half time, or a different meter simultaneously; the drummer is a co-improviser.","form":"Irregular section lengths, forms that don't loop cleanly, and pieces where the solo section differs structurally from the head.","instrumentation":"Quintet and sextet formats; vibraphone becomes prominent, and the piano sometimes drops out entirely.","improvisation":"Freer relationship to the written harmony — playing 'outside' deliberately and resolving back, or ignoring the changes while keeping the form.","timbre":"Cooler and more transparent than hard bop. Space and dynamics are compositional elements."},"earMarkers":["Chords that never settle — you can't tell what key the tune is in","The drummer commenting and disrupting rather than keeping steady time","Forms that don't come around when you expect them to","Solos that go deliberately 'wrong' against the harmony, then snap back","Heads that are short, strange, and hard to hum"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Wayne Shorter","instrument":"tenor and soprano saxophone, composition","why":"Wrote the style's core repertoire — harmonically slippery pieces that are still being played and still resist easy analysis."},{"name":"Herbie Hancock","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Developed a comping approach that suggests rather than states harmony, and wrote the floating pieces that define the sound."},{"name":"Tony Williams","instrument":"drums","why":"Joined Miles at 17 and rebuilt the drummer's role as an equal improviser who can move the time without losing it."},{"name":"Andrew Hill","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"The most structurally radical of the Blue Note post-bop writers; his forms are asymmetric in ways that still sound unusual."},{"name":"Joe Henderson","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"Moved between inside and outside playing more fluently than almost anyone, on both hard bop and avant-garde dates."},{"name":"Bobby Hutcherson","instrument":"vibraphone","why":"Made the vibraphone a colour instrument for ambiguous harmony rather than a novelty."}],"keyLabels":["Blue Note","Columbia","Milestone"],"contested":"The term is used two incompatible ways: narrowly for the mid-1960s Blue Note/Miles quintet music described here, and broadly for any acoustic jazz made after bebop that isn't free. Some writers file Kind of Blue and much of the 1970s mainstream here too. This guide uses the narrow definition and says so.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"afro-cuban","name":"Afro-Cuban & Latin Jazz","aka":["Cubop","Latin jazz","Salsa jazz"],"family":"global","era":{"start":1940,"peak":[1947,1975],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"Havana / New York","country":"Cuba / USA"},"oneLine":"Jazz harmony and soloing built on Cuban clave instead of swing — the rhythm section is doing something completely different.","summary":"The change is rhythmic and it is total. Swing divides the beat into a lilting triplet feel; Afro-Cuban music divides it evenly and organises everything around clave, a two-bar asymmetric pattern (three strokes in one bar, two in the other) that every other part must align with. Get the clave backwards and the whole band sounds wrong to anyone who knows. Layer on top the tumbao bass pattern that anticipates the downbeat rather than landing on it, the montuno piano figure, congas, timbales, bongos, cowbell — and you have a rhythm section that is polyrhythmic by design rather than by accident. Dizzy Gillespie and Cuban percussionist Chano Pozo made the fusion explicit in 1947 with 'Manteca' and 'Cubana Be, Cubana Bop', and Machito's orchestra with arranger Mario Bauzá had been doing it in New York since 1940. Later waves brought Brazilian, Puerto Rican and Panamanian currents, and by the 1970s the Fania-era salsa scene and jazz were sharing personnel constantly. The African retentions in Cuban music were less interrupted than in the US, which is part of why the meeting felt like a reunion.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Jazz harmony largely intact — bebop changes, modal vamps, and montuno progressions that cycle a short chord sequence indefinitely.","rhythm":"Clave-organised, with even (straight) eighth notes rather than swung ones. Tumbao bass anticipates beat 1, and multiple percussion parts interlock.","form":"Often two-part: a written, arranged section followed by a montuno — an open vamp for solos, mambo horn riffs and percussion features.","instrumentation":"Full jazz horn section plus congas, timbales, bongos, güiro, claves, cowbell. Piano plays montuno figures rather than comping freely.","improvisation":"Bebop-derived soloing over vamps, plus percussion soloing as a featured element in its own right.","timbre":"Bright, brassy and dense. Percussion is foregrounded rather than treated as accompaniment."},"earMarkers":["Even, straight eighth notes instead of the loping swing feel","A repeating two-bar pattern (clave) that everything else lines up against","Congas and timbales, not just a drum kit","The bass landing just before the downbeat rather than on it","A long open section where the band vamps on two chords and the percussion takes over"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Mario Bauzá","instrument":"trumpet, arranging","why":"Machito's musical director and the person who actually engineered the meeting of Cuban rhythm and jazz orchestration."},{"name":"Chano Pozo","instrument":"congas, composition","why":"Brought authentic Afro-Cuban drumming and its religious repertoire into Gillespie's band; murdered in 1948 at 33."},{"name":"Dizzy Gillespie","instrument":"trumpet, bandleading","why":"The bebop figure who insisted the rhythmic connection mattered and had the platform to prove it."},{"name":"Tito Puente","instrument":"timbales, vibraphone, arranging","why":"Bridged mambo dance music and jazz for five decades with formidable arranging chops."},{"name":"Eddie Palmieri","instrument":"piano, bandleading","why":"Brought McCoy Tyner's harmonic density and free-jazz edges into salsa without losing the dance floor."},{"name":"Chucho Valdés","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Led Irakere in Cuba, fusing Yoruba batá drumming with post-bop harmony under conditions of near-total isolation from the US scene."}],"keyLabels":["Verve","Tico","Fania","Blue Note","Concord Picante"],"contested":"Whether 'Latin jazz' is one genre or a shorthand for several unrelated ones. Cuban-derived, Brazilian, Puerto Rican and Argentine jazz traditions have different rhythmic bases and grouping them can obscure more than it explains. This guide separates the Brazilian stream out for that reason.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"bossa-brazilian","name":"Bossa Nova & Brazilian Jazz","aka":["Bossa nova","Samba-jazz","MPB-jazz"],"family":"global","era":{"start":1958,"peak":[1962,1974],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"Rio de Janeiro","country":"Brazil"},"oneLine":"Samba played quietly, with jazz harmony and a voice barely above speech.","summary":"Bossa nova is samba with the volume and the percussion battery removed and the harmony made much more complicated. João Gilberto worked out the guitar pattern — a syncopated thumb-and-fingers figure that implies a whole samba percussion section on one instrument — and a way of singing almost without projection, so close to the microphone that it sounds like someone thinking out loud. Antônio Carlos Jobim wrote the songs, with chromatic inner voices and chord movements that come as much from Debussy and Chopin as from any Brazilian source. Vinicius de Moraes wrote many of the lyrics. It arrived in the US in 1962 through Charlie Byrd and Stan Getz, and 'The Girl from Ipanema' turned it into a global commercial phenomenon so complete that it became lounge wallpaper, which has damaged its reputation ever since. Underneath the easy-listening reception there is a serious body of work, and the wider Brazilian instrumental tradition — Hermeto Pascoal, Egberto Gismonti, Airto Moreira, Milton Nascimento — pushed far past bossa into territory that fed 1970s fusion directly.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"The most harmonically complex popular song repertoire of its era: chromatic descending inner lines, altered dominants, and modulations that arrive without warning.","rhythm":"The bossa guitar pattern displaces accents across two bars, derived from samba but stripped to essentials. Even eighths, soft dynamics, brushes or light sticks on the kit.","form":"Song forms, often through-composed and irregular rather than 32-bar AABA. Instrumental versions extend them with solo choruses.","instrumentation":"Nylon-string guitar, voice, piano, bass, drums with brushes; often flute or saxophone. Percussion is minimal compared with samba.","improvisation":"Restrained and melodic in bossa proper; the later samba-jazz and Brazilian instrumental scenes are far more virtuosic.","timbre":"Soft, warm, close-miked. Gilberto's near-whispered singing and the nylon-string guitar define the palette."},"earMarkers":["One nylon-string guitar playing a syncopated pattern that implies a whole percussion section","Singing that is quiet, conversational and almost never held out","Chords that shift in unexpected directions under a very simple melody","Even eighth notes with no swing lilt","Drums played with brushes and a rim-click, kept very low in the mix"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"João Gilberto","instrument":"guitar, voice","why":"Invented both the guitar pattern and the vocal delivery; without him bossa nova does not exist as a sound."},{"name":"Antônio Carlos Jobim","instrument":"composition, piano, guitar","why":"Wrote the repertoire, and wrote it with a harmonic sophistication that made it durable jazz material."},{"name":"Stan Getz","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"The American who carried it to a mass audience, with a tone light enough not to crush the material."},{"name":"Hermeto Pascoal","instrument":"multi-instrumentalist, composition","why":"The Brazilian tradition at its most unrestrained — folk forms, free improvisation, and sounds made from anything at hand."},{"name":"Airto Moreira","instrument":"percussion","why":"Brought Brazilian percussion into American fusion, appearing on Bitches Brew and with Weather Report and Return to Forever."},{"name":"Egberto Gismonti","instrument":"guitar, piano, composition","why":"Combined Brazilian folk material, classical technique and improvisation, mostly through ECM."}],"keyLabels":["Odeon","Elenco","Verve","ECM","Milestone"],"contested":"Whether bossa nova is jazz at all. In Brazil it is classified as popular song, and Brazilian musicians often find the American framing of it as a jazz style distorting. Its 1960s American reception also flattened a sharply defined art movement into background music, a reading that Brazilian critics have pushed back on for sixty years.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"ethio-jazz","name":"Ethio-Jazz","aka":["Ethiopian jazz","Ethio-groove"],"family":"global","era":{"start":1966,"peak":[1969,1978],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"Addis Ababa","country":"Ethiopia"},"oneLine":"Ethiopian five-note modes and jazz-funk instrumentation, producing a sound that seems to be in a minor key and a major key at once.","summary":"Ethiopian traditional music uses a system of pentatonic modes called qignit, of which four are common — tizita, bati, ambassel and anchihoye. They are five-note scales with wide intervals, and to ears trained on Western music they sound simultaneously mournful and unresolved. Mulatu Astatke, who studied in London and at Berklee in Boston and had absorbed Latin jazz along the way, put those modes over jazz and funk instrumentation — vibraphone, electric bass, organ, horn sections, congas — and called the result Ethio-jazz. The rhythmic feel is its own thing: a loping, slightly dragging groove often in 6/8 or a swung 4, quite unlike either American funk or Cuban clave. The music flourished under Haile Selassie's later years, then was largely shut down by the Derg regime's curfews and nationalisation of the record industry after 1974. It was rediscovered internationally through the Éthiopiques reissue series, launched in 1997 by Francis Falceto on Buda Musique, and Jim Jarmusch's use of Astatke's music in Broken Flowers in 2005.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Built on pentatonic qignit modes rather than functional harmony. Chords are often ambiguous or drone-like, supporting the mode rather than moving through changes.","rhythm":"Loping and slightly behind, frequently in compound meters. Neither swing nor clave — the feel derives from Ethiopian dance rhythms like the chik chika.","form":"Short repeating structures with extended vamps for solos; horn riffs stated and restated rather than developed.","instrumentation":"Vibraphone (Astatke's signature), electric organ, electric bass, horn section, congas and kit; sometimes the traditional krar and masenqo.","improvisation":"Modal soloing within the qignit, often quite short. The groove and the mode, rather than solo display, carry the music.","timbre":"Warm, slightly murky analogue recording; vibraphone and Farfisa-style organ against a reedy horn section."},"earMarkers":["A five-note scale with big gaps in it that sounds neither major nor minor","Vibraphone playing lead lines over an electric bass groove","A groove that drags slightly behind the beat rather than pushing","Horn riffs that repeat without developing, hypnotically","Organ tones that sound closer to 1960s garage rock than to jazz"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Mulatu Astatke","instrument":"vibraphone, percussion, arranging","why":"Named and defined the style; the only person who deliberately set out to fuse Ethiopian modes with jazz as a project."},{"name":"Girma Bèyènè","instrument":"piano, arranging, voice","why":"Arranger on much of the Addis golden-age catalogue and a distinctive singer in his own right."},{"name":"Mahmoud Ahmed","instrument":"voice","why":"The great vocalist of the era; his Erè Mèla Mèla was the record that broke Ethiopian music in Europe in the mid-1980s."},{"name":"Getatchew Mekurya","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"Adapted the shellela, a sung Ethiopian war chant, to saxophone, arriving at something that sounds like free jazz by a completely different route."},{"name":"Hailu Mergia","instrument":"keyboards, accordion","why":"Led the Walias Band, then spent decades driving a taxi in Washington DC before his rediscovery."}],"keyLabels":["Amha Records","Kaifa Records","Philips-Ethiopia","Buda Musique (Éthiopiques)","Awesome Tapes From Africa"],"contested":"Whether Ethio-jazz names a genre or one man's project. Astatke coined the term for his own music, and much of what gets filed under it — Mahmoud Ahmed's vocal records, the Walias Band's instrumentals — is Ethiopian popular music with horns rather than a jazz-derived fusion. The Éthiopiques series bundled all of it together for a European audience.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"j-jazz","name":"J-Jazz","aka":["Japanese jazz","Deep modern jazz from Japan"],"family":"global","era":{"start":1961,"peak":[1969,1984],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"Tokyo","country":"Japan"},"oneLine":"Japanese players taking modal and spiritual jazz somewhere harder, denser and more audiophile-recorded than anyone in America was.","summary":"Japan had a jazz scene from the 1920s, but the thing worth hearing starts in the late 1960s, when a generation of players stopped copying American records and started producing something with its own weight. The reference points are Coltrane's quartet, Blue Note post-bop and free jazz, but the results are distinct: harder-edged, often darker, with a taste for extended modal vamps, unusual instrumentation, and a physical intensity in the playing. Yosuke Yamashita's trio played free jazz with the violence of a rock band. Masabumi Kikuchi and Terumasa Hino went deep into electric fusion. Masahiko Togashi built a percussion language after a spinal injury left him unable to use his legs. Traditional Japanese instruments and materials appear — shakuhachi, koto, folk melodies — but usually as structure rather than decoration. Crucially, the independent labels, above all Three Blind Mice, founded in 1970 by Takeshi Fujii with the explicit ambition of being 'the Blue Note of Japan', recorded the music with extraordinary audio quality, which is part of why the reissue market went so hard for it after the BBE compilations from 2018 onward.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Modal and post-bop derived, with a marked preference for minor modes, quartal voicings and long static vamps.","rhythm":"Often heavier and more insistent than American models; rock and funk backbeats appear alongside free time and traditional Japanese metrical ideas.","form":"Extended pieces, frequently side-long, with slow builds from near-silence to collective intensity.","instrumentation":"Standard jazz groups plus electric keyboards, and occasionally shakuhachi, koto, biwa or taiko.","improvisation":"Physical and sustained. The free wing plays at very high energy; the modal wing favours long, patient development.","timbre":"Exceptional recording quality — Three Blind Mice and Nippon Columbia's engineers captured bass and drums with unusual depth and dynamic range."},"earMarkers":["Modal vamps held far longer than an American record of the same period would","Bass and drums recorded with startling clarity and presence","Piano attacked percussively, sometimes almost violently","Traditional Japanese instruments used structurally rather than as exotic colour","Long slow builds from near-silence to full intensity across ten minutes or more"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Terumasa Hino","instrument":"trumpet","why":"The most internationally visible Japanese jazz musician of the era; moved from hard bop through free to electric fusion."},{"name":"Yosuke Yamashita","instrument":"piano","why":"His drummerless-free trio played with a physical ferocity that made European free-jazz audiences take Japan seriously in the 1970s."},{"name":"Masabumi Kikuchi","instrument":"piano","why":"Moved between Gil Evans' orchestra, electric fusion and, much later, an extremely spare ECM trio; one of the most restless careers in the music."},{"name":"Masahiko Togashi","instrument":"percussion","why":"Rebuilt his entire approach to the kit after a 1969 injury, producing a uniquely spacious percussion language."},{"name":"Sadao Watanabe","instrument":"alto saxophone, flute","why":"Studied at Berklee and returned to teach; the central populariser and educator of the Japanese scene."},{"name":"Toshiko Akiyoshi","instrument":"piano, composition, bandleading","why":"The first Japanese musician to break through in the US, and a major big-band composer who wrote Japanese material into the idiom."}],"keyLabels":["Three Blind Mice","Nippon Columbia","Victor Japan","Nippon Phonogram/Philips","BBE (reissues)"],"contested":"Whether 'J-jazz' is a genre or a nationality with a reissue market attached. The term was popularised by European compilers in the 2010s rather than by Japanese musicians, and it lumps free jazz, spiritual jazz, fusion and hard bop together on the basis of geography. This guide keeps it because the scene had its own labels, its own recording aesthetic and a distinct set of shared reference points — but the objection is fair.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"euro-free-improv","name":"European Free Improvisation","aka":["Free improv","Non-idiomatic improvisation","Echtzeitmusik (later)"],"family":"avant","era":{"start":1965,"peak":[1968,1985],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"London / Amsterdam / Wuppertal","country":"UK / Netherlands / Germany"},"oneLine":"Improvisation that cut its ties to jazz entirely — no tunes, no pulse, no blues, no American vocabulary.","summary":"American free jazz kept a great deal: a rhythmic pulse most of the time, a blues sensibility, the sound of the saxophone as a voice, and often a head to start from. European improvisers in the mid-1960s decided to cut all of it. Derek Bailey called the result 'non-idiomatic improvisation' and meant it as a technical description — music that deliberately avoids belonging to any style, including free jazz. In practice that means extended techniques, extreme dynamic range, silence used structurally, and a rejection of anything that would let a listener predict the next event. The scenes were national and distinct: London around the Spontaneous Music Ensemble, Bailey, Evan Parker and the Incus label, favouring quiet, fast, detailed interaction; Amsterdam around Misha Mengelberg, Han Bennink and the Instant Composers Pool, full of theatre, absurdity and sudden collapses into Monk tunes; Wuppertal and Berlin around Peter Brötzmann, at maximum volume and maximum density. Politics were explicit — several of these musicians framed the work as a rejection of American cultural dominance and of the market itself, and set up their own labels to prove it.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"None in any functional sense. Pitch is used as texture and colour; consonance appears as one option among many, never as a resting place.","rhythm":"No steady pulse. Rhythm exists as gesture, density and interruption. When a groove appears it is usually a joke or a quotation.","form":"Real-time and emergent. Pieces begin and end when the players decide; recordings are often single unbroken improvisations.","instrumentation":"Anything. Prepared and amplified instruments, homemade devices, objects, voice. Bailey used amplification and volume pedals as compositional tools.","improvisation":"The entire content. Listening and response operate on a very fast timescale, closer to conversation than to soloing.","timbre":"The primary parameter. Multiphonics, circular breathing, scraped strings, feedback, near-inaudible sounds next to sudden violence."},"earMarkers":["No steady beat anywhere, and no melody that repeats","Instruments making sounds you can't identify — scrapes, squeaks, air, feedback","Long silences used as part of the music, not as mistakes","Volume that jumps from almost nothing to overwhelming without warning","Players reacting to each other within a fraction of a second, like fast conversation"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Derek Bailey","instrument":"guitar","why":"Theorised and practised non-idiomatic improvisation more rigorously than anyone; also wrote the movement's key book."},{"name":"Evan Parker","instrument":"soprano and tenor saxophone","why":"Developed a circular-breathing, multiphonic solo soprano language that has no antecedent in jazz saxophone."},{"name":"Peter Brötzmann","instrument":"reeds","why":"The German wing's ferocity in one player; Machine Gun in 1968 remains a hard line in the sand."},{"name":"Han Bennink","instrument":"drums, percussion","why":"Brought physical comedy and total unpredictability to the kit, and played anything in the room as an instrument."},{"name":"Misha Mengelberg","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Ran the Dutch scene's mix of rigour and absurdism, and kept Monk and Herbie Nichols alive inside free improvisation."},{"name":"John Stevens","instrument":"drums","why":"The Spontaneous Music Ensemble's organiser, whose 'insect music' aesthetic prioritised listening over projection."}],"keyLabels":["Incus","FMP","ICP","Emanem","Ogun"],"contested":"Whether it belongs in a jazz lineage at all. Bailey argued explicitly that free improvisation is not a jazz style, and many practitioners reject the connection. The counter-argument is genealogical rather than musical: nearly all the first generation came out of playing jazz, and the break only makes sense in relation to what it broke from.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"free-jazz","name":"Free Jazz","aka":["The New Thing","Energy music"],"family":"avant","era":{"start":1959,"peak":[1960,1969],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York / Los Angeles","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Jazz that abandoned the chord changes, and then, in some hands, the beat and the form as well.","summary":"The chord sequence had been the container for jazz improvisation for forty years. Free jazz threw it out. Ornette Coleman's quartet — no piano, so nothing stating harmony — played tunes with clear melodies and then improvised on the melodic and emotional content rather than a fixed harmonic grid, keeping a pulse. Cecil Taylor came from the other side, with a percussive, dense, atonal piano attack rooted in twentieth-century composition as much as in jazz. Albert Ayler kept marches, folk melodies and hymns as raw material, then obliterated them with a huge shuddering tone and mass collective improvisation. Coltrane arrived in 1965 with Ascension and took his audience with him. Common features: no pre-set changes, frequently no fixed meter, collective rather than sequential improvisation, and an expanded idea of acceptable sound — squeals, overtones, screams. The reception was hostile in a way that is hard to overstate; musicians lost work over it. The politics were inseparable, running alongside the civil rights and Black Power movements, and several key figures framed the music explicitly in those terms.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"No pre-agreed progression. Pitch centres emerge and dissolve; players may follow a melodic contour or a mood rather than a key.","rhythm":"Pulse is optional. Ornette's groups mostly keep one; Taylor and Ayler's often abandon meter entirely for waves of density.","form":"Frequently a stated theme, then open collective improvisation, then a return. Duration is determined by the performance, not the form.","instrumentation":"Often piano-less, so no instrument is fixing the harmony. Double bassists sometimes doubled; two drummers appear regularly.","improvisation":"Simultaneous and collective. Everyone can lead at once; the New Orleans front line comes back in radically altered form.","timbre":"Enormously expanded — overblowing, altissimo screams, split tones, vocalised cries. Sound as expression, not as tone production."},"earMarkers":["No repeating chord pattern to follow — the harmony never comes around","Several players soloing at once instead of taking turns","Saxophones screaming, honking and producing more than one note at a time","The beat disappearing entirely for long stretches","A clear, almost naive melody at the start and end of something otherwise chaotic"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Ornette Coleman","instrument":"alto saxophone, composition","why":"Removed the chord changes while keeping melody and pulse; his 1959–61 Atlantic records are the founding statement."},{"name":"Cecil Taylor","instrument":"piano","why":"Built a percussive, atonal, physically overwhelming piano language from a different starting point than Coleman's."},{"name":"Albert Ayler","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"Used hymns, marches and folk tunes as launch material and a vast vibrato-drenched tone; the most emotionally extreme voice in the music."},{"name":"John Coltrane","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"His move into free playing after 1965 gave the style credibility and an audience it could not otherwise have reached."},{"name":"Sun Ra","instrument":"piano, keyboards, composition","why":"Ran the Arkestra from the mid-1950s with a mix of big-band writing, electronics, free improvisation and cosmology all at once."},{"name":"Archie Shepp","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"Tied the music explicitly to Black political struggle in his writing and speech as well as his playing."}],"keyLabels":["Atlantic","ESP-Disk","Impulse!","Blue Note","BYG Actuel"],"contested":"Whether 'free jazz' is one thing. Ornette Coleman's melodic, pulse-keeping music and Cecil Taylor's atonal density have almost nothing in common technically, and Coleman himself disliked the term. 'The New Thing' was the period label; 'energy music' names only one wing of it.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"aacm-avant","name":"AACM & Great Black Music","aka":["Chicago avant-garde","Creative music","BAG (St. Louis)"],"family":"avant","era":{"start":1965,"peak":[1969,1985],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"Chicago","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Free jazz that got organised — a musicians' collective that added composition, silence, theatre and a hundred small instruments.","summary":"The Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians was founded in Chicago in 1965 by Muhal Richard Abrams and others as a self-governing organisation: members ran their own concerts, taught a free music school, and refused to depend on club owners or record companies. That structure produced a music distinct from New York free jazz. Where the New York scene favoured continuous high-energy blowing, the AACM built pieces with composed sections, extreme dynamic contrast, and long stretches of near-silence. The Art Ensemble of Chicago's slogan, 'Great Black Music, Ancient to the Future', announces the scope: field hollers, marches, bebop, free improvisation and African percussion inside a single performance, often in face paint and costume, with theatre as an explicit element. 'Little instruments' — bells, whistles, gongs, toys, bicycle horns, anything — became a signature, and members routinely play four or five instruments each. Anthony Braxton took the compositional side furthest, into diagrammatically titled works and multi-orchestra pieces. A parallel collective, BAG, formed in St. Louis in 1968, and both fed the New York loft scene in the 1970s.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Ranges from fully atonal to plain diatonic depending on the section. Harmony is a compositional choice made per passage, not a governing system.","rhythm":"Anything from strict written meter to free time to processional march. Silence is treated as a rhythmic value.","form":"Composed multi-section works, graphic and verbal scores, and structures that allocate freedom differently in each part.","instrumentation":"Multi-instrumentalism as standard, plus a percussion battery of small instruments. Contrabass clarinet, sopranino saxophone, bassoon and non-Western instruments all appear.","improvisation":"Inside a designed frame. The question is usually 'what kind of freedom does this section have', not 'is this free'.","timbre":"Extreme range and extreme contrast — a bicycle horn next to a bass saxophone next to four minutes of almost nothing."},"earMarkers":["Very long quiet passages where almost nothing happens, then sudden density","Bells, whistles, gongs and toys used as seriously as the horns","One player switching between four or five instruments within a piece","Abrupt swerves between styles — a march, then chaos, then a bebop line","Composed written sections that clearly aren't improvised"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Muhal Richard Abrams","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Co-founded the AACM and ran the Experimental Band that trained most of its first generation."},{"name":"Roscoe Mitchell","instrument":"saxophones","why":"Sound in 1966 is the first AACM record and one of the first jazz records to use silence as a structural material."},{"name":"Anthony Braxton","instrument":"reeds, composition","why":"Built the largest and most systematic compositional apparatus in the music, while also playing standards and solo saxophone."},{"name":"Art Ensemble of Chicago","instrument":"collective","why":"The style's public face: theatre, costume, little instruments, and a repertoire spanning the whole history of Black music."},{"name":"Henry Threadgill","instrument":"saxophones, flute, composition","why":"Wrote for unusual fixed instrumentations — tuba and cello ensembles, two guitars, two tubas — with a compositional rigour that won a Pulitzer in 2016."},{"name":"Julius Hemphill","instrument":"alto saxophone, composition","why":"The central figure of St. Louis's BAG and founder of the World Saxophone Quartet."}],"keyLabels":["Delmark","Nessa","BYG Actuel","ECM","Black Saint/Soul Note","Arista Freedom"],"contested":"Whether the AACM constitutes a genre or an institution whose members made very different music. Braxton, Threadgill and the Art Ensemble share an approach to structure and self-organisation more than a sound. 'Great Black Music' is the Art Ensemble's own framing and is broader than any style label.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"spiritual-jazz","name":"Spiritual Jazz","aka":["Astral jazz","Cosmic jazz","Devotional jazz"],"family":"avant","era":{"start":1964,"peak":[1969,1977],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York / Detroit / Los Angeles","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Modal jazz aimed at transcendence — drones, chanting, harps, and long ecstatic builds that function as devotion.","summary":"A Love Supreme in 1965 established the template: a suite structured as a religious offering, with a chanted mantra, a bass ostinato functioning as a drone, and a build to ecstatic release. What follows takes those elements and adds the instruments and modes of Indian, African and Middle Eastern devotional music — tanpura drones, harp, sitar, hand percussion, bells, kalimba, voices chanting in unison. Alice Coltrane brought the harp and Hindu devotional practice; Pharoah Sanders combined near-nursery-rhyme melodies with tenor playing at the edge of what the horn can produce; Sun Ra was already there by a different route. Detroit's Tribe collective and Strata-East in New York provided the institutional base — artist-run labels making records that no major would touch, which is also why originals are so scarce and so expensive. The category is largely retrospective: it was assembled by DJs, reissue compilers and record collectors from the 1990s onward, and the musicians themselves generally just said they were playing jazz. That does not make it unreal, but it is worth knowing.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Modal and drone-based. Long pedal points, pentatonic and Eastern-derived modes, and simple two-chord frames that can be sustained indefinitely.","rhythm":"Ostinato-driven, often in 6/8 or 3/4, with hand percussion layered over the kit. Tempo is usually stable but not the focus.","form":"Suites and long-form pieces with distinct movements; a common shape is calm statement, escalation to collective intensity, and return.","instrumentation":"Saxophones and piano plus harp, tanpura, sitar, oud, bells, shakers, kalimba, choral voices, and occasionally strings.","improvisation":"Long, cumulative and intensity-driven rather than harmonically navigational; overblowing and multiphonics used as devotional expression.","timbre":"Reverb-heavy, wide, with drones underneath. The harp and bells give the style its distinctive shimmer."},"earMarkers":["A drone or repeated bass figure that holds under the whole piece","Voices chanting a short phrase over and over","Harp, bells, or hand percussion where you would expect a piano","Saxophone playing that goes from gentle melody to screaming and back","Pieces built as slow builds over ten or fifteen minutes, structured like a rite"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"John Coltrane","instrument":"tenor and soprano saxophone","why":"A Love Supreme gave the idiom its structure, its intent and its permission."},{"name":"Alice Coltrane","instrument":"harp, piano, organ, composition","why":"Brought the harp, Hindu devotional practice and string orchestration into jazz and made a body of work nobody has matched."},{"name":"Pharoah Sanders","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"Paired simple, almost childlike melodies with extreme overblowing — the style's characteristic contrast in a single player."},{"name":"Sun Ra","instrument":"keyboards, composition","why":"Was doing cosmology, robes and collective ritual a decade before the term existed."},{"name":"Don Cherry","instrument":"trumpet, multi-instrumentalist","why":"Took the impulse global — Turkish, Indian, Malian and Scandinavian folk material inside an improvising frame."},{"name":"Doug Carn","instrument":"organ, composition","why":"With vocalist Jean Carn, made the most song-based version of the style for Black Jazz Records."}],"keyLabels":["Impulse!","Strata-East","Black Jazz","Tribe","Flying Dutchman","Soul Jazz / Jazzman (reissues)"],"contested":"Whether it is a genre at all. The term was constructed retrospectively by compilers and collectors, largely from the 1990s onward, and the musicians described by it saw themselves as playing modal or free jazz with religious intent. Reading it as a genre risks flattening Alice Coltrane's Vedantic practice, Sanders' Islam and the Detroit collectives' Black nationalism into one aesthetic mood.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"loft-jazz","name":"Loft Jazz","aka":["New York loft scene","The Wildflowers generation"],"family":"avant","era":{"start":1970,"peak":[1975,1979],"end":1982},"origin":{"city":"New York (SoHo / NoHo / Lower East Side)","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"The second generation of the avant-garde, playing in their own apartments because no club would book them.","summary":"By the early 1970s the New York jazz club economy had collapsed for anyone playing anything challenging, and Manhattan was full of cheap industrial loft space. Musicians rented lofts, lived in them, and put on concerts in them. Sam Rivers and Beatrice Rivers ran Studio Rivbea on Bond Street from 1970 until around 1980; Rashied Ali ran Ali's Alley; there was also Environ, the Ladies' Fort and Studio We. What resulted was less a style than a scene with a shared condition: no owner taking a cut, no set length, no drinks minimum, an audience that came specifically for this. The music sat between free jazz and composition — the AACM and BAG diaspora had arrived from Chicago and St. Louis, and their compositional discipline mixed with New York energy playing. Duos and unusual small formats flourished because they were cheap. The five-LP Wildflowers set, recorded at Studio Rivbea over ten days in May 1976, is the scene's document. Gentrification ended it: the same loft space that had been affordable in 1972 was not by 1982.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Wide open — freely atonal, modal, and occasionally straightforwardly tonal within one set. No single harmonic system.","rhythm":"Free time predominates, but the second generation reintroduced grooves, ostinatos and explicit metric structures more readily than the 1960s players.","form":"Composed frames around free sections. Duo and trio formats where structure has to be negotiated live.","instrumentation":"Saxophone-heavy, with the all-saxophone ensemble emerging here. Cello, tuba, bass clarinet and multiple percussionists are common.","improvisation":"Collective, long-form and highly interactive, typically in small groups where every player is audible at all times.","timbre":"Dry, close, unglamorous — most of these recordings were made in the rooms themselves, and the acoustics are part of the document."},"earMarkers":["Recordings that sound like a room, not a studio — you can hear the space and the audience","Unusual small line-ups: two saxophones and nothing else, or saxophone and drums alone","Written composed sections bracketing long free improvisations","Grooves and repeated figures returning, which 1960s free jazz mostly avoided","Sets that clearly ran as long as the musicians wanted"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Sam Rivers","instrument":"tenor and soprano saxophone, flute, piano","why":"Ran Studio Rivbea, the scene's centre, and played long-form free music with unusual structural clarity."},{"name":"David Murray","instrument":"tenor saxophone, bass clarinet","why":"Arrived from California as a very young man and became the scene's most prolific figure; his tone reaches back to Ayler and Hawkins at once."},{"name":"Julius Hemphill","instrument":"alto saxophone, composition","why":"Brought BAG's compositional approach from St. Louis and formed the World Saxophone Quartet out of the scene."},{"name":"Anthony Braxton","instrument":"reeds, composition","why":"A constant presence, bringing AACM structural thinking into the New York environment."},{"name":"Rashied Ali","instrument":"drums","why":"Coltrane's last drummer, who ran Ali's Alley and issued the scene's records on his own Survival label."},{"name":"Oliver Lake","instrument":"alto saxophone, composition","why":"BAG co-founder and WSQ member; among the scene's most consistent composer-bandleaders."}],"keyLabels":["Douglas","India Navigation","Black Saint/Soul Note","Survival","hatHUT"],"contested":"Whether loft jazz is a style or a real-estate situation. Critics at the time used it as a catch-all for New York avant-garde music in the 1970s, and the musicians involved played very differently from each other. This guide treats it as a scene-genre — defined by its conditions of production, which genuinely shaped the music that came out.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"fusion","name":"Jazz Fusion","aka":["Jazz-rock","Electric jazz"],"family":"electric","era":{"start":1968,"peak":[1970,1977],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Jazz plugged into amplifiers and rebuilt around rock volume and funk rhythm, with the improvising kept intact.","summary":"Two things happen at once around 1969. Jazz musicians start using electric instruments — Fender Rhodes, electric bass, distorted guitar, ring modulators — and they stop swinging, replacing the triplet lilt with straight-eighth rock and funk feels. Miles Davis is the hinge: In a Silent Way in 1969 is quiet and static, Bitches Brew in 1970 is dense and assembled in the edit room by Teo Macero, and by 1972 On the Corner is an unbroken funk machine. Nearly everyone in those bands then formed their own: Weather Report, Mahavishnu Orchestra, Return to Forever, Headhunters, Lifetime. The results were technically formidable and often extremely popular, which is why the backlash was severe — critics called it a commercial capitulation and the neo-bop movement in the 1980s defined itself against it. The music itself varies enormously: Mahavishnu is aggressive odd-meter virtuosity, Weather Report is collective and textural, Headhunters is groove-first. What they share is electricity, straight eighths, and the assumption that a rock audience's volume and energy could be jazz's too.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Modal vamps and pedal points dominate, with slash chords, suspended voicings, and stretches where harmony is essentially static.","rhythm":"Straight eighths from rock and funk, plus odd meters — 7/8, 9/8, 5/4 — as a virtuosic marker in the Mahavishnu and Return to Forever wing.","form":"Long, sectional, often built from vamps rather than song forms. Studio editing becomes a compositional tool.","instrumentation":"Fender Rhodes and synthesizers, electric bass, distorted electric guitar, expanded percussion. Horns often processed with wah or echo.","improvisation":"Modal and pentatonic soloing at rock volume, with electric guitar and synthesizer as lead voices alongside horns.","timbre":"Effects as composition — wah, ring modulation, fuzz, echo, and the Rhodes' bell-like decay. Studio production is part of the music."},"earMarkers":["Electric piano (Fender Rhodes) instead of acoustic — bell-like and slightly bent","Straight, even eighth notes with a rock or funk feel, no swing","Distorted electric guitar taking solos where a saxophone would have","Bars that don't come out in fours — count and you'll lose your place","Long stretches on one chord with the intensity coming from rhythm and volume"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Miles Davis","instrument":"trumpet, bandleading","why":"Made the transition audible across four years and staffed most of the bands that followed."},{"name":"Joe Zawinul","instrument":"keyboards, composition","why":"Weather Report's principal writer and an early master of the synthesizer as an orchestral rather than a novelty instrument."},{"name":"Wayne Shorter","instrument":"soprano and tenor saxophone","why":"Weather Report's other half; brought post-bop compositional ambiguity into an electric setting."},{"name":"John McLaughlin","instrument":"guitar","why":"Mahavishnu Orchestra defined the high-velocity, odd-meter, Indian-influenced wing of fusion."},{"name":"Herbie Hancock","instrument":"keyboards, composition","why":"Head Hunters in 1973 took fusion into a funk-first direction and became one of the best-selling jazz records ever made."},{"name":"Jaco Pastorius","instrument":"electric bass","why":"Redefined the fretless electric bass as a lead melodic instrument with a singing, harmonics-rich tone."},{"name":"Tony Williams","instrument":"drums","why":"Lifetime, formed in 1969, was arguably louder and more rock-committed than anything Miles did."}],"keyLabels":["Columbia","CTI","Warner Bros.","Nemperor","Arista"],"contested":"Whether fusion is a genre or a period. 'Jazz-rock' and 'fusion' are used interchangeably by some and distinguished by others (jazz-rock for the 1969–74 aggressive wing, fusion for what came after). The critical rehabilitation of the era is recent and still contested; a lot of writers still treat 1975–85 fusion as a lost decade.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"jazz-funk","name":"Jazz-Funk","aka":["Funky jazz","Crossover jazz","Rare groove"],"family":"electric","era":{"start":1969,"peak":[1972,1979],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York / Los Angeles / Detroit","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"The groove is the composition — jazz players building on a bass line and a drum break instead of a chord sequence.","summary":"Fusion took jazz toward rock; jazz-funk took it toward James Brown and Sly Stone. The difference is priority: in fusion the solo is usually the point and the groove supports it, while in jazz-funk the groove is the piece and the solo is one layer in it. The rhythm is on the one, with a syncopated electric bass line as the primary compositional material, clavinet or Rhodes stabs, a horn section playing tight riffs, and drum parts built for dancing. Herbie Hancock's Head Hunters, Donald Byrd's work with the Mizell brothers, Roy Ayers' Ubiquity, Lonnie Liston Smith and the whole CTI catalogue mark the range from serious to slick. It was commercially the most successful jazz of the 1970s and, for that reason, the most despised by critics of the period. Its afterlife is disproportionate: hip-hop producers mined these records so thoroughly that a huge share of the sampled jazz in rap comes from a narrow band of 1969–79 jazz-funk, which then fed acid jazz, jazz rap and the whole Brainfeeder orbit.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Vamp-based — usually one or two chords, often minor seventh or dominant with a raised ninth, sustained for the whole track.","rhythm":"Sixteenth-note funk with the accent on the one. Syncopated bass lines and drum patterns designed to be looped, whether or not anyone intended that.","form":"Groove, horn riff, solos over the groove, breakdown, out. Song forms in the vocal material; open vamps otherwise.","instrumentation":"Electric bass, clavinet, Rhodes, ARP and Moog synths, wah guitar, horn section, congas alongside the kit.","improvisation":"Present but rationed and blues-pentatonic in orientation. Nobody is running changes.","timbre":"Warm analogue production, heavy compression on the drums, synth bass and clavinet as textural signatures."},"earMarkers":["A bass line you'd recognise on its own — it is the tune","Clavinet or wah guitar chopping out sixteenth notes","The whole band hitting hard on beat one and leaving space after it","Horn riffs that repeat as a hook rather than developing","Drum breaks where everything drops out except the kit — later sampled to death"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Herbie Hancock","instrument":"keyboards, composition","why":"Head Hunters put a jazz musician's harmonic mind inside a Sly Stone rhythm section and sold enormously."},{"name":"Donald Byrd","instrument":"trumpet","why":"His Mizell brothers records are the most fully realised jazz-funk productions and the most sampled."},{"name":"Roy Ayers","instrument":"vibraphone, vocals","why":"Made vibraphone-led jazz-funk with a songwriting sensibility; sampled more than almost anyone in the idiom."},{"name":"Lonnie Liston Smith","instrument":"keyboards","why":"Connected the spiritual jazz wing to the dancefloor with a floating, echo-heavy sound."},{"name":"Eddie Harris","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"Early adopter of the electrified saxophone and a genuine funk writer, not a jazz musician visiting."},{"name":"Grover Washington Jr.","instrument":"saxophones","why":"The commercial bridge from jazz-funk into smooth jazz, and a much better player than that lineage suggests."}],"keyLabels":["CTI/Kudu","Blue Note (1970s)","Polydor","Prestige","Fantasy"],"contested":"The boundary with fusion, which is a matter of emphasis rather than kind. Also whether the 1990s 'rare groove' reissue framing — which selected records for danceability and sample potential — has distorted which artists are remembered.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"free-funk","name":"Free Funk & Harmolodics","aka":["Harmolodic funk","Punk jazz (early)"],"family":"electric","era":{"start":1975,"peak":[1977,1988],"end":1998},"origin":{"city":"New York","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Free jazz over a hard funk backbeat, with everybody playing a lead line at once.","summary":"In 1975 Ornette Coleman plugged in. Prime Time paired two electric guitars, two electric basses and two drummers with his alto, playing his harmolodic theory in an idiom built on funk backbeats. Harmolodics is Coleman's own concept and he explained it inconsistently, but the working version is: harmony, movement and melody have equal status, no instrument is subordinate, and players can transpose a line to whatever register or key suits their instrument while it remains 'the same' line. In practice that produces a thick, deliberately unresolved texture where four or five people are all playing melody over a groove that stays put. Ronald Shannon Jackson's Decoding Society and James Blood Ulmer took it in harder, more rock-facing directions, and it became a direct input to the downtown New York scene and to the whole idea that free improvisation could coexist with a heavy beat. Coleman's own Prime Time records remain the clearest statement, and Song X with Pat Metheny in 1985 dragged the approach onto a much larger stage.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Harmolodic — no fixed key centre, instruments playing lines in different keys simultaneously, consonance and dissonance treated as equally usable.","rhythm":"Hard funk and second-line backbeats, often with two drummers producing overlapping rather than unified patterns.","form":"Themes stated in unison then abandoned; the groove continues while the melodic content roams free.","instrumentation":"Doubled everything — two guitars, two basses, two drummers — plus alto saxophone or trumpet on top.","improvisation":"Simultaneous and non-hierarchical. There is often no soloist because everyone is soloing.","timbre":"Bright, trebly, deliberately cluttered. Guitars are clean-toned and busy rather than distorted and sustaining."},"earMarkers":["A rock-solid funk beat underneath music that is otherwise completely unresolved","Two guitars and two basses all playing different lines at once","Melodies that sound simple and singable, dropped into total harmonic chaos","No obvious soloist — several people appear to be leading simultaneously","Everything sounding slightly out of tune with everything else, on purpose"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Ornette Coleman","instrument":"alto saxophone, composition","why":"Invented harmolodics and Prime Time; the entire idiom is his theory made audible."},{"name":"Ronald Shannon Jackson","instrument":"drums, composition","why":"The Decoding Society took harmolodic principles into denser, more composed and harder-hitting territory."},{"name":"James Blood Ulmer","instrument":"guitar, vocals","why":"Applied harmolodics to blues and rock forms; his guitar tuning and attack have no real precedent."},{"name":"Jamaaladeen Tacuma","instrument":"electric bass","why":"Prime Time's bassist, who treated the bass as a lead melodic voice rather than a foundation."},{"name":"Bern Nix","instrument":"guitar","why":"Prime Time's clean-toned guitarist, whose refusal to play rock guitar clichés is central to the band's sound."}],"keyLabels":["Artists House","Antilles","Island/Antilles","Caravan of Dreams","DIW"],"contested":"Whether harmolodics is a coherent theory or an idiosyncratic vocabulary. Coleman's explanations varied and musicians who played the music describe it in incompatible ways. This guide treats free funk as a genre and harmolodics as one leader's method for producing it — which is an interpretive choice, not a settled position.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"smooth-jazz","name":"Smooth Jazz","aka":["Contemporary jazz (radio format)","Quiet storm jazz"],"family":"electric","era":{"start":1975,"peak":[1987,2002],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"Los Angeles","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Jazz-funk sanded down for radio: melody in front, solos short, nothing that would make a listener flinch.","summary":"Smooth jazz is a radio format that became a genre, and it should be described that way rather than sneered at. Its ancestry is real — Grover Washington Jr., Wes Montgomery's late CTI records, Bob James, the Crusaders — and its ingredients are consistent: a strong singable melody carried by soprano saxophone or guitar, a mid-tempo funk or quiet-storm groove, glossy production with a lot of reverb, and improvisation kept short and consonant. Kenny G's Breathless in 1992 sold in the millions and became the reference point for everything wrong with the format for jazz critics, which is a lot of weight to put on one record. The format's radio dominance from the late 1980s through the early 2000s meant that for a large number of people this was what 'jazz' meant, which is part of why the hostility was so intense. Musically the interesting question is what got removed: harmonic risk, rhythmic disruption, and the possibility that a solo might fail. Nothing is at stake, deliberately. Some of the players — Washington, Larry Carlton, Lee Ritenour — could do far more than the format asked.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Consonant and stable — major sevenths, gentle ii–V motion, no altered dominants. Modulations are for lift, not tension.","rhythm":"Mid-tempo funk, quiet-storm ballad feels, and later programmed drums and quantised grids.","form":"Pop song form. Melody, short solo, melody, fade. Tracks run to radio length.","instrumentation":"Soprano and alto saxophone, clean electric guitar, synthesizers and DX7-era electric pianos, fretless bass, programmed and live drums.","improvisation":"Brief and melodic, staying inside the harmony. Solos are usually written or worked out in advance.","timbre":"Highly produced — chorus, digital reverb, gated drums. The 1980s studio sound is inseparable from the genre."},"earMarkers":["A soprano saxophone playing a melody you could hum after one listen","Everything mixed clean, wide and glossy, with a lot of reverb","Solos that last eight bars and never go outside the chord","A steady mid-tempo groove that stays exactly the same throughout","Tracks that end in a fade at about four and a half minutes"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Grover Washington Jr.","instrument":"saxophones","why":"The genuine link between jazz-funk and the format; a serious improviser whose commercial records set the template."},{"name":"Bob James","instrument":"keyboards, arranging","why":"CTI arranger whose 1970s records supplied both the format's sound and a great many hip-hop samples."},{"name":"David Sanborn","instrument":"alto saxophone","why":"His piercing, blues-derived alto tone became the most imitated sound in the idiom, though his own playing has more bite than the copies."},{"name":"Kenny G","instrument":"soprano saxophone","why":"The best-selling instrumentalist of the era and the lightning rod for every argument about the format's legitimacy."},{"name":"The Crusaders","instrument":"group","why":"Went from hard bop as the Jazz Crusaders to a groove-based crossover sound that prefigured the whole format."}],"keyLabels":["GRP","Warner Bros.","Arista","Verve Forecast"],"contested":"Whether it counts as jazz. The critical consensus has been dismissive for forty years, and the argument that it removes improvisational risk has force. The counter-argument is that dismissing it wholesale erases the Black radio and quiet-storm context it grew out of, and flattens genuine players into one caricature. This guide includes it as a real branch with a real lineage, and does not pretend to be neutral about the ceiling.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"acid-jazz","name":"Acid Jazz","aka":["Jazz dance","Rare groove revival"],"family":"electric","era":{"start":1987,"peak":[1991,1998],"end":2004},"origin":{"city":"London","country":"UK"},"oneLine":"DJs rediscovering 1970s jazz-funk on the dancefloor, then hiring bands to play new music that sounded like it.","summary":"This one starts with record collecting, not with musicians. London club DJs in the mid-1980s — Gilles Peterson and Chris Bangs among them — were playing forgotten 1960s and 70s jazz-funk, soul jazz and Latin records to dancers, and the name 'acid jazz' began as a joke on acid house. From there it went two ways: reissues and compilations that resurrected a whole layer of neglected recordings, and new bands playing original material in that idiom — Brand New Heavies, Incognito, Jamiroquai, the James Taylor Quartet, Galliano — often with hip-hop production techniques, live rhythm sections and rap or soul vocals over the top. The Acid Jazz label was founded in 1987 by Peterson and Eddie Piller; Peterson later left to start Talkin' Loud. Musically it is more a curatorial position than a technique: the groove and the crate-digger's ear are the constants. Its importance is downstream — it built the UK infrastructure of clubs, labels and radio that later produced broken beat, the 2010s London jazz scene, and much of the audience for both.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Borrowed wholesale from soul jazz and jazz-funk — modal vamps, minor sevenths, gospel cadences. Rarely harmonically ambitious on its own terms.","rhythm":"Danceable funk and boogaloo at club tempos, later incorporating breakbeats and programmed drums alongside live players.","form":"Song and groove structures built for DJ use — long intros, breakdowns, clean beat-matchable sections.","instrumentation":"Hammond organ, Rhodes, electric bass, horn section, turntables and samplers, plus soul and rap vocals.","improvisation":"Short and functional. The idiom is about feel, not solo development.","timbre":"Deliberately retro — analogue keyboards, warm bass, drum sounds modelled on 1970s records even when programmed."},"earMarkers":["Hammond organ and horn riffs that sound like 1971 but were recorded in 1993","A rock-steady club tempo designed for a DJ to mix in and out of","Rapping or soul singing over what is otherwise a jazz-funk band","Sampled drum breaks sitting alongside a live rhythm section","Very short solos, if any"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Gilles Peterson","instrument":"DJ, label founder, broadcaster","why":"Co-named the movement, co-founded Acid Jazz then Talkin' Loud, and has kept building the UK's jazz infrastructure ever since."},{"name":"Brand New Heavies","instrument":"band","why":"The most successful of the live acid jazz bands and the closest to a functioning 1970s jazz-funk group."},{"name":"Incognito","instrument":"band","why":"Jean-Paul 'Bluey' Maunick's group provided the idiom's most durable songwriting."},{"name":"James Taylor Quartet","instrument":"band","why":"Hammond-driven and closest to the soul jazz source material, minus the improvisation."},{"name":"Roy Ayers","instrument":"vibraphone","why":"The most important living ancestor, adopted by the scene and actively recording with it."}],"keyLabels":["Acid Jazz","Talkin' Loud","Blue Note (Blue Break Beats)","Dorado"],"contested":"Whether it is a genre or a DJ marketing category. Musicians in it frequently rejected the label. It also has an uneasy relationship with the music it revived — the acid jazz audience discovered soul jazz and jazz-funk via compilations that often stripped the original context away.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"jazz-rap","name":"Jazz Rap","aka":["Hip-hop jazz","Jazzy hip-hop"],"family":"electric","era":{"start":1988,"peak":[1991,1996],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Hip-hop producers building beats from jazz records, and then jazz musicians answering back.","summary":"It starts with sampling. Producers in the late 1980s went looking for breaks in their parents' jazz collections and found that upright bass, Rhodes and horn stabs sat beautifully under rapping. A Tribe Called Quest's The Low End Theory in 1991 is the pivot: sparse, bass-forward, built on jazz samples, with Ron Carter playing upright bass on one track. Gang Starr, Digable Planets, Pete Rock, Us3 and the Native Tongues collective make up the rest of the first wave. Then it goes both ways. Guru's Jazzmatazz in 1993 put live jazz musicians in the studio with a rapper; Miles Davis's last album, Doo-Bop, was a hip-hop collaboration; Branford Marsalis's Buckshot LeFonque and Steve Coleman's projects came from the jazz side. The lasting influence is rhythmic: the boom-bap feel — a heavy, slightly-behind, sampled-sounding backbeat — became a legitimate jazz groove, and by the 2010s drummers like Chris Dave, Karriem Riggins and Marcus Gilmore had absorbed it as a first language. That is the line running into the LA beat scene and the London revival.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Sample-derived. Loops of jazz records supply the harmony wholesale, often two or four bars repeated with no development.","rhythm":"Boom-bap — a hard, slightly loose backbeat around 85–95 BPM, with swung sixteenths and deliberate timing imperfections.","form":"Verse–chorus rap structure over looped material, with the loop as the harmonic and formal constant.","instrumentation":"Sampler and turntables first; upright bass, Rhodes, saxophone and trumpet added live in the second wave.","improvisation":"Initially the rapping is the improvisation-descended element. Live solos appear from Jazzmatazz onward.","timbre":"Warm, dusty, filtered — the sound of vinyl sampled at low bit rates is an aesthetic choice, not a limitation."},"earMarkers":["A short loop of an old jazz record repeating under the whole track","Upright bass, which almost no other hip-hop uses","A drum feel that's slightly behind and slightly loose rather than machine-tight","Muted trumpet or Rhodes chords used as a hook","Vinyl crackle and filtering left audible in the mix"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"A Tribe Called Quest","instrument":"group","why":"The Low End Theory established the sparse, bass-forward jazz-sample aesthetic that everything else worked from."},{"name":"Guru","instrument":"MC","why":"Gang Starr's rapper and the Jazzmatazz series' architect — the first sustained attempt to put rappers and live jazz players in one room."},{"name":"DJ Premier","instrument":"production","why":"Gang Starr's producer and the definitive boom-bap sound designer; his chopping technique is a compositional method."},{"name":"Pete Rock","instrument":"production","why":"Built the lushest horn-sample production of the era, working from soul jazz and hard bop records."},{"name":"Digable Planets","instrument":"group","why":"Took the aesthetic furthest into explicit jazz reference, right down to the bebop-cool persona."},{"name":"J Dilla","instrument":"production","why":"His deliberately unquantised drum programming changed how a generation of jazz drummers thinks about time."}],"keyLabels":["Jive","Chrysalis/EMI","Blue Note","Elektra","Pendulum"],"contested":"Whether it belongs in a jazz lineage or a hip-hop one. It is unambiguously hip-hop as music; its inclusion here is on the grounds of what it sent back — the boom-bap feel is now standard jazz drumming vocabulary. Filing it as a jazz genre risks the old habit of claiming Black popular music for jazz whenever it becomes respectable.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"nu-jazz","name":"Nu Jazz & Electronic Jazz","aka":["Electro-jazz","Future jazz","Broken beat (adjacent)"],"family":"electric","era":{"start":1995,"peak":[1998,2007],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"London / Oslo / Cologne / Paris","country":"UK / Norway / Germany / France"},"oneLine":"Jazz improvisation over programmed electronic rhythm — the drum machine as bandmate rather than backing track.","summary":"Where acid jazz revived old records and jazz rap sampled them, nu jazz starts from electronic dance music and adds improvisers. The rhythmic base is drum and bass, house, broken beat or downtempo — programmed, quantised or deliberately un-quantised — and horns, keyboards and bass improvise across it. There were several parallel scenes. In Norway, Bugge Wesseltoft's New Conception of Jazz and Nils Petter Molvær's Khmer in 1997 put ECM-style trumpet lyricism over dub and electronica, and Jaga Jazzist built a large band around programming. In London, the broken beat scene around West London and labels like Talkin' Loud extended acid jazz's clubbing into more complex rhythmic territory. In Germany, Jazzanova and the Compost/Sonar Kollektiv orbit did it as production. St Germain in Paris put house beats under blues and jazz samples and sold two million records. The best of it treats the beat as a partner — something to play against — and the weakest of it is a lounge compilation. Its influence runs directly into the current London scene.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Modal and vamp-based, with a strong debt to ECM's floating consonance and to house music's static two-chord loops.","rhythm":"Programmed — drum and bass at 170 BPM, house at 125, broken beat's displaced syncopations, downtempo at 90. Live drums often play with or against the grid.","form":"Track-based rather than song-based: build, drop, breakdown. Solos happen inside a producer's arrangement.","instrumentation":"Laptop, sampler and drum machine alongside trumpet, saxophone, Rhodes and double or electric bass.","improvisation":"Real but constrained. The soloist works over a fixed grid and a fixed harmony, closer to a dub toaster's role than a bebop soloist's.","timbre":"Processed heavily — delay and reverb on horns, filtered pads, sub-bass. Molvær's echo-drenched trumpet is the signature sound."},"earMarkers":["Programmed drums that are clearly electronic sitting under acoustic solos","Trumpet or saxophone soaked in delay and reverb, played sparsely","Deep sub-bass that no acoustic instrument could produce","A track that builds and drops like dance music rather than stating a head","Harmony that barely moves across five or six minutes"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Nils Petter Molvær","instrument":"trumpet, production","why":"Khmer in 1997 defined the ECM-meets-electronica sound and remains the idiom's clearest statement."},{"name":"Bugge Wesseltoft","instrument":"keyboards, production","why":"New Conception of Jazz and the Jazzland label built the Norwegian scene as an ongoing project."},{"name":"Jazzanova","instrument":"production collective","why":"The Berlin production team that connected broken beat, Brazilian material and jazz into a coherent European sound."},{"name":"St Germain","instrument":"production","why":"Ludovic Navarre's Tourist sold to an audience far outside jazz and proved the format could carry a mass market."},{"name":"Jaga Jazzist","instrument":"band","why":"A large Norwegian ensemble writing for horns, strings and electronics as one machine."},{"name":"Cinematic Orchestra","instrument":"band","why":"Jason Swinscoe's group moved from sample-based production to a fully live ensemble without changing the aesthetic."}],"keyLabels":["Jazzland","Ninja Tune","Compost","Sonar Kollektiv","Talkin' Loud","F Communications"],"contested":"The name. 'Nu jazz' was largely a retail category and few of the musicians used it. It has also been criticised as jazz's least improvisational branch, where the improviser is a texture inside someone else's production. Both charges are fair for a lot of the catalogue and unfair for the best of it.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"la-beat","name":"LA Beat-Scene Jazz","aka":["Brainfeeder jazz","West Coast Get Down","Beat music"],"family":"electric","era":{"start":2008,"peak":[2015,2023],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"Los Angeles","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Jazz musicians who grew up on J Dilla and Flying Lotus, playing with a producer's ear and a beatmaker's sense of time.","summary":"Two Los Angeles streams converged. One was the beat scene around Low End Theory, the club night that ran from 2006, and Flying Lotus's Brainfeeder label — instrumental hip-hop built on sample manipulation, sub-bass and deliberately wonky, off-grid timing inherited from J Dilla. The other was the West Coast Get Down, a group of musicians who had come up together in Los Angeles schools and played together for a decade before anyone noticed: Kamasi Washington, Thundercat, Terrace Martin, Ronald Bruner Jr., Cameron Graves, Miles Mosley. Thundercat is the physical link, playing bass on Brainfeeder records and in jazz contexts simultaneously. Kendrick Lamar's To Pimp a Butterfly in 2015 put nearly all of them on a mainstream rap record; Washington's three-hour The Epic the same year presented the jazz side with choir, strings and unembarrassed spiritual-jazz ambition. The music combines Coltrane-quartet modal intensity, 1970s spiritual jazz orchestration, and rhythm sections that think in terms of feel and pocket rather than swing.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Modal and pentatonic, McCoy Tyner-derived, over pedal points, with gospel and soul progressions and lush orchestral or choral layering.","rhythm":"Dilla-descended: deliberately off-grid, with the snare pushed or dragged so the beat feels drunk but stays locked. Hip-hop tempos alongside fast swing.","form":"Long-form and suite-like on the jazz side; loop-and-transform on the beat side. Both prefer accumulation to development.","instrumentation":"Six-string electric bass, synthesizers, saxophone, keys, two drummers on occasion, plus choir and strings.","improvisation":"Extended and intensity-driven, closer to spiritual jazz than to post-bop. Solos are long and go for transcendence rather than cleverness.","timbre":"Producer-shaped: heavy sub-bass, saturated synths, vocal layering, and a mix aesthetic drawn from hip-hop rather than jazz engineering."},"earMarkers":["A beat that sounds slightly drunk — snare landing late — but never actually loses time","Sub-bass and synthesizers sitting under acoustic saxophone and drums","Choir or massed vocals used as an orchestral section","Long modal solos that build toward ecstasy in the Coltrane manner","Production values from hip-hop: everything loud, wide and compressed"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Kamasi Washington","instrument":"tenor saxophone, composition","why":"The Epic put large-scale spiritual jazz in front of an audience that had not been listening to jazz."},{"name":"Thundercat","instrument":"electric bass, vocals","why":"The scene's connective tissue, moving between Brainfeeder, Kendrick Lamar and jazz contexts as one practice."},{"name":"Flying Lotus","instrument":"production","why":"Founded Brainfeeder and built the beat aesthetic; also Alice Coltrane's great-nephew, which is not incidental to the music."},{"name":"Terrace Martin","instrument":"alto saxophone, keyboards, production","why":"The producer-musician who brokered the jazz players onto To Pimp a Butterfly."},{"name":"Robert Glasper","instrument":"piano, production","why":"Not LA-based, but Black Radio in 2012 established the template of jazz pianist as R&B and hip-hop bandleader."},{"name":"Chris Dave","instrument":"drums","why":"The drummer who most fully translated Dilla's programmed feel back onto an acoustic kit."}],"keyLabels":["Brainfeeder","Young Turks/XL","Blue Note","Stones Throw"],"contested":"Whether it is a genre or a network. Kamasi Washington's large-ensemble spiritual jazz and Flying Lotus's electronic production have little in common technically; what links them is a city, a label, and a set of collaborations. Some critics argue the 'jazz renaissance' framing around 2015 oversold a scene that had been working for a decade already.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"ecm-nordic","name":"ECM & Nordic Chamber Jazz","aka":["European chamber jazz","Nordic tone","The ECM sound"],"family":"avant","era":{"start":1969,"peak":[1973,1990],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"Munich / Oslo","country":"Germany / Norway"},"oneLine":"Jazz built around space, silence and sustained sound — recorded so precisely the room becomes an instrument.","summary":"Manfred Eicher founded ECM in Munich in 1969 and produced almost everything on it himself, with engineer Jan Erik Kongshaug at Talent Studio in Oslo for a large part of the catalogue. The recording aesthetic — long natural reverb, extreme dynamic range, every instrument separated and audible, piano and cymbals given room to decay — is so consistent that it constitutes a compositional element, and the phrase 'the most beautiful sound next to silence' came from a review and stuck as the label's own slogan. Musically the identifying features are slow harmonic rhythm, folk-derived melody, restraint, and a relationship to European classical music that is unembarrassed rather than programmatic. Jan Garbarek's saxophone tone — cold, pure, enormous — became the sound people mean by 'Nordic'. Keith Jarrett's solo concerts, especially Köln in 1975, made the label commercially significant. It is contested whether this is a genre or a producer's taste, and the argument is real: ECM also released Art Ensemble of Chicago, Steve Reich and Meredith Monk. But the Nordic chamber wing is coherent enough to name.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Slow-moving, modal, often built on suspended and open voicings that avoid clear resolution. Folk modes from Scandinavian and Eastern European sources.","rhythm":"Rubato and free-time passages alongside straight eighths; swing appears rarely. Drummers play colour and texture as much as pulse.","form":"Long, patient, often through-composed. Pieces develop by accumulation and change of density rather than by chorus structure.","instrumentation":"Piano, saxophone, double bass, drums, but also guitar, oud, tabla, string quartet and voice; unusual pairings are routine.","improvisation":"Restrained and thematic. Melodic development matters more than harmonic navigation or rhythmic display.","timbre":"The defining parameter. Long natural reverb, wide dynamics, the decay of notes treated as content."},"earMarkers":["Enormous natural-sounding reverb — the room is clearly part of the record","Very quiet passages that demand you turn it up, then sudden fullness","Melodies that sound like folk songs rather than jazz heads","Drums played with mallets and cymbals left to ring out","Almost no swing feel, and often no steady pulse at all"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Manfred Eicher","instrument":"production","why":"Produced the overwhelming majority of the catalogue; the label's aesthetic is a single person's editorial ear applied for fifty years."},{"name":"Jan Garbarek","instrument":"saxophones","why":"His tone and his use of Norwegian folk material define what 'Nordic jazz' means to most listeners."},{"name":"Keith Jarrett","instrument":"piano","why":"The solo concerts made improvised piano a concert-hall event and gave ECM a commercial base."},{"name":"Eberhard Weber","instrument":"electric upright bass, composition","why":"Built an entirely personal instrument and a body of chamber-jazz writing around it."},{"name":"Terje Rypdal","instrument":"guitar","why":"Brought sustained, distorted, orchestral electric guitar into the label's otherwise acoustic world."},{"name":"Tomasz Stańko","instrument":"trumpet","why":"The Polish wing — darker, more Miles-derived, and connected to a Central European free-jazz history the Nordic players did not share."}],"keyLabels":["ECM","Jazzland (Norway)","Winter & Winter"],"contested":"Whether 'ECM jazz' names a genre or a label's house taste. The catalogue includes free jazz, contemporary classical, Renaissance music and Art Ensemble of Chicago records, which no stylistic definition covers. This guide names the Nordic chamber wing specifically and treats the label as its institutional home rather than as a synonym. There is also a persistent criticism that the aesthetic prizes surface beauty over risk.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"neo-bop","name":"Neo-Bop & the Young Lions","aka":["Neoclassical jazz","The Young Lions","Neo-traditionalism"],"family":"trad-mainstream","era":{"start":1980,"peak":[1984,1996],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York / New Orleans","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"A deliberate return to acoustic hard bop and swing, made by conservatory-trained players who thought fusion had been a wrong turn.","summary":"Wynton Marsalis signed to Columbia in 1981 at nineteen, won Grammys in both jazz and classical categories in 1983, and became the public face of an argument: that jazz had a tradition, that the tradition was acoustic and blues-based and ran from New Orleans through Ellington and Parker to the mid-1960s, and that fusion and free jazz had taken the music away from it. The music that followed is technically formidable and largely acoustic — quintets and sextets, standards and originals in a post-bop language, sharp suits, no synthesizers. It coincided with the institutionalisation of jazz: university programmes, Jazz at Lincoln Center from 1987 with Marsalis as artistic director, the Ken Burns documentary in 2001, repertory orchestras, NEA Jazz Masters. The criticism, made forcefully by Nicholas Payton, Greg Osby and others, is that this froze the music into a museum canon at exactly the moment it should have been moving. The defence is that a generation of musicians got a rigorous grounding they would not otherwise have had. Both are true and the argument has not settled.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Bebop and post-bop harmony played with conservatory precision — full changes, extended forms, and a deliberate refusal of modal shortcuts.","rhythm":"Acoustic swing restored as the default, with New Orleans second-line and Latin feels used as compositional colour.","form":"Standards and originals in traditional forms, plus extended suites, especially in Marsalis's later large-scale works.","instrumentation":"Acoustic quintet and sextet, big band, no electric instruments as a matter of policy in the strict wing.","improvisation":"Highly schooled, harmonically precise, and consciously in dialogue with recorded history — quotation and stylistic allusion are common.","timbre":"Warm, controlled, well-recorded acoustic sound. Digital-era production without digital-era instruments."},"earMarkers":["Entirely acoustic instruments, with a swing feel front and centre","Playing that is audibly virtuosic and audibly schooled","Deliberate references to earlier styles — a New Orleans strut, an Ellington voicing","Standards played straight rather than deconstructed","A clean, warm, modern recording of a determinedly old-fashioned band"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Wynton Marsalis","instrument":"trumpet, composition","why":"The movement's musician, spokesman and institution-builder; his 1997 Pulitzer for Blood on the Fields was the first awarded to a jazz work."},{"name":"Branford Marsalis","instrument":"saxophones","why":"Took the same training in a looser direction, including work with Sting and a hip-hop project his brother would not have made."},{"name":"Terence Blanchard","instrument":"trumpet, composition","why":"Came through the Jazz Messengers, then built a major second career in film scoring and opera."},{"name":"Joshua Redman","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"Won the Monk competition in 1991 and became the most visible of the second Young Lions wave."},{"name":"Roy Hargrove","instrument":"trumpet","why":"The one who most obviously refused the boundaries — hard bop, Afro-Cuban, and the RH Factor's neo-soul, all seriously."},{"name":"Marcus Roberts","instrument":"piano","why":"Went furthest back, rebuilding stride and ragtime technique inside a modern practice."}],"keyLabels":["Columbia","Blue Note","Warner Bros.","Verve","Nonesuch"],"contested":"This genre is an argument as much as a style. Critics including Nicholas Payton and Greg Osby have argued it produced a canon-policing orthodoxy that damaged the music's forward motion and marginalised the avant-garde. Defenders point to the training, the archives and the institutions that resulted. The dispute over who gets to define the tradition is the actual content of the category.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"m-base","name":"M-Base","aka":["Macro-Basic Array of Structured Extemporization"],"family":"avant","era":{"start":1984,"peak":[1988,1998],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York (Brooklyn)","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Improvisation over interlocking, cyclic rhythmic structures drawn from West African and diasporic music — jazz thinking in loops rather than choruses.","summary":"A group of young musicians in Brooklyn in the mid-1980s — Steve Coleman, Greg Osby, Cassandra Wilson, Geri Allen, Robin Eubanks, Graham Haynes, Marvin 'Smitty' Smith — worked out an approach that was neither neo-bop nor free. The rhythmic basis is nested cycles: several repeating patterns of different lengths running simultaneously, so the downbeat is a matter of which layer you follow. That idea comes from West African drumming traditions and their diasporic descendants, and it makes the music feel simultaneously funky and disorienting. Over it, the melodic language is angular, chromatic, and phrased against the grid rather than with it. Coleman coined the acronym, and he has consistently insisted that M-Base names a philosophy of creative growth and shared language rather than a style of music — a distinction most journalism has ignored. That correction matters: the players involved sound quite different from each other, and treating M-Base as a sound rather than a working method misrepresents its own self-description. The influence is enormous and mostly indirect — Vijay Iyer, Steve Lehman, Ambrose Akinmusire and much of what is now called contemporary creative music runs through it.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Chromatic and non-functional, often built from symmetrical structures and pitch cells rather than chord progressions.","rhythm":"The centre of the concept. Nested, interlocking cycles of unequal length, odd groupings, and a funk feel that keeps displacing.","form":"Cyclic rather than chorus-based. Sections repeat at different rates and phase against one another.","instrumentation":"Alto saxophone, trumpet, trombone, guitar, electric bass, drums; voice used as an instrumental line. Electric and acoustic freely mixed.","improvisation":"Improvising across a shifting grid — the challenge is orientation as much as invention. Collective interaction is expected.","timbre":"Dry, tight, unromantic. Little reverb, hard articulation, and a deliberately unlush ensemble sound."},"earMarkers":["A groove that is clearly funky but that you cannot easily count","Several repeating patterns of different lengths running at once","Melodic lines that cut across the beat rather than sitting on it","Voice used as another horn, singing wordless lines in unison","A dry, clipped band sound with almost no reverb"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Steve Coleman","instrument":"alto saxophone, composition","why":"Coined the term and has pursued the rhythmic research for forty years, including fieldwork in Ghana, Cuba and India."},{"name":"Greg Osby","instrument":"alto saxophone","why":"The other central altoist; later a Blue Note bandleader and a persistent critic of neo-traditionalist orthodoxy."},{"name":"Cassandra Wilson","instrument":"voice","why":"Came out of the collective and then built a singular body of work applying its rhythmic thinking to blues and folk repertoire."},{"name":"Geri Allen","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Connected M-Base to the AACM lineage and to the mainstream, with a harmonic imagination distinct from anyone else in the group."},{"name":"Robin Eubanks","instrument":"trombone","why":"Made the trombone work in a rhythmically demanding idiom that gives it no natural place."},{"name":"Marvin 'Smitty' Smith","instrument":"drums","why":"Executed the interlocking-cycle concept on the kit and made it feel like a groove rather than an exercise."}],"keyLabels":["JMT","Novus/RCA","Blue Note","Pi Recordings","Label Bleu"],"contested":"Whether M-Base is a genre at all. Steve Coleman has stated repeatedly that it is a concept and a way of working, not a style, and that critics who describe an 'M-Base sound' have misunderstood it. This guide includes it as a node because the influence is traceable and the rhythmic approach is identifiable — while noting that the person who named it would object to the framing.","confidence":"high"},{"id":"downtown","name":"Downtown & Punk Jazz","aka":["Knitting Factory scene","No wave jazz","Radical Jewish Culture (adjacent)"],"family":"avant","era":{"start":1978,"peak":[1986,1999],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York (Lower East Side)","country":"USA"},"oneLine":"Free jazz colliding with hardcore punk, surf, klezmer and cartoon music, usually at high speed and without warning.","summary":"Lower Manhattan in the late 1970s and 80s had a scene where the boundary between avant-garde jazz, no wave, punk and contemporary composition simply was not enforced. John Zorn is the organising figure. His game pieces — Cobra above all, from 1984 — are structured improvisations run by a prompter using cue cards, where the rules govern who plays and when but not what. Naked City compressed hardcore punk, film noir soundtracks, country and free jazz into pieces lasting under a minute, switching genre every few seconds. The Lounge Lizards played what Zorn's contemporary John Lurie called 'fake jazz', deadpan and hard-edged. Bill Frisell, Wayne Horvitz, Elliott Sharp, Marc Ribot and Tim Berne all worked across the same rooms — the Knitting Factory from 1987, and before it a network of lofts and galleries. Zorn's Tzadik label from 1995 institutionalised it. The Jewish identity strand — Masada, the Radical Jewish Culture series — brought klezmer modes into an Ornette Coleman quartet format. What holds the scene together is a compositional attitude: rapid juxtaposition, genre as material, and structure imposed on improvisation from outside.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Anything, and often several things per minute. Klezmer modes, surf progressions, free atonality and hardcore power chords used as blocks.","rhythm":"Extreme contrast — blast beats next to free time next to a shuffle. Metric stability is a choice, not a default.","form":"Game pieces, file-card compositions, and blocks of material spliced with hard cuts. Duration ranges from 20 seconds to an hour.","instrumentation":"Alto saxophone, guitar, bass, drums, plus turntables, samplers, voice, and whatever else. Electric and amplified by default.","improvisation":"Central but governed. The composer sets rules of engagement rather than notes; players improvise inside a designed system.","timbre":"Loud, abrasive, and stylistically promiscuous. Studio and live production are aggressive rather than transparent."},"earMarkers":["The style changing completely every few seconds without transition","Hardcore punk speed and screaming next to something that swings","Pieces that last thirty seconds and then stop","Klezmer or surf melodies played by a free jazz rhythm section","A group that is clearly following rules you can't hear"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"John Zorn","instrument":"alto saxophone, composition","why":"The scene's organiser, composer and label head; the game pieces and Masada are the two poles of his output."},{"name":"Bill Frisell","instrument":"guitar","why":"Developed a volume-pedal, delay-heavy Americana-inflected guitar language that was equally at home downtown and on ECM."},{"name":"Marc Ribot","instrument":"guitar","why":"The scene's most abrasive and versatile guitarist, moving between Zorn, Tom Waits and Cuban repertoire."},{"name":"Tim Berne","instrument":"alto saxophone, composition","why":"Wrote long, intricate compositions for working bands, connecting Julius Hemphill's lineage to the downtown environment."},{"name":"John Lurie","instrument":"saxophone","why":"The Lounge Lizards' deadpan 'fake jazz' set the scene's tone of ironic seriousness."},{"name":"Elliott Sharp","instrument":"guitar, reeds, composition","why":"Brought algorithmic and mathematical structures into an otherwise intuitive scene."}],"keyLabels":["Tzadik","Knitting Factory Works","Nonesuch","JMT","Elektra Nonesuch"],"contested":"Whether it is jazz. A large part of the output has no swing, no blues and no improvisation on changes, and 'downtown' names a geography and a social network rather than a style. Its inclusion here rests on personnel and lineage — most of these players came through free jazz — and on the fact that the scene fed contemporary creative music directly.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"contemporary-creative","name":"Contemporary Creative Jazz","aka":["Modern creative","Post-genre jazz","Creative music (current)"],"family":"avant","era":{"start":1998,"peak":[2010,2026],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"New York / Chicago / Berlin","country":"USA / Germany"},"oneLine":"The current avant-garde mainstream: rhythmically intricate, harmonically dense, conservatory-trained, and drawing on everything at once.","summary":"This is where the M-Base rhythmic project, the AACM's compositional discipline, downtown's genre promiscuity and neo-bop's technical training all end up in the same room. The identifying features are rhythmic: odd meters treated as ordinary, metric modulation, polyrhythm as a structural device rather than an effect, and a comfort with hip-hop and electronic feels. Harmonically it draws on spectral and post-tonal composition as readily as on jazz. Vijay Iyer works with rhythmic ratios and cognitive research on rhythm; Steve Lehman applies spectral harmony derived from Tristan Murail; Mary Halvorson has built a guitar language out of pitch-bending and unexpected form; Tyshawn Sorey writes long-form composition that has largely left jazz behind while remaining in dialogue with it; Ambrose Akinmusire, Jason Moran, Craig Taborn, Matana Roberts and Henry Threadgill's later ensembles fill in the range. Institutional support — grants, Pulitzers, MacArthur fellowships, university positions — has replaced the club economy that supported earlier generations, which shapes the music in ways worth noticing: it is composed more and jammed less.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Post-tonal, spectral and cluster-based alongside jazz extensions. Harmony is often generated by a system rather than inherited from song form.","rhythm":"The defining domain — metric modulation, nested tuplets, polymeter, and grooves borrowed from hip-hop, West African and South Indian traditions.","form":"Through-composed, modular, or generated by rules. Extended suites and album-length structures are common.","instrumentation":"Standard jazz groups plus strings, electronics, laptop processing, and idiosyncratic fixed instrumentations built for a single band.","improvisation":"Deeply integrated with composition — the line between written and improvised is often deliberately hard to hear.","timbre":"Precisely engineered. Electronics and acoustic instruments blended rather than contrasted."},"earMarkers":["Meters that shift constantly and never settle into four","Written material so intricate it is hard to tell what was improvised","Harmony that sounds like contemporary classical music, not like jazz chords","Hip-hop-derived beats played by an acoustic drummer with total precision","Long album-scale structures rather than a set of tunes"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Vijay Iyer","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Combines rhythmic research, M-Base's cyclic thinking and a physical piano attack; also the most articulate writer among the group."},{"name":"Mary Halvorson","instrument":"guitar, composition","why":"Built an immediately recognisable guitar vocabulary from pitch-shifting and lurching form, out of Anthony Braxton's tutelage."},{"name":"Tyshawn Sorey","instrument":"drums, composition","why":"Works across improvised music and contemporary composition without treating them as different jobs; won the Pulitzer in 2024."},{"name":"Steve Lehman","instrument":"alto saxophone, composition","why":"Imported spectral harmony into an improvising octet, a genuinely new harmonic resource for the music."},{"name":"Jason Moran","instrument":"piano, composition","why":"Connects stride and Fats Waller to hip-hop and conceptual art, often within one piece."},{"name":"Ambrose Akinmusire","instrument":"trumpet, composition","why":"Writes long-form records about American violence and race with a trumpet language full of cracks and half-valves."},{"name":"Craig Taborn","instrument":"piano, electronics","why":"Moves between acoustic trio, solo piano and modular electronics with the same structural mind."}],"keyLabels":["Pi Recordings","ECM","Firehouse 12","Intakt","Blue Note","International Anthem"],"contested":"Whether this names anything at all, or is just 'jazz now'. The category is a critics' and grant-committees' convenience for music too composed for neo-bop and too structured for free jazz. Several of the musicians in it reject 'jazz' as a description of their work outright — a position with a long history in this music, from Ellington to Nicholas Payton's #BAM argument.","confidence":"medium"},{"id":"uk-jazz-revival","name":"UK Jazz Revival","aka":["New London jazz","The London scene","We Out Here generation"],"family":"global","era":{"start":2012,"peak":[2016,2026],"end":null},"origin":{"city":"London","country":"UK"},"oneLine":"London jazz built on Caribbean, West African and soundsystem rhythm, made by musicians who came up together and play for dancers.","summary":"The most striking thing about the current London scene is the rhythm, and where it comes from. These musicians grew up with grime, jungle, Afrobeats, dub, calypso and broken beat as native languages, and the jazz they make is organised around those feels rather than around swing. Sons of Kemet built a band from tuba, two drummers and Shabaka Hutchings' saxophone, playing what amounts to a Caribbean carnival band's rhythmic logic. Ezra Collective run Afrobeat and reggae under jazz soloing. Nubya Garcia, Moses Boyd, Theon Cross, Emma-Jean Thackray and Yussef Dayes fill out the range. The infrastructure is as important as the music: Tomorrow's Warriors, the free youth programme co-founded in 1991 by bassist Gary Crosby and Janine Irons, trained a large proportion of the scene and did so with an explicit commitment to reaching Black and female musicians who the conservatory route was not reaching. Gilles Peterson's Brownswood released the We Out Here compilation in 2018, which is when the rest of the world noticed. The audience is young, and it dances, which has not been true of jazz in Britain for a very long time.","musicalTraits":{"harmony":"Modal, vamp-based and often minor-key, with spiritual jazz's pedal points and Afrobeat's two-chord frames. Harmonic complexity is not the priority.","rhythm":"The centre of everything: Afrobeat, dub, jungle, grime, calypso and second-line feels, frequently with two drummers or a drummer plus percussion.","form":"Groove-driven and often long, with slow-building structures aimed at a live, standing audience rather than a seated one.","instrumentation":"Tuba as a bass instrument, tenor and soprano saxophone, keys, twin drum kits, electronics, and MCs or singers as guests.","improvisation":"Present and extended, but subordinate to the groove — solos build collective intensity rather than displaying harmonic navigation.","timbre":"Soundsystem-informed: heavy low end, dub-style delay and reverb, and mixes made for large speakers."},"earMarkers":["Tuba playing the bass line instead of a double bass or electric bass","Two drummers, playing complementary rather than identical patterns","Grooves that come from Afrobeat, dub or jungle rather than from swing","Dub-style echo thrown across horns and drums","Music clearly made for a crowd that is standing up"],"keyFigures":[{"name":"Shabaka Hutchings","instrument":"tenor saxophone, clarinet, flutes","why":"The scene's most visible figure, running Sons of Kemet, The Comet Is Coming and Shabaka and the Ancestors simultaneously, then abandoning the saxophone for flutes in 2023."},{"name":"Nubya Garcia","instrument":"tenor saxophone","why":"The clearest link back to spiritual jazz and to Caribbean rhythm, with a big, unhurried tone."},{"name":"Moses Boyd","instrument":"drums, production","why":"Works as both jazz drummer and electronic producer, which is exactly the scene's premise."},{"name":"Theon Cross","instrument":"tuba","why":"Made the tuba a lead bass instrument capable of sub-bass weight and dancehall articulation."},{"name":"Gary Crosby","instrument":"bass, education","why":"Co-founded Tomorrow's Warriors in 1991; the scene's existence is substantially attributable to that programme."},{"name":"Ezra Collective","instrument":"band","why":"Won the Mercury Prize in 2023, the first jazz act to do so, playing Afrobeat and reggae-driven jazz."}],"keyLabels":["Brownswood","Gearbox","Impulse!","Native Rebel","Concord Jazz","Total Refreshment Centre (studio/scene hub)"],"contested":"Whether 'revival' is the right word, since British jazz did not stop — the Jazz Warriors in the 1980s, Courtney Pine, and a continuous free improvisation scene were all active. Critics also note the media framing conflated a genuinely diverse set of bands into one marketable story. Several of the musicians have said they do not consider themselves a scene.","confidence":"high"}],"lineage":[{"from":"ragtime","to":"new-orleans","type":"direct-descendant","year":1905,"aspects":["repertoire","form","rhythm"],"explanation":"Rags were standard New Orleans band repertoire, and the multi-strain march form came across intact — a lot of early jazz is a rag played by a band. What changed is that the printed notes stopped being binding: players began paraphrasing the strains, and blues inflection entered a music that had none. Jelly Roll Morton's Red Hot Peppers sides are rag architecture with improvisation and blues poured into it.","hingeAlbum":"birth-of-the-hot-morton","strength":"strong"},{"from":"ragtime","to":"stride","type":"direct-descendant","year":1919,"aspects":["form","rhythm","instrumentation"],"explanation":"Stride is ragtime's alternating bass with the metronome removed. The left-hand mechanism is inherited almost unchanged; what the Harlem players added was swung eighth notes, blues harmony, right-hand improvisation, and a competitive tempo escalation that had no place in Joplin's instruction to never play fast.","hingeAlbum":"james-p-johnson-carolina-shout","strength":"strong"},{"from":"new-orleans","to":"chicago-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1924,"aspects":["instrumentation","form","improvisation"],"explanation":"Young Chicagoans learned the New Orleans front line off King Oliver and Armstrong records and then reweighted it. The collective texture thinned, the saxophone pushed in, tempos rose, and the individual solo chorus began displacing the group weave — the change that makes the swing small group possible.","hingeAlbum":"louis-armstrong-hot-fives-sevens","strength":"strong"},{"from":"new-orleans","to":"swing","type":"direct-descendant","year":1930,"aspects":["repertoire","instrumentation","rhythm"],"explanation":"The big band took the New Orleans front line's division of labour and multiplied it into sections: what one clarinet did against one cornet, five reeds now do against four trumpets. Fletcher Henderson's charts are the translation step, turning improvised counterpoint into written call-and-response.","hingeAlbum":"fletcher-henderson-a-study-in-frustration","strength":"strong"},{"from":"new-orleans","to":"vocal-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1928,"aspects":["improvisation","timbre","repertoire"],"explanation":"Armstrong established that a jazz singer phrases like a horn player — displacing the beat, bending pitch, treating the written melody as a suggestion — and popularised scat as an extension of that. Every jazz singer after him is working from this premise whether or not they scat.","hingeAlbum":"louis-armstrong-hot-fives-sevens","strength":"strong"},{"from":"new-orleans","to":"kansas-city","type":"direct-descendant","year":1928,"aspects":["instrumentation","repertoire"],"explanation":"The territory bands working the Midwest carried the New Orleans repertoire and instrumentation outward, but the KC scene reweighted it toward blues form and riff construction, which the New Orleans model used far less.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"stride","to":"swing","type":"direct-descendant","year":1932,"aspects":["harmony","rhythm"],"explanation":"Ellington learned directly from Willie 'The Lion' Smith and James P. Johnson, and the harmonic richness of his orchestral writing comes out of stride's keyboard voicings scaled up to a horn section. The stride left hand is also what the swing rhythm section replaced — the bass and guitar took over the job one hand had been doing.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"chicago-jazz","to":"swing","type":"direct-descendant","year":1932,"aspects":["rhythm","improvisation"],"explanation":"Chicago's shift from a two-beat march feel toward an even four, and its promotion of the individual solo over collective improvisation, are the two things swing then standardised. Gene Krupa carried the Chicago drive straight into Benny Goodman's band.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"kansas-city","to":"swing","type":"parallel-influence","year":1936,"aspects":["rhythm","form"],"explanation":"The Basie rhythm section arrived in New York in 1936 and changed the era's feel from underneath: even four-to-the-bar, timekeeping moved to the hi-hat and ride, bass drum reserved for accents, and enormous space. Every swing band adjusted. Riff-based head arrangements spread the same way.","hingeAlbum":"count-basie-original-decca","strength":"strong"},{"from":"swing","to":"vocal-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1935,"aspects":["repertoire","instrumentation","form"],"explanation":"The big band vocalist slot gave singers a professional route and fixed the repertoire as the popular songbook. Holiday's small-group sides for Teddy Wilson are the version where the singer is a soloist among soloists rather than a feature act.","hingeAlbum":"billie-holiday-quintessential-vol3","strength":"strong"},{"from":"swing","to":"manouche","type":"direct-descendant","year":1934,"aspects":["repertoire","harmony","rhythm"],"explanation":"Django and Grappelli took the American swing repertoire and its harmony wholesale, then rebuilt the rhythm section without drums, piano or horns. The other parent is not on this map: Romani string-band practice and Parisian musette, which supply the ornamentation, the minor-key chromaticism and the fingered vibrato.","hingeAlbum":"django-quintette-hot-club-1936","strength":"strong"},{"from":"swing","to":"bebop","type":"reaction-against","year":1944,"aspects":["form","instrumentation","social-context"],"explanation":"Bebop kept swing's harmony and rhythmic base and rejected almost everything else: the big band, the written arrangement, the dance floor, the entertainment function, and the economics that put Black musicians in front of white audiences as employees. The small group playing at tempos nobody could dance to was a position, not just a sound.","hingeAlbum":"charlie-parker-savoy-dial","strength":"strong"},{"from":"kansas-city","to":"bebop","type":"direct-descendant","year":1944,"aspects":["improvisation","rhythm","harmony"],"explanation":"Charlie Parker came out of Jay McShann's Kansas City band, and Lester Young's light tone, long asymmetric phrases and behind-the-beat placement are the direct model for bebop line construction. The KC jam-session culture — many choruses, blues form, cutting contests — is bebop's working method before bebop existed.","hingeAlbum":"lester-young-kansas-city-sessions","strength":"strong"},{"from":"stride","to":"bebop","type":"direct-descendant","year":1945,"aspects":["harmony","instrumentation"],"explanation":"Bud Powell's right hand is Parker's line and his left hand is stride's, stripped to sparse rootless punctuation. The route runs through Art Tatum, whose substitutions and passing harmony gave the bebop generation most of its reharmonisation vocabulary.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"bebop","to":"vocal-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1946,"aspects":["harmony","improvisation"],"explanation":"Sarah Vaughan and Eddie Jefferson brought bebop harmony and phrasing into singing, and vocalese — writing lyrics to a recorded improvised solo and singing it — makes the debt literal. Vaughan sang with Earl Hines and Billy Eckstine's bands alongside Parker and Gillespie.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"swing","to":"afro-cuban","type":"fusion-of","year":1943,"aspects":["instrumentation","form"],"explanation":"Machito's Afro-Cubans, with Mario Bauzá as musical director, put a full jazz horn section and big-band arranging over a Cuban rhythm section. The orchestration is American; the rhythmic organisation underneath is entirely Cuban, and the two do not blend so much as coexist under tension.","hingeAlbum":"machito-afro-cuban-jazz-suite","strength":"strong"},{"from":"bebop","to":"afro-cuban","type":"fusion-of","year":1947,"aspects":["harmony","improvisation","rhythm"],"explanation":"Gillespie hired Chano Pozo in 1947 and 'Manteca' and 'Cubana Be, Cubana Bop' followed within months. Bebop supplied the harmonic language and the soloing; Pozo supplied clave, the tumbao and drum patterns carried from Afro-Cuban religious practice. The swing lilt is what got discarded — the eighth notes go straight.","hingeAlbum":"dizzy-gillespie-complete-rca-victor","strength":"strong"},{"from":"bebop","to":"cool-jazz","type":"reaction-against","year":1949,"aspects":["timbre","rhythm","form"],"explanation":"The Miles Davis nonet kept bebop's harmony and threw out its heat: soft attack, no vibrato, medium tempos, and written contrapuntal arrangement where bebop had head-and-blow. Gil Evans' charts restore the arranger to the centre of a music that had just deposed him.","hingeAlbum":"birth-of-the-cool","strength":"strong"},{"from":"kansas-city","to":"cool-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1949,"aspects":["timbre","improvisation"],"explanation":"Lester Young's dry, vibratoless tenor and unhurried melodic placement is what 'cool' names as a sound. Lee Konitz and Stan Getz descend from Young rather than from Parker, which is why they are the two major post-war saxophonists who do not sound like everyone else.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"cool-jazz","to":"west-coast","type":"direct-descendant","year":1952,"aspects":["instrumentation","form","timbre"],"explanation":"Mulligan and other nonet alumni moved to Los Angeles and found a studio-musician labour pool that could read anything. The piano-less quartet took the nonet's contrapuntal premise and reduced it to two horns implying the harmony between them.","hingeAlbum":"gerry-mulligan-quartet-chet-baker","strength":"strong"},{"from":"bebop","to":"hard-bop","type":"direct-descendant","year":1954,"aspects":["harmony","form","improvisation"],"explanation":"Same quintet, same changes, same head–solos–head. What hard bop adds is weight and blues content: slower tempos, heavier drums, composed introductions and interludes, and heads written to be memorable rather than abstract.","hingeAlbum":"horace-silver-art-blakey-jazz-messengers-1955","strength":"strong"},{"from":"cool-jazz","to":"hard-bop","type":"reaction-against","year":1954,"aspects":["timbre","rhythm","social-context"],"explanation":"Hard bop's heat is aimed at cool jazz's restraint, and the aim was partly racial and geographic: an East Coast, largely Black music positioning itself against a West Coast, largely white studio scene that the press had been treating as jazz's future. Gospel and rhythm-and-blues content was reclaimed rather than merely added.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"cool-jazz","to":"third-stream","type":"direct-descendant","year":1957,"aspects":["form","instrumentation","harmony"],"explanation":"The nonet's arrangers — John Lewis, Gunther Schuller, Gil Evans — are the same people who then argued for a formal marriage with concert music. Schuller played French horn on the nonet sessions before he coined 'third stream' at Brandeis in 1957.","hingeAlbum":"modern-jazz-quartet-django","strength":"strong"},{"from":"west-coast","to":"third-stream","type":"direct-descendant","year":1957,"aspects":["form","instrumentation"],"explanation":"The Los Angeles scene's appetite for fugue, canon and unusual instrumentation put it halfway to third stream already. Jimmy Giuffre's drumless chamber groups and Shorty Rogers' woodwind writing sit on the boundary.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"hard-bop","to":"soul-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1956,"aspects":["rhythm","harmony","instrumentation"],"explanation":"Soul jazz is hard bop with the gospel and R&B content promoted from flavour to structure, and the Hammond organ replacing piano and bass. Horace Silver's funky tunes are the shared ancestor both styles claim; the split happens when the backbeat and the vamp stop being devices and become the format.","hingeAlbum":"jimmy-smith-back-at-the-chicken-shack","strength":"strong"},{"from":"kansas-city","to":"soul-jazz","type":"parallel-influence","year":1958,"aspects":["form","rhythm"],"explanation":"The riff-over-blues construction and the long open jam are Kansas City procedures reappearing in a neighbourhood bar twenty years later. Interpretation: the organ trio's working economy — nightly, local, audience-facing — is closer to 1930s KC than to the 1950s concert circuit.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"bebop","to":"modal","type":"reaction-against","year":1958,"aspects":["harmony","improvisation"],"explanation":"Bebop handed a soloist a chord every two beats; modal jazz hands them one scale for sixteen bars. The reduction is aimed squarely at bebop's harmonic congestion — Miles Davis described wanting fewer chords so there was more to do with each one.","hingeAlbum":"kind-of-blue","strength":"strong"},{"from":"cool-jazz","to":"modal","type":"direct-descendant","year":1958,"aspects":["harmony","timbre"],"explanation":"Bill Evans brought Ravel- and Debussy-derived voicings into the Davis sextet, and those quartal, ambiguous chords are what make static harmony sound rich rather than empty. Davis himself carried the cool tone and the taste for space directly across.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"third-stream","to":"modal","type":"parallel-influence","year":1959,"aspects":["harmony"],"explanation":"George Russell's Lydian Chromatic Concept, published in 1953, supplied the theoretical frame that let musicians think in scales rather than chords. Russell taught it to Davis and Evans among others; it is the closest thing modal jazz has to a written source.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"cool-jazz","to":"bossa-brazilian","type":"direct-descendant","year":1958,"aspects":["harmony","timbre","rhythm"],"explanation":"The Rio musicians who built bossa nova were listening to Mulligan, Chet Baker and Barney Kessel records — the soft attack, the restraint and the absence of vibrato come from there. Jobim's harmony draws on Debussy as much as on jazz, but the performance manner is cool jazz's.","hingeAlbum":"joao-gilberto-chega-de-saudade","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"vocal-jazz","to":"bossa-brazilian","type":"parallel-influence","year":1959,"aspects":["timbre","repertoire","form"],"explanation":"Gilberto's near-inaudible delivery is a deliberate rejection of the big-voiced Brazilian singing of the era, and the model for singing quietly and conversationally into a microphone was American. The songbook idea — a body of harmonically rich standards that instrumentalists then colonise — repeats exactly.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"hard-bop","to":"post-bop","type":"direct-descendant","year":1963,"aspects":["instrumentation","improvisation","repertoire"],"explanation":"Same Blue Note quintets, same players, often the same session week. What changes is that the forms stop being regular and the harmony stops resolving — Wayne Shorter and Herbie Hancock were writing hard bop dates in 1961 and post-bop ones by 1964.","hingeAlbum":"wayne-shorter-speak-no-evil","strength":"strong"},{"from":"modal","to":"post-bop","type":"direct-descendant","year":1964,"aspects":["harmony","form","rhythm"],"explanation":"Post-bop takes modal jazz's harmonic ambiguity and puts it back inside moving forms, so the chords change but never resolve. The Davis quintet's 'time, no changes' approach is modal freedom applied to a rhythm section rather than to a scale.","hingeAlbum":"miles-davis-miles-smiles","strength":"strong"},{"from":"free-jazz","to":"post-bop","type":"parallel-influence","year":1964,"aspects":["improvisation","rhythm","harmony"],"explanation":"Post-bop is what musicians made after hearing Ornette Coleman and deciding to go partway. Tony Williams' elastic time, Andrew Hill's asymmetric forms and the habit of playing deliberately outside the harmony all depend on free jazz having made those moves thinkable first.","hingeAlbum":"eric-dolphy-out-to-lunch","strength":"strong"},{"from":"hard-bop","to":"free-jazz","type":"reaction-against","year":1959,"aspects":["harmony","form","social-context"],"explanation":"Ornette Coleman came up playing rhythm-and-blues and bebop in Texas and Los Angeles, and the quartet that arrived at the Five Spot in 1959 kept hard bop's melodic directness while removing the chord sequence entirely. The hostility from hard bop musicians was immediate and personal.","hingeAlbum":"ornette-coleman-shape-of-jazz-to-come","strength":"strong"},{"from":"modal","to":"free-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1961,"aspects":["harmony","improvisation","form"],"explanation":"Modal jazz had already reduced harmonic motion to almost nothing; free jazz took the last step and removed the pre-agreed frame altogether. Coltrane's route runs directly through this — the extended vamps of 'Impressions' become the open playing of 1965 without an obvious break.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"third-stream","to":"free-jazz","type":"parallel-influence","year":1960,"aspects":["harmony","form","timbre"],"explanation":"Cecil Taylor studied at the New England Conservatory and his atonal cluster language owes more to Bartók, Stockhausen and Henry Cowell than to any jazz pianist. The permission to abandon tonality came partly from European concert music, which third stream had already brought into the room.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"new-orleans","to":"free-jazz","type":"parallel-influence","year":1961,"aspects":["improvisation","instrumentation"],"explanation":"Interpretation, though one participants made themselves: collective simultaneous improvisation with no designated soloist is a New Orleans procedure returning after forty years of one-at-a-time soloing. Ornette Coleman's Free Jazz double quartet and Albert Ayler's use of marches and hymns both make the connection audible rather than merely arguable.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"free-jazz","to":"aacm-avant","type":"direct-descendant","year":1965,"aspects":["improvisation","harmony","form"],"explanation":"The AACM's founding generation were playing free jazz in Chicago before the organisation existed. What the collective added was structure — composed sections, notated frames, and a refusal of the continuous-blowing model that New York had made standard.","hingeAlbum":"roscoe-mitchell-sound","strength":"strong"},{"from":"third-stream","to":"aacm-avant","type":"parallel-influence","year":1967,"aspects":["form","instrumentation"],"explanation":"Anthony Braxton's compositional apparatus, Muhal Richard Abrams' orchestral writing and Roscoe Mitchell's use of silence all draw on twentieth-century concert music as directly as third stream did — but from inside a Black self-organised institution rather than through the conservatory. That difference is the point.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"new-orleans","to":"aacm-avant","type":"revival-of","year":1969,"aspects":["repertoire","timbre","social-context"],"explanation":"'Great Black Music, Ancient to the Future' is a claim on the whole history, and the Art Ensemble made it audible by putting marches, field hollers and New Orleans polyphony inside free improvisation. The vocalised brass timbres and the collective front line are reached for deliberately, not inherited passively.","hingeAlbum":"art-ensemble-of-chicago-les-stances-a-sophie","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"modal","to":"spiritual-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1965,"aspects":["harmony","form","rhythm"],"explanation":"A Love Supreme is a modal record with a devotional programme attached: a bass ostinato functioning as a drone, one mode held for minutes, and a chanted mantra where a head would be. Everything the idiom does harmonically is already in the modal toolkit.","hingeAlbum":"a-love-supreme","strength":"strong"},{"from":"free-jazz","to":"spiritual-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1966,"aspects":["improvisation","timbre","social-context"],"explanation":"Overblowing, multiphonics and collective ecstatic climaxes come straight from the free jazz vocabulary — Pharoah Sanders was in Coltrane's late band and Sun Ra's before that. The difference is intent: the same sounds are framed as devotion rather than as rupture.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"free-jazz","to":"euro-free-improv","type":"reaction-against","year":1966,"aspects":["timbre","rhythm","social-context","harmony"],"explanation":"European improvisers took free jazz's central permission and then rejected the rest of it as American idiom: the blues inflection, the residual pulse, the saxophone-as-preaching-voice, the whole gestural vocabulary. Derek Bailey's term 'non-idiomatic' is aimed at free jazz as much as at bebop, and the politics of refusing American cultural dominance were stated openly.","hingeAlbum":"peter-brotzmann-machine-gun","strength":"strong"},{"from":"aacm-avant","to":"euro-free-improv","type":"parallel-influence","year":1969,"aspects":["instrumentation","form"],"explanation":"The Art Ensemble and other AACM members moved to Paris in 1969 and recorded prolifically for BYG Actuel, putting little instruments, extreme dynamic contrast and composed framing in front of European improvisers who were already working in that direction. Traffic ran both ways from then on.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"west-coast","to":"euro-free-improv","type":"parallel-influence","year":1963,"aspects":["instrumentation","timbre","form"],"explanation":"Interpretation: Jimmy Giuffre's 1961–62 trio with Paul Bley and Steve Swallow — no drums, near-silent dynamics, free but chamber-scaled — anticipates the quiet end of European improvisation by several years. The direct historical link is thin; the resemblance is not.","strength":"weak"},{"from":"free-jazz","to":"loft-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1970,"aspects":["improvisation","social-context"],"explanation":"Same music, different economics. When the club circuit closed to free players, they rented lofts and became their own promoters — which removed set lengths, drink minimums and owner veto, and produced longer, more patient performances than any club would have allowed.","hingeAlbum":"wildflowers-loft-jazz-sessions","strength":"strong"},{"from":"aacm-avant","to":"loft-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1973,"aspects":["form","instrumentation","social-context"],"explanation":"The AACM and St. Louis's BAG both sent members to New York in the early 1970s, and they brought the self-organising model and the compositional discipline with them. The all-saxophone ensemble and the duo format both flourish here for the same reason: they are cheap and they demand structure.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"modal","to":"fusion","type":"fusion-of","year":1969,"aspects":["harmony","form","technology"],"explanation":"Fusion's harmony is modal jazz's — one chord for a long time — transferred to a Fender Rhodes and set over a rock backbeat. The two parents not on this map are rock and funk, which supply the volume, the straight eighths and the electric instruments.","hingeAlbum":"bitches-brew","strength":"strong"},{"from":"post-bop","to":"fusion","type":"fusion-of","year":1969,"aspects":["improvisation","instrumentation","rhythm"],"explanation":"The personnel are identical: Hancock, Shorter, Williams, Corea, Zawinul and Holland all came out of the post-bop world and went electric more or less at once. The interactive rhythm-section thinking of the Davis quintet survives the change of instruments.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"soul-jazz","to":"fusion","type":"parallel-influence","year":1969,"aspects":["technology","instrumentation"],"explanation":"Soul jazz had been amplifying jazz for over a decade — the Hammond organ, the electric guitar, the R&B backbeat — and had absorbed the critical contempt for it first. Fusion's electric turn is less unprecedented than the 1970s controversy suggested.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"bossa-brazilian","to":"fusion","type":"parallel-influence","year":1972,"aspects":["rhythm","timbre","instrumentation"],"explanation":"Airto Moreira played on Bitches Brew and then with Weather Report and Return to Forever, and Flora Purim sang on the latter. Brazilian percussion and straight-eighth feels are woven into fusion from the beginning rather than added later.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"soul-jazz","to":"jazz-funk","type":"direct-descendant","year":1969,"aspects":["rhythm","instrumentation","form"],"explanation":"The organ trio's groove-first premise scaled up: horn sections, clavinet, electric bass, and a sixteenth-note funk feel replacing the shuffle. The soloist's role shrinks further, and the bass line becomes the composition.","hingeAlbum":"herbie-hancock-head-hunters","strength":"strong"},{"from":"fusion","to":"jazz-funk","type":"direct-descendant","year":1972,"aspects":["technology","instrumentation","harmony"],"explanation":"Same electric instruments, different priority. Where fusion uses the groove to support virtuoso soloing, jazz-funk uses the soloing to decorate the groove — Herbie Hancock made both records, two years apart, with overlapping personnel.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"free-jazz","to":"free-funk","type":"direct-descendant","year":1975,"aspects":["harmony","improvisation"],"explanation":"Ornette Coleman's harmolodic principle — every instrument carrying melody, no fixed key centre, no accompanist role — is carried unchanged from the 1959 quartet into Prime Time. Only the rhythmic floor and the electricity are new.","hingeAlbum":"ornette-coleman-dancing-in-your-head","strength":"strong"},{"from":"jazz-funk","to":"free-funk","type":"fusion-of","year":1975,"aspects":["rhythm","instrumentation","technology"],"explanation":"The backbeat, the doubled electric instruments and the repeating groove come from funk as it stood in the mid-1970s. Placing a hard, danceable floor under fully free melodic playing is the whole proposition, and nobody had done it seriously before.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"jazz-funk","to":"smooth-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1975,"aspects":["rhythm","instrumentation","timbre"],"explanation":"Smooth jazz is jazz-funk with the risk removed: the same groove, the same instruments, but consonant harmony, short solos and a melody engineered for radio recall. Grover Washington Jr. is on both sides of the line and the transition is audible across his 1970s records.","hingeAlbum":"grover-washington-winelight","strength":"strong"},{"from":"fusion","to":"smooth-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1979,"aspects":["technology","timbre"],"explanation":"The studio apparatus transferred wholesale — synthesizers, gated drums, digital reverb, multitrack polish — while the odd meters and extended solos that justified it were dropped. Several fusion players moved into the format when the fusion market collapsed.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"jazz-funk","to":"jazz-rap","type":"direct-descendant","year":1991,"aspects":["technology","harmony","rhythm"],"explanation":"The descent is physical: producers took the actual recorded material — two bars of Rhodes, a bass figure, a drum break — and looped it. Bob James, Donald Byrd and Roy Ayers records supply a disproportionate share of the harmony in early 1990s hip-hop.","hingeAlbum":"tribe-called-quest-low-end-theory","strength":"strong"},{"from":"hard-bop","to":"jazz-rap","type":"direct-descendant","year":1991,"aspects":["harmony","timbre","repertoire"],"explanation":"The other sample source is the Blue Note and Prestige catalogue: upright bass, muted trumpet and hard bop piano voicings give jazz rap the sonic signature that separates it from other hip-hop. Us3's 1993 record licensed Blue Note directly rather than clearing samples one at a time.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"jazz-funk","to":"acid-jazz","type":"revival-of","year":1987,"aspects":["rhythm","instrumentation","timbre"],"explanation":"Acid jazz began as London DJs playing forgotten 1970s jazz-funk to dancers, and the bands that followed set out to make new records that sounded like the old ones — same instruments, same tempos, same production references, fifteen years later.","hingeAlbum":"brand-new-heavies-brand-new-heavies","strength":"strong"},{"from":"soul-jazz","to":"acid-jazz","type":"revival-of","year":1988,"aspects":["instrumentation","rhythm","repertoire"],"explanation":"The Hammond organ, the boogaloo feel and a good deal of the actual repertoire came from 1960s soul jazz records that had been out of print for twenty years. The James Taylor Quartet's early sides are close to reconstruction.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"jazz-rap","to":"acid-jazz","type":"parallel-influence","year":1992,"aspects":["technology","rhythm"],"explanation":"The two scenes were digging the same crates on opposite sides of the Atlantic. Sampling technique, breakbeat programming and rapping over live jazz bands crossed into acid jazz from hip-hop, most obviously in Galliano and Us3.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"third-stream","to":"ecm-nordic","type":"direct-descendant","year":1970,"aspects":["form","instrumentation","harmony"],"explanation":"George Russell lived and taught in Scandinavia from 1964, and Jan Garbarek, Terje Rypdal and Jon Christensen all played in his groups there. The label's comfort with composed structure, chamber instrumentation and European concert-music values comes down this line directly.","hingeAlbum":"jan-garbarek-afric-pepperbird","strength":"strong"},{"from":"modal","to":"ecm-nordic","type":"direct-descendant","year":1971,"aspects":["harmony","form"],"explanation":"Slow harmonic rhythm, pedal points and suspended voicings are modal jazz's inheritance, applied to folk-derived rather than blues-derived melody. Keith Jarrett's solo concerts are extended modal improvisation with the jazz repertoire removed.","hingeAlbum":"keith-jarrett-koln-concert","strength":"strong"},{"from":"free-jazz","to":"ecm-nordic","type":"direct-descendant","year":1970,"aspects":["improvisation","timbre"],"explanation":"Garbarek, Rypdal and Christensen came out of Norwegian free jazz, and the label's early catalogue is closer to the avant-garde than its later reputation suggests. What survived the shift to lyricism is the freedom from song form and the treatment of timbre as content.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"west-coast","to":"ecm-nordic","type":"parallel-influence","year":1972,"aspects":["timbre","instrumentation"],"explanation":"Interpretation: the piano-less chamber group, the soft attack and the arranger's ear are West Coast preoccupations arriving in Europe two decades later. Direct influence is hard to document; the family resemblance between Giuffre's trios and early ECM is strong enough to note.","strength":"weak"},{"from":"hard-bop","to":"j-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1961,"aspects":["harmony","form","instrumentation"],"explanation":"Japanese players learned from Blue Note records and initially reproduced the format exactly. The first decade of Japanese modern jazz is hard bop played extremely well and without much local inflection; what follows is the departure.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"modal","to":"j-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1969,"aspects":["harmony","form"],"explanation":"The Coltrane quartet is the central reference point for the Japanese scene, and its modal vamps get stretched further there than in the US — side-long single-mode pieces are common on Three Blind Mice and Nippon Columbia sessions.","hingeAlbum":"terumasa-hino-hi-nology","strength":"strong"},{"from":"free-jazz","to":"j-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1969,"aspects":["improvisation","timbre","rhythm"],"explanation":"Yosuke Yamashita's trio and Masayuki Takayanagi's groups played free music with a physical violence that had few American parallels, and the Japanese free scene developed largely without contact with the New York players it started from.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"afro-cuban","to":"ethio-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1966,"aspects":["rhythm","instrumentation"],"explanation":"Mulatu Astatke's training in London and Boston was in Latin jazz, and his vibraphone-and-congas instrumentation comes straight from that world. He described building Ethio-jazz by fitting Ethiopian modes into arrangements he already knew how to write.","hingeAlbum":"mulatu-astatke-afro-latin-soul","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"soul-jazz","to":"ethio-jazz","type":"direct-descendant","year":1969,"aspects":["instrumentation","rhythm","form"],"explanation":"Electric organ, electric bass, a horn section playing short riffs, and a groove held without harmonic movement — the Addis Ababa records of the golden age use the soul jazz and early jazz-funk template, with pentatonic qignit modes replacing blues scales.","hingeAlbum":"mulatu-astatke-ethiopiques-4","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"fusion","to":"neo-bop","type":"reaction-against","year":1981,"aspects":["technology","instrumentation","social-context"],"explanation":"The Young Lions defined themselves by what they refused: electric instruments, rock rhythm, crossover marketing, and the idea that jazz needed to chase a pop audience. Wynton Marsalis's public argument that fusion was a wrong turn is inseparable from the music he made instead.","hingeAlbum":"wynton-marsalis-black-codes","strength":"strong"},{"from":"hard-bop","to":"neo-bop","type":"revival-of","year":1980,"aspects":["harmony","form","improvisation"],"explanation":"The Jazz Messengers were still running, and Wynton and Branford Marsalis, Terence Blanchard and Donald Harrison all passed through Blakey's band before leading their own. The revival is literal — the same institution, twenty-five years on.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"post-bop","to":"neo-bop","type":"direct-descendant","year":1982,"aspects":["harmony","form","improvisation"],"explanation":"The actual template for the Marsalis quintet is the 1964–68 Miles Davis group: same instrumentation, same elastic time, same harmonically ambiguous writing. Herbie Hancock, Ron Carter and Tony Williams appear on Wynton Marsalis's early records in person.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"swing","to":"neo-bop","type":"revival-of","year":1986,"aspects":["instrumentation","repertoire","form"],"explanation":"The second phase of the movement reached back past bebop to Ellington, Morton and the blues, and built repertory orchestras to play that material. Jazz at Lincoln Center from 1987 institutionalised the claim that this history is the tradition.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"new-orleans","to":"neo-bop","type":"revival-of","year":1989,"aspects":["repertoire","rhythm","social-context"],"explanation":"Marsalis's own turn toward New Orleans — second-line rhythm, collective polyphony, plunger-muted brass — reframed the movement as a claim on the music's whole lineage rather than on hard bop alone. It is also a claim about who owns that lineage.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"post-bop","to":"m-base","type":"direct-descendant","year":1984,"aspects":["harmony","improvisation"],"explanation":"The chromatic, non-functional melodic writing and the expectation that a rhythm section improvises collectively come from the post-bop world these players trained in. Steve Coleman worked in Sam Rivers' and Cecil Taylor's large ensembles before forming Five Elements.","hingeAlbum":"steve-coleman-motherland-pulse","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"jazz-funk","to":"m-base","type":"direct-descendant","year":1985,"aspects":["rhythm","instrumentation"],"explanation":"The rhythmic floor is funk — electric bass, backbeat, sixteenth-note subdivision — and the group sound is deliberately dry and unswung. What M-Base does to it is stack cycles of unequal length so the groove keeps displacing under you.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"free-funk","to":"m-base","type":"direct-descendant","year":1985,"aspects":["harmony","instrumentation","improvisation"],"explanation":"Ornette Coleman's harmolodic groups had already established that free melodic playing and a hard electric groove could occupy the same bar, and that no instrument need be subordinate. Coleman and Steve Coleman recorded together, and the alto lineage is audible.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"afro-cuban","to":"m-base","type":"parallel-influence","year":1993,"aspects":["rhythm","social-context"],"explanation":"Steve Coleman's rhythmic research took him to Ghana, Cuba, Brazil and India, and he recorded in Havana with AfroCuba de Matanzas in 1996. The nested-cycle model is drawn from West African and Afro-Caribbean drumming practice rather than derived from jazz.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"free-jazz","to":"downtown","type":"direct-descendant","year":1978,"aspects":["improvisation","timbre","instrumentation"],"explanation":"The extended techniques, the abandonment of changes and the tolerance for noise are free jazz's, and most of the downtown players came up through it. What downtown adds is a composer imposing structure from outside — the freedom is scheduled.","hingeAlbum":"john-zorn-cobra","strength":"strong"},{"from":"loft-jazz","to":"downtown","type":"direct-descendant","year":1980,"aspects":["social-context","instrumentation","form"],"explanation":"Physical and institutional continuity: the same Lower Manhattan rooms, the same self-organising economics, and in several cases the same musicians. The Knitting Factory in 1987 is the loft model with a liquor licence.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"free-funk","to":"downtown","type":"direct-descendant","year":1981,"aspects":["rhythm","instrumentation","technology"],"explanation":"Harmolodic funk demonstrated that a rock rhythm section and free playing could coexist, which is the precondition for punk jazz. Ronald Shannon Jackson, James Blood Ulmer and Bill Laswell moved between both scenes constantly.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"euro-free-improv","to":"downtown","type":"parallel-influence","year":1982,"aspects":["form","timbre","improvisation"],"explanation":"John Zorn played in Derek Bailey's Company gatherings, and the game pieces owe their premise — structure that governs interaction rather than pitch — to that encounter. The European emphasis on timbre over line is audible throughout downtown playing.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"acid-jazz","to":"nu-jazz","type":"fusion-of","year":1996,"aspects":["rhythm","technology","social-context"],"explanation":"Acid jazz established the club as jazz's venue and the DJ as its curator; nu jazz kept both and replaced the retro live band with contemporary electronic production. Gilles Peterson's move from Acid Jazz to Talkin' Loud tracks the shift.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"ecm-nordic","to":"nu-jazz","type":"fusion-of","year":1997,"aspects":["timbre","harmony","improvisation"],"explanation":"Nils Petter Molvær and Bugge Wesseltoft were ECM musicians, and Khmer is the label's reverb-drenched trumpet lyricism and static harmony placed over drum programming. The Nordic tone is what makes nu jazz sound like jazz at all.","hingeAlbum":"nils-petter-molvaer-khmer","strength":"strong"},{"from":"jazz-rap","to":"la-beat","type":"direct-descendant","year":2008,"aspects":["rhythm","technology","harmony"],"explanation":"J Dilla's deliberately unquantised drum programming is the LA beat scene's founding grammar, and Flying Lotus built Brainfeeder on it. The musicians who then played acoustically had internalised that feel as their natural time.","hingeAlbum":"flying-lotus-cosmogramma","strength":"strong"},{"from":"spiritual-jazz","to":"la-beat","type":"revival-of","year":2015,"aspects":["form","harmony","social-context"],"explanation":"Kamasi Washington's three-hour record with choir and strings is 1970s spiritual jazz reconstructed without irony, and Flying Lotus is Alice Coltrane's great-nephew, which is a lineage rather than a coincidence. The Impulse! and Strata-East aesthetic returns intact.","hingeAlbum":"kamasi-washington-the-epic","strength":"strong"},{"from":"fusion","to":"la-beat","type":"direct-descendant","year":2013,"aspects":["instrumentation","technology","harmony"],"explanation":"Thundercat's six-string electric bass, his chord-melody approach and his falsetto singing descend from Stanley Clarke, Jaco Pastorius and George Duke directly, and he has said so. The synthesizer palette is 1970s fusion's, reactivated.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"m-base","to":"contemporary-creative","type":"direct-descendant","year":2000,"aspects":["rhythm","harmony","improvisation"],"explanation":"The rhythmic research is the direct inheritance: nested cycles, odd groupings and improvisation across a shifting grid are now standard equipment. Vijay Iyer, Steve Lehman and Dafnis Prieto all worked with or studied Steve Coleman's approach closely.","hingeAlbum":"vijay-iyer-historicity","strength":"strong"},{"from":"aacm-avant","to":"contemporary-creative","type":"direct-descendant","year":1998,"aspects":["form","improvisation","instrumentation"],"explanation":"Anthony Braxton taught at Wesleyan for over twenty years and Mary Halvorson, Taylor Ho Bynum and others came directly out of that. The habit of designing a bespoke ensemble and a bespoke structure for each project is the AACM's, not the club scene's.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"downtown","to":"contemporary-creative","type":"direct-descendant","year":2000,"aspects":["form","instrumentation","technology"],"explanation":"Genre as material to be cut and juxtaposed, structure imposed by rule rather than by song form, and a label-and-venue economy built by musicians — all carried forward. Tzadik and the Stone remained the connective infrastructure into the 2010s.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"neo-bop","to":"contemporary-creative","type":"parallel-influence","year":2002,"aspects":["improvisation","social-context"],"explanation":"An uncomfortable inheritance: the conservatory programmes, festival circuits and funding structures the Young Lions built are what train and support this generation, even where the music rejects neo-traditionalist premises entirely. The technical fluency is a direct product of that institutionalisation.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"ecm-nordic","to":"contemporary-creative","type":"parallel-influence","year":2005,"aspects":["timbre","form"],"explanation":"ECM became a principal outlet for this generation — Iyer, Sorey, Halvorson and Moran have all recorded for it — and the label's premium on space, dynamic range and composed structure shapes what the records sound like.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"jazz-rap","to":"contemporary-creative","type":"parallel-influence","year":2008,"aspects":["rhythm","technology"],"explanation":"The boom-bap feel entered acoustic jazz drumming as a first language rather than a borrowing, and producers' techniques — looping, layering, sound design — became compositional tools for musicians who never used a sampler.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"acid-jazz","to":"uk-jazz-revival","type":"direct-descendant","year":2012,"aspects":["social-context","rhythm","technology"],"explanation":"The infrastructure is inherited whole: Gilles Peterson's radio show and Brownswood label, a club culture that treats jazz as dance music, and an audience trained by twenty-five years of crate-digging. We Out Here in 2018 came out on Peterson's label.","hingeAlbum":"we-out-here-compilation","strength":"strong"},{"from":"spiritual-jazz","to":"uk-jazz-revival","type":"revival-of","year":2016,"aspects":["harmony","form","social-context"],"explanation":"Modal vamps, pedal points, long ecstatic builds and an explicitly political and spiritual framing are taken up directly — Nubya Garcia and Shabaka Hutchings both cite the Impulse! and Strata-East catalogue. Shabaka and the Ancestors is the most literal continuation.","strength":"strong"},{"from":"free-jazz","to":"uk-jazz-revival","type":"direct-descendant","year":2016,"aspects":["improvisation","timbre"],"explanation":"Sons of Kemet and The Comet Is Coming both use collective free playing and overblown saxophone as climactic devices, and the London scene overlaps with a continuous British free improvisation tradition that never went away.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"nu-jazz","to":"uk-jazz-revival","type":"direct-descendant","year":2014,"aspects":["technology","rhythm"],"explanation":"Broken beat and its West London scene are the immediate rhythmic ancestor, and several of the current players — Moses Boyd above all — work as electronic producers as readily as instrumentalists.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"la-beat","to":"uk-jazz-revival","type":"parallel-influence","year":2016,"aspects":["rhythm","technology","social-context"],"explanation":"The two scenes emerged in parallel and recognised each other quickly, sharing bills, remixes and a shared debt to Dilla-derived timing. Neither is a parent of the other; the traffic is lateral and continuous.","strength":"moderate"},{"from":"ethio-jazz","to":"uk-jazz-revival","type":"parallel-influence","year":2016,"aspects":["harmony","rhythm"],"explanation":"Interpretation: the Éthiopiques reissues became common currency in London crate-digging culture, and Mulatu Astatke recorded with the UK's Heliocentrics in 2009. Pentatonic modal vamps over a loping groove are a recognisable ingredient in the current scene, though rarely acknowledged as a specific source.","strength":"weak"},{"from":"j-jazz","to":"uk-jazz-revival","type":"parallel-influence","year":2018,"aspects":["timbre","form"],"explanation":"Interpretation, and a weak claim: the BBE J Jazz reissue series from 2018 landed in the same London DJ and record-shop ecosystem that supports the current scene, and the deep-modal, long-form Japanese records are part of its listening. Direct musical influence is unproven.","strength":"weak"}]},"albums":[{"id":"rifkin-piano-rags-joplin","genreIds":["ragtime"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Joshua Rifkin","title":"Piano Rags by Scott Joplin","recorded":1970,"released":1970,"label":"Nonesuch","catalogNo":"H-71248","personnel":["Joshua Rifkin (p)"],"whyThisOne":"A musicologist played Joplin slowly, quietly and exactly as written, treating the rags as concert repertoire rather than saloon music — which is what Joplin had asked for and never got. It became one of Nonesuch's best-selling records and started the ragtime revival single-handedly.","listenFor":"The left hand never varies its oom-pah while the right keeps landing between the beats; that friction is the entire idea, and at Rifkin's unhurried tempo you can hear it clearly.","startTrack":"Maple Leaf Rag","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"joplin-treemonisha-1976","genreIds":["ragtime"],"tier":"core","artist":"Scott Joplin / Houston Grand Opera, Gunther Schuller","title":"Treemonisha","recorded":1975,"released":1976,"label":"Deutsche Grammophon","catalogNo":"2707 083","personnel":["Carmen Balthrop (s)","Betty Allen (ms)","Curtis Rayam (t)","Gunther Schuller (cond)"],"whyThisOne":"Joplin's opera, which he could not get staged in his lifetime and which was not fully produced until 1972. It is the evidence for what he thought ragtime was for, and it is nothing like a novelty rag.","listenFor":"The ragtime syncopation is there, but it is carrying recitative and chorus writing — listen to how the rhythmic figure survives being put under sung words.","startTrack":"A Real Slow Drag","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"nec-red-back-book","genreIds":["ragtime"],"tier":"core","artist":"New England Conservatory Ragtime Ensemble, Gunther Schuller","title":"Scott Joplin: The Red Back Book","recorded":1973,"released":1973,"label":"Angel","catalogNo":"S-36060","personnel":["Gunther Schuller (cond)"],"whyThisOne":"Ragtime as most people actually heard it in 1910 — not solo piano but a small orchestra playing from published stock arrangements. Won a Grammy and reframed the music as ensemble repertoire.","listenFor":"The four strains are now shared between clarinet, cornet and strings, so you can hear the multi-strain march form as separate tunes rather than as one continuous piece.","startTrack":"The Entertainer","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"joseph-lamb-study-classic-ragtime","genreIds":["ragtime"],"tier":"core","artist":"Joseph Lamb","title":"Joseph Lamb: A Study in Classic Ragtime","recorded":1959,"released":1960,"label":"Folkways","catalogNo":"FG 3562","personnel":["Joseph Lamb (p)"],"whyThisOne":"The last surviving member of ragtime's 'big three', recorded at home in his seventies playing his own compositions. He is technically rusty and it does not matter — this is a primary source.","listenFor":"Lamb's rags are harmonically the richest of the classic repertoire; listen for the unexpected chromatic turns in the trio sections that Joplin rarely reaches for.","startTrack":"American Beauty Rag","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium","note":"Recorded by Sam Charters in Lamb's Brooklyn home in 1959; release year is given variously as 1959 or 1960."},{"id":"eubie-blake-86-years","genreIds":["ragtime","stride"],"tier":"core","artist":"Eubie Blake","title":"The Eighty-Six Years of Eubie Blake","recorded":1968,"released":1969,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"C2S 847","personnel":["Eubie Blake (p, voc)","Noble Sissle (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Blake was born in 1887, played in the ragtime era and was still recording in his eighties. This double album is a living witness moving between rag, stride and show tune in one sitting.","listenFor":"His left hand is bigger and looser than Joplin's classic ragtime allows — you can hear ragtime becoming stride inside a single performer's technique.","startTrack":"Charleston Rag","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"hamlisch-the-sting","genreIds":["ragtime"],"tier":"core","artist":"Marvin Hamlisch","title":"The Sting: Original Motion Picture Soundtrack","recorded":1973,"released":1974,"label":"MCA","catalogNo":"MCA-390","personnel":["Marvin Hamlisch (p, arr)"],"whyThisOne":"Historically unavoidable: this record put 'The Entertainer' in the singles chart in 1974 and made ragtime briefly ubiquitous. It is also anachronistic — the film is set in 1936, by which point ragtime was long over.","listenFor":"Hamlisch swings the eighth notes slightly, which classic ragtime does not. It is a small change and it is why this sounds like 1974 rather than 1902.","startTrack":"The Entertainer","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bolcom-heliotrope-bouquet","genreIds":["ragtime"],"tier":"deep","artist":"William Bolcom","title":"Heliotrope Bouquet: Piano Rags 1900–1970","recorded":1971,"released":1971,"label":"Nonesuch","catalogNo":"H-71257","personnel":["William Bolcom (p)"],"whyThisOne":"A composer-pianist putting classic rags next to his own, showing the form as a living compositional resource rather than a period style. Bolcom later won a Pulitzer; this is where he made the argument.","listenFor":"Scott Joplin and Louis Chauvin's 'Heliotrope Bouquet' is unusually sad for a rag — the syncopation stays but the harmony keeps sinking.","startTrack":"Heliotrope Bouquet","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"morath-best-of-scott-joplin","genreIds":["ragtime"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Max Morath","title":"The Best of Scott Joplin and Other Rag Classics","recorded":1972,"released":1972,"label":"Vanguard","catalogNo":"VSD 39/40","personnel":["Max Morath (p)"],"whyThisOne":"Morath was performing and broadcasting ragtime a decade before the revival and plays it as entertainment rather than as recovered art music — a useful corrective to the Rifkin approach.","listenFor":"Compare his tempo on 'Maple Leaf Rag' with Rifkin's. Both claim authenticity; they cannot both be right, and the argument about how fast to play a rag is a hundred years old.","startTrack":"Maple Leaf Rag","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"reginald-robinson-sounds-in-silhouette","genreIds":["ragtime"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Reginald R. Robinson","title":"Sounds in Silhouette","recorded":1998,"released":1998,"label":"Delmark","catalogNo":"DE-508","personnel":["Reginald R. Robinson (p)"],"whyThisOne":"Robinson writes new rags. He received a MacArthur Fellowship in 2004 for doing so, which settles the question of whether the form is dead.","listenFor":"The multi-strain form and the marching left hand are intact, but the harmony has absorbed eighty years that Joplin did not have.","startTrack":"Sounds in Silhouette","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"louis-armstrong-hot-fives-sevens","genreIds":["new-orleans"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Louis Armstrong","title":"The Complete Hot Five and Hot Seven Recordings","recorded":1925,"released":2000,"label":"Columbia/Legacy","catalogNo":"C4K 63527","personnel":["Louis Armstrong (cor, tp, voc)","Kid Ory (tb)","Johnny Dodds (cl)","Lil Hardin Armstrong (p)","Johnny St. Cyr (bj)"],"whyThisOne":"The single most consequential body of recordings in jazz. Across 1925–28 you can hear the music stop being an ensemble art and become a soloist's art, in real time, in one man's playing.","listenFor":"On 'West End Blues' the unaccompanied opening cadenza announces that a soloist can now command a record on his own. Nothing before 1928 sounds like that.","startTrack":"West End Blues","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high","note":"Recorded 1925–1928; the Columbia/Legacy four-CD set collecting them was issued in 2000. Earlier equivalent collections exist."},{"id":"king-oliver-creole-jazz-band-1923","genreIds":["new-orleans"],"tier":"core","artist":"King Oliver's Creole Jazz Band","title":"King Oliver's Jazz Band 1923","recorded":1923,"released":1974,"label":"Smithsonian Collection","catalogNo":"R 001","personnel":["King Oliver (cor)","Louis Armstrong (cor)","Johnny Dodds (cl)","Honoré Dutrey (tb)","Lil Hardin (p)","Baby Dodds (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The best documentation of a working New Orleans band with two cornets, recorded before the style was diluted. Armstrong is the second cornet, playing harmony parts, three years before he takes over.","listenFor":"The two cornets play worked-out breaks in perfect unison, apparently improvised. They were not — Oliver signalled them in advance, and the illusion is the point.","startTrack":"Dippermouth Blues","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high","note":"Recorded 1923 for Gennett, OKeh, Columbia and Paramount; multiple later compilations exist. Catalogue given here is the widely cited Smithsonian reissue."},{"id":"birth-of-the-hot-morton","genreIds":["new-orleans","ragtime"],"tier":"core","artist":"Jelly Roll Morton's Red Hot Peppers","title":"Birth of the Hot: The Classic Chicago 'Red Hot Peppers' Sessions 1926–27","recorded":1926,"released":1995,"label":"RCA Bluebird","catalogNo":"07863 66641-2","personnel":["Jelly Roll Morton (p, arr)","George Mitchell (cor)","Kid Ory (tb)","Omer Simeon (cl)","Johnny St. Cyr (bj)","John Lindsay (b)","Andrew Hilaire (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Morton wrote and rehearsed these, and they are the clearest demonstration that New Orleans jazz could be composed. The rag's multi-strain architecture is intact underneath the improvising.","listenFor":"'Black Bottom Stomp' changes texture every sixteen bars — full ensemble, then a break, then a solo with stop-time, then a different combination. That is arrangement, not jamming.","startTrack":"Black Bottom Stomp","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high","note":"Recorded Chicago 1926–27 for Victor; the Bluebird CD compilation dates from 1995."},{"id":"sidney-bechet-best-of","genreIds":["new-orleans"],"tier":"core","artist":"Sidney Bechet","title":"The Best of Sidney Bechet","recorded":1939,"released":1994,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"CDP 7243 8 28891 2 3","personnel":["Sidney Bechet (ss, cl)","various"],"whyThisOne":"Bechet's soprano saxophone is the loudest, widest, most dominating sound in early jazz, and he refused to be a section player for sixty years. His 'Summertime' from 1939 is the New Orleans idiom outliving its era.","listenFor":"The vibrato is enormous and slow, wobbling nearly a semitone. Modern saxophone teaching would call it a fault; here it is the whole personality.","startTrack":"Summertime","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium","note":"Compilation of Bechet's Blue Note sessions, chiefly 1939–1953."},{"id":"odjb-complete-original-dixieland","genreIds":["new-orleans"],"tier":"core","artist":"Original Dixieland Jazz Band","title":"The Complete Original Dixieland Jazz Band 1917–1936","recorded":1917,"released":1996,"label":"RCA Bluebird","catalogNo":"ND 82483","personnel":["Nick LaRocca (cor)","Larry Shields (cl)","Eddie Edwards (tb)","Henry Ragas (p)","Tony Sbarbaro (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The first jazz record, in February 1917, made by a white band from New Orleans who then claimed to have invented the music. Both the musical and the historical facts here matter, and they point in different directions.","listenFor":"The energy is real and the collective front line works, but compare the rhythmic looseness to Oliver's band six years later — the difference is what the record industry took twelve years to document.","startTrack":"Livery Stable Blues","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"johnny-dodds-blue-clarinet-stomp","genreIds":["new-orleans"],"tier":"core","artist":"Johnny Dodds","title":"Blue Clarinet Stomp","recorded":1928,"released":1991,"label":"RCA Bluebird","catalogNo":"ND 82293","personnel":["Johnny Dodds (cl)","Natty Dominique (cor)","Bill Johnson (b)","Baby Dodds (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Dodds is the New Orleans clarinet voice — blues-drenched, vocal, and far rougher than the Chicago players who copied him. These small-group sides show the idiom without Armstrong dominating it.","listenFor":"He bends into notes from below and lets them sag, which is a blues singer's technique applied to a clarinet.","startTrack":"Blue Clarinet Stomp","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"george-lewis-jazz-at-vespers","genreIds":["new-orleans"],"tier":"deep","artist":"George Lewis","title":"Jazz at Vespers","recorded":1954,"released":1954,"label":"Riverside","catalogNo":"RLP 12-230","personnel":["George Lewis (cl)","Avery 'Kid' Howard (tp)","Jim Robinson (tb)","Alton Purnell (p)","Lawrence Marrero (bj)","Alcide 'Slow Drag' Pavageau (b)","Joe Watkins (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The New Orleans revival's central document, recorded in a Ohio college chapel by musicians who had never left the idiom. Hymns played by a jazz band, which is where a lot of the repertoire came from in the first place.","listenFor":"The band plays hymns almost straight, then lets the collective improvisation build very gradually. It never becomes a showpiece.","startTrack":"Just a Closer Walk with Thee","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"preservation-hall-jazz-band-so-it-is","genreIds":["new-orleans"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Preservation Hall Jazz Band","title":"So It Is","recorded":2016,"released":2017,"label":"Legacy","catalogNo":"88985419112","personnel":["Ben Jaffe (b, tuba)","Charlie Gabriel (ts, cl)","Branden Lewis (tp)","Ronell Johnson (tb)","Kyle Roussel (p)","Walter Harris (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Proof that the tradition is not a museum. The band went to Cuba, came back, and made a record of original material that is recognisably New Orleans and unmistakably contemporary.","listenFor":"The Afro-Cuban rhythms sit naturally against second-line drumming, which is the point — these are branches of one root that were separated by geography rather than by kind.","startTrack":"Santiago","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"wynton-marsalis-majesty-of-the-blues","genreIds":["new-orleans","neo-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Wynton Marsalis","title":"The Majesty of the Blues","recorded":1988,"released":1989,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CK 45091","personnel":["Wynton Marsalis (tp)","Todd Williams (ts)","Wessell Anderson (as)","Marcus Roberts (p)","Reginald Veal (b)","Herlin Riley (d)","Dr. Michael White (cl)","Freddie Lonzo (tb)"],"whyThisOne":"The record where Marsalis turned back past bebop to New Orleans polyphony and second-line rhythm, with a spoken sermon in the middle. It reset his own project and the neo-traditionalist argument.","listenFor":"The 'New Orleans Function' suite uses a real funeral-march-to-second-line structure: slow dirge out, uptempo strut back.","startTrack":"The Death of Jazz","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bix-beiderbecke-singin-the-blues","genreIds":["chicago-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Bix Beiderbecke","title":"Singin' the Blues","recorded":1927,"released":1990,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CK 45450","personnel":["Bix Beiderbecke (cor)","Frankie Trumbauer (Cm)","Eddie Lang (g)","various"],"whyThisOne":"Beiderbecke's cornet is the first great alternative to Armstrong: warm, unforced, harmonically curious, and completely without showmanship. He was dead at 28 and the recorded evidence is small and perfect.","listenFor":"The title track's solo is built from notes chosen for their colour against the chord rather than for display; it was transcribed and memorised by musicians for decades.","startTrack":"Singin' the Blues","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high","note":"Recorded 1927 for OKeh under Frankie Trumbauer's name; the Columbia CD compilation dates from 1990."},{"id":"eddie-condon-chronological-1927-1938","genreIds":["chicago-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Eddie Condon","title":"The Chronological Eddie Condon 1927–1938","recorded":1927,"released":1996,"label":"Classics","catalogNo":"Classics 742","personnel":["Eddie Condon (g, bj)","Pee Wee Russell (cl)","Jack Teagarden (tb)","various"],"whyThisOne":"Condon organised the Chicago players for four decades and these sides are the style at its rawest, before it got polished for concert halls.","listenFor":"The 'explosion' — a hard accented hit right before a new chorus begins, which the Chicago players used constantly and New Orleans bands did not.","startTrack":"Nobody's Sweetheart","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"mckenzie-condon-chicagoans","genreIds":["chicago-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"McKenzie and Condon's Chicagoans","title":"The Austin High School Gang","recorded":1927,"released":1990,"label":"Retrieval","catalogNo":"RTR 79001","personnel":["Jimmy McPartland (cor)","Frank Teschemacher (cl)","Bud Freeman (ts)","Joe Sullivan (p)","Eddie Condon (bj)","Gene Krupa (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The December 1927 OKeh session is the style's founding document — four sides by teenagers who had learned jazz from records, playing faster and harder than their models.","listenFor":"Krupa's bass drum is audible and driving in a way that 1927 recording technology usually could not handle, and it pushes the whole band forward.","startTrack":"Nobody's Sweetheart","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"pee-wee-russell-portrait","genreIds":["chicago-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Pee Wee Russell","title":"Portrait of Pee Wee","recorded":1958,"released":1958,"label":"Counterpoint","catalogNo":"CPT 565","personnel":["Pee Wee Russell (cl)","Ruby Braff (cor)","Bud Freeman (ts)","Vic Dickenson (tb)","Nat Pierce (p)","Tommy Potter (b)","Karl Kiffe (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Russell played clarinet like nobody else — croaking, hesitating, choosing notes that sound like mistakes and resolve like arguments. This is the best-recorded document of a genuinely strange musician.","listenFor":"He will start a phrase, appear to lose it, and land somewhere unrelated that turns out to be correct. It is deliberate.","startTrack":"Pee Wee's Blues","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"bud-freeman-chicagoans-in-new-york","genreIds":["chicago-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Bud Freeman","title":"Bud Freeman and His Famous Chicagoans","recorded":1940,"released":1990,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CK 45142","personnel":["Bud Freeman (ts)","Max Kaminsky (tp)","Jack Teagarden (tb)","Pee Wee Russell (cl)","Dave Bowman (p)","Eddie Condon (g)","Dave Tough (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Freeman built a tenor style that owes nothing to Coleman Hawkins — lighter, drier, more skittish — and it is one of the few genuinely independent saxophone lineages of the 1930s.","listenFor":"The tenor tone is thin and papery compared with Hawkins, and the phrasing skips rather than drives. Lester Young was listening.","startTrack":"The Eel","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"benny-goodman-bg-trio-quartet","genreIds":["chicago-jazz","swing"],"tier":"core","artist":"Benny Goodman","title":"The Complete Benny Goodman Trios and Quartets","recorded":1935,"released":1998,"label":"RCA Bluebird","catalogNo":"09026-68764-2","personnel":["Benny Goodman (cl)","Teddy Wilson (p)","Gene Krupa (d)","Lionel Hampton (vib)"],"whyThisOne":"Goodman came out of Chicago and these small groups are where his playing is least constrained. They were also racially integrated on a public stage in 1936, which took real professional risk.","listenFor":"With only three or four players, the Chicago habit of driving hard through a chorus has nowhere to hide — the tempo pushes constantly.","startTrack":"Body and Soul","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"muggsy-spanier-ragtime-band","genreIds":["chicago-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Muggsy Spanier","title":"The Great 16","recorded":1939,"released":1994,"label":"RCA Bluebird","catalogNo":"ND 82932","personnel":["Muggsy Spanier (cor)","Rod Cless (cl)","George Brunies (tb)","Joe Bushkin (p)","Bob Casey (b)","Marty Greenberg (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Sixteen sides cut in 1939 by a Chicago cornetist who had nearly died the year before, playing plunger-muted and unhurried. It is the point where Chicago style stops chasing swing and becomes a preserved idiom.","listenFor":"Spanier's plunger work on 'Relaxin' at the Touro' — the mute is doing what a singer's mouth does to a vowel.","startTrack":"Relaxin' at the Touro","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"jack-teagarden-king-of-the-blues-trombone","genreIds":["chicago-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Jack Teagarden","title":"King of the Blues Trombone","recorded":1928,"released":1970,"label":"Epic","catalogNo":"JSN 6044","personnel":["Jack Teagarden (tb, voc)","various"],"whyThisOne":"Teagarden made the trombone a legato blues instrument rather than a tailgate noisemaker, and he sang with the same relaxed drawl. Nobody in New Orleans played trombone like this.","listenFor":"He barely uses the slide's smearing capability — the lines are as smooth as a saxophone's, which was almost unheard of in 1929.","startTrack":"Basin Street Blues","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"art-hodes-sittin-in","genreIds":["chicago-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Art Hodes","title":"Sittin' In","recorded":1944,"released":1993,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"CDP 7243 8 32082 2 6","personnel":["Art Hodes (p)","Sidney Bechet (ss)","Max Kaminsky (tp)","Ray Conniff (tb)","Danny Alvin (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Blue Note's early 1940s traditional sessions, before the label became modern jazz's home. Hodes was a Chicago blues pianist by temperament and the sessions are unusually unhurried.","listenFor":"This is Blue Note two decades before Rudy Van Gelder, and the label's entire identity was traditional jazz at this point.","startTrack":"Blues for Jelly","difficulty":3,"confidence":"low","note":"Blue Note's 1940s Hodes material has been reissued under several titles and catalogue numbers; the compilation identity here is less certain than the recordings themselves."},{"id":"james-p-johnson-carolina-shout","genreIds":["stride"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"James P. Johnson","title":"Carolina Shout","recorded":1921,"released":1991,"label":"Biograph","catalogNo":"BCD 105","personnel":["James P. Johnson (p)"],"whyThisOne":"The founding stride record and the test piece every Harlem pianist had to learn — Ellington taught himself by slowing down Johnson's piano roll and copying the fingering off the keys.","listenFor":"The left hand jumps a tenth or more between the bass note and the chord, on every beat, at speed. That leap is the style's name and its physical difficulty.","startTrack":"Carolina Shout","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high","note":"'Carolina Shout' was cut as a piano roll in 1918 and as a disc in October 1921; Biograph collections gather both."},{"id":"fats-waller-a-good-man","genreIds":["stride","vocal-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Fats Waller","title":"A Good Man Is Hard to Find","recorded":1935,"released":1993,"label":"RCA Bluebird","catalogNo":"ND 82482","personnel":["Fats Waller (p, voc)","Herman Autrey (tp)","Gene Sedric (ts, cl)","Al Casey (g)","Charles Turner (b)","Yank Porter (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Waller sold stride to a mass audience by wrapping it in comedy, and the joke conceals how much piano is going on. The rhythm section is essentially decorative — he does not need it.","listenFor":"Ignore the singing for one chorus and follow the left hand alone. It never stops and it never simplifies.","startTrack":"Ain't Misbehavin'","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"art-tatum-solo-masterpieces","genreIds":["stride"],"tier":"core","artist":"Art Tatum","title":"The Complete Pablo Solo Masterpieces","recorded":1953,"released":1991,"label":"Pablo","catalogNo":"7PACD-4404-2","personnel":["Art Tatum (p)"],"whyThisOne":"Norman Granz recorded Tatum alone over several marathon days in 1953–55, producing the largest body of solo jazz piano by the most technically formidable player the music has had.","listenFor":"Inside any ballad, he will reharmonise a phrase two or three different ways in a single chorus. The stride left hand is still there under the ornament, holding time.","startTrack":"Tea for Two","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"willie-the-lion-smith-memoirs","genreIds":["stride"],"tier":"core","artist":"Willie 'The Lion' Smith","title":"The Lion Roars","recorded":1957,"released":1957,"label":"Dot","catalogNo":"DLP 3094","personnel":["Willie 'The Lion' Smith (p)"],"whyThisOne":"The most harmonically impressionistic of the Harlem pianists, and the one Ellington named as a direct influence. His own compositions sound like Debussy who learned to stride.","listenFor":"'Echoes of Spring' has almost no blues in it and a great deal of whole-tone colour — stride reaching toward the concert hall.","startTrack":"Echoes of Spring","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"duke-ellington-piano-reflections","genreIds":["stride","swing"],"tier":"core","artist":"Duke Ellington","title":"The Duke Plays Ellington (Piano Reflections)","recorded":1953,"released":1954,"label":"Capitol","catalogNo":"T 477","personnel":["Duke Ellington (p)","Wendell Marshall (b)","Butch Ballard (d)","Dave Black (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Ellington almost never recorded as a piano soloist, and this trio session shows how much stride is still in his hands thirty years after Harlem — and how much space he had learned to leave.","listenFor":"He plays far fewer notes than Waller or Johnson, but the left-hand placement is identical. Stride reduced to its rhythmic essentials.","startTrack":"Reflections in D","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium","note":"Issued on Capitol as 'The Duke Plays Ellington'; the 1989 CD reissue with extra tracks renamed it 'Piano Reflections', which is now the commoner title. Sources give the original LP as 1953 or 1954."},{"id":"thelonious-monk-solo-monk","genreIds":["stride"],"tier":"core","artist":"Thelonious Monk","title":"Solo Monk","recorded":1964,"released":1965,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CL 2349","personnel":["Thelonious Monk (p)"],"whyThisOne":"Monk's debt to stride is clearest when he is alone: the left hand strides, the ballads come from the 1920s repertoire, and the dissonance sits on top of an old-fashioned foundation.","listenFor":"On 'Dinah' the stride is unmistakable and completely lopsided — he keeps dropping or adding a beat's worth of weight where the pattern should be even.","startTrack":"Dinah","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"dick-wellstood-from-ragtime-on","genreIds":["stride"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Dick Wellstood","title":"From Ragtime On","recorded":1971,"released":1971,"label":"Chiaroscuro","catalogNo":"CR 109","personnel":["Dick Wellstood (p)"],"whyThisOne":"A generation later, a white pianist who had played with Sidney Bechet made stride a working modern language rather than a period recreation. He is also very funny, which the style rewards.","listenFor":"He will play a bebop line with a stride left hand underneath it, which should not work.","startTrack":"Squeeze Me","difficulty":2,"confidence":"low","note":"Wellstood's Chiaroscuro discography is not well documented online; title, year and catalogue number should be treated as provisional."},{"id":"donald-lambert-harlem-stride-classics","genreIds":["stride"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Donald Lambert","title":"Harlem Stride Classics","recorded":1941,"released":1990,"label":"Pumpkin","catalogNo":"PU 117","personnel":["Donald Lambert (p)"],"whyThisOne":"Lambert barely recorded and worked New Jersey bars for decades, but the surviving sides — including stride arrangements of Massenet and Wagner — show a technique the size of Tatum's.","listenFor":"His stride version of the 'Pilgrims' Chorus' from Tannhäuser is not a joke; the left hand keeps perfect time through material that has no business swinging.","startTrack":"Pilgrim's Chorus","difficulty":3,"confidence":"low","note":"Lambert's recordings survive mainly through small reissue labels; catalogue details are poorly documented."},{"id":"jaki-byard-blues-for-smoke","genreIds":["stride","post-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Jaki Byard","title":"Blues for Smoke","recorded":1960,"released":1988,"label":"Candid","catalogNo":"CCD 79018","personnel":["Jaki Byard (p)"],"whyThisOne":"Byard could move between stride, bebop and free playing within one chorus, and did it constantly. This solo session is the clearest demonstration that jazz history is available all at once to a player who knows it.","listenFor":"Time-travel inside a single track: a stride passage gives way to something Cecil Taylor might have played, then back, with no seam.","startTrack":"Blues for Smoke","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high","note":"Recorded for Candid in December 1960 but not issued until the Candid catalogue was revived in the late 1980s."},{"id":"count-basie-original-decca","genreIds":["kansas-city"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Count Basie","title":"The Original American Decca Recordings","recorded":1937,"released":1992,"label":"GRP/Decca","catalogNo":"GRD-3-611","personnel":["Count Basie (p)","Lester Young (ts)","Herschel Evans (ts)","Buck Clayton (tp)","Freddie Green (g)","Walter Page (b)","Jo Jones (d)","Jimmy Rushing (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"The 1937–39 Decca sides are the Kansas City rhythm section in its first flush, and they changed how every band in America kept time. Lester Young's solos here are the other half of the reason this matters.","listenFor":"The rhythm section floats rather than pushes. Freddie Green plays quarter notes on guitar and never solos in his fifty years with the band; Jo Jones moves the pulse to the hi-hat.","startTrack":"One O'Clock Jump","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"lester-young-kansas-city-sessions","genreIds":["kansas-city"],"tier":"core","artist":"Lester Young","title":"The Kansas City Sessions","recorded":1938,"released":1997,"label":"Commodore","catalogNo":"CMD-402","personnel":["Lester Young (ts, cl)","Buck Clayton (tp)","Eddie Durham (g)","Freddie Green (g)","Walter Page (b)","Jo Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Small-group sessions from 1938 and 1944 where Young has nothing to hide behind. This is the tenor playing that Charlie Parker studied and that cool jazz descends from.","listenFor":"He plays behind the beat, uses almost no vibrato, and builds long phrases that cross the bar lines. Compare it to Coleman Hawkins in the same years — they sound like different instruments.","startTrack":"Lester Leaps In","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high","note":"Commodore sessions recorded 1938 (Kansas City Six) and 1944; various reissue configurations exist."},{"id":"bennie-moten-basie-beginnings","genreIds":["kansas-city"],"tier":"core","artist":"Bennie Moten's Kansas City Orchestra","title":"Basie Beginnings","recorded":1929,"released":1989,"label":"RCA Bluebird","catalogNo":"ND 90403","personnel":["Bennie Moten (p)","Count Basie (p)","Hot Lips Page (tp)","Eddie Durham (tb, g)","Ben Webster (ts)","Walter Page (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The December 1932 Victor session is where the Kansas City rhythmic shift first arrives on record, three years before Basie's own band. Moten died in 1935 and Basie inherited the personnel.","listenFor":"'Toby' and 'Prince of Wails' from 1932 move to an even four in a way nothing else that year does. The band sounds ten years ahead of itself.","startTrack":"Toby","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"andy-kirk-mary-lou-williams","genreIds":["kansas-city"],"tier":"core","artist":"Andy Kirk and His Twelve Clouds of Joy","title":"Mary's Idea","recorded":1936,"released":1993,"label":"GRP/Decca","catalogNo":"GRD-622","personnel":["Mary Lou Williams (p, arr)","Andy Kirk (ldr)","Dick Wilson (ts)","Pha Terrell (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Mary Lou Williams wrote and arranged most of this band's book while also playing piano in it, and she is the strongest compositional mind Kansas City produced.","listenFor":"Her arrangements use the riff principle but keep varying the harmony underneath it, which most KC bands did not bother with.","startTrack":"Mary's Idea","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"jay-mcshann-blues-from-kansas-city","genreIds":["kansas-city","bebop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Jay McShann","title":"Blues from Kansas City","recorded":1941,"released":1992,"label":"GRP/Decca","catalogNo":"GRD-614","personnel":["Jay McShann (p)","Charlie Parker (as)","Gene Ramey (b)","Gus Johnson (d)","Walter Brown (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Charlie Parker's first recorded solos, on a blues-based Kansas City band's Decca sides in 1941. The bebop vocabulary is not there yet, but the phrasing already is.","listenFor":"Parker's eight bars on 'Hootie Blues' — the line already crosses bar lines and lands on the notes nobody else was choosing.","startTrack":"Hootie Blues","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"count-basie-atomic-mr-basie","genreIds":["kansas-city","swing"],"tier":"core","artist":"Count Basie","title":"The Atomic Mr. Basie","recorded":1957,"released":1958,"label":"Roulette","catalogNo":"R 52003","personnel":["Count Basie (p)","Neal Hefti (arr)","Thad Jones (tp)","Frank Wess (ts, fl)","Freddie Green (g)","Eddie Jones (b)","Sonny Payne (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Twenty years on, the second Basie band with Neal Hefti's charts — the KC rhythmic feel intact inside far more sophisticated writing. It is also simply one of the best-recorded big band albums.","listenFor":"'Li'l Darlin'' is taken at an almost absurdly slow tempo and the band never rushes it. That discipline is the KC rhythm section's inheritance.","startTrack":"Li'l Darlin'","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high","note":"Also issued as 'Basie' and 'E=MC²'; original Roulette release 1958."},{"id":"big-joe-turner-boss-of-the-blues","genreIds":["kansas-city"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Big Joe Turner","title":"The Boss of the Blues","recorded":1956,"released":1956,"label":"Atlantic","catalogNo":"1234","personnel":["Big Joe Turner (voc)","Pete Johnson (p)","Lawrence Brown (tb)","Pete Brown (as)","Frank Wess (ts)","Jimmy Rushing's KC lineage"],"whyThisOne":"Turner shouted blues over Kansas City bands in the 1930s, then had rock and roll hits in the 1950s. This record puts him back with Pete Johnson and a jazz band, and the continuity is startling.","listenFor":"He is singing over jazz arrangements at rock and roll volume, which is exactly what a KC blues shouter did before either category existed.","startTrack":"Cherry Red","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"mary-lou-williams-zodiac-suite","genreIds":["kansas-city","third-stream"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Mary Lou Williams","title":"Zodiac Suite","recorded":1945,"released":1945,"label":"Asch","catalogNo":"620","personnel":["Mary Lou Williams (p)","Al Lucas (b)","Jack Parker (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Twelve pieces, one per sign, written for trio and later orchestrated — a jazz musician writing extended concert music in 1945, more than a decade before anyone called it third stream.","listenFor":"The harmony moves in ways that have nothing to do with swing-era practice; some of it is closer to Bartók than to Kansas City.","startTrack":"Aries","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"hot-lips-page-after-hours","genreIds":["kansas-city"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Hot Lips Page","title":"After Hours in Harlem","recorded":1940,"released":1988,"label":"Onyx","catalogNo":"OD 207","personnel":["Hot Lips Page (tp, voc)","various"],"whyThisOne":"Live jam session recordings from Minton's and Monroe's around 1940–41, capturing the after-hours culture that Kansas City exported to Harlem and that bebop then came out of.","listenFor":"These are amateur acetates of a real jam session, so the sound is poor and the playing is uninhibited. This is what the scene actually sounded like off the clock.","startTrack":"Sweet Georgia Brown","difficulty":4,"confidence":"low","note":"Private acetate recordings from Minton's Playhouse and Monroe's Uptown House; provenance, dates and personnel are disputed across reissues."},{"id":"ellington-blanton-webster-band","genreIds":["swing"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Duke Ellington","title":"The Blanton–Webster Band","recorded":1940,"released":1986,"label":"RCA Bluebird","catalogNo":"5659-2-RB","personnel":["Duke Ellington (p, comp)","Billy Strayhorn (comp, arr)","Jimmy Blanton (b)","Ben Webster (ts)","Johnny Hodges (as)","Cootie Williams (tp)","Tricky Sam Nanton (tb)"],"whyThisOne":"The 1940–42 band is the peak of orchestral jazz writing. Ellington had Strayhorn, a bassist who reinvented the instrument's role, and a saxophone section he had been writing for individually for fifteen years.","listenFor":"'Ko-Ko' is built almost entirely from a minor blues and short repeated figures, and it is genuinely dark — closer to Bartók's sense of orchestration than to dance music.","startTrack":"Ko-Ko","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"benny-goodman-carnegie-hall-1938","genreIds":["swing"],"tier":"core","artist":"Benny Goodman","title":"The Famous 1938 Carnegie Hall Jazz Concert","recorded":1938,"released":1950,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"SL 160","personnel":["Benny Goodman (cl)","Harry James (tp)","Gene Krupa (d)","Teddy Wilson (p)","Lionel Hampton (vib)","Count Basie (p)","Lester Young (ts)"],"whyThisOne":"January 1938: jazz played at Carnegie Hall for the first time, with an integrated line-up and members of the Basie and Ellington bands sitting in. The 'Sing, Sing, Sing' performance runs twelve minutes and does not sag.","listenFor":"Jess Stacy's unplanned piano solo in 'Sing, Sing, Sing' — quiet, wandering and completely unlike the rest of the concert. Goodman just let it run.","startTrack":"Sing, Sing, Sing","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"fletcher-henderson-a-study-in-frustration","genreIds":["swing"],"tier":"core","artist":"Fletcher Henderson","title":"A Study in Frustration","recorded":1923,"released":1961,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"C4L 19","personnel":["Fletcher Henderson (p, ldr)","Louis Armstrong (tp)","Coleman Hawkins (ts)","Don Redman (arr)","Benny Carter (as, arr)"],"whyThisOne":"Henderson and Don Redman invented the big band arrangement — reeds against brass, call-and-response, soloist framed by written backgrounds — and then Henderson sold the charts to Goodman and watched him get rich.","listenFor":"Armstrong joins in 1924 and the whole band's rhythmic conception changes within months. You can hear a rhythm section learning to swing.","startTrack":"Copenhagen","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"ellington-at-newport-1956","genreIds":["swing"],"tier":"core","artist":"Duke Ellington","title":"Ellington at Newport","recorded":1956,"released":1956,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CL 934","personnel":["Duke Ellington (p)","Paul Gonsalves (ts)","Johnny Hodges (as)","Sam Woodyard (d)","Jimmy Woode (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Paul Gonsalves plays 27 blues choruses, the crowd loses its composure, and Ellington's career restarts at 57. It is the most famous single performance in big band jazz.","listenFor":"Sam Woodyard's drumming and Jimmy Woode's bass during the Gonsalves solo — they simply hold a groove for six minutes and the tension does the rest.","startTrack":"Diminuendo and Crescendo in Blue","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high","note":"The 1956 Columbia LP is partly a studio re-recording; the 1999 'Complete' edition restores the actual concert tapes."},{"id":"artie-shaw-self-portrait","genreIds":["swing"],"tier":"core","artist":"Artie Shaw","title":"Self Portrait","recorded":1936,"released":2001,"label":"RCA Victor","catalogNo":"09026-63808-2","personnel":["Artie Shaw (cl)","Billy Butterfield (tp)","Roy Eldridge (tp)","Buddy Rich (d)","Barney Kessel (g)"],"whyThisOne":"Shaw was a better clarinettist than Goodman and a far more reluctant bandleader; his Gramercy Five small groups and the string-band experiments are the era's most restless writing.","listenFor":"The Gramercy Five uses a harpsichord, in 1940, in a swing band. It is not a novelty — it is a genuine attempt at a different ensemble colour.","startTrack":"Summit Ridge Drive","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"woody-herman-thundering-herds","genreIds":["swing","bebop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Woody Herman","title":"The Thundering Herds 1945–1947","recorded":1945,"released":1988,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CK 44108","personnel":["Woody Herman (cl, as)","Flip Phillips (ts)","Bill Harris (tb)","Ralph Burns (arr)","Stan Getz (ts)","Zoot Sims (ts)"],"whyThisOne":"The First Herd absorbed bebop harmony into a big band while it was still happening, and Stravinsky wrote the Ebony Concerto for them in 1945. This is swing refusing to die quietly.","listenFor":"The Four Brothers saxophone voicing — three tenors and a baritone instead of the standard two altos on top — gives the section a darker, thicker sound than any other band.","startTrack":"Four Brothers","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"ellington-such-sweet-thunder","genreIds":["swing","third-stream"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Duke Ellington and Billy Strayhorn","title":"Such Sweet Thunder","recorded":1957,"released":1957,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CL 1033","personnel":["Duke Ellington (p)","Billy Strayhorn (comp)","Johnny Hodges (as)","Clark Terry (tp)","Ray Nance (tp, vln)"],"whyThisOne":"A twelve-part Shakespeare suite, written with Strayhorn, where each movement is a character portrait. It is the strongest evidence that Ellington's real form was the suite, not the three-minute side.","listenFor":"'Sonnet for Caesar' is fourteen lines long in music — Ellington wrote the sonnets as fourteen-bar pieces. The conceit is not decorative.","startTrack":"Such Sweet Thunder","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jimmie-lunceford-rhythm-is-our-business","genreIds":["swing"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Jimmie Lunceford","title":"Rhythm Is Our Business","recorded":1934,"released":1995,"label":"Decca","catalogNo":"GRD-608","personnel":["Jimmie Lunceford (ldr)","Sy Oliver (tp, arr)","Willie Smith (as)","Trummy Young (tb)","Jimmy Crawford (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The most precisely drilled band of the era, with Sy Oliver's two-beat arrangements and a showmanship that made them nearly unbeatable live. Basie and Ellington both said Lunceford's band was the one to fear.","listenFor":"Oliver's charts sit on a loping two-beat rather than an even four, which makes the band sound like it is leaning back while moving fast.","startTrack":"For Dancers Only","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"maria-schneider-concert-in-the-garden","genreIds":["swing","contemporary-creative"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Maria Schneider Orchestra","title":"Concert in the Garden","recorded":2004,"released":2004,"label":"ArtistShare","catalogNo":"AS0001","personnel":["Maria Schneider (comp, cond)","Ingrid Jensen (tp)","Donny McCaslin (ts)","Frank Kimbrough (p)","Gary Versace (accordion)"],"whyThisOne":"Big band writing is not a historical practice. Schneider studied with Gil Evans and writes long-form orchestral pieces with Brazilian and flamenco rhythm underneath; this was the first record to win a Grammy on internet-funded distribution alone.","listenFor":"The saxophone section is used for colour and texture more than for block-chord punch — closer to Ravel's orchestration than to Fletcher Henderson's.","startTrack":"Choro Dançado","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"django-quintette-hot-club-1936","genreIds":["manouche"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Django Reinhardt & Stéphane Grappelli","title":"Djangologie 1936–1937","recorded":1936,"released":2009,"label":"Frémeaux & Associés","catalogNo":"FA 303","personnel":["Django Reinhardt (g)","Stéphane Grappelli (vln)","Joseph Reinhardt (g)","Roger Chaput (g)","Louis Vola (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The Quintette du Hot Club de France at its peak, with the classic all-string line-up. 'Minor Swing', 'Djangology' and 'Nuages' are here and everything the idiom later became is already fully formed.","listenFor":"There is no drummer. The two rhythm guitars playing 'la pompe' — a hard chop with the chord instantly damped — do the entire job of a kit.","startTrack":"Minor Swing","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium","note":"The Quintette's 1930s sides appear in many reissue configurations; the Frémeaux 'Djangologie' series is the most complete and best documented."},{"id":"django-nuages-1940","genreIds":["manouche"],"tier":"core","artist":"Django Reinhardt","title":"Nuages","recorded":1940,"released":2003,"label":"Naxos Jazz Legends","catalogNo":"8.120631","personnel":["Django Reinhardt (g)","Hubert Rostaing (cl)","Pierre Fouad (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Under German occupation Grappelli was stranded in London, so Django rebuilt the group around clarinet and drums. The wartime quintet is darker and more harmonically searching than the pre-war one.","listenFor":"'Nuages' has a descending chromatic inner line that is pure Parisian musette, not American jazz. This is the non-jazz half of the style showing itself.","startTrack":"Nuages","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"grappelli-violin-summit","genreIds":["manouche"],"tier":"core","artist":"Stéphane Grappelli, Svend Asmussen, Jean-Luc Ponty, Stuff Smith","title":"Violin Summit","recorded":1966,"released":1967,"label":"SABA","catalogNo":"SB 15108","personnel":["Stéphane Grappelli (vln)","Stuff Smith (vln)","Svend Asmussen (vln)","Jean-Luc Ponty (vln)","Kenny Drew (p)","Niels-Henning Ørsted Pedersen (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Four generations of jazz violin in one room in Basel, 1966. Grappelli's manouche lineage sits next to Stuff Smith's swing and Ponty's incipient fusion, and the differences are immediately audible.","listenFor":"Grappelli's tone is the sweetest and his time the most elastic; Ponty's is hard and fast and points straight at the electric violin he would take into fusion.","startTrack":"It Don't Mean a Thing","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"birelli-lagrene-routes-to-django","genreIds":["manouche"],"tier":"core","artist":"Biréli Lagrène","title":"Routes to Django","recorded":1980,"released":1980,"label":"Antilles","catalogNo":"AN-1010","personnel":["Biréli Lagrène (g)","Jorg Reiter (p)","Jan Jankeje (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Lagrène was thirteen when this was recorded and already played the Django vocabulary fluently. It is both an astonishing document and a demonstration that the idiom is transmitted within Romani families rather than through schools.","listenFor":"The tremolo and the octave passages are lifted directly from Django, but the time feel is a teenager's — faster and more aggressive.","startTrack":"Nuages","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"rosenberg-trio-seresta","genreIds":["manouche"],"tier":"core","artist":"Rosenberg Trio","title":"Seresta","recorded":1990,"released":1990,"label":"Polydor","catalogNo":"847 251-2","personnel":["Stochelo Rosenberg (g)","Nous'che Rosenberg (g)","Nonnie Rosenberg (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The Dutch Sinti trio that carried the style into the 1990s with a cleanness of execution Django never had access to. This is the record that made the modern manouche festival circuit possible.","listenFor":"Stochelo Rosenberg's picking is faster and more even than Django's, which raises a real question about whether technical perfection suits this music.","startTrack":"Bossa Dorado","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"django-peche-a-la-mouche","genreIds":["manouche","bebop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Django Reinhardt","title":"Péché à la Mouche: The Great Blue Star Sessions 1947/1953","recorded":1947,"released":1992,"label":"Verve","catalogNo":"835 418-2","personnel":["Django Reinhardt (elg)","Maurice Vander (p)","Pierre Michelot (b)","Jean-Louis Viale (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Django's last sessions, on electric guitar with a bebop rhythm section, weeks before his death in 1953. He had absorbed Parker and Gillespie and was building something new when he stopped.","listenFor":"The lines are longer and more chromatic than anything from the 1930s, and the amplified tone removes the acoustic bark. It sounds like a different guitarist who happens to phrase identically.","startTrack":"Nuages","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"tchavolo-schmitt-alors-question","genreIds":["manouche"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Tchavolo Schmitt","title":"Alors?... Voilà!","recorded":2000,"released":2000,"label":"Le Chant du Monde","catalogNo":"2741246","personnel":["Tchavolo Schmitt (g)","Marcel Loeffler (accordion)","Mandino Reinhardt (g)"],"whyThisOne":"Schmitt plays with the roughness and swing that the technically polished modern school has lost, and the accordion puts the musette half of the ancestry back in the room.","listenFor":"The accordion is not a novelty here — it is the sound of the Parisian bal-musette that jazz manouche grew out of alongside American swing.","startTrack":"Alors?... Voilà!","difficulty":2,"confidence":"low","note":"French independent release; catalogue number and exact recording year should be treated as provisional."},{"id":"romane-ombre","genreIds":["manouche"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Romane","title":"Ombre","recorded":1993,"released":1993,"label":"Iris Musique","catalogNo":"IRIS 3001","personnel":["Romane (g)","Mandino Reinhardt (g)","Michel Gaucher (ss)"],"whyThisOne":"A French guitarist writing original material in the idiom rather than replaying the Django book, which is rarer than it should be.","listenFor":"The harmony reaches for post-bop extensions that swing-era changes do not contain, while la pompe carries on underneath unchanged.","startTrack":"Ombre","difficulty":2,"confidence":"low","note":"Small French label; discographic details are sparsely documented and should be verified before citing."},{"id":"martin-taylor-spirit-of-django","genreIds":["manouche"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Martin Taylor","title":"Spirit of Django","recorded":1994,"released":1994,"label":"Linn","catalogNo":"AKD 030","personnel":["Martin Taylor (g)","Dave Kelbie (g)","John Goldie (g)","Terry Packer (b)","Jack Emblow (accordion)"],"whyThisOne":"A British guitarist who had played with Grappelli for eleven years making a Django record that does not imitate Django. The distinction between homage and copying is the whole subject.","listenFor":"Taylor plays chord-melody where Django played single lines, so the same repertoire comes out with a completely different centre of gravity.","startTrack":"Nuages","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"billie-holiday-quintessential-vol3","genreIds":["vocal-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Billie Holiday","title":"The Quintessential Billie Holiday, Volume 3 (1936–1937)","recorded":1936,"released":1987,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CK 44048","personnel":["Billie Holiday (voc)","Lester Young (ts)","Teddy Wilson (p)","Buck Clayton (tp)","Freddie Green (g)","Jo Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The Teddy Wilson small-group sides are the most perfect singer-and-band records in jazz. Holiday and Lester Young answer each other phrase by phrase, and the material is often disposable pop that they simply transform.","listenFor":"Follow the conversation between voice and tenor. Young plays around her line rather than under it, and neither of them ever raises their voice.","startTrack":"This Year's Kisses","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"ella-fitzgerald-cole-porter-songbook","genreIds":["vocal-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Ella Fitzgerald","title":"Ella Fitzgerald Sings the Cole Porter Song Book","recorded":1956,"released":1956,"label":"Verve","catalogNo":"MGV 4001-2","personnel":["Ella Fitzgerald (voc)","Buddy Bregman (arr, cond)"],"whyThisOne":"The first of the eight Songbook sets, which between them defined the Great American Songbook as a canon. Fitzgerald sings the melodies with unusual fidelity here, which was the point — Norman Granz wanted the songs documented.","listenFor":"How little she embellishes. The improvisation is in the time and the timbre, not in the notes, which is a harder discipline than scatting.","startTrack":"Ev'ry Time We Say Goodbye","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"sarah-vaughan-with-clifford-brown","genreIds":["vocal-jazz","hard-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Sarah Vaughan","title":"Sarah Vaughan","recorded":1954,"released":1955,"label":"EmArcy","catalogNo":"MG 36004","personnel":["Sarah Vaughan (voc)","Clifford Brown (tp)","Paul Quinichette (ts)","Herbie Mann (fl)","Jimmy Jones (p)","Joe Benjamin (b)","Roy Haynes (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Vaughan with a working bebop band rather than an orchestra, and Clifford Brown answering her line for line. It is the clearest recording of a singer improvising at an instrumentalist's level.","listenFor":"On 'Lullaby of Birdland' she reharmonises her own melody line in real time — moving to notes that imply different chords than the band is playing.","startTrack":"Lullaby of Birdland","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high","note":"Commonly known as 'Sarah Vaughan with Clifford Brown'; original EmArcy issue was titled simply 'Sarah Vaughan'."},{"id":"frank-sinatra-only-the-lonely","genreIds":["vocal-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Frank Sinatra","title":"Frank Sinatra Sings for Only the Lonely","recorded":1958,"released":1958,"label":"Capitol","catalogNo":"W 1053","personnel":["Frank Sinatra (voc)","Nelson Riddle (arr, cond)"],"whyThisOne":"Whether this is jazz is exactly the argument, and it is worth having in front of this record. Sinatra's phrasing came out of listening to Billie Holiday and Tommy Dorsey's trombone breathing, and Riddle's charts are jazz orchestration.","listenFor":"He stretches phrases across the bar line and delays consonants until the last possible moment. That is jazz phrasing regardless of what genre the record sits in.","startTrack":"Angel Eyes","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"betty-carter-audience-with","genreIds":["vocal-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Betty Carter","title":"The Audience with Betty Carter","recorded":1979,"released":1980,"label":"Bet-Car","catalogNo":"MK 1003","personnel":["Betty Carter (voc)","John Hicks (p)","Curtis Lundy (b)","Kenny Washington (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Carter took melodic abstraction further than any other singer and ran her own label to do it. Her 'Sounds (Movin' On)' runs past twenty minutes and the melody is never stated intact.","listenFor":"Tempos so slow the band almost stops, and so fast the words become pure sound. She is treating a standard the way Sonny Rollins treats one.","startTrack":"The Trolley Song","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"lambert-hendricks-ross-sing-basie","genreIds":["vocal-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Lambert, Hendricks & Ross","title":"Sing a Song of Basie","recorded":1957,"released":1958,"label":"ABC-Paramount","catalogNo":"ABC-223","personnel":["Dave Lambert (voc)","Jon Hendricks (voc, lyr)","Annie Ross (voc)","Nat Pierce (p)","Freddie Green (g)","Eddie Jones (b)","Sonny Payne (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Vocalese at its most audacious: three singers, multitracked, singing Jon Hendricks' lyrics to transcribed Basie arrangements including the solos. It made a whole big band out of voices.","listenFor":"Hendricks writes words to Lester Young's recorded solo on 'One O'Clock Jump' and Annie Ross sings it at pitch and tempo. The improvisation has become a text.","startTrack":"Everyday I Have the Blues","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"abbey-lincoln-straight-ahead","genreIds":["vocal-jazz","hard-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Abbey Lincoln","title":"Straight Ahead","recorded":1961,"released":1961,"label":"Candid","catalogNo":"CJM 8015","personnel":["Abbey Lincoln (voc)","Booker Little (tp)","Eric Dolphy (as, fl, bcl)","Coleman Hawkins (ts)","Mal Waldron (p)","Max Roach (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Lincoln singing with a band that includes Dolphy and Hawkins, on material that is explicitly political a year after We Insist! Freedom Now Suite. Nobody was making vocal jazz records like this.","listenFor":"Her diction is deliberately hard and unbeautiful in places. She is refusing the supper-club role that her earlier records had put her in.","startTrack":"Straight Ahead","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"cassandra-wilson-blue-light-til-dawn","genreIds":["vocal-jazz","m-base"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Cassandra Wilson","title":"Blue Light 'Til Dawn","recorded":1993,"released":1993,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"CDP 7 81357 2","personnel":["Cassandra Wilson (voc)","Brandon Ross (g)","Olu Dara (cor)","Charlie Burnham (vln)","Kenny Davis (b)","Lance Carter (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Wilson left the M-Base circle's angular funk and made a record of blues, Robert Johnson and Joni Mitchell songs with acoustic guitars and hand percussion. It changed what a jazz singer's repertoire could be.","listenFor":"Her voice sits very low and very far back in the beat, and the arrangements have no piano at all — the harmony is implied by guitars and left deliberately vague.","startTrack":"You Don't Know What Love Is","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"kurt-elling-close-your-eyes","genreIds":["vocal-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Kurt Elling","title":"Close Your Eyes","recorded":1994,"released":1995,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"CDP 7243 8 30645 2 6","personnel":["Kurt Elling (voc)","Laurence Hobgood (p)","Rob Amster (b)","Paul Wertico (d)","Eddie Johnson (ts)","Von Freeman (ts)"],"whyThisOne":"The strongest argument that vocalese and scat are still live techniques rather than period devices. Elling writes lyrics to instrumental solos and delivers them with a four-octave range and no irony.","listenFor":"His vocalese setting of Dexter Gordon's solo on 'Close Your Eyes' — the words are his, the notes are Gordon's, and the phrasing has to serve both.","startTrack":"Close Your Eyes","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"charlie-parker-savoy-dial","genreIds":["bebop"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Charlie Parker","title":"The Complete Savoy and Dial Studio Recordings","recorded":1944,"released":2000,"label":"Savoy Jazz","catalogNo":"92911-2","personnel":["Charlie Parker (as)","Dizzy Gillespie (tp)","Miles Davis (tp)","Bud Powell (p)","Max Roach (d)","Duke Jordan (p)","Tommy Potter (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The 1945–48 Savoy and Dial sides are bebop's primary text. They are short, most are three minutes, and the alternate takes let you hear a solo being reconsidered from scratch each time.","listenFor":"On 'Ko-Ko' the head is played at a tempo nobody had attempted, and Parker's first chorus lands on the flattened fifth of every chord as though it were consonant. In 1945 it was not.","startTrack":"Ko-Ko","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high","note":"Recorded 1944–48 for Savoy and Dial; multiple box-set configurations exist under different catalogue numbers."},{"id":"dizzy-gillespie-complete-rca-victor","genreIds":["bebop","afro-cuban"],"tier":"core","artist":"Dizzy Gillespie","title":"The Complete RCA Victor Recordings","recorded":1947,"released":1995,"label":"RCA Victor","catalogNo":"07863 66528-2","personnel":["Dizzy Gillespie (tp)","Chano Pozo (congas)","John Lewis (p)","Milt Jackson (vib)","Al McKibbon (b)","George Russell (arr)"],"whyThisOne":"Gillespie's big band, where bebop met both orchestral arranging and Afro-Cuban rhythm. 'Manteca' and 'Cubana Be, Cubana Bop' are on this and they start a whole branch of the music.","listenFor":"'Manteca' has an A section that is pure Cuban vamp and a bridge that is pure bebop. Gillespie later said Pozo wrote the vamp and could not write a bridge because it needed changes.","startTrack":"Manteca","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bud-powell-amazing-vol1","genreIds":["bebop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Bud Powell","title":"The Amazing Bud Powell, Volume 1","recorded":1949,"released":1952,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 1503","personnel":["Bud Powell (p)","Fats Navarro (tp)","Sonny Rollins (ts)","Tommy Potter (b)","Roy Haynes (d)","Curley Russell (b)","Max Roach (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Powell invented the modern jazz piano role here: right-hand horn lines over sparse left-hand chords, with the timekeeping handed to the bass and drums. Every pianist since works from this.","listenFor":"'Un Poco Loco' has Max Roach playing a cowbell figure that fights the piano's meter for the whole track. There are three released takes and the argument gets more extreme in each.","startTrack":"Un Poco Loco","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high","note":"Recorded August 1949 and May 1951; issued as a 12-inch LP in 1955 after earlier 10-inch releases."},{"id":"thelonious-monk-brilliant-corners","genreIds":["bebop","post-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Thelonious Monk","title":"Brilliant Corners","recorded":1956,"released":1957,"label":"Riverside","catalogNo":"RLP 12-226","personnel":["Thelonious Monk (p)","Sonny Rollins (ts)","Ernie Henry (as)","Oscar Pettiford (b)","Max Roach (d)","Clark Terry (tp)","Paul Chambers (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Monk's compositions are the strangest thing in the bebop era and this is the best-documented session of them. The title track was so difficult that the released version is spliced from multiple incomplete takes.","listenFor":"'Brilliant Corners' alternates between two tempos, doubling and halving every eight bars. The band audibly struggles, and that struggle is on the record.","startTrack":"Brilliant Corners","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"clifford-brown-max-roach-1954","genreIds":["bebop","hard-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Clifford Brown & Max Roach","title":"Clifford Brown and Max Roach","recorded":1954,"released":1955,"label":"EmArcy","catalogNo":"MG 36036","personnel":["Clifford Brown (tp)","Max Roach (d)","Harold Land (ts)","Richie Powell (p)","George Morrow (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The quintet that connects bebop to hard bop, with the most fluent trumpet playing anyone had heard. Brown died in a car crash in June 1956 at twenty-five.","listenFor":"Brown's tone never breaks, at any speed or register. Compare the strain audible in most bebop trumpet — this is why every trumpeter after him tried to sound like this.","startTrack":"Joy Spring","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"dexter-gordon-go","genreIds":["bebop","hard-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Dexter Gordon","title":"Go","recorded":1962,"released":1962,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4112","personnel":["Dexter Gordon (ts)","Sonny Clark (p)","Butch Warren (b)","Billy Higgins (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Gordon was the first tenor player to translate Parker's alto language to the bigger horn, and by 1962 he had had twenty years to relax into it. This is bebop played with total ease.","listenFor":"He plays a long way behind the beat and quotes constantly — nursery rhymes, other standards, opera. It is a conversational habit, not a trick.","startTrack":"Cheese Cake","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"charlie-parker-with-strings","genreIds":["bebop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Charlie Parker","title":"Charlie Parker with Strings","recorded":1949,"released":1950,"label":"Mercury","catalogNo":"MG C-101","personnel":["Charlie Parker (as)","Mitch Miller (oboe)","Jimmy Carroll (arr)","string section"],"whyThisOne":"Parker's own favourite of his records, and the one purists have always found embarrassing. The arrangements are lush and unadventurous; his playing inside them is not.","listenFor":"'Just Friends' — his entry after the string introduction is a single unbroken phrase that reorganises the whole harmonic frame around it.","startTrack":"Just Friends","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"fats-navarro-tadd-dameron-fabulous","genreIds":["bebop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Fats Navarro & Tadd Dameron","title":"The Complete Blue Note and Capitol Recordings","recorded":1947,"released":1995,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"CDP 7243 8 33373 2 4","personnel":["Fats Navarro (tp)","Tadd Dameron (p, arr)","Ernie Henry (as)","Charlie Rouse (ts)","Kenny Clarke (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Dameron was bebop's finest composer-arranger and Navarro its most lyrical trumpeter before Clifford Brown. Navarro died of tuberculosis at twenty-six in 1950.","listenFor":"Dameron writes voicings for three or four horns that sound like an orchestra. Bebop is usually thought of as unarranged; this is the counter-example.","startTrack":"Our Delight","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bud-powell-scene-changes","genreIds":["bebop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Bud Powell","title":"The Scene Changes","recorded":1958,"released":1959,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4009","personnel":["Bud Powell (p)","Paul Chambers (b)","Art Taylor (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Late Powell, after years of institutionalisation and electroshock treatment, still writing and still playing. 'Cleopatra's Dream' is one of his best compositions and comes from this period.","listenFor":"The technique is less reliable than in 1949 and the harmonic imagination is not. Hearing what he still does with a limited physical instrument is the point.","startTrack":"Cleopatra's Dream","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"birth-of-the-cool","genreIds":["cool-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Miles Davis","title":"Birth of the Cool","recorded":1949,"released":1957,"label":"Capitol","catalogNo":"T 762","personnel":["Miles Davis (tp)","Gerry Mulligan (bs, arr)","Lee Konitz (as)","J.J. Johnson (tb)","Gunther Schuller (frh)","Bill Barber (tuba)","Gil Evans (arr)","John Lewis (arr)","Max Roach (d)","Kenny Clarke (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Nine players including French horn and tuba, arranged as chamber music, recorded across three sessions in 1949–50. The sides sold poorly and then reorganised a decade of jazz.","listenFor":"'Boplicity' — the ensemble breathes as one instrument, and the voicings move in ways that no big band section writing had attempted. Gil Evans arranged it.","startTrack":"Boplicity","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high","note":"Recorded January 1949, April 1949 and March 1950; collected as a Capitol LP under this title in 1957."},{"id":"lennie-tristano-crosscurrents","genreIds":["cool-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Lennie Tristano","title":"Crosscurrents","recorded":1949,"released":1972,"label":"Capitol","catalogNo":"M-11060","personnel":["Lennie Tristano (p)","Lee Konitz (as)","Warne Marsh (ts)","Billy Bauer (g)","Arnold Fishkin (b)","Denzil Best (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The 1949 Capitol sessions include 'Intuition' and 'Digression' — completely free improvisations with no theme, key or tempo agreed in advance, recorded ten years before Ornette Coleman.","listenFor":"On the composed tracks, listen to how evenly everything is phrased. Tristano trained his students to eliminate rhythmic emphasis entirely, so the line carries all the interest.","startTrack":"Intuition","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"lee-konitz-subconscious-lee","genreIds":["cool-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Lee Konitz","title":"Subconscious-Lee","recorded":1949,"released":1955,"label":"Prestige","catalogNo":"PRLP 7004","personnel":["Lee Konitz (as)","Lennie Tristano (p)","Warne Marsh (ts)","Billy Bauer (g)","Arnold Fishkin (b)","Shelly Manne (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The one major altoist of his generation who did not sound like Charlie Parker. Konitz's tone is pale and dry and his lines avoid every bebop cliché deliberately.","listenFor":"'Subconscious-Lee' is built on the changes of 'What Is This Thing Called Love' but the melody line does not once land where a bebop head would.","startTrack":"Subconscious-Lee","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"modern-jazz-quartet-django","genreIds":["cool-jazz","third-stream"],"tier":"core","artist":"Modern Jazz Quartet","title":"Django","recorded":1953,"released":1956,"label":"Prestige","catalogNo":"PRLP 7057","personnel":["John Lewis (p)","Milt Jackson (vib)","Percy Heath (b)","Kenny Clarke (d)","Connie Kay (d)"],"whyThisOne":"John Lewis's fugal writing against Milt Jackson's completely unreconstructed blues vibraphone. The tension between those two impulses is what the MJQ is, for forty years.","listenFor":"The title track, written for Django Reinhardt, has a stately composed theme and then Jackson solos over it as though it were a blues. Neither gives way.","startTrack":"Django","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"miles-davis-gil-evans-miles-ahead","genreIds":["cool-jazz","third-stream"],"tier":"core","artist":"Miles Davis + 19","title":"Miles Ahead","recorded":1957,"released":1957,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CL 1041","personnel":["Miles Davis (flugelhorn)","Gil Evans (arr, cond)","Lee Konitz (as)","Bill Barber (tuba)","Paul Chambers (b)","Art Taylor (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The nonet idea scaled to nineteen players, with Davis on flugelhorn as the only soloist across a continuous suite. Evans' writing here is the most sophisticated orchestration in jazz to that date.","listenFor":"There is no saxophone section in the conventional sense. Evans built the ensemble around flutes, French horns, tuba and bass clarinet, so the colour is closer to Ravel's orchestra than to a big band.","startTrack":"The Maids of Cadiz","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"stan-getz-west-coast-jazz","genreIds":["cool-jazz","west-coast"],"tier":"core","artist":"Stan Getz","title":"West Coast Jazz","recorded":1955,"released":1956,"label":"Norgran","catalogNo":"MGN 1032","personnel":["Stan Getz (ts)","Conte Candoli (tp)","Lou Levy (p)","Leroy Vinnegar (b)","Shelly Manne (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Getz's tone descends directly from Lester Young and is the purest example of what 'cool' means as a sound. The band is Los Angeles' best rhythm section of the period.","listenFor":"There is almost no vibrato and almost no attack — notes appear rather than start. Then listen to how hard he actually swings inside that soft surface.","startTrack":"Shine","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jimmy-giuffre-fusion-1961","genreIds":["cool-jazz","west-coast","euro-free-improv"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Jimmy Giuffre 3","title":"Fusion","recorded":1961,"released":1961,"label":"Verve","catalogNo":"V6-8397","personnel":["Jimmy Giuffre (cl)","Paul Bley (p)","Steve Swallow (b)"],"whyThisOne":"No drums, almost no volume, and a freedom of pulse that arrives before European free improvisation existed. It sold nothing and Giuffre's career did not recover for years.","listenFor":"The trio breathes together with no timekeeper at all. When the pulse disappears, nothing collapses — they are listening at a level that makes a drummer unnecessary.","startTrack":"Jesus Maria","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"warne-marsh-jazz-of-two-cities","genreIds":["cool-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Warne Marsh","title":"Jazz of Two Cities","recorded":1956,"released":1957,"label":"Imperial","catalogNo":"LP 9027","personnel":["Warne Marsh (ts)","Ted Brown (ts)","Ronnie Ball (p)","Ben Tucker (b)","Jeff Morton (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Marsh was Tristano's most rigorous student and possibly the least commercially concerned musician in jazz history. The lines here are long, unaccented and harmonically ruthless.","listenFor":"Two tenors improvising counterpoint simultaneously, without either taking the lead. It is the Tristano school's ideal and almost nobody else attempted it.","startTrack":"Jazz of Two Cities","difficulty":4,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"gil-evans-out-of-the-cool","genreIds":["cool-jazz","modal"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Gil Evans Orchestra","title":"Out of the Cool","recorded":1960,"released":1961,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"A-4","personnel":["Gil Evans (p, arr)","Johnny Coles (tp)","Jimmy Knepper (tb)","Budd Johnson (ts)","Ron Carter (b)","Elvin Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Evans without Miles Davis, writing for his own band and leaving far more room for improvisation. 'La Nevada' is fifteen minutes built from almost nothing — a vamp and some cues.","listenFor":"'La Nevada' has no real composition to speak of. Evans shapes it in real time by bringing sections in and out over a static harmony, which is arranging as live improvisation.","startTrack":"La Nevada","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"gerry-mulligan-quartet-chet-baker","genreIds":["west-coast"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Gerry Mulligan Quartet","title":"The Original Gerry Mulligan Quartet with Chet Baker","recorded":1952,"released":1953,"label":"Pacific Jazz","catalogNo":"PJLP-1","personnel":["Gerry Mulligan (bs)","Chet Baker (tp)","Bob Whitlock (b)","Chico Hamilton (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The piano-less quartet, which forced two horns to imply the harmony between them through counterpoint. 'My Funny Valentine' from these sessions is one of the most-copied records in jazz.","listenFor":"Nobody is playing chords. Baker and Mulligan weave two independent lines and your ear assembles the harmony from the intervals between them.","startTrack":"My Funny Valentine","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high","note":"The 1952–53 Pacific Jazz quartet sides appear in many configurations; the original 10-inch LP is PJLP-1."},{"id":"shorty-rogers-giants","genreIds":["west-coast"],"tier":"core","artist":"Shorty Rogers and His Giants","title":"Shorty Rogers and His Giants","recorded":1953,"released":1953,"label":"RCA Victor","catalogNo":"LPM 3137 (10-inch); LPM 1195 (12-inch, 1956)","personnel":["Shorty Rogers (tp, arr)","Art Pepper (as)","Jimmy Giuffre (ts)","Shelly Manne (d)","Hampton Hawes (p)"],"whyThisOne":"Rogers wrote the arrangements that defined the LA small-band sound: tight, contrapuntal, and playable by people who could read anything at sight.","listenFor":"The horn writing is chamber-scaled but the swing is genuine, which is the West Coast claim in one record.","startTrack":"Popo","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high","note":"Issued as a 10-inch LP in 1953 (LPM 3137) and expanded to 12-inch with four extra tracks in 1956 (LPM 1195); the 1954 session dates belong to the added material."},{"id":"art-pepper-meets-rhythm-section","genreIds":["west-coast"],"tier":"core","artist":"Art Pepper","title":"Art Pepper Meets the Rhythm Section","recorded":1957,"released":1957,"label":"Contemporary","catalogNo":"C 3532","personnel":["Art Pepper (as)","Red Garland (p)","Paul Chambers (b)","Philly Joe Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Pepper was reportedly given no warning and had not played in weeks when Miles Davis's rhythm section arrived at his house. The record is a West Coast altoist stress-tested by the hardest-swinging East Coast trio available.","listenFor":"He is playing catch-up for the first minute and then locks in. The lightness of his sound against Philly Joe Jones's drive is the whole East–West argument in one band.","startTrack":"You'd Be So Nice to Come Home To","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"shelly-manne-my-fair-lady","genreIds":["west-coast"],"tier":"core","artist":"Shelly Manne & His Friends","title":"My Fair Lady","recorded":1956,"released":1956,"label":"Contemporary","catalogNo":"C 3527","personnel":["Shelly Manne (d)","André Previn (p)","Leroy Vinnegar (b)"],"whyThisOne":"A jazz trio playing a Broadway score, which became one of the best-selling jazz records of the 1950s and invented the show-tune concept album.","listenFor":"Manne plays almost entirely with brushes and rims. He is a colourist here rather than a timekeeper, which is the West Coast drummer's characteristic move.","startTrack":"I Could Have Danced All Night","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"chico-hamilton-quintet-1955","genreIds":["west-coast"],"tier":"core","artist":"Chico Hamilton Quintet","title":"The Chico Hamilton Quintet featuring Buddy Collette","recorded":1955,"released":1955,"label":"Pacific Jazz","catalogNo":"PJ-1209","personnel":["Chico Hamilton (d)","Buddy Collette (fl, cl, as, ts)","Fred Katz (cello)","Jim Hall (g)","Carson Smith (b)"],"whyThisOne":"A jazz group with a cello and no piano, playing what amounts to chamber music with a rhythm section. Jim Hall's first significant recordings are here.","listenFor":"The cello is a full member, playing arco lines and pizzicato counterpoint. In 1955 nothing else in jazz sounded like this.","startTrack":"The Sage","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"dave-brubeck-time-out","genreIds":["west-coast","cool-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"The Dave Brubeck Quartet","title":"Time Out","recorded":1959,"released":1959,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CL 1397","personnel":["Dave Brubeck (p)","Paul Desmond (as)","Eugene Wright (b)","Joe Morello (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The first jazz album to sell a million copies, built entirely on time signatures other than 4/4. Its commercial success is the reason odd meters entered jazz's ordinary vocabulary.","listenFor":"'Blue Rondo à la Turk' is in 9/8 grouped 2+2+2+3, then drops into a straight 4/4 blues for the solos and back. Count the introduction and you will feel the asymmetry.","startTrack":"Take Five","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"curtis-counce-you-get-more-bounce","genreIds":["west-coast","hard-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Curtis Counce","title":"You Get More Bounce with Curtis Counce!","recorded":1957,"released":1957,"label":"Contemporary","catalogNo":"C 3539","personnel":["Curtis Counce (b)","Jack Sheldon (tp)","Harold Land (ts)","Carl Perkins (p)","Frank Butler (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The corrective to the idea that West Coast jazz was white and polite. Counce's group was Black, Los Angeles-based and played harder than most New York bands of the period.","listenFor":"Frank Butler's drumming is aggressive and busy in a way no West Coast stereotype accommodates. This is what the LA scene actually contained.","startTrack":"Sonar","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"hampton-hawes-trio-vol1","genreIds":["west-coast","hard-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Hampton Hawes Trio","title":"The Trio, Vol. 1","recorded":1955,"released":1956,"label":"Contemporary","catalogNo":"C 3505","personnel":["Hampton Hawes (p)","Red Mitchell (b)","Chuck Thompson (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Hawes was a Los Angeles pianist with a blues attack as hard as anyone in New York, and this trio session was cut in a single night.","listenFor":"The left hand punches rather than comps, and the blues content is constant. Nothing about this fits the cool West Coast description.","startTrack":"I Got Rhythm","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bud-shank-bob-cooper-blowin-country","genreIds":["west-coast"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Bud Shank & Bob Cooper","title":"Blowin' Country","recorded":1956,"released":1958,"label":"Pacific Jazz","catalogNo":"PJ-1226","personnel":["Bud Shank (as, fl)","Bob Cooper (ts, oboe)","Claude Williamson (p)","Don Prell (b)","Chuck Flores (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Flute and oboe as jazz lead instruments, played by studio musicians who could actually handle them. It is the most literal expression of the West Coast scene's double-life economics.","listenFor":"The oboe is genuinely being improvised on, not merely featured. It is an instrument with almost no jazz literature and Cooper builds one here.","startTrack":"Blowin' Country","difficulty":2,"confidence":"low","note":"Pacific Jazz reissued and recombined these Shank–Cooper sessions repeatedly; title and catalogue attribution should be verified."},{"id":"moanin-1959","genreIds":["hard-bop"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Art Blakey & The Jazz Messengers","title":"Moanin'","recorded":1958,"released":1959,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4003","personnel":["Lee Morgan (tp)","Benny Golson (ts)","Bobby Timmons (p)","Jymie Merritt (b)","Art Blakey (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The record that fixed what hard bop sounded like: a gospel head everyone can sing, Golson's arranging, Morgan at twenty playing like he has nothing to lose, and Blakey underneath refusing to be background.","listenFor":"The opening: Timmons plays a churchy piano phrase and the horns answer 'amen'. That call-and-response is lifted from Black church practice and dropped whole into jazz.","startTrack":"Moanin'","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"horace-silver-art-blakey-jazz-messengers-1955","genreIds":["hard-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Horace Silver and the Jazz Messengers","title":"Horace Silver and the Jazz Messengers","recorded":1954,"released":1956,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 1518","personnel":["Horace Silver (p)","Kenny Dorham (tp)","Hank Mobley (ts)","Doug Watkins (b)","Art Blakey (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The band that became the Jazz Messengers, with Silver's tunes — 'The Preacher', 'Doodlin'' — that made gospel and blues content the point rather than a garnish.","listenFor":"'The Preacher' is built on a sixteen-bar structure closer to a hymn than to a jazz form, and Blue Note's Alfred Lion initially thought it was too old-fashioned to release.","startTrack":"The Preacher","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"cannonball-adderley-somethin-else","genreIds":["hard-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Cannonball Adderley","title":"Somethin' Else","recorded":1958,"released":1958,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 1595","personnel":["Cannonball Adderley (as)","Miles Davis (tp)","Hank Jones (p)","Sam Jones (b)","Art Blakey (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Adderley's date with Davis as sideman, a year before Kind of Blue. The version of 'Autumn Leaves' here is the one everyone knows, and it is a masterclass in setting a mood in eight bars.","listenFor":"Davis's muted entry on 'Autumn Leaves' after the vamp — three notes, almost nothing, and the whole temperature of the track is set.","startTrack":"Autumn Leaves","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"sonny-rollins-saxophone-colossus","genreIds":["hard-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Sonny Rollins","title":"Saxophone Colossus","recorded":1956,"released":1956,"label":"Prestige","catalogNo":"PRLP 7079","personnel":["Sonny Rollins (ts)","Tommy Flanagan (p)","Doug Watkins (b)","Max Roach (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Rollins builds solos by developing a motif across whole choruses rather than running changes, and 'Blue 7' is the record where that method is most exposed. Gunther Schuller wrote an analysis of it that changed how jazz criticism worked.","listenFor":"On 'Blue 7' he takes a three-note fragment and keeps turning it over for chorus after chorus. Almost nothing new is introduced and it never stops developing.","startTrack":"St. Thomas","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"john-coltrane-blue-train","genreIds":["hard-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"John Coltrane","title":"Blue Train","recorded":1957,"released":1958,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 1577","personnel":["John Coltrane (ts)","Lee Morgan (tp)","Curtis Fuller (tb)","Kenny Drew (p)","Paul Chambers (b)","Philly Joe Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Coltrane's only session as leader for Blue Note, made in the year he got clean, with a sextet and his own writing. It is the last record where he sounds unambiguously like a hard bop tenor player.","listenFor":"The three-horn voicing on the title theme is dark and thick — trombone underneath rather than a second saxophone, which is why the minor blues sounds so heavy.","startTrack":"Blue Train","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"lee-morgan-sidewinder","genreIds":["hard-bop","soul-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Lee Morgan","title":"The Sidewinder","recorded":1963,"released":1964,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4157","personnel":["Lee Morgan (tp)","Joe Henderson (ts)","Barry Harris (p)","Bob Cranshaw (b)","Billy Higgins (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Blue Note's biggest hit, a boogaloo built on a bass riff that crossed onto radio and briefly rescued the label's finances. It also pushed Blue Note toward chasing a groove hit on every subsequent date.","listenFor":"The 24-bar structure is unusual and the whole thing sits on one insistent bass figure. Morgan's solo builds by repeating a single phrase up in pitch until it breaks.","startTrack":"The Sidewinder","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"hank-mobley-soul-station","genreIds":["hard-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Hank Mobley","title":"Soul Station","recorded":1960,"released":1960,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4031","personnel":["Hank Mobley (ts)","Wynton Kelly (p)","Paul Chambers (b)","Art Blakey (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Mobley was called the middleweight champion of the tenor saxophone, meaning he was neither Coltrane nor Rollins, which was intended as faint praise and reads now as a description of perfect proportion.","listenFor":"He never overplays and he never coasts. The tone is round and slightly withheld, and every phrase resolves where you want it to about a beat later than you expect.","startTrack":"This I Dig of You","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"art-blakey-free-for-all","genreIds":["hard-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Art Blakey & The Jazz Messengers","title":"Free for All","recorded":1964,"released":1965,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4170","personnel":["Freddie Hubbard (tp)","Wayne Shorter (ts)","Curtis Fuller (tb)","Cedar Walton (p)","Reggie Workman (b)","Art Blakey (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The most violent record the Messengers made. Shorter was writing and directing by then and Blakey plays the title track as if trying to destroy the drum kit.","listenFor":"The title track's tempo and volume never let up for eleven minutes. This is hard bop pushed to the edge of where free jazz starts.","startTrack":"Free for All","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"clifford-jordan-glass-bead-games","genreIds":["hard-bop","spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Clifford Jordan","title":"Glass Bead Games","recorded":1973,"released":1974,"label":"Strata-East","catalogNo":"SES-19737/8","personnel":["Clifford Jordan (ts)","Stanley Cowell (p)","Cedar Walton (p)","Bill Lee (b)","Sam Jones (b)","Billy Higgins (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Hard bop still developing in 1973, on a musician-owned label, with a spiritual jazz undertow. It is proof that the style did not stop when the critics moved on.","listenFor":"The playing is straight hard bop but the album's shape and the modal pieces put it in a different world from a 1959 Blue Note date.","startTrack":"Powerful Paul Robeson","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jimmy-smith-back-at-the-chicken-shack","genreIds":["soul-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Jimmy Smith","title":"Back at the Chicken Shack","recorded":1960,"released":1963,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4117","personnel":["Jimmy Smith (org)","Stanley Turrentine (ts)","Kenny Burrell (g)","Donald Bailey (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The organ trio plus tenor, at its most relaxed. Nothing is being proved and the groove never stops, which is precisely the format's proposition.","listenFor":"There is no bass player. Smith is playing the bass line with his left hand and the pedals while soloing with his right — listen for the walk continuing underneath the solo.","startTrack":"Back at the Chicken Shack","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high","note":"Recorded April 1960 in the same session block as 'Midnight Special'; Blue Note held it until 1963."},{"id":"jimmy-smith-the-sermon","genreIds":["soul-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Jimmy Smith","title":"The Sermon!","recorded":1957,"released":1959,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4011","personnel":["Jimmy Smith (org)","Lee Morgan (tp)","Kenny Burrell (g)","Tina Brooks (ts)","Art Blakey (d)","George Coleman (as)"],"whyThisOne":"The title track runs past twenty minutes over a slow blues and simply does not need to end. It is the organ jam session as a form.","listenFor":"Each soloist enters and takes as long as they want. The structure is entirely determined by how the room is responding.","startTrack":"The Sermon","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high","note":"Recorded February 1958 and August 1957 across two dates; released 1959."},{"id":"grant-green-idle-moments","genreIds":["soul-jazz","hard-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Grant Green","title":"Idle Moments","recorded":1963,"released":1965,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4154","personnel":["Grant Green (g)","Joe Henderson (ts)","Bobby Hutcherson (vib)","Duke Pearson (p)","Bob Cranshaw (b)","Al Harewood (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The title track was supposed to run seven minutes and the band misread the form, so it runs fifteen. Nobody stopped them and it is the best thing on the record.","listenFor":"Green plays single notes almost exclusively — no chords, no doubling — with a rhythmic placement that comes from horn players rather than guitarists.","startTrack":"Idle Moments","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jack-mcduff-live","genreIds":["soul-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Brother Jack McDuff","title":"Live!","recorded":1963,"released":1963,"label":"Prestige","catalogNo":"PRLP 7274","personnel":["Jack McDuff (org)","George Benson (g)","Red Holloway (ts)","Joe Dukes (d)"],"whyThisOne":"George Benson at nineteen, in a working organ band, recorded in a club with the audience audible. This is the format in its native habitat rather than in a studio.","listenFor":"Joe Dukes' drumming is closer to R&B than to jazz, and the crowd responds to the groove rather than to the solos.","startTrack":"Rock Candy","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"stanley-turrentine-sugar","genreIds":["soul-jazz","jazz-funk"],"tier":"core","artist":"Stanley Turrentine","title":"Sugar","recorded":1970,"released":1971,"label":"CTI","catalogNo":"CTI 6005","personnel":["Stanley Turrentine (ts)","Freddie Hubbard (tp)","George Benson (g)","Butch Cornell (org)","Ron Carter (b)","Billy Kaye (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Soul jazz crossing into the 1970s and into CTI's glossier production, with the groove intact and the solos still substantial. The title track became a standard.","listenFor":"Turrentine's tone is enormous and never strained — the sound of a saxophonist who came up playing behind R&B singers.","startTrack":"Sugar","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"ramsey-lewis-the-in-crowd","genreIds":["soul-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Ramsey Lewis Trio","title":"The In Crowd","recorded":1965,"released":1965,"label":"Argo","catalogNo":"LP-757","personnel":["Ramsey Lewis (p)","Eldee Young (b)","Redd Holt (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A piano trio taking a soul record into the pop top five by playing it as a groove with an audience clapping along. The commercial success reset what a jazz trio could aim at.","listenFor":"The crowd is not incidental — they are part of the arrangement, and the trio plays to them rather than past them.","startTrack":"The 'In' Crowd","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"big-john-patton-lets-roll","genreIds":["soul-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Big John Patton","title":"Let 'Em Roll","recorded":1965,"released":1966,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4239","personnel":["John Patton (org)","Bobby Hutcherson (vib)","Grant Green (g)","Otis Finch (d)"],"whyThisOne":"An organ record with vibraphone instead of a horn, which changes everything — the texture goes cool and floating while the groove stays hard.","listenFor":"Hutcherson brings post-bop harmonic ambiguity into a soul jazz date. The two languages sit together uneasily and productively.","startTrack":"The Turnaround","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"larry-young-unity","genreIds":["soul-jazz","post-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Larry Young","title":"Unity","recorded":1965,"released":1966,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4221","personnel":["Larry Young (org)","Woody Shaw (tp)","Joe Henderson (ts)","Elvin Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Young played the Hammond organ as if it were John Coltrane's piano — modal, harmonically searching, and entirely uninterested in the blues shuffle the instrument was hired for.","listenFor":"The organ voicings are quartal and open rather than bluesy, and Elvin Jones is playing his Coltrane-quartet triplet feel. This is soul jazz's format with modal jazz's content.","startTrack":"Zoltan","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"shirley-scott-queen-of-the-organ","genreIds":["soul-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Shirley Scott","title":"Queen of the Organ","recorded":1964,"released":1965,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"A-81","personnel":["Shirley Scott (org)","Stanley Turrentine (ts)","Bob Cranshaw (b)","Otis Finch (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Scott was the major organist the histories keep leaving out, with a lighter touch than Jimmy Smith and a stronger harmonic sense. Recorded live at Birdland with Turrentine, then her husband.","listenFor":"Her registrations are brighter and thinner than Smith's, so the lines come through with more clarity and less growl.","startTrack":"The Blues Ain't Nothin' but Some Pain","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"kind-of-blue","genreIds":["modal"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Miles Davis","title":"Kind of Blue","recorded":1959,"released":1959,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CL 1355","personnel":["Miles Davis (tp)","John Coltrane (ts)","Cannonball Adderley (as)","Bill Evans (p)","Wynton Kelly (p)","Paul Chambers (b)","Jimmy Cobb (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The best-selling jazz album ever made, and it earns it. Davis brought sketches rather than charts to two sessions and the band read them nearly cold, which is why nothing sounds worked over.","listenFor":"'So What' gives the soloist two chords in thirty-two bars. Listen to how Coltrane and Adderley respond completely differently to the same emptiness — one fills it, one plays around it.","startTrack":"So What","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"miles-davis-milestones","genreIds":["modal","hard-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Miles Davis","title":"Milestones","recorded":1958,"released":1958,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CL 1193","personnel":["Miles Davis (tp)","John Coltrane (ts)","Cannonball Adderley (as)","Red Garland (p)","Paul Chambers (b)","Philly Joe Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The title track is the first fully modal piece Davis recorded, a year before Kind of Blue. The rest of the album is hard bop, so you can hear the two languages side by side.","listenFor":"'Milestones' alternates between two modes with no chord changes inside either. Then play 'Dr. Jackle' from the same album and hear how much harder the changes are moving.","startTrack":"Milestones","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"john-coltrane-impressions","genreIds":["modal"],"tier":"core","artist":"John Coltrane","title":"Impressions","recorded":1961,"released":1963,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"A-42","personnel":["John Coltrane (ts, ss)","Eric Dolphy (as, bcl)","McCoy Tyner (p)","Jimmy Garrison (b)","Elvin Jones (d)","Reggie Workman (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The title track, recorded live at the Village Vanguard, is 'So What' taken at speed and stretched to fourteen minutes. It is modal jazz turned into an endurance event.","listenFor":"Tyner's left hand plays almost nothing but stacked fourths, and Elvin Jones stops marking bars entirely — the pulse is a wave rather than a grid.","startTrack":"Impressions","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bill-evans-sunday-at-village-vanguard","genreIds":["modal","post-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Bill Evans Trio","title":"Sunday at the Village Vanguard","recorded":1961,"released":1961,"label":"Riverside","catalogNo":"RLP 9376","personnel":["Bill Evans (p)","Scott LaFaro (b)","Paul Motian (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The trio that made the bass a melodic equal rather than a timekeeper. Scott LaFaro died in a car crash ten days after this recording, at twenty-five.","listenFor":"LaFaro is not walking. He plays counter-melodies in the upper register while Motian keeps time with brushes and Evans leaves gaps for both of them.","startTrack":"Gloria's Step","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"mccoy-tyner-the-real-mccoy","genreIds":["modal","post-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"McCoy Tyner","title":"The Real McCoy","recorded":1967,"released":1967,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BST 84264","personnel":["McCoy Tyner (p)","Joe Henderson (ts)","Ron Carter (b)","Elvin Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Tyner's first record after leaving Coltrane, with Elvin Jones alongside him. It codifies the modal piano language — quartal voicings, pentatonic right hand, pedal points — that became the default.","listenFor":"'Passion Dance' sits on one chord for its entire solo section. The interest is generated entirely by rhythm, register and Tyner's left-hand attack.","startTrack":"Passion Dance","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"herbie-hancock-maiden-voyage","genreIds":["modal","post-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Herbie Hancock","title":"Maiden Voyage","recorded":1965,"released":1965,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4195","personnel":["Herbie Hancock (p)","Freddie Hubbard (tp)","George Coleman (ts)","Ron Carter (b)","Tony Williams (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Modal harmony that floats rather than sits still. The suspended chords never resolve and the whole record has a weightlessness nothing else in 1965 achieved.","listenFor":"The title track uses four suspended chords that each last four bars and none of which function as a tonic. Your ear keeps waiting for a resolution that is never coming.","startTrack":"Maiden Voyage","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"george-russell-jazz-workshop","genreIds":["modal","third-stream"],"tier":"deep","artist":"George Russell","title":"The Jazz Workshop","recorded":1956,"released":1957,"label":"RCA Victor","catalogNo":"LPM-1372","personnel":["George Russell (comp, arr)","Art Farmer (tp)","Hal McKusick (as)","Bill Evans (p)","Barry Galbraith (g)","Milt Hinton (b)","Joe Harris (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Russell's theory in practice three years before Kind of Blue, with Bill Evans in the band. If you want the intellectual source of modal jazz rather than its popular expression, this is it.","listenFor":"'Concerto for Billy the Kid' gives Evans a solo section built on Russell's Lydian scale relationships rather than on functional changes.","startTrack":"Concerto for Billy the Kid","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"john-coltrane-my-favorite-things","genreIds":["modal"],"tier":"deep","artist":"John Coltrane","title":"My Favorite Things","recorded":1960,"released":1961,"label":"Atlantic","catalogNo":"SD 1361","personnel":["John Coltrane (ss, ts)","McCoy Tyner (p)","Steve Davis (b)","Elvin Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A Broadway waltz turned into a thirteen-minute modal vamp, and the record that made the soprano saxophone a jazz instrument again after Bechet.","listenFor":"The tune alternates between E minor and E major and Coltrane simply stays in each for as long as he wants. The song's form has become a switch, not a structure.","startTrack":"My Favorite Things","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"wayne-shorter-juju","genreIds":["modal","post-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Wayne Shorter","title":"JuJu","recorded":1964,"released":1965,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4182","personnel":["Wayne Shorter (ts)","McCoy Tyner (p)","Reggie Workman (b)","Elvin Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Shorter with three-quarters of the Coltrane quartet, writing modal pieces with a darker and more elliptical cast than Coltrane's own.","listenFor":"Shorter phrases in short, broken fragments where Coltrane would run continuous lines over the same material. Same band, opposite instinct.","startTrack":"JuJu","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"modern-jazz-quartet-third-stream-music","genreIds":["third-stream"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Modern Jazz Quartet","title":"Third Stream Music","recorded":1957,"released":1960,"label":"Atlantic","catalogNo":"1345","personnel":["John Lewis (p)","Milt Jackson (vib)","Percy Heath (b)","Connie Kay (d)","Jimmy Giuffre (cl)","Beaux Arts String Quartet"],"whyThisOne":"The record that carries the name, with the MJQ playing alongside a string quartet and Giuffre's trio. It is the most accessible entry to an idea that usually arrives as a lecture.","listenFor":"When the string quartet plays, it plays classical music; when the MJQ plays, it swings. Listening for the seam is the most useful thing you can do with third stream.","startTrack":"Sketch for Double String Quartet","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"charles-mingus-black-saint","genreIds":["third-stream","post-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Charles Mingus","title":"The Black Saint and the Sinner Lady","recorded":1963,"released":1963,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"A-35","personnel":["Charles Mingus (b, p, comp)","Charlie Mariano (as)","Jaki Byard (p)","Quentin Jackson (tb)","Dannie Richmond (d)","Bob Hammer (arr)"],"whyThisOne":"An eleven-piece band playing a continuous six-part ballet score with flamenco guitar, gospel shouting and heavy tape editing. Mingus rejected the third stream label and made its most convincing record anyway.","listenFor":"The whole thing was assembled with overdubs and splices, which was near-heretical for jazz in 1963. Mingus treated the tape as a compositional surface.","startTrack":"Track A – Solo Dancer","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"gunther-schuller-jazz-abstractions","genreIds":["third-stream","free-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Gunther Schuller / John Lewis","title":"Jazz Abstractions","recorded":1960,"released":1961,"label":"Atlantic","catalogNo":"1365","personnel":["Ornette Coleman (as)","Eric Dolphy (as, bcl)","Jim Hall (g)","Bill Evans (p)","Scott LaFaro (b)","Gunther Schuller (comp, cond)"],"whyThisOne":"Schuller writing twelve-tone frameworks for Ornette Coleman and Eric Dolphy to improvise inside. It is the one third stream record where the improvisers are more radical than the composition.","listenFor":"'Abstraction' puts Coleman in front of a string quartet playing a serial texture. He plays exactly as he would with his own group and the collision is the piece.","startTrack":"Abstraction","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"stan-getz-focus","genreIds":["third-stream"],"tier":"core","artist":"Stan Getz","title":"Focus","recorded":1961,"released":1961,"label":"Verve","catalogNo":"V6-8412","personnel":["Stan Getz (ts)","Eddie Sauter (comp, arr)","string orchestra","Roy Haynes (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Eddie Sauter wrote a seven-movement work for string orchestra and left no written part for Getz at all — he improvises across the whole thing. Getz called it his favourite of his own records.","listenFor":"The strings are not accompanying. They have their own complete piece, and Getz is a second, unscripted layer on top of it.","startTrack":"I'm Late, I'm Late","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"george-russell-jazz-in-the-space-age","genreIds":["third-stream","modal"],"tier":"core","artist":"George Russell","title":"Jazz in the Space Age","recorded":1960,"released":1960,"label":"Decca","catalogNo":"DL 9219","personnel":["George Russell (comp, cond)","Bill Evans (p)","Paul Bley (p)","Hal McKusick (as)","Milt Hinton (b)","Don Lamond (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Two pianists improvising simultaneously inside a large-ensemble score built on Russell's Lydian theory. It is ambitious in a way that only a theorist with a band would attempt.","listenFor":"Evans and Bley improvise against each other with no agreed harmony beyond the scale relationships Russell specified. It works far more often than it should.","startTrack":"Chromatic Universe – Part I","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jimmy-giuffre-free-fall","genreIds":["third-stream","euro-free-improv"],"tier":"core","artist":"Jimmy Giuffre 3","title":"Free Fall","recorded":1962,"released":1963,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CL 1964","personnel":["Jimmy Giuffre (cl)","Paul Bley (p)","Steve Swallow (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Solo clarinet pieces alongside trio improvisations at almost inaudible volume. Columbia dropped him afterwards and the trio broke up because they were earning nothing.","listenFor":"The dynamic range is extreme and downward — much of this is quieter than a conversation. Turn it up and the detail is enormous.","startTrack":"Propulsion","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"charles-mingus-ah-um","genreIds":["third-stream","hard-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Charles Mingus","title":"Mingus Ah Um","recorded":1959,"released":1959,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CL 1370","personnel":["Charles Mingus (b, comp)","John Handy (as)","Booker Ervin (ts)","Shafi Hadi (ts)","Jimmy Knepper (tb)","Horace Parlan (p)","Dannie Richmond (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Mingus at his most direct: gospel, blues, Ellington homage and an explicitly political protest song, all played by a band he conducted by shouting at them mid-take.","listenFor":"'Fables of Faubus' is about the governor who blocked school integration in Arkansas. Columbia refused to let the lyrics be sung, so the sarcasm has to come through the horns.","startTrack":"Goodbye Pork Pie Hat","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"eddie-sauter-bill-finegan","genreIds":["third-stream","swing"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Sauter-Finegan Orchestra","title":"New Directions in Music","recorded":1952,"released":1953,"label":"RCA Victor","catalogNo":"LPM-3115","personnel":["Eddie Sauter (comp, arr)","Bill Finegan (comp, arr)"],"whyThisOne":"Two of swing's best arrangers building an orchestra with harp, recorders, percussion and woodwinds, aimed at concert listening rather than dancing. It anticipates third stream by five years and is largely forgotten.","listenFor":"The instrumentation is closer to a film score than a jazz band, and the group barely swings by design. Whether that counts as a failure depends on what you think jazz is.","startTrack":"Doodletown Fifers","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"anthony-braxton-for-alto","genreIds":["third-stream","aacm-avant"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Anthony Braxton","title":"For Alto","recorded":1969,"released":1971,"label":"Delmark","catalogNo":"DS-420/421","personnel":["Anthony Braxton (as)"],"whyThisOne":"The first full-length album of solo saxophone improvisation, with pieces dedicated to John Cage and Cecil Taylor. It is where the compositional and improvisational streams meet in a single unaccompanied player.","listenFor":"Each piece has a defined structural premise — a set of intervals, a rhythmic cell — that Braxton then works through. This is composition executed live, not free blowing.","startTrack":"To Composer John Cage","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"miles-davis-miles-smiles","genreIds":["post-bop"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Miles Davis","title":"Miles Smiles","recorded":1966,"released":1967,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CL 2601","personnel":["Miles Davis (tp)","Wayne Shorter (ts)","Herbie Hancock (p)","Ron Carter (b)","Tony Williams (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The Second Great Quintet's clearest statement of 'time, no changes' — the pulse holds while the harmony becomes negotiable. Hancock lays out for long stretches so nothing states the chord at all.","listenFor":"On 'Footprints' the bass line implies one harmony and the piano implies another, while Williams shifts between a 6/4 and a 4/4 feel underneath. It never resolves and never falls apart.","startTrack":"Footprints","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"wayne-shorter-speak-no-evil","genreIds":["post-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Wayne Shorter","title":"Speak No Evil","recorded":1964,"released":1966,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4194","personnel":["Wayne Shorter (ts)","Freddie Hubbard (tp)","Herbie Hancock (p)","Ron Carter (b)","Elvin Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Shorter's compositions at their most perfectly balanced between hard bop's directness and post-bop's ambiguity. Every tune here is still played, and none of them analyse cleanly.","listenFor":"'Infant Eyes' has a thirteen-bar form — an odd number that you will not notice until you try to count it. The asymmetry is what makes the melody feel suspended.","startTrack":"Infant Eyes","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"eric-dolphy-out-to-lunch","genreIds":["post-bop","free-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Eric Dolphy","title":"Out to Lunch!","recorded":1964,"released":1964,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4163","personnel":["Eric Dolphy (as, bcl, fl)","Freddie Hubbard (tp)","Bobby Hutcherson (vib)","Richard Davis (b)","Tony Williams (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Dolphy's last studio album as leader, recorded four months before he died at thirty-six. There is no piano; the vibraphone leaves the harmony deliberately unstated and everything wobbles.","listenFor":"Tony Williams plays around the beat rather than on it for the entire record, and Hutcherson answers with chords that never confirm a key. The instability is composed, not accidental.","startTrack":"Hat and Beard","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"andrew-hill-point-of-departure","genreIds":["post-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Andrew Hill","title":"Point of Departure","recorded":1964,"released":1965,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4167","personnel":["Andrew Hill (p)","Eric Dolphy (as, bcl, fl)","Kenny Dorham (tp)","Joe Henderson (ts)","Richard Davis (b)","Tony Williams (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The most structurally radical of the Blue Note post-bop records. Hill's forms are asymmetric and his harmony moves by intervals nobody else was using, and the band handles it without strain.","listenFor":"'Refuge' has a form that will not resolve into a countable number of bars on first hearing. Hill wrote it that way and the band solos over it without visible difficulty.","startTrack":"Refuge","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"joe-henderson-inner-urge","genreIds":["post-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Joe Henderson","title":"Inner Urge","recorded":1964,"released":1966,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4189","personnel":["Joe Henderson (ts)","McCoy Tyner (p)","Bob Cranshaw (b)","Elvin Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Henderson with the Coltrane rhythm section, on a title track whose chords move by tritone and never sit still. He plays inside and outside the harmony continuously without ever sounding lost.","listenFor":"The changes on 'Inner Urge' move in a pattern that gives no comfortable resting place, and Henderson keeps finding one anyway.","startTrack":"Inner Urge","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"herbie-hancock-empyrean-isles","genreIds":["post-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Herbie Hancock","title":"Empyrean Isles","recorded":1964,"released":1964,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4175","personnel":["Herbie Hancock (p)","Freddie Hubbard (cor)","Ron Carter (b)","Tony Williams (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A quartet with no chordal instrument except the piano and a cornet instead of a trumpet, which makes the whole record lighter. 'Cantaloupe Island' has had a second life through sampling.","listenFor":"'The Egg' is fourteen minutes of near-free playing on a Blue Note record in 1964, which shows how close post-bop had come to the edge.","startTrack":"Cantaloupe Island","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bobby-hutcherson-dialogue","genreIds":["post-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Bobby Hutcherson","title":"Dialogue","recorded":1965,"released":1965,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4198","personnel":["Bobby Hutcherson (vib, marimba)","Freddie Hubbard (tp)","Sam Rivers (ts, bcl, fl)","Andrew Hill (p)","Richard Davis (b)","Joe Chambers (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Hutcherson's first date as leader, with Andrew Hill's writing and Joe Chambers' — two of the most structurally adventurous composers Blue Note had. It goes further out than Out to Lunch in places.","listenFor":"'Ghetto Lights' has passages of collective free improvisation inside a composed frame. The vibraphone-and-piano combination makes the harmony deliberately ambiguous.","startTrack":"Catta","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"sam-rivers-fuchsia-swing-song","genreIds":["post-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Sam Rivers","title":"Fuchsia Swing Song","recorded":1964,"released":1965,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4184","personnel":["Sam Rivers (ts)","Jaki Byard (p)","Ron Carter (b)","Tony Williams (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Rivers had just been in Miles Davis's band and would soon be running the loft scene. This is him operating exactly at the boundary — the forms hold, barely, and the playing keeps pushing past them.","listenFor":"Rivers described this as 'inside-outside' playing: the tune is there, the harmony is there, and the solo relates to both by choice rather than obligation.","startTrack":"Fuchsia Swing Song","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"tony-williams-lifetime-spring","genreIds":["post-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Tony Williams","title":"Spring","recorded":1965,"released":1966,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4216","personnel":["Tony Williams (d)","Wayne Shorter (ts)","Sam Rivers (ts)","Herbie Hancock (p)","Gary Peacock (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Williams was nineteen and leading a session with two tenors and no chord instrument on half of it. The rhythmic freedom is further out than anything he did with Miles.","listenFor":"'Extras' has no piano, no stated harmony and no fixed meter — Williams is composing with drums rather than accompanying.","startTrack":"Extras","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"machito-afro-cuban-jazz-suite","genreIds":["afro-cuban"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Machito and His Afro-Cubans","title":"Afro-Cuban Jazz Suite","recorded":1950,"released":1952,"label":"Mercury (Clef series)","catalogNo":"MG C-505","personnel":["Machito (maracas, voc)","Mario Bauzá (mus. dir)","Charlie Parker (as)","Flip Phillips (ts)","Buddy Rich (d)","Chico O'Farrill (comp, arr)"],"whyThisOne":"Chico O'Farrill's five-movement suite with Charlie Parker soloing over an authentic Cuban rhythm section. It is the meeting stated at full length rather than as a novelty single.","listenFor":"Parker plays exactly as he would over bebop changes, and the rhythm section does not adjust to him at all. Both languages stay intact and the friction is the record.","startTrack":"Afro-Cuban Jazz Suite","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium","note":"Recorded in New York on 21 December 1950; first issued on Mercury's Clef series as MG C-505 and reissued repeatedly on Clef, Norgran and Verve through the 1950s, so the release year is given variously as 1951-1957."},{"id":"tito-puente-dance-mania","genreIds":["afro-cuban"],"tier":"core","artist":"Tito Puente","title":"Dance Mania","recorded":1957,"released":1958,"label":"RCA Victor","catalogNo":"LPM-1692","personnel":["Tito Puente (timbales, vib)","Santos Colón (voc)","Ray Barretto (congas)"],"whyThisOne":"The mambo orchestra at its sharpest, with big-band arranging chops and a rhythm section that never lets up. This is the dance-floor half of the tradition that jazz kept borrowing from.","listenFor":"The horn section is arranged with the precision of a Basie chart while the percussion carries a completely independent rhythmic structure underneath.","startTrack":"El Cayuco","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"cal-tjader-soul-sauce","genreIds":["afro-cuban","soul-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Cal Tjader","title":"Soul Sauce","recorded":1964,"released":1965,"label":"Verve","catalogNo":"V6-8614","personnel":["Cal Tjader (vib)","Willie Bobo (timbales, voc)","Armando Peraza (congas)","Donald Byrd (tp)","Kenny Burrell (g)"],"whyThisOne":"The most successful Latin jazz crossover of the 1960s, built on a Dizzy Gillespie–Chano Pozo tune that Willie Bobo renamed. Accessible without being thin.","listenFor":"Tjader's vibraphone sits on top of a genuine Cuban rhythm section rather than a jazz drummer playing Latin. Willie Bobo and Armando Peraza are the real thing.","startTrack":"Soul Sauce (Guachi Guaro)","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"eddie-palmieri-harlem-river-drive","genreIds":["afro-cuban","jazz-funk"],"tier":"core","artist":"Eddie Palmieri","title":"Harlem River Drive","recorded":1971,"released":1971,"label":"Roulette","catalogNo":"SR 3004","personnel":["Eddie Palmieri (p)","Charlie Palmieri (org)","Ronnie Cuber (bs)","Bernard Purdie (d)","Victor Venegas (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Salsa, soul, jazz and Black political consciousness in one record, made by a Puerto Rican bandleader from the Bronx with Bernard Purdie on drums. Nothing else sounds quite like it.","listenFor":"The rhythm section plays clave and a hard funk backbeat at the same time, which should not be possible and is exactly what New York Latin music does.","startTrack":"Harlem River Drive (Theme Song)","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"irakere-irakere-1979","genreIds":["afro-cuban","fusion"],"tier":"core","artist":"Irakere","title":"Irakere","recorded":1978,"released":1979,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"JC 35655","personnel":["Chucho Valdés (p)","Paquito D'Rivera (as, cl)","Arturo Sandoval (tp)","Óscar Valdés (voc, percussion)"],"whyThisOne":"Cuba's leading band recorded live in the US during a brief diplomatic thaw, playing Yoruba batá drumming, post-bop harmony and rock guitar in the same piece. It won a Grammy and then the members started defecting.","listenFor":"The batá drums are sacred instruments from Santería practice, used here inside a jazz arrangement. That combination was politically and religiously loaded in Cuba at the time.","startTrack":"Misa Negra","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"gonzalo-rubalcaba-diz","genreIds":["afro-cuban","post-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Gonzalo Rubalcaba","title":"Diz","recorded":1993,"released":1993,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"CDP 7243 8 27639 2 5","personnel":["Gonzalo Rubalcaba (p)","Reginald Veal (b)","Roy Hargrove (tp)","Julio Barreto (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A Cuban pianist of formidable technique playing Gillespie's repertoire, which closes a circle that opened in 1947. Rubalcaba's rhythmic displacement is at a level almost nobody else attempts.","listenFor":"He plays with the beat as though it were elastic, stretching phrases far past where they should land and arriving exactly right. The clave is still underneath it all.","startTrack":"Woody 'n' You","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"mongo-santamaria-afro-roots","genreIds":["afro-cuban"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Mongo Santamaría","title":"Afro Roots","recorded":1958,"released":1972,"label":"Prestige","catalogNo":"PR 24018","personnel":["Mongo Santamaría (congas)","Willie Bobo (timbales)","Armando Peraza (bongos)","Francisco Aguabella (batá)"],"whyThisOne":"Mostly drums and voices, with almost no jazz instrumentation — the Afro-Cuban religious and folkloric material that everything else in this genre is built on top of.","listenFor":"Strip away the horns and the piano and this is what remains: interlocking drum parts and call-and-response singing in Yoruba-derived Lucumí.","startTrack":"Afro Blue","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium","note":"Combines the 1958 Fantasy albums 'Yambú' and 'Mongo'; the Prestige double LP reissue dates from 1972."},{"id":"jerry-gonzalez-rumba-para-monk","genreIds":["afro-cuban","post-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Jerry González and the Fort Apache Band","title":"Rumba para Monk","recorded":1988,"released":1989,"label":"Sunnyside","catalogNo":"SSC 1036","personnel":["Jerry González (tp, congas)","Carter Jefferson (ts)","Larry Willis (p)","Andy González (b)","Steve Berrios (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Monk's tunes played in Afro-Cuban rhythm, and they fit disturbingly well — Monk's angularity and clave's asymmetry turn out to be compatible.","listenFor":"'Evidence' in a rumba feel. Monk's melody was already displacing accents; putting it over clave doubles the effect.","startTrack":"Evidence","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"arturo-ofarrill-cuba-the-conversation","genreIds":["afro-cuban"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Arturo O'Farrill & The Afro Latin Jazz Orchestra","title":"Cuba: The Conversation Continues","recorded":2015,"released":2015,"label":"Motéma","catalogNo":"MTM-193","personnel":["Arturo O'Farrill (p, cond)","Rudresh Mahanthappa (as)","Vijay Iyer (comp)","Bobby Sanabria (percussion)"],"whyThisOne":"Recorded in Havana days after the US–Cuba diplomatic reopening was announced, by the son of the man who wrote the Afro-Cuban Jazz Suite. The lineage is literal and the commissioned new pieces are serious.","listenFor":"Vijay Iyer and Rudresh Mahanthappa's contributions put contemporary rhythmic thinking on top of the clave tradition, which is a different fusion than 1947's.","startTrack":"Cuba: The Conversation Continues","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"getz-gilberto-1964","genreIds":["bossa-brazilian"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Stan Getz & João Gilberto","title":"Getz/Gilberto","recorded":1963,"released":1964,"label":"Verve","catalogNo":"V6-8545","personnel":["Stan Getz (ts)","João Gilberto (g, voc)","Antônio Carlos Jobim (p)","Astrud Gilberto (voc)","Sebastião Neto (b)","Milton Banana (d)"],"whyThisOne":"It sold enormously, won Album of the Year, and made bossa nova a global commodity — and it is also, underneath the ubiquity, a very well-made record with three principals at their best.","listenFor":"João Gilberto's guitar alone. The right hand is doing what an entire samba percussion section does, and the singing sits so far inside the microphone that it is almost private.","startTrack":"The Girl from Ipanema","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"joao-gilberto-chega-de-saudade","genreIds":["bossa-brazilian"],"tier":"core","artist":"João Gilberto","title":"Chega de Saudade","recorded":1958,"released":1959,"label":"Odeon","catalogNo":"MOFB 3073","personnel":["João Gilberto (g, voc)","Antônio Carlos Jobim (arr, cond)"],"whyThisOne":"The record that started it. Gilberto's guitar pattern and vocal delivery arrive fully formed, and Brazilian popular music divides into before and after.","listenFor":"The rhythm is displaced across two bars rather than repeating every bar, which is why it never sounds mechanical. Count two bars, not one.","startTrack":"Chega de Saudade","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jobim-wave","genreIds":["bossa-brazilian"],"tier":"core","artist":"Antônio Carlos Jobim","title":"Wave","recorded":1967,"released":1967,"label":"A&M/CTI","catalogNo":"SP-3002","personnel":["Antônio Carlos Jobim (p, g, voc)","Claus Ogerman (arr)","Ron Carter (b)","Claudio Slon (d)","Urbie Green (tb)"],"whyThisOne":"Jobim as leader rather than as the songwriter behind someone else, with Claus Ogerman's orchestrations. The compositions are the most harmonically inventive popular songs of their era.","listenFor":"The title track's melody sits on two notes for long stretches while the chords move underneath it. Almost all the information is in the harmony.","startTrack":"Wave","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"elis-tom-1974","genreIds":["bossa-brazilian"],"tier":"core","artist":"Elis Regina & Antônio Carlos Jobim","title":"Elis & Tom","recorded":1974,"released":1974,"label":"Philips","catalogNo":"6349 112","personnel":["Elis Regina (voc)","Antônio Carlos Jobim (p, g, voc)","César Camargo Mariano (p)","Hélio Delmiro (g)"],"whyThisOne":"Brazil's greatest singer meeting its greatest songwriter, recorded in Los Angeles in a session both later described as tense. It is the definitive reading of most of this repertoire.","listenFor":"Regina sings with far more force and rhythmic attack than the bossa nova convention allows, which is why the record has an edge the earlier ones do not.","startTrack":"Águas de Março","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"hermeto-pascoal-slaves-mass","genreIds":["bossa-brazilian","fusion"],"tier":"core","artist":"Hermeto Pascoal","title":"Slaves Mass","recorded":1976,"released":1977,"label":"Warner Bros.","catalogNo":"BS 2980","personnel":["Hermeto Pascoal (multi-instruments)","Airto Moreira (percussion)","Flora Purim (voc)","Ron Carter (b)","Alphonso Johnson (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Pascoal is the Brazilian tradition without any of the restraint bossa nova imposed — folk forms, free improvisation, and on this record a chorus of live piglets used as an instrument.","listenFor":"The piglets are not a joke about avant-garde music; Pascoal tunes and arranges them. His whole practice is that any sound can be composed with.","startTrack":"Little Cry","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"milton-nascimento-clube-da-esquina","genreIds":["bossa-brazilian"],"tier":"core","artist":"Milton Nascimento & Lô Borges","title":"Clube da Esquina","recorded":1971,"released":1972,"label":"EMI-Odeon","catalogNo":"SGDF 2-4001","personnel":["Milton Nascimento (voc, g)","Lô Borges (voc, g)","Wagner Tiso (keys)","Robertinho Silva (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The Minas Gerais collective's double album, made under dictatorship, mixing Brazilian folk, Beatles-derived song, jazz harmony and choral writing. Wayne Shorter recruited Nascimento for Native Dancer afterwards.","listenFor":"The vocal writing — Nascimento's falsetto against layered harmony parts — has almost no precedent in either Brazilian or American music.","startTrack":"Cravo e Canela","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"egberto-gismonti-danca-das-cabecas","genreIds":["bossa-brazilian","ecm-nordic"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Egberto Gismonti & Naná Vasconcelos","title":"Dança das Cabeças","recorded":1976,"released":1977,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 1089","personnel":["Egberto Gismonti (g, p, fl)","Naná Vasconcelos (percussion, berimbau, voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Brazilian folk material, classical guitar technique and free improvisation, recorded with ECM's clarity. It is where the Brazilian tradition meets European chamber jazz on equal terms.","listenFor":"Vasconcelos's berimbau — a single-string bow from capoeira practice — is used as a melodic and rhythmic instrument, not as local colour.","startTrack":"Dança das Cabeças","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"airto-moreira-fingers","genreIds":["bossa-brazilian","fusion"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Airto Moreira","title":"Fingers","recorded":1972,"released":1973,"label":"CTI","catalogNo":"CTI 6028","personnel":["Airto Moreira (percussion, d, voc)","Flora Purim (voc)","David Amaro (g)","Hugo Fattoruso (keys)","Ringo Thielmann (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The Brazilian percussionist who had just played on Bitches Brew making his own record, with Brazilian forms driving fusion instrumentation rather than decorating it.","listenFor":"The percussion is a front-line instrument, and the rhythms are Brazilian rather than the generic Latin feel that most American fusion settled for.","startTrack":"Fingers","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"tania-maria-come-with-me","genreIds":["bossa-brazilian","jazz-funk"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Tania Maria","title":"Come with Me","recorded":1982,"released":1983,"label":"Concord Picante","catalogNo":"CJP-200","personnel":["Tania Maria (p, voc)","Eddie Duran (g)","John Peña (b)","Steve Thornton (percussion)"],"whyThisOne":"A pianist who sings her own improvised lines in unison, at speed, in Portuguese and scat. The technical demand is enormous and the records are pure momentum.","listenFor":"She doubles her piano solo with her voice, note for note. It is a specific and rare technique and it changes how the lines are shaped.","startTrack":"Come with Me","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"mulatu-astatke-ethiopiques-4","genreIds":["ethio-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Mulatu Astatke","title":"Éthiopiques 4: Ethio Jazz & Musique Instrumentale 1969–1974","recorded":1969,"released":1998,"label":"Buda Musique","catalogNo":"82964-2","personnel":["Mulatu Astatke (vib, percussion, arr)"],"whyThisOne":"The compilation that introduced Ethio-jazz to the world outside Ethiopia, collecting Astatke's Amha and Philips sides from the golden age. Jim Jarmusch used several of these in Broken Flowers.","listenFor":"'Yèkèrmo Sèw' — the five-note mode has wide gaps in it, so the melody sounds neither major nor minor, and the groove drags slightly behind the beat throughout.","startTrack":"Yèkèrmo Sèw","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"mulatu-astatke-afro-latin-soul","genreIds":["ethio-jazz","afro-cuban"],"tier":"core","artist":"Mulatu Astatke and His Ethiopian Quintet","title":"Afro-Latin Soul","recorded":1966,"released":1966,"label":"Worthy Records","catalogNo":"W-1014","personnel":["Mulatu Astatke (vib, congas)","Rudy Houston (voc)","Felix Torres (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Recorded in New York before Astatke returned to Ethiopia, and it is Latin jazz with Ethiopian melodic material starting to appear. The genre is visibly under construction.","listenFor":"The rhythm section is straightforwardly Latin. The Ethiopian element is in the melodies and in the vibraphone lines, which is the exact seam Astatke was working on.","startTrack":"Mascaram Setaba","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"mahmoud-ahmed-ere-mela-mela","genreIds":["ethio-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Mahmoud Ahmed","title":"Ere Mela Mela","recorded":1975,"released":1975,"label":"Kaifa Records","catalogNo":"KF 20","personnel":["Mahmoud Ahmed (voc)","Ibex Band"],"whyThisOne":"The record that broke Ethiopian music in Europe when Crammed Discs reissued it in 1986 — a decade before the Éthiopiques series. Ahmed's singing is the vocal counterpart to Astatke's instrumental project.","listenFor":"The vocal ornamentation moves in microtonal steps that the Western scale does not contain, over a band playing a fairly straight organ-and-horns groove.","startTrack":"Ere Mela Mela","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"getatchew-mekurya-ethiopiques-14","genreIds":["ethio-jazz","free-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Getatchew Mekurya","title":"Éthiopiques 14: Negus of Ethiopian Sax","recorded":1972,"released":2003,"label":"Buda Musique","catalogNo":"82250-2","personnel":["Getatchew Mekurya (ts)"],"whyThisOne":"Mekurya adapted the shellela — a sung Ethiopian war chant, delivered solo and unaccompanied — to the tenor saxophone, and arrived at something that sounds like Albert Ayler by an entirely independent route.","listenFor":"The long unaccompanied declamatory passages have no jazz precedent behind them at all. He had not heard the free jazz his playing resembles.","startTrack":"Shellela","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"hailu-mergia-tche-belew","genreIds":["ethio-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Hailu Mergia & The Walias Band","title":"Tche Belew","recorded":1977,"released":1977,"label":"Kaifa Records","catalogNo":"KF 41","personnel":["Hailu Mergia (org, keys)","Moges Habte (ts)","Melake Gebre (b)"],"whyThisOne":"An all-instrumental Ethiopian funk record from the Derg years, with Mergia's organ carrying the modes. He later spent decades driving a taxi in Washington DC before being rediscovered.","listenFor":"'Musicawi Silt' is built on a two-bar riff that never changes for the whole track. The interest is in the groove's slight drag and the organ's ornamentation.","startTrack":"Musicawi Silt","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"girma-beyene-ethiopiques-30","genreIds":["ethio-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Girma Bèyènè & Akalé Wubé","title":"Ethiopiques 30: Mistakes on Purpose","recorded":2016,"released":2017,"label":"Buda Musique","catalogNo":"860269","personnel":["Girma Bèyènè (voc, p)","Akalé Wubé (band)"],"whyThisOne":"The arranger behind much of the golden-age catalogue, returning to record after roughly thirty years of silence, backed by a young French Ethio-jazz band. The transmission across generations is the story.","listenFor":"The band plays the idiom with the accuracy of students, and Bèyènè's phrasing is looser and stranger than theirs. The gap is instructive.","startTrack":"Ené Négn Bay Manesh","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"ethiopiques-13-ethiopian-groove","genreIds":["ethio-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Various Artists","title":"Éthiopiques 13: Ethiopian Groove — The Golden Seventies","recorded":1969,"released":2003,"label":"Buda Musique","catalogNo":"82249-2","personnel":["Alèmayèhu Eshèté (voc)","Muluqèn Mèllèssè (voc)","various bands"],"whyThisOne":"The vocal and dance side of the same scene, showing that Ethio-jazz sat inside a broader Ethiopian popular music rather than standing apart from it.","listenFor":"Alèmayèhu Eshèté was called the Ethiopian Elvis and the influence is audible, sitting on top of entirely non-Western melodic material.","startTrack":"Tashamanaletch","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"mulatu-heliocentrics-inspiration-information","genreIds":["ethio-jazz","nu-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Mulatu Astatke & The Heliocentrics","title":"Inspiration Information 3","recorded":2008,"released":2009,"label":"Strut","catalogNo":"STRUT045CD","personnel":["Mulatu Astatke (vib, keys)","Malcolm Catto (d)","Jake Ferguson (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Astatke at sixty-five with a London psych-jazz band, and the collaboration is genuinely two-way rather than a legend being backed. It is also the main channel by which Ethio-jazz entered the current UK scene.","listenFor":"The Heliocentrics play with a deliberately murky, dubbed-out production that suits the modes better than the clean 1970s Addis recordings did.","startTrack":"Cha Cha","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"alemayehu-eshete-ethiopiques-9","genreIds":["ethio-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Alèmayèhu Eshèté","title":"Éthiopiques 9: Alèmayèhu Eshèté 1969–1974","recorded":1969,"released":2000,"label":"Buda Musique","catalogNo":"82966-2","personnel":["Alèmayèhu Eshèté (voc)","Walias Band","Shebele's Band"],"whyThisOne":"The golden age's most charismatic singer, with the Walias Band behind him. It is the pop end of the scene and shows how thin the line between Ethio-jazz and Ethiopian pop actually was.","listenFor":"The band's horn riffs and organ are lifted from American soul records, while the vocal line stays entirely inside Ethiopian modal practice.","startTrack":"Tèlantèna Zaré","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"terumasa-hino-hi-nology","genreIds":["j-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Terumasa Hino","title":"Hi-Nology","recorded":1969,"released":1969,"label":"Columbia (Japan)","catalogNo":"YS-10022-J","personnel":["Terumasa Hino (tp)","Hiromasa Suzuki (p)","Kunimitsu Inaba (b)","Motohiko Hino (d)","Takeru Muraoka (ts)"],"whyThisOne":"The record that announced Japanese modern jazz as its own thing rather than an import. Modal, hard-driving, and recorded with a punch that American records of the same year rarely had.","listenFor":"The title track sits on a vamp and builds by sheer accumulation. Motohiko Hino's drumming is heavier and more rock-inflected than any Blue Note session of 1969.","startTrack":"Hi-Nology","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"masabumi-kikuchi-poo-sun","genreIds":["j-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Masabumi Kikuchi","title":"Poo-Sun","recorded":1970,"released":1970,"label":"Philips (Japan)","catalogNo":"FX-8501","personnel":["Masabumi Kikuchi (p, elp)","Terumasa Hino (tp)","Kohsuke Mine (as)","Yoshio Ikeda (b)","Motohiko Hino (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Kikuchi's early electric work, with the Hino brothers, sitting between modal jazz and something more abrasive. It is one of the most sought-after Japanese jazz records for good reason.","listenFor":"The electric piano is used percussively and harshly rather than for warmth, which is the opposite of how American fusion was using it in 1970.","startTrack":"Poo-Sun","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"yosuke-yamashita-clay","genreIds":["j-jazz","free-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Yosuke Yamashita Trio","title":"Clay","recorded":1974,"released":1974,"label":"Enja","catalogNo":"enja 2050","personnel":["Yosuke Yamashita (p)","Akira Sakata (as, cl)","Takeo Moriyama (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Recorded live at the Moers festival in Germany, this is the trio that made European audiences take Japanese free jazz seriously. The physical violence of the playing is the point.","listenFor":"Yamashita attacks the piano with his forearms and elbows in places, but the harmonic thinking underneath never becomes random. Sakata's alto screams and stays in tune.","startTrack":"Clay","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"isao-suzuki-blow-up","genreIds":["j-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Isao Suzuki Trio/Quartet","title":"Blow Up","recorded":1973,"released":1973,"label":"Three Blind Mice","catalogNo":"TBM-15","personnel":["Isao Suzuki (b, cello)","Kunihiko Sugano (p)","Tetsujiro Obara (d)","Takashi Mizuhashi (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The Three Blind Mice house sound at its best: audiophile recording, a bassist leading, and material that swings hard without being retro. This is the label's calling card.","listenFor":"The bass is recorded with a presence and depth that almost no American jazz record of 1973 attempted. TBM's engineering is a genuine part of the music's identity.","startTrack":"Aqua Marine","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"masahiko-togashi-we-now-create","genreIds":["j-jazz","free-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Masahiko Togashi","title":"We Now Create: Music for Strings, Winds and Percussion","recorded":1969,"released":1969,"label":"Victor (Japan)","catalogNo":"SJV-259","personnel":["Masahiko Togashi (percussion)","Masayuki Takayanagi (g)","Mototeru Takagi (ts)","Motoharu Yoshizawa (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Recorded shortly before the injury that paralysed Togashi's legs and forced him to rebuild his entire approach to percussion. The use of silence and space here is unlike anything in American free jazz.","listenFor":"Long stretches of near-nothing, then sudden density. The dynamic strategy is closer to the AACM than to New York energy playing, arrived at independently.","startTrack":"We Now Create","difficulty":5,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"ryo-fukui-scenery","genreIds":["j-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Ryo Fukui","title":"Scenery","recorded":1976,"released":1976,"label":"Trio Records","catalogNo":"PA-7153","personnel":["Ryo Fukui (p)","Satoshi Denpo (b)","Yoshinori Fukui (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A self-taught pianist from Sapporo making a straight-ahead trio record that went almost unnoticed for forty years and then found an enormous online audience. The playing is unshowy and completely assured.","listenFor":"His version of 'Willow Weep for Me' is unhurried to the point of stillness, and the touch is the whole story — every note is placed rather than played.","startTrack":"Early Summer","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"toshiko-akiyoshi-kogun","genreIds":["j-jazz","swing"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Toshiko Akiyoshi–Lew Tabackin Big Band","title":"Kogun","recorded":1974,"released":1974,"label":"RCA (Japan)","catalogNo":"JRS-7211","personnel":["Toshiko Akiyoshi (p, comp, arr)","Lew Tabackin (ts, fl)","tsuzumi drums","noh vocal"],"whyThisOne":"Akiyoshi wrote Japanese traditional instruments and noh vocal technique into big band scoring, on a piece about a soldier still fighting the Second World War in 1972. Nobody else was writing like this.","listenFor":"The tsuzumi hand drums and the noh-derived vocal cry are structural, not decorative — the whole arrangement is built to accommodate their sense of time.","startTrack":"Kogun","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"kohsuke-mine-mine","genreIds":["j-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Kohsuke Mine","title":"Mine","recorded":1970,"released":1970,"label":"Three Blind Mice","catalogNo":"TBM-1","personnel":["Kohsuke Mine (as, ss)","Masabumi Kikuchi (p)","Hideo Ichikawa (p)","Juni Booth (b)","Hiroshi Murakami (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Three Blind Mice's first release, and the record that set the label's standard. Mine plays with a hard, keening alto tone that owes something to Coltrane's soprano and nothing to Cannonball.","listenFor":"The engineering — this was TBM-1, and Yoshihiko Kannari's recording of the drum kit already sounds better than most contemporary American jazz.","startTrack":"Morning Tide","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"koichi-matsukaze-at-the-room-427","genreIds":["j-jazz","spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Koichi Matsukaze Trio","title":"At the Room 427","recorded":1975,"released":1975,"label":"ALM Records","catalogNo":"AL-1","personnel":["Koichi Matsukaze (ts, ss)","Toshiyuki Daitoku (p)","Hiroshi Kato (b)","Ryojiro Furusawa (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Spiritual jazz from Japan, made outside the major-label system, with the modal intensity of the Impulse! catalogue and a distinctly Japanese sense of space.","listenFor":"The long modal build follows the Coltrane quartet's shape, but the dynamics drop far lower than an American band of the period would have allowed.","startTrack":"Earth Mother","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"peter-brotzmann-machine-gun","genreIds":["euro-free-improv"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Peter Brötzmann Octet","title":"Machine Gun","recorded":1968,"released":1968,"label":"BRÖ (self-released), later FMP","catalogNo":"BRÖ 2","personnel":["Peter Brötzmann (ts)","Evan Parker (ts)","Willem Breuker (ts)","Fred Van Hove (p)","Peter Kowald (b)","Buschi Niebergall (b)","Han Bennink (d)","Sven-Åke Johansson (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The line in the sand. Eight players at maximum volume for the whole record, self-released because no label would touch it. It is not the gentlest introduction but it is the clearest statement of what European free music was refusing.","listenFor":"There is a written theme — a repeated stabbing figure — that returns at intervals. Once you hear it, the record stops being noise and becomes a structure.","startTrack":"Machine Gun","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high","note":"Originally self-issued on Brötzmann's own BRÖ label in 1968 and later reissued by FMP."},{"id":"derek-bailey-solo-guitar-vol1","genreIds":["euro-free-improv"],"tier":"core","artist":"Derek Bailey","title":"Solo Guitar Volume 1","recorded":1971,"released":1971,"label":"Incus","catalogNo":"Incus 2","personnel":["Derek Bailey (g)"],"whyThisOne":"Bailey alone, on the label he co-founded to avoid the record industry entirely. This is non-idiomatic improvisation as a fully worked-out practice rather than a manifesto.","listenFor":"No phrase resembles a jazz line, a rock riff or a classical figure. He is actively avoiding every idiom the guitar has, and the discipline of that is audible.","startTrack":"Improvisation 1","difficulty":5,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"evan-parker-monoceros","genreIds":["euro-free-improv"],"tier":"core","artist":"Evan Parker","title":"Monoceros","recorded":1978,"released":1978,"label":"Incus","catalogNo":"Incus 27","personnel":["Evan Parker (ss)"],"whyThisOne":"Solo soprano saxophone, direct to disc, using circular breathing and multiphonics to produce continuous overlapping lines from one horn. There is no jazz antecedent for this technique.","listenFor":"He never stops to breathe, and you will hear two or three simultaneous lines from a monophonic instrument. It is a physical achievement and a compositional one.","startTrack":"Monoceros I","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"spontaneous-music-ensemble-karyobin","genreIds":["euro-free-improv"],"tier":"core","artist":"Spontaneous Music Ensemble","title":"Karyōbin","recorded":1968,"released":1968,"label":"Island","catalogNo":"ILPS 9079","personnel":["John Stevens (d)","Evan Parker (ss)","Derek Bailey (g)","Kenny Wheeler (tp)","Dave Holland (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The quiet, fast, densely interactive end of British improvisation — Stevens called it 'insect music'. Dave Holland is on it, months before Miles Davis hired him.","listenFor":"Everyone is playing quietly and reacting within a fraction of a second. Nobody projects and nobody leads. It is the opposite pole from Machine Gun in the same year.","startTrack":"Karyōbin Are the Imaginary Birds Said to Live in Paradise","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"icp-tetterettet","genreIds":["euro-free-improv"],"tier":"core","artist":"ICP Orchestra","title":"Tetterettet","recorded":1977,"released":1978,"label":"ICP","catalogNo":"ICP 026","personnel":["Misha Mengelberg (p)","Han Bennink (d)","Michael Moore (as, cl)","Wolter Wierbos (tb)"],"whyThisOne":"The Dutch approach: absurdism, sudden collapses into Monk or Herbie Nichols tunes, and a refusal to take the avant-garde's seriousness at face value. It is also very funny.","listenFor":"A free improvisation will resolve without warning into a fully swinging Monk tune, then fall apart again. The joke has a real point about how idioms work.","startTrack":"Tetterettet","difficulty":4,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"globe-unity-orchestra-1966","genreIds":["euro-free-improv"],"tier":"core","artist":"Globe Unity Orchestra","title":"Globe Unity","recorded":1966,"released":1967,"label":"SABA","catalogNo":"SB 15109","personnel":["Alexander von Schlippenbach (p, comp)","Peter Brötzmann (ts)","Manfred Schoof (tp)","Peter Kowald (b)","Jaki Byard (p)"],"whyThisOne":"A large-ensemble free improvisation commissioned for the Berlin Jazz Festival in 1966, which is remarkable in itself. Schlippenbach's writing gives the chaos a shape.","listenFor":"Composed passages emerge from and dissolve into collective improvisation. The orchestra is a compositional instrument, not just more people playing freely.","startTrack":"Globe Unity","difficulty":5,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"alexander-von-schlippenbach-pakistani-pomade","genreIds":["euro-free-improv"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Alexander von Schlippenbach Trio","title":"Pakistani Pomade","recorded":1972,"released":1973,"label":"FMP","catalogNo":"FMP 0110","personnel":["Alexander von Schlippenbach (p)","Evan Parker (ss, ts)","Paul Lovens (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The trio that has played together for over fifty years, on one of its earliest records. This is European free improvisation as a working long-term ensemble language rather than a one-off encounter.","listenFor":"The three of them move as a single organism, and the piano is being used percussively and pointillistically rather than harmonically.","startTrack":"Pakistani Pomade","difficulty":5,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"company-6-and-7","genreIds":["euro-free-improv"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Company","title":"Company 6 & 7","recorded":1977,"released":1977,"label":"Incus","catalogNo":"Incus 29/30","personnel":["Derek Bailey (g)","Evan Parker (sax)","Anthony Braxton (reeds)","Steve Lacy (ss)","Han Bennink (d)","Leo Smith (tp)","Tristan Honsinger (cello)"],"whyThisOne":"Bailey's Company was an ad hoc gathering where musicians who had never played together were put on stage in changing combinations. This is the week-long 1977 London festival, and it is where the American and European avant-gardes actually met.","listenFor":"Each track is a different combination. Compare how Braxton and Bailey behave together versus Braxton and Steve Lacy — the same player sounds like a different musician.","startTrack":"Company 6","difficulty":5,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"schweizer-moholo-duo","genreIds":["euro-free-improv"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Irène Schweizer & Louis Moholo","title":"Irène Schweizer & Louis Moholo","recorded":1986,"released":1987,"label":"Intakt","catalogNo":"Intakt CD 200","personnel":["Irène Schweizer (p)","Louis Moholo (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A Swiss pianist and a South African drummer from the Blue Notes, whose exile brought township rhythm into European free music. The duo is one of the great long partnerships in the idiom.","listenFor":"Moholo's playing carries South African township feels underneath free improvisation, so a groove keeps threatening to break out and sometimes does.","startTrack":"Free Improvisation","difficulty":4,"confidence":"low","note":"Intakt has reissued this duo under several catalogue numbers; the original release details are not consistently documented."},{"id":"ornette-coleman-shape-of-jazz-to-come","genreIds":["free-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Ornette Coleman","title":"The Shape of Jazz to Come","recorded":1959,"released":1959,"label":"Atlantic","catalogNo":"SD 1317","personnel":["Ornette Coleman (as)","Don Cherry (cor)","Charlie Haden (b)","Billy Higgins (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The most approachable free jazz record ever made. The melodies are singable, the pulse is steady, and there is simply no chord sequence — which is the only thing that was actually removed.","listenFor":"'Lonely Woman' has a clear, mournful theme that anyone can follow. Then notice that the bass and horns are not agreeing on a tempo, and no piano is telling you what key you are in.","startTrack":"Lonely Woman","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"ornette-coleman-free-jazz","genreIds":["free-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Ornette Coleman Double Quartet","title":"Free Jazz: A Collective Improvisation","recorded":1960,"released":1961,"label":"Atlantic","catalogNo":"SD 1364","personnel":["Ornette Coleman (as)","Eric Dolphy (bcl)","Don Cherry (pocket tp)","Freddie Hubbard (tp)","Charlie Haden (b)","Scott LaFaro (b)","Billy Higgins (d)","Ed Blackwell (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Two quartets, one in each stereo channel, improvising together for thirty-seven unbroken minutes with only brief written cues. It gave the movement its name and remains its most extreme statement of collective method.","listenFor":"Play it with headphones. The two quartets are completely separated left and right, and you can follow one band at a time or hear them collide.","startTrack":"Free Jazz","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"cecil-taylor-unit-structures","genreIds":["free-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Cecil Taylor","title":"Unit Structures","recorded":1966,"released":1966,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BLP 4237","personnel":["Cecil Taylor (p)","Jimmy Lyons (as)","Ken McIntyre (as, ob, bcl)","Henry Grimes (b)","Alan Silva (b)","Andrew Cyrille (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Taylor's densest and most organised record, on a major label, with his own written notes explaining the structural units. It is atonal, percussive, and completely unlike Coleman's version of freedom.","listenFor":"The piano is being played as a percussion instrument — clusters, forearms, extreme velocity — but the pitch material is organised, not random. Follow one interval and you will hear it return.","startTrack":"Steps","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"albert-ayler-spiritual-unity","genreIds":["free-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Albert Ayler Trio","title":"Spiritual Unity","recorded":1964,"released":1965,"label":"ESP-Disk","catalogNo":"ESP 1002","personnel":["Albert Ayler (ts)","Gary Peacock (b)","Sunny Murray (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Thirty minutes, three players, no chords, no meter, and melodies that sound like nursery rhymes or Salvation Army hymns played by someone in distress. It is the most emotionally extreme record in jazz.","listenFor":"'Ghosts' has a simple, almost childish tune. Ayler states it plainly and then destroys it with a vibrato so wide it becomes a howl, then returns to the tune intact.","startTrack":"Ghosts: First Variation","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"john-coltrane-ascension","genreIds":["free-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"John Coltrane","title":"Ascension","recorded":1965,"released":1966,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"A-95","personnel":["John Coltrane (ts)","Pharoah Sanders (ts)","Archie Shepp (ts)","Marion Brown (as)","John Tchicai (as)","Freddie Hubbard (tp)","Dewey Johnson (tp)","McCoy Tyner (p)","Jimmy Garrison (b)","Art Davis (b)","Elvin Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Eleven musicians, forty minutes, alternating between full collective improvisation and individual solos. Coltrane's audience followed him here, which is why free jazz got a hearing it would not otherwise have had.","listenFor":"The structure is simple and audible: massed ensemble, then a solo, then massed ensemble again. Once you hear the alternation the wall of sound becomes navigable.","startTrack":"Ascension (Edition II)","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high","note":"Two complete takes exist; Impulse! substituted Edition II for Edition I shortly after release."},{"id":"sun-ra-heliocentric-worlds-vol1","genreIds":["free-jazz","spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Sun Ra and His Solar Arkestra","title":"The Heliocentric Worlds of Sun Ra, Volume One","recorded":1965,"released":1965,"label":"ESP-Disk","catalogNo":"ESP 1014","personnel":["Sun Ra (p, bass marimba, tympani)","Marshall Allen (as, piccolo)","John Gilmore (ts)","Pat Patrick (bs)","Ronnie Boykins (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The Arkestra at its most abstract, using tuned percussion, tympani and space rather than horn blowing. It sounds closer to Varèse than to jazz, which is the point.","listenFor":"There is almost no steady pulse and a great deal of tuned percussion. Sun Ra was working with orchestral colour and silence a decade before the AACM made it a method.","startTrack":"Heliocentric","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"archie-shepp-fire-music","genreIds":["free-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Archie Shepp","title":"Fire Music","recorded":1965,"released":1965,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"A-86","personnel":["Archie Shepp (ts)","Ted Curson (tp)","Joseph Orange (tb)","Marion Brown (as)","Reggie Johnson (b)","Joe Chambers (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Shepp ties the music explicitly to Black political struggle — there is an elegy for Malcolm X with spoken text — while also playing a Duke Ellington ballad straight. The combination is deliberate.","listenFor":"'Malcolm, Malcolm — Semper Malcolm' is Shepp reciting his own poem over the band. Free jazz's politics are usually inferred; here they are stated.","startTrack":"Hambone","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"coltrane-interstellar-space","genreIds":["free-jazz","spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"John Coltrane","title":"Interstellar Space","recorded":1967,"released":1974,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"ASD-9277","personnel":["John Coltrane (ts)","Rashied Ali (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Duets for tenor saxophone and drums, recorded in February 1967, five months before Coltrane's death. There is nothing left to hold onto — no bass, no harmony, no meter.","listenFor":"Each piece is named for a planet and starts with bells. Ali is not keeping time; he is producing a continuous field that Coltrane moves through.","startTrack":"Mars","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"charles-mingus-changes-one","genreIds":["free-jazz","post-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Charles Mingus","title":"Changes One","recorded":1974,"released":1975,"label":"Atlantic","catalogNo":"SD 1677","personnel":["Charles Mingus (b)","George Adams (ts, voc)","Jack Walrath (tp)","Don Pullen (p)","Dannie Richmond (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Mingus's last great band, where the tunes are solidly composed and the solos go completely outside. Don Pullen plays with his knuckles and the record still swings.","listenFor":"Pullen's solos start inside the changes and end in clusters played with the backs of his hands, then return. The transition between languages is seamless and audible.","startTrack":"Sue's Changes","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"art-ensemble-of-chicago-les-stances-a-sophie","genreIds":["aacm-avant"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Art Ensemble of Chicago","title":"Les Stances à Sophie","recorded":1970,"released":1970,"label":"Nessa","catalogNo":"N-4","personnel":["Lester Bowie (tp)","Roscoe Mitchell (reeds)","Joseph Jarman (reeds)","Malachi Favors (b)","Don Moye (percussion)","Fontella Bass (voc, p)"],"whyThisOne":"A film soundtrack recorded in Paris, and the most immediately enjoyable thing the Art Ensemble made. 'Theme de Yoyo' has a bass line, a backbeat and Fontella Bass singing, and it is still recognisably them.","listenFor":"'Theme de Yoyo' is a funk record made by an avant-garde collective, and the free playing sits on top of the groove without disturbing it.","startTrack":"Theme de Yoyo","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"roscoe-mitchell-sound","genreIds":["aacm-avant"],"tier":"core","artist":"Roscoe Mitchell Sextet","title":"Sound","recorded":1966,"released":1966,"label":"Delmark","catalogNo":"DS-408","personnel":["Roscoe Mitchell (as, cl, recorder)","Lester Bowie (tp)","Lester Lashley (tb, cello)","Malachi Favors (b)","Alvin Fielder (d)","Maurice McIntyre (ts)"],"whyThisOne":"The first AACM record, and the first jazz album to treat silence as a compositional material rather than an absence. It sounds nothing like the New York free jazz of the same year.","listenFor":"The title track has stretches where almost nothing happens for a long time. That was a deliberate rejection of the continuous-blowing model, and it defined the Chicago approach.","startTrack":"Sound","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"art-ensemble-people-in-sorrow","genreIds":["aacm-avant"],"tier":"core","artist":"Art Ensemble of Chicago","title":"People in Sorrow","recorded":1969,"released":1969,"label":"Nessa","catalogNo":"N-3","personnel":["Lester Bowie (tp)","Roscoe Mitchell (reeds)","Joseph Jarman (reeds)","Malachi Favors (b)"],"whyThisOne":"A single forty-minute piece built almost entirely from small sounds, bells, whistles and space. It is the fullest expression of 'Great Black Music' as a compositional programme.","listenFor":"The 'little instruments' — bicycle horns, gongs, toys — are played with the same seriousness as the horns. Listen for how carefully they are placed.","startTrack":"People in Sorrow","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"muhal-richard-abrams-levels-and-degrees","genreIds":["aacm-avant"],"tier":"core","artist":"Muhal Richard Abrams","title":"Levels and Degrees of Light","recorded":1967,"released":1968,"label":"Delmark","catalogNo":"DS-413","personnel":["Muhal Richard Abrams (p, cl)","Anthony Braxton (as)","Leroy Jenkins (vln)","Leo Smith (tp)","Penelope Taylor (voc)","David Moore (poetry)"],"whyThisOne":"The AACM's founder on record, with Braxton and Leroy Jenkins making early appearances. It includes poetry, vibraphone drones and a clarinet-and-voice texture that has no obvious precedent.","listenFor":"The opening is a slow vibraphone drone with a soprano voice over it, for several minutes. This is a jazz record from 1967 that begins like an ambient piece.","startTrack":"Levels and Degrees of Light","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"anthony-braxton-creative-orchestra-music","genreIds":["aacm-avant"],"tier":"core","artist":"Anthony Braxton","title":"Creative Orchestra Music 1976","recorded":1976,"released":1976,"label":"Arista","catalogNo":"AL 4080","personnel":["Anthony Braxton (reeds, comp)","Leo Smith (tp)","Kenny Wheeler (tp)","George Lewis (tb)","Muhal Richard Abrams (p)","Dave Holland (b)","Barry Altschul (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A major label paid for a large-ensemble avant-garde record, and Braxton delivered one that includes a genuine circus march alongside the abstraction. It won critics' polls and is still startling.","listenFor":"Track four is a straight-faced march, played properly. Braxton's point is that the whole history is available material, not that the past is a joke.","startTrack":"Piece Three","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"henry-threadgill-air-lore","genreIds":["aacm-avant"],"tier":"core","artist":"Air","title":"Air Lore","recorded":1979,"released":1979,"label":"Arista Novus","catalogNo":"AN 3014","personnel":["Henry Threadgill (as, ts, fl)","Fred Hopkins (b)","Steve McCall (d)"],"whyThisOne":"An avant-garde trio playing Scott Joplin and Jelly Roll Morton, seriously and without irony, and finding that ragtime's structure holds up under free treatment.","listenFor":"'The Ragtime Dance' keeps Joplin's form intact while the trio pulls the time apart underneath it. Ancient to the future, demonstrated on one track.","startTrack":"The Ragtime Dance","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"world-saxophone-quartet-revue","genreIds":["aacm-avant","loft-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"World Saxophone Quartet","title":"Revue","recorded":1980,"released":1982,"label":"Black Saint","catalogNo":"BSR 0056","personnel":["Julius Hemphill (as, ss)","Oliver Lake (as, ss)","David Murray (ts, bcl)","Hamiet Bluiett (bs)"],"whyThisOne":"Four saxophones and nothing else — no rhythm section at all — playing composed material with the density of a big band. It came out of the loft scene and the BAG/AACM diaspora.","listenFor":"Without drums or bass, all the rhythmic drive has to come from articulation and phrasing. Listen to how the baritone functions as both bass and horn.","startTrack":"Ballad for Eddie Jefferson","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"revolutionary-ensemble-peoples-republic","genreIds":["aacm-avant"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Revolutionary Ensemble","title":"The People's Republic","recorded":1975,"released":1976,"label":"A&M Horizon","catalogNo":"SP-708","personnel":["Leroy Jenkins (vln, viola)","Sirone (b)","Jerome Cooper (d, percussion)"],"whyThisOne":"Violin, bass and drums as a fully equal collective trio, with all three writing. The string-led texture makes it one of the strangest and most chamber-like records in the whole avant-garde.","listenFor":"Jenkins plays violin with a jazz sensibility and a contemporary-classical technique at the same time. There is almost no other model for this instrument in the music.","startTrack":"New York","difficulty":5,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"threadgill-you-know-the-number","genreIds":["aacm-avant","contemporary-creative"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Henry Threadgill Sextett","title":"You Know the Number","recorded":1986,"released":1986,"label":"RCA Novus","catalogNo":"3013-1-N","personnel":["Henry Threadgill (as, fl)","Rasul Siddik (tp)","Frank Lacy (tb)","Diedre Murray (cello)","Fred Hopkins (b)","Pheeroan akLaff (d)","Reggie Nicholson (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Threadgill's sextett has seven members — two drummers count as one — and uses cello as a middle voice. The writing is contrapuntal, marching, and unlike anyone else's.","listenFor":"The two drummers play complementary rather than doubled parts, and the cello sits where a piano would, so nothing states the harmony conventionally.","startTrack":"Bermuda Blues","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"a-love-supreme","genreIds":["spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"John Coltrane","title":"A Love Supreme","recorded":1964,"released":1965,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"A-77","personnel":["John Coltrane (ts, voc)","McCoy Tyner (p)","Jimmy Garrison (b)","Elvin Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A four-part suite offered explicitly as a religious statement, recorded in one session in December 1964. It gave the whole idiom its structure, its intent and its permission.","listenFor":"The four-note bass figure in 'Acknowledgement' is the whole record's seed. Coltrane plays it in every key before finally singing it as words — the melody becomes the mantra.","startTrack":"Acknowledgement","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"pharoah-sanders-karma","genreIds":["spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Pharoah Sanders","title":"Karma","recorded":1969,"released":1969,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"AS-9181","personnel":["Pharoah Sanders (ts)","Leon Thomas (voc)","Lonnie Liston Smith (p)","Reggie Workman (b)","Julius Watkins (frh)","Billy Hart (d)"],"whyThisOne":"'The Creator Has a Master Plan' runs thirty-two minutes and moves from a serene pentatonic melody to total collective violence and back. It is the idiom's defining shape in one track.","listenFor":"Leon Thomas's yodelling — a technique he adapted from Central African pygmy singing — is doing something no jazz vocalist had attempted.","startTrack":"The Creator Has a Master Plan","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"alice-coltrane-journey-in-satchidananda","genreIds":["spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Alice Coltrane","title":"Journey in Satchidananda","recorded":1970,"released":1971,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"AS-9203","personnel":["Alice Coltrane (harp, p)","Pharoah Sanders (ss)","Cecil McBee (b)","Rashied Ali (d)","Tulsi (tanpura)","Majid Shabazz (bells)"],"whyThisOne":"The harp, the tanpura drone and the Hindu devotional framework in one record, made by a musician who was practising what she was playing. Nothing else in jazz sounds like this.","listenFor":"The tanpura drone under everything is not an effect — it is the harmonic foundation, and it means the music has no chord changes at all, only a fixed centre.","startTrack":"Journey in Satchidananda","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"pharoah-sanders-thembi","genreIds":["spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Pharoah Sanders","title":"Thembi","recorded":1970,"released":1971,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"AS-9206","personnel":["Pharoah Sanders (ts, ss, percussion)","Lonnie Liston Smith (p, elp)","Cecil McBee (b)","Roy Haynes (d)","Michael White (vln)"],"whyThisOne":"More varied than Karma and more concise, taking in Cecil McBee's solo bass piece, electric piano textures and African percussion. It is the idiom's most producer-minded record.","listenFor":"'Astral Traveling' opens with Lonnie Liston Smith's electric piano through echo, which is essentially the sound that nu jazz rediscovered twenty-five years later.","startTrack":"Astral Traveling","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"doug-carn-infant-eyes","genreIds":["spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Doug Carn","title":"Infant Eyes","recorded":1971,"released":1971,"label":"Black Jazz","catalogNo":"BJ/2","personnel":["Doug Carn (org, p)","Jean Carn (voc)","George Harper (ts)","Nate Morgan (p)"],"whyThisOne":"Jean Carn sings lyrics written to Wayne Shorter and McCoy Tyner instrumentals, over organ-led spiritual jazz on a Black-owned label. It is the most song-shaped version of the idiom.","listenFor":"Putting words to 'Infant Eyes' should not work — Shorter's melody is not a song melody — and the strain of making it fit is part of what makes it moving.","startTrack":"Infant Eyes","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"don-cherry-brown-rice","genreIds":["spiritual-jazz","fusion"],"tier":"core","artist":"Don Cherry","title":"Brown Rice","recorded":1975,"released":1976,"label":"Horizon/A&M","catalogNo":"SP-717","personnel":["Don Cherry (tp, voc, keys)","Charlie Haden (b)","Billy Higgins (d)","Frank Lowe (ts)","Ricky Cherry (elp)","Bunchie Fox (tabla)"],"whyThisOne":"Cherry's global project at its most electric — Indian tabla, African chant, electric bass and a Moroccan-influenced drone, made by Ornette Coleman's original trumpeter.","listenFor":"The title track has a bass line that would work on a funk record and a vocal chant that would not. Cherry never treated those as different categories.","startTrack":"Brown Rice","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high","note":"Originally issued in Italy as 'Don Cherry' on Horizon; the A&M US release used the title Brown Rice."},{"id":"alice-coltrane-ptah-el-daoud","genreIds":["spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Alice Coltrane","title":"Ptah, the El Daoud","recorded":1970,"released":1970,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"AS-9196","personnel":["Alice Coltrane (p, harp)","Pharoah Sanders (ts, bcl)","Joe Henderson (ts)","Ron Carter (b)","Ben Riley (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Recorded in the basement of her home in Dix Hills with two tenor players panned hard left and right. Joe Henderson and Pharoah Sanders playing the same material from opposite aesthetics is the whole record.","listenFor":"Henderson is on one channel and Sanders on the other. Same tune, same harmony, completely different ideas about what a saxophone is for.","startTrack":"Turiya and Ramakrishna","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"phil-ranelin-vibes-from-the-tribe","genreIds":["spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Phil Ranelin","title":"Vibes from the Tribe","recorded":1976,"released":1976,"label":"Tribe","catalogNo":"PRSD 2224","personnel":["Phil Ranelin (tb)","Wendell Harrison (ts)","Harold McKinney (p)","Marcus Belgrave (tp)"],"whyThisOne":"Detroit's Tribe collective ran its own label, magazine and concerts, and this is its central record. Spiritual jazz with an explicitly Black-nationalist organisational politics attached.","listenFor":"The horn arrangements are tight and hard-swinging under the modal drift — this is a working Detroit band, not a studio assembly.","startTrack":"Vibes from the Tribe","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"shabaka-and-the-ancestors-wisdom","genreIds":["spiritual-jazz","uk-jazz-revival"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Shabaka and the Ancestors","title":"Wisdom of Elders","recorded":2016,"released":2016,"label":"Brownswood","catalogNo":"BWOOD0155","personnel":["Shabaka Hutchings (ts)","Mthunzi Mvubu (as)","Ariel Zamonsky (b)","Tumi Mogorosi (d)","Siyabonga Mthembu (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Recorded in Johannesburg in one day with South African musicians, and it is the most direct continuation of the 1970s spiritual jazz lineage anyone has made this century.","listenFor":"The South African township harmony under the modal vamps gives it a lift that the American records do not have. The lineage runs through Abdullah Ibrahim as much as through Coltrane.","startTrack":"Mzwandile","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"wildflowers-loft-jazz-sessions","genreIds":["loft-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Various Artists","title":"Wildflowers: The New York Loft Jazz Sessions","recorded":1976,"released":1977,"label":"Douglas","catalogNo":"NBLP 7045-7049","personnel":["Sam Rivers (ts, fl)","David Murray (ts)","Anthony Braxton (reeds)","Julius Hemphill (as)","Randy Weston (p)","Andrew Cyrille (d)","Oliver Lake (as)"],"whyThisOne":"Five LPs recorded at Studio Rivbea over ten days in May 1976, capturing almost a hundred musicians in a room that musicians owned. It is the scene's only comprehensive document.","listenFor":"The sound of the room and the audience is left in. These are not concert recordings or studio dates — they are a loft, on a weeknight, with about forty people present.","startTrack":"Ismell (Sam Rivers Trio)","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"sam-rivers-streams","genreIds":["loft-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Sam Rivers","title":"Streams","recorded":1973,"released":1973,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"AS-9251","personnel":["Sam Rivers (ts, ss, fl, p)","Cecil McBee (b)","Norman Connors (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A single continuous performance from the Montreux festival where Rivers moves through four instruments without a break, structuring forty minutes of free playing entirely by ear.","listenFor":"Nothing is composed and it never sounds shapeless. Rivers builds sections, closes them and starts new ones — free improvisation with an architect's discipline.","startTrack":"Streams","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"david-murray-flowers-for-albert","genreIds":["loft-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"David Murray","title":"Flowers for Albert","recorded":1976,"released":1977,"label":"India Navigation","catalogNo":"IN 1026","personnel":["David Murray (ts)","Olu Dara (tp)","Fred Hopkins (b)","Phillip Wilson (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Murray at twenty-one, newly arrived in New York, with a tone that reaches back past free jazz to Ben Webster and Coleman Hawkins. The title track is an elegy for Albert Ayler.","listenFor":"His vibrato and his enormous low register are pre-bebop devices used inside free playing. The avant-garde reconnecting to the tenor tradition happens here.","startTrack":"Flowers for Albert","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"julius-hemphill-dogon-ad","genreIds":["loft-jazz","aacm-avant"],"tier":"core","artist":"Julius Hemphill","title":"Dogon A.D.","recorded":1972,"released":1972,"label":"Mbari","catalogNo":"MPC 500","personnel":["Julius Hemphill (as, fl)","Baikida Carroll (tp)","Abdul Wadud (cello)","Phillip Wilson (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Recorded in St. Louis for Hemphill's own label, with a cello instead of a bass and a rhythmic figure derived from Dogon material. It is one of the great records almost nobody heard until its reissue.","listenFor":"The title track's groove is in an odd meter and repeats hypnotically for over a quarter of an hour. Abdul Wadud's cello is playing bass, harmony and counter-melody at once.","startTrack":"Dogon A.D.","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"arthur-blythe-lenox-avenue-breakdown","genreIds":["loft-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Arthur Blythe","title":"Lenox Avenue Breakdown","recorded":1979,"released":1979,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"JC 35638","personnel":["Arthur Blythe (as)","James Newton (fl)","Bob Stewart (tuba)","James 'Blood' Ulmer (g)","Cecil McBee (b)","Jack DeJohnette (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A major label signed a loft-scene altoist and he made a record with tuba, flute and harmolodic guitar that grooves hard. It is the moment the scene briefly touched the mainstream.","listenFor":"Bob Stewart's tuba is the bass instrument and the hook. Forty years later the London scene built a whole aesthetic on that choice.","startTrack":"Down San Diego Way","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"air-air-time","genreIds":["loft-jazz","aacm-avant"],"tier":"core","artist":"Air","title":"Air Time","recorded":1977,"released":1978,"label":"Nessa","catalogNo":"N-12","personnel":["Henry Threadgill (as, ts, fl, hubkaphone)","Fred Hopkins (b)","Steve McCall (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The AACM trio working in New York's loft environment, with Threadgill's homemade 'hubkaphone' built from car hubcaps. Composition and free playing are indistinguishable here.","listenFor":"Fred Hopkins plays bass as a lead voice throughout. In a trio with no chordal instrument, he is carrying both the bottom and a large part of the melodic content.","startTrack":"Keep Right on Playing Thru the Mirror Over the Water","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"oliver-lake-holding-together","genreIds":["loft-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Oliver Lake","title":"Holding Together","recorded":1976,"released":1976,"label":"Black Saint","catalogNo":"BSR 0006","personnel":["Oliver Lake (as, fl)","Michael Gregory Jackson (g)","Fred Hopkins (b)","Paul Maddox (d)"],"whyThisOne":"BAG's approach applied in New York, with an electric guitar in a free context years before that became normal. Lake's writing is tighter and more angular than most of the scene's.","listenFor":"Michael Gregory Jackson's guitar is clean and rock-derived rather than jazz-toned, which puts this closer to what downtown would sound like a few years later.","startTrack":"Holding Together","difficulty":4,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"anthony-braxton-new-york-fall-1974","genreIds":["loft-jazz","aacm-avant"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Anthony Braxton","title":"New York, Fall 1974","recorded":1974,"released":1975,"label":"Arista","catalogNo":"AL 4032","personnel":["Anthony Braxton (reeds)","Kenny Wheeler (tp)","Dave Holland (b)","Jerome Cooper (d)","Julius Hemphill (as)","Oliver Lake (as)","Hamiet Bluiett (bs)"],"whyThisOne":"Braxton's first major-label album, made in New York during the loft years and including a saxophone quartet piece that anticipates the World Saxophone Quartet by two years.","listenFor":"The diagram titles are not affectation — each piece has a specified structure, and the quartet track's density comes from four horns executing written counterpoint.","startTrack":"No. 23B","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"frank-lowe-black-beings","genreIds":["loft-jazz","free-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Frank Lowe","title":"Black Beings","recorded":1973,"released":1973,"label":"ESP-Disk","catalogNo":"ESP 3013","personnel":["Frank Lowe (ts)","Joseph Jarman (as)","Raymond Lee Cheng (vln)","William Parker (b)","Rashid Sinan (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Loft-era energy playing at full intensity, with William Parker's first recorded appearance. It is uncompromising in a way that even the loft scene's other records are not.","listenFor":"The violin is used as a screaming upper voice rather than a lyrical one, and the whole record operates at a single very high level of intensity for its duration.","startTrack":"In Trane's Name","difficulty":5,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"bitches-brew","genreIds":["fusion"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Miles Davis","title":"Bitches Brew","recorded":1969,"released":1970,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"GP 26","personnel":["Miles Davis (tp)","Wayne Shorter (ss)","Bennie Maupin (bcl)","Chick Corea (elp)","Joe Zawinul (elp)","John McLaughlin (g)","Dave Holland (b)","Harvey Brooks (elb)","Jack DeJohnette (d)","Lenny White (d)","Airto Moreira (percussion)"],"whyThisOne":"The record that made fusion a commercial and critical event. Thirteen musicians, three keyboards, two drummers, and Teo Macero assembling the released tracks from tape splices after the fact.","listenFor":"The harmony barely moves for twenty minutes at a time. What changes is density and texture — musicians entering and dropping out — and the editing that Macero imposed afterwards.","startTrack":"Miles Runs the Voodoo Down","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"miles-davis-in-a-silent-way","genreIds":["fusion"],"tier":"core","artist":"Miles Davis","title":"In a Silent Way","recorded":1969,"released":1969,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CS 9875","personnel":["Miles Davis (tp)","Wayne Shorter (ss)","John McLaughlin (g)","Herbie Hancock (elp)","Chick Corea (elp)","Joe Zawinul (org)","Dave Holland (b)","Tony Williams (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Quieter and stranger than Bitches Brew, and the first place the electric approach fully works. Two side-long pieces built by Macero from short takes, with Tony Williams playing only the hi-hat for long stretches.","listenFor":"The opening and closing sections of the title suite are the same take, used twice. Macero built the record's shape in the edit, and Davis approved it.","startTrack":"In a Silent Way / It's About That Time","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"weather-report-heavy-weather","genreIds":["fusion"],"tier":"core","artist":"Weather Report","title":"Heavy Weather","recorded":1976,"released":1977,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"PC 34418","personnel":["Joe Zawinul (keys)","Wayne Shorter (ss, ts)","Jaco Pastorius (elb)","Alex Acuña (d)","Manolo Badrena (percussion)"],"whyThisOne":"Fusion at its most melodically generous, with Jaco Pastorius in the band and 'Birdland' as an actual hit. The group had a rule that everyone soloed all the time and nobody soloed, and you can hear it work.","listenFor":"Pastorius plays fretless electric bass with harmonics and a singing tone that had no precedent. On 'Teen Town' he plays bass and drums both.","startTrack":"Birdland","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"mahavishnu-inner-mounting-flame","genreIds":["fusion"],"tier":"core","artist":"Mahavishnu Orchestra","title":"The Inner Mounting Flame","recorded":1971,"released":1971,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"KC 31067","personnel":["John McLaughlin (g)","Jerry Goodman (vln)","Jan Hammer (keys)","Rick Laird (b)","Billy Cobham (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The loudest, fastest and most technically aggressive fusion band, playing odd meters at rock volume with a distorted guitar and an amplified violin. It genuinely frightened people in 1971.","listenFor":"'Dance of Maya' moves between 20/8 and a shuffle blues. Count the opening and you will fail; feel it and it works.","startTrack":"Meeting of the Spirits","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"return-to-forever-romantic-warrior","genreIds":["fusion"],"tier":"core","artist":"Return to Forever","title":"Romantic Warrior","recorded":1976,"released":1976,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"PC 34076","personnel":["Chick Corea (keys)","Al Di Meola (g)","Stanley Clarke (elb)","Lenny White (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Fusion's most unembarrassed virtuoso record, with a medieval concept and playing of frightening precision. It is the high-water mark of the technique-forward wing and the point where the backlash became inevitable.","listenFor":"Everything is executed in unison at speed — guitar, bass and keyboard playing the same intricate line. The ensemble precision is the achievement, not the solos.","startTrack":"Medieval Overture","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"tony-williams-lifetime-emergency","genreIds":["fusion"],"tier":"core","artist":"The Tony Williams Lifetime","title":"Emergency!","recorded":1969,"released":1969,"label":"Polydor","catalogNo":"25-3001","personnel":["Tony Williams (d, voc)","John McLaughlin (g)","Larry Young (org)"],"whyThisOne":"Recorded months before Bitches Brew and considerably more violent. Organ, distorted guitar and Williams playing at full volume, badly recorded, and all the better for it.","listenFor":"The recording is genuinely poor — distorted and muddy — and the band plays through it. This is jazz musicians choosing rock's volume without rock's production.","startTrack":"Emergency","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"miles-davis-on-the-corner","genreIds":["fusion","jazz-funk"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Miles Davis","title":"On the Corner","recorded":1972,"released":1972,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"KC 31906","personnel":["Miles Davis (tp)","Michael Henderson (elb)","John McLaughlin (g)","Herbie Hancock (keys)","Bennie Maupin (bcl)","Badal Roy (tabla)","Jack DeJohnette (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Reviled on release and now treated as prophetic. It is an unbroken funk machine with tabla and sitar, aimed explicitly at a young Black audience that Davis felt he had lost.","listenFor":"The bass line barely changes for the whole record and the trumpet is buried in the mix. Davis is making a groove record, and the soloing is a texture inside it.","startTrack":"On the Corner","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jaco-pastorius-jaco-pastorius","genreIds":["fusion"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Jaco Pastorius","title":"Jaco Pastorius","recorded":1975,"released":1976,"label":"Epic","catalogNo":"PE 33949","personnel":["Jaco Pastorius (elb)","Herbie Hancock (keys)","Wayne Shorter (ss)","Michael Brecker (ts)","Don Alias (percussion)","Sam & Dave (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"A debut that reintroduced the electric bass as a lead instrument, covering Charlie Parker, playing solo bass with harmonics, and putting a soul vocal duo on a jazz record.","listenFor":"'Portrait of Tracy' is solo fretless bass played almost entirely in artificial harmonics. Nobody had made the instrument sound like that.","startTrack":"Donna Lee","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"soft-machine-third","genreIds":["fusion"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Soft Machine","title":"Third","recorded":1970,"released":1970,"label":"CBS","catalogNo":"66246","personnel":["Mike Ratledge (org, p)","Elton Dean (as, saxello)","Hugh Hopper (elb)","Robert Wyatt (d, voc)"],"whyThisOne":"The British route into fusion, coming from psychedelic rock rather than from jazz. Four side-long pieces, tape loops, fuzz organ, and a rhythmic approach that owes more to minimalism than to funk.","listenFor":"'Out-Bloody-Rageous' opens with tape loops that predate most electronic jazz by two decades. The Canterbury scene arrived at fusion independently.","startTrack":"Facelift","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"herbie-hancock-head-hunters","genreIds":["jazz-funk"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Herbie Hancock","title":"Head Hunters","recorded":1973,"released":1973,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"KC 32731","personnel":["Herbie Hancock (keys, clavinet, synths)","Bennie Maupin (reeds)","Paul Jackson (elb)","Harvey Mason (d)","Bill Summers (percussion)"],"whyThisOne":"Hancock deliberately built a band around a groove rather than around soloing, and it became one of the best-selling jazz albums ever made. Everything in jazz-funk after this is measured against it.","listenFor":"'Chameleon' is one bass line for fifteen minutes. The clavinet and the synth bass do all the harmonic work and nothing modulates.","startTrack":"Chameleon","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"donald-byrd-black-byrd","genreIds":["jazz-funk"],"tier":"core","artist":"Donald Byrd","title":"Black Byrd","recorded":1972,"released":1973,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"BN.LA047-F","personnel":["Donald Byrd (tp)","Larry Mizell (prod, arr)","Fonce Mizell (prod)","Joe Sample (keys)","Chuck Rainey (elb)","Harvey Mason (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Blue Note's best-selling album of all time at the point of release, produced by the Mizell brothers with a hard bop trumpeter in front. It was treated as a betrayal at the time and has been mined by producers ever since.","listenFor":"Byrd's trumpet is one colour among many; the arrangement, the vocal harmonies and the bass line are the composition. That inversion is what upset the critics.","startTrack":"Flight Time","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"roy-ayers-everybody-loves-the-sunshine","genreIds":["jazz-funk"],"tier":"core","artist":"Roy Ayers Ubiquity","title":"Everybody Loves the Sunshine","recorded":1976,"released":1976,"label":"Polydor","catalogNo":"PD-1-6070","personnel":["Roy Ayers (vib, voc, keys)","William Allen (elb)","Justo Almario (ts, fl)","Philip Woo (keys)"],"whyThisOne":"The title track is one of the most sampled recordings in existence, and the album around it is a study in doing almost nothing with total conviction.","listenFor":"The title track has essentially two chords and a mist of Rhodes and vibraphone. The mood is the content, and it is why hip-hop producers kept returning to it.","startTrack":"Everybody Loves the Sunshine","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"les-mccann-eddie-harris-swiss-movement","genreIds":["jazz-funk","soul-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Les McCann & Eddie Harris","title":"Swiss Movement","recorded":1969,"released":1969,"label":"Atlantic","catalogNo":"SD 1537","personnel":["Les McCann (p, voc)","Eddie Harris (ts)","Benny Bailey (tp)","Leroy Vinnegar (b)","Donald Dean (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Recorded at Montreux in 1969 by musicians who had barely rehearsed, and 'Compared to What' became a protest anthem. It is jazz-funk before the studio production arrived.","listenFor":"Eddie Harris is playing an electrified tenor through a Varitone attachment. The horn's processed tone is a deliberate technological choice, not a studio effect.","startTrack":"Compared to What","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"lonnie-liston-smith-expansions","genreIds":["jazz-funk","spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Lonnie Liston Smith and the Cosmic Echoes","title":"Expansions","recorded":1974,"released":1975,"label":"Flying Dutchman","catalogNo":"BDL1-0934","personnel":["Lonnie Liston Smith (keys)","Donald Smith (voc, fl)","Cecil McBee (b)","David Hubbard (ts)","Art Gore (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The bridge between spiritual jazz and the dancefloor: modal, echoing, mystical, and built on a bass line that London DJs were still playing fifteen years later.","listenFor":"The title track's bass figure and the electric piano wash. This is the specific sound that acid jazz set out to recreate.","startTrack":"Expansions","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jimmy-smith-root-down","genreIds":["jazz-funk","soul-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Jimmy Smith","title":"Root Down","recorded":1972,"released":1972,"label":"Verve","catalogNo":"V6-8806","personnel":["Jimmy Smith (org)","Arthur Adams (g)","Wilton Felder (elb)","Paul Humphrey (d)","Buck Clarke (congas)"],"whyThisOne":"The organ pioneer, live in Los Angeles in 1972, playing over a hard funk rhythm section rather than a swing one. The Beastie Boys built a track around it two decades later.","listenFor":"Smith's vocabulary has not changed since 1958; the rhythm section underneath it has changed completely. That is the whole soul-jazz-to-jazz-funk transition in one record.","startTrack":"Root Down (And Get It)","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bob-james-two","genreIds":["jazz-funk","smooth-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Bob James","title":"Two","recorded":1975,"released":1975,"label":"CTI","catalogNo":"CTI 6057","personnel":["Bob James (keys, arr)","Grover Washington Jr. (ss)","Eric Gale (g)","Gary King (elb)","Idris Muhammad (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Historically important for a reason James did not intend: 'Take Me to the Mardi Gras' contains a bell-and-drum break that became one of hip-hop's foundational samples.","listenFor":"The break at the start of 'Take Me to the Mardi Gras' — sleigh bells and drums — turns up in dozens of rap records. Here it is, in context, doing nothing much.","startTrack":"Take Me to the Mardi Gras","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"gary-bartz-ntu-troop-harlem-bush-music","genreIds":["jazz-funk","spiritual-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Gary Bartz NTU Troop","title":"Harlem Bush Music – Taifa","recorded":1970,"released":1971,"label":"Milestone","catalogNo":"MSP 9032","personnel":["Gary Bartz (as, ss, voc)","Andy Bey (voc, p)","Ron Carter (b)","Harold White (d)","Juni Booth (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Bartz had just left Miles Davis and made a record with chanted Swahili, political text and a groove. It sits precisely between spiritual jazz's intent and jazz-funk's body.","listenFor":"Andy Bey's voice is used as an instrument in unison with the alto, and the material is explicitly about Black self-determination rather than about transcendence.","startTrack":"Uhuru Sasa","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"idris-muhammad-power-of-soul","genreIds":["jazz-funk"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Idris Muhammad","title":"Power of Soul","recorded":1974,"released":1974,"label":"Kudu","catalogNo":"KU-17","personnel":["Idris Muhammad (d)","Grover Washington Jr. (ts, ss)","Bob James (keys, arr)","Joe Beck (g)","Gary King (elb)"],"whyThisOne":"A New Orleans drummer leading a CTI/Kudu session, which means the funk has a second-line looseness that the New York studio records lack.","listenFor":"Muhammad's snare placement is slightly behind the grid throughout — the New Orleans feel inside a mid-1970s studio production.","startTrack":"Piece of Mind","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"ornette-coleman-dancing-in-your-head","genreIds":["free-funk"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Ornette Coleman","title":"Dancing in Your Head","recorded":1975,"released":1977,"label":"A&M Horizon","catalogNo":"SP-722","personnel":["Ornette Coleman (as)","Bern Nix (g)","Charles Ellerbee (g)","Rudy McDaniel [Jamaaladeen Tacuma] (elb)","Ronald Shannon Jackson (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Prime Time's arrival. Two guitars, electric bass and drums playing a hard groove while a simple, repeated melody is bent in every direction on top. Also includes Coleman playing with Moroccan musicians in Joujouka.","listenFor":"The tune is one short phrase, repeated for over twenty minutes. Everything else — five people playing melody simultaneously — happens around and against it.","startTrack":"Theme from a Symphony (Variation One)","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"ornette-coleman-of-human-feelings","genreIds":["free-funk"],"tier":"core","artist":"Ornette Coleman and Prime Time","title":"Of Human Feelings","recorded":1979,"released":1982,"label":"Antilles","catalogNo":"AN 2001","personnel":["Ornette Coleman (as)","Bern Nix (g)","Charles Ellerbee (g)","Jamaaladeen Tacuma (elb)","Calvin Weston (d)","Denardo Coleman (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Prime Time fully formed: doubled guitars, doubled basses, doubled drummers, and one of the first jazz records recorded digitally. Harmolodics as a working band language rather than a theory.","listenFor":"There is no accompanist. Every instrument is playing a melodic line, and the groove emerges from six independent melodies happening to coincide rhythmically.","startTrack":"Sleep Talk","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"james-blood-ulmer-tales-of-captain-black","genreIds":["free-funk"],"tier":"core","artist":"James Blood Ulmer","title":"Tales of Captain Black","recorded":1978,"released":1979,"label":"Artists House","catalogNo":"AH 7","personnel":["James Blood Ulmer (g)","Ornette Coleman (as)","Jamaaladeen Tacuma (elb)","Denardo Coleman (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Ulmer's debut with Coleman as sideman, and the clearest recording of harmolodic guitar. His tuning and attack have no obvious ancestor in either jazz or blues guitar.","listenFor":"Ulmer tunes his guitar so that all the strings are related to one pitch, which makes chords ambiguous by design. Nothing sounds like it is in a key.","startTrack":"Theme from Captain Black","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"ronald-shannon-jackson-eye-on-you","genreIds":["free-funk"],"tier":"core","artist":"Ronald Shannon Jackson and the Decoding Society","title":"Eye on You","recorded":1980,"released":1980,"label":"About Time","catalogNo":"AT 1001","personnel":["Ronald Shannon Jackson (d, comp)","Vernon Reid (g)","Byard Lancaster (as)","Melvin Gibbs (elb)"],"whyThisOne":"Jackson had played with Ornette Coleman, Cecil Taylor and Albert Ayler, and the Decoding Society is what he built out of all three plus Texas funk. Vernon Reid is on guitar, years before Living Colour.","listenFor":"Jackson's drumming is a second-line New Orleans feel underneath free playing. He grew up in Fort Worth and it shows in every bar.","startTrack":"When Souls Speak","difficulty":4,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"ornette-coleman-song-x","genreIds":["free-funk","contemporary-creative"],"tier":"core","artist":"Pat Metheny & Ornette Coleman","title":"Song X","recorded":1985,"released":1986,"label":"Geffen","catalogNo":"GHS 24096","personnel":["Ornette Coleman (as, vln)","Pat Metheny (g, guitar synth)","Charlie Haden (b)","Jack DeJohnette (d)","Denardo Coleman (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A guitarist with a huge mainstream audience made a Coleman record on a rock label and did not soften it. It confused Metheny's listeners and introduced harmolodics to a lot of them anyway.","listenFor":"Metheny's guitar synth is used for noise and texture rather than for melody. He is not visiting the idiom politely.","startTrack":"Song X","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jamaaladeen-tacuma-showstopper","genreIds":["free-funk"],"tier":"core","artist":"Jamaaladeen Tacuma","title":"Show Stopper","recorded":1982,"released":1983,"label":"Gramavision","catalogNo":"GR 8301","personnel":["Jamaaladeen Tacuma (elb)","Cornell Rochester (d)","Bill Bruford (d)","Julius Hemphill (as)","Olu Dara (tp)"],"whyThisOne":"Prime Time's bassist as a leader, treating electric bass as a lead melodic voice with a bright, trebly tone that nobody else used. The writing is funkier and more song-shaped than Coleman's.","listenFor":"The bass is mixed as the lead instrument and plays in the upper register throughout. The bottom end is being handled by the kick drum alone.","startTrack":"Dancing in Your Head","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"last-exit-last-exit","genreIds":["free-funk","downtown"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Last Exit","title":"Last Exit","recorded":1986,"released":1986,"label":"Enemy","catalogNo":"EMY 101","personnel":["Peter Brötzmann (ts)","Sonny Sharrock (g)","Bill Laswell (elb)","Ronald Shannon Jackson (d)"],"whyThisOne":"European free jazz, harmolodic drums, noise guitar and a producer's bass, playing at metal volume with no material prepared at all. It is the loudest music in this guide.","listenFor":"Nothing is composed. Four musicians with completely different backgrounds improvising at maximum volume, and it coheres — barely, and on purpose.","startTrack":"Discharge","difficulty":5,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"decoding-society-mandance","genreIds":["free-funk"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Ronald Shannon Jackson and the Decoding Society","title":"Mandance","recorded":1982,"released":1982,"label":"Antilles","catalogNo":"AN 1180","personnel":["Ronald Shannon Jackson (d, comp)","Vernon Reid (g)","Zane Massey (ts)","Henry Scott (tp)","Melvin Gibbs (elb)"],"whyThisOne":"Jackson's compositions at their most intricate, with written horn lines over grooves in unusual meters. It is more composed than most free funk and consequently harder to play.","listenFor":"The horn writing is in unison and very fast, over a groove that keeps shifting its subdivision. The band has to be right together or the whole thing collapses.","startTrack":"Belly Button","difficulty":4,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"sonny-sharrock-ask-the-ages","genreIds":["free-funk","free-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Sonny Sharrock","title":"Ask the Ages","recorded":1991,"released":1991,"label":"Axiom","catalogNo":"314-512 070-2","personnel":["Sonny Sharrock (g)","Pharoah Sanders (ts)","Charnett Moffett (b)","Elvin Jones (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Sharrock's last major record, produced by Bill Laswell, with Pharoah Sanders and Elvin Jones. It is a Coltrane-quartet record with an electric guitarist in Coltrane's chair, and it works.","listenFor":"Sharrock plays with sustained feedback and slide as melodic devices. Elvin Jones plays exactly as he did in 1965 and the two languages fit.","startTrack":"Promises Kept","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"grover-washington-winelight","genreIds":["smooth-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Grover Washington Jr.","title":"Winelight","recorded":1980,"released":1980,"label":"Elektra","catalogNo":"6E-305","personnel":["Grover Washington Jr. (ss, as, ts)","Bill Withers (voc)","Marcus Miller (elb)","Eric Gale (g)","Ralph MacDonald (percussion)"],"whyThisOne":"The record that defined the format, made by a musician who could genuinely play. 'Just the Two of Us' with Bill Withers was a top-three single and the album won two Grammys.","listenFor":"Marcus Miller's bass playing is far more interesting than the format requires. Underneath the gloss there is a serious rhythm section doing careful work.","startTrack":"Winelight","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"crusaders-street-life","genreIds":["smooth-jazz","jazz-funk"],"tier":"core","artist":"The Crusaders","title":"Street Life","recorded":1979,"released":1979,"label":"MCA","catalogNo":"MCA-3094","personnel":["Joe Sample (keys)","Wilton Felder (ts, elb)","Stix Hooper (d)","Randy Crawford (voc)","Barry Finnerty (g)"],"whyThisOne":"The Jazz Crusaders were a hard bop band in 1961; by 1979 they had dropped the 'Jazz' and had a hit single with a vocalist. The whole crossover arc is visible in one group's discography.","listenFor":"Wilton Felder's tenor still phrases like a hard bop player. The arrangement around him has changed completely and he has not.","startTrack":"Street Life","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"george-benson-breezin","genreIds":["smooth-jazz","jazz-funk"],"tier":"core","artist":"George Benson","title":"Breezin'","recorded":1976,"released":1976,"label":"Warner Bros.","catalogNo":"BS 2919","personnel":["George Benson (g, voc)","Ronnie Foster (keys)","Jorge Dalto (keys)","Phil Upchurch (g)","Stanley Banks (elb)","Harvey Mason (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The first jazz album to be certified platinum, made by a guitarist who had been Jack McDuff's sideman at nineteen. It is where a formidable bebop player chose the crossover route deliberately.","listenFor":"Benson sings in unison with his guitar solos, an octave apart. It became a signature and it is technically much harder than it sounds.","startTrack":"Breezin'","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"david-sanborn-voyeur","genreIds":["smooth-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"David Sanborn","title":"Voyeur","recorded":1980,"released":1981,"label":"Warner Bros.","catalogNo":"BSK 3546","personnel":["David Sanborn (as)","Marcus Miller (elb)","Buzz Feiten (g)","Steve Gadd (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Sanborn's alto tone — piercing, blues-derived, instantly identifiable — became the most imitated sound in the format. This is the record where it locked into place.","listenFor":"The tone comes from playing rhythm and blues behind singers, not from Charlie Parker. It is a different lineage and the intensity in it is real.","startTrack":"Let's Just Say Goodbye","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"spyro-gyra-morning-dance","genreIds":["smooth-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Spyro Gyra","title":"Morning Dance","recorded":1979,"released":1979,"label":"Infinity","catalogNo":"INF 9004","personnel":["Jay Beckenstein (as, ss)","Jeremy Wall (keys)","Tom Schuman (keys)","Chet Catallo (g)"],"whyThisOne":"The commercial template in its purest form: a steel-drum hook, an unvarying mid-tempo groove and a saxophone melody you cannot dislodge. It sold over a million copies.","listenFor":"Nothing is at stake at any point, by design. Hearing what has been engineered out is more informative than hearing what is there.","startTrack":"Morning Dance","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"kenny-g-breathless","genreIds":["smooth-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Kenny G","title":"Breathless","recorded":1992,"released":1992,"label":"Arista","catalogNo":"18646","personnel":["Kenny G (ss, as, ts)","Walter Afanasieff (prod, keys)"],"whyThisOne":"One of the best-selling instrumental albums in history and the record that made 'smooth jazz' an insult. It belongs here because pretending it does not exist explains nothing about how the genre worked.","listenFor":"The melodies are written to be memorable on one hearing and the improvisation is minimal. Whatever else it is, it is expertly built for its purpose.","startTrack":"Forever in Love","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"wes-montgomery-a-day-in-the-life","genreIds":["smooth-jazz","jazz-funk"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Wes Montgomery","title":"A Day in the Life","recorded":1967,"released":1967,"label":"A&M/CTI","catalogNo":"SP-3001","personnel":["Wes Montgomery (g)","Don Sebesky (arr)","Herbie Hancock (p)","Ron Carter (b)","Grady Tate (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Creed Taylor put one of the great jazz guitarists in front of a string orchestra playing Beatles covers, and it sold. This is the ancestral form of the smooth jazz proposition.","listenFor":"Montgomery's octave technique and thumb-picked tone are entirely intact; the setting around them is pop. The argument about whether that is a compromise starts here.","startTrack":"A Day in the Life","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bob-james-earl-klugh-one-on-one","genreIds":["smooth-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Bob James & Earl Klugh","title":"One on One","recorded":1979,"released":1979,"label":"Tappan Zee/Columbia","catalogNo":"JC 36241","personnel":["Bob James (keys)","Earl Klugh (g)","Gary King (elb)","Harvey Mason (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Nylon-string guitar and electric piano as a duo-led format, which is the most chamber-like the genre gets. It won a Grammy and set the acoustic-guitar wing of the format going.","listenFor":"Klugh plays fingerstyle nylon-string, a classical technique, in a pop-funk setting. The combination is more unusual than it sounds now.","startTrack":"Kari","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"pat-metheny-still-life-talking","genreIds":["smooth-jazz","fusion"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Pat Metheny Group","title":"Still Life (Talking)","recorded":1987,"released":1987,"label":"Geffen","catalogNo":"GHS 24145","personnel":["Pat Metheny (g, guitar synth)","Lyle Mays (keys)","Steve Rodby (b)","Paul Wertico (d)","Armando Marçal (percussion)","Mark Ledford (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"The most musically ambitious record that regularly gets shelved next to smooth jazz, which says something about how blunt the category is. Brazilian rhythm, wordless vocals and genuinely complex writing.","listenFor":"'Last Train Home' has an ostinato that never stops and a melody that arrives late and leaves early. It is doing what smooth jazz does and doing it with real compositional care.","startTrack":"Last Train Home","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"brand-new-heavies-brand-new-heavies","genreIds":["acid-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"The Brand New Heavies","title":"The Brand New Heavies","recorded":1990,"released":1990,"label":"Acid Jazz","catalogNo":"JAZID LP 013","personnel":["Simon Bartholomew (g)","Andrew Levy (elb)","Jan Kincaid (d)","N'Dea Davenport (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"The scene's best band playing original material rather than pastiche, with a rhythm section tight enough to pass for 1973. It is the most listenable entry point to a movement that was often more about curation than composition.","listenFor":"The drums and bass are recorded dry and up-front in the 1970s manner, with no 1990s reverb. The production is as much a period reference as the playing.","startTrack":"Never Stop","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"incognito-positivity","genreIds":["acid-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Incognito","title":"Positivity","recorded":1993,"released":1993,"label":"Talkin' Loud","catalogNo":"518 214-2","personnel":["Jean-Paul 'Bluey' Maunick (g, prod)","Maysa Leak (voc)","Peter Hinds (keys)","Richard Bull (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Bluey Maunick's writing is the most durable in the idiom — actual songs rather than grooves with a topline — and this is the record where the band's own material outgrew its influences.","listenFor":"The horn arrangements and the layered backing vocals are doing the work of a much bigger band. It is a studio construction with live playing at its core.","startTrack":"Still a Friend of Mine","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"us3-hand-on-the-torch","genreIds":["acid-jazz","jazz-rap"],"tier":"core","artist":"Us3","title":"Hand on the Torch","recorded":1992,"released":1993,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"CDP 7 80883 2","personnel":["Geoff Wilkinson (prod)","Mel Simpson (prod)","Rahsaan Kelly (MC)","Gerard Presencer (tp)"],"whyThisOne":"Blue Note gave a British production duo unrestricted access to its master tapes, and the result was the label's first platinum record. It is the clearest case of the sampled and the sampling generations sharing a contract.","listenFor":"'Cantaloop' is built on Herbie Hancock's 'Cantaloupe Island' from Empyrean Isles, with a live trumpet playing over the loop. Both records are in this guide.","startTrack":"Cantaloop (Flip Fantasia)","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"james-taylor-quartet-mission-impossible","genreIds":["acid-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"The James Taylor Quartet","title":"Mission Impossible","recorded":1986,"released":1987,"label":"Re-Elect the President","catalogNo":"TAYLOR 1","personnel":["James Taylor (Hammond org)","David Taylor (elb)","Allan Crockford (g)","Simon Howard (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Hammond organ instrumentals covering TV themes, released before 'acid jazz' had a name. It is the scene's rawest and most literal reconstruction of 1960s soul jazz.","listenFor":"There is essentially no improvisation. It is a groove band playing arrangements, which is what most of acid jazz actually was.","startTrack":"Blow Up","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"jamiroquai-return-of-the-space-cowboy","genreIds":["acid-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Jamiroquai","title":"The Return of the Space Cowboy","recorded":1994,"released":1994,"label":"Sony Soho Square","catalogNo":"477813 2","personnel":["Jay Kay (voc)","Toby Smith (keys)","Stuart Zender (elb)","Wallis Buchanan (didgeridoo)"],"whyThisOne":"The most commercially enormous thing acid jazz produced, and the second album is where the band's playing caught up with its ambition. Stuart Zender's bass is the reason it holds up.","listenFor":"The bass lines are genuinely difficult and carry the harmonic content. Strip the vocals and this is a jazz-funk instrumental band.","startTrack":"Space Cowboy","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"galliano-a-joyful-noise","genreIds":["acid-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Galliano","title":"A Joyful Noise Unto the Creator","recorded":1992,"released":1992,"label":"Talkin' Loud","catalogNo":"512 197-2","personnel":["Rob Gallagher (voc)","Constantine Weir (voc)","Crispin Taylor (d)","Ernie McKone (elb)"],"whyThisOne":"The first act signed to Talkin' Loud, combining spoken-word and rap with live jazz-funk and an explicitly spiritual bent. It is closer to the Last Poets than to a dance record.","listenFor":"The rapping is British, unhurried and consciously not American in cadence — one of the earliest examples of a UK voice in this space.","startTrack":"Prince of Peace","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"rebirth-of-cool-vol1","genreIds":["acid-jazz","nu-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Various Artists","title":"Rebirth of Cool Volume One","recorded":1991,"released":1991,"label":"4th & Broadway","catalogNo":"BRCD 573","personnel":["compiled by Gilles Peterson and Norman Jay"],"whyThisOne":"The compilation series that defined the sensibility, mixing hip-hop, jazz, dub and soul with no regard for genre boundaries. It shows acid jazz as a listening position rather than a sound.","listenFor":"The sequencing is the argument. Records that had no relationship to each other are placed side by side until a common thread becomes audible.","startTrack":"Various","difficulty":1,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"corduroy-out-of-here","genreIds":["acid-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Corduroy","title":"Out of Here","recorded":1994,"released":1994,"label":"Acid Jazz","catalogNo":"JAZID CD 90","personnel":["Ben Addison (d, voc)","Scott Addison (org, voc)","Simon Nelson-Smith (g)","Richard Searle (elb)"],"whyThisOne":"The mod end of the scene — Hammond-driven, sharp-suited, and closer to 1960s British R&B than to American soul jazz. It documents acid jazz as a British subculture rather than a revival.","listenFor":"The organ sound and the arrangements come from Georgie Fame and Brian Auger rather than from Jimmy Smith. The lineage is local.","startTrack":"Mo' Chuvva","difficulty":1,"confidence":"low","note":"Acid Jazz label catalogue numbering from this period is inconsistently documented; details should be verified."},{"id":"roy-ayers-wake-up","genreIds":["acid-jazz","jazz-funk"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Roy Ayers","title":"Wake Up","recorded":1989,"released":1989,"label":"Ichiban","catalogNo":"ICH 1064","personnel":["Roy Ayers (vib, voc, keys)"],"whyThisOne":"The living ancestor recording during the acid jazz years and being adopted by the scene that had rediscovered him. Ayers toured with these bands and the traffic was genuinely two-way.","listenFor":"The production is late-1980s and the playing is unchanged from 1976. Hearing the mismatch clarifies what the revival was and was not reproducing.","startTrack":"Wake Up","difficulty":1,"confidence":"low","note":"Ayers' late-1980s discography on small labels is patchily documented; verify before citing."},{"id":"tribe-called-quest-low-end-theory","genreIds":["jazz-rap"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"A Tribe Called Quest","title":"The Low End Theory","recorded":1991,"released":1991,"label":"Jive","catalogNo":"1418-2-J","personnel":["Q-Tip (MC, prod)","Phife Dawg (MC)","Ali Shaheed Muhammad (DJ)","Ron Carter (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The record that proved jazz samples and rapping were not a novelty pairing. Sparse, bass-forward, and with Ron Carter playing upright on one track at Q-Tip's request.","listenFor":"There is almost nothing in the mix — a bass line, drums, one sample, two voices. The restraint is what makes the low end sound so enormous.","startTrack":"Excursions","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"gang-starr-daily-operation","genreIds":["jazz-rap"],"tier":"core","artist":"Gang Starr","title":"Daily Operation","recorded":1992,"released":1992,"label":"Chrysalis","catalogNo":"F2 21910","personnel":["Guru (MC)","DJ Premier (prod, DJ)"],"whyThisOne":"DJ Premier's chopping technique at its sharpest — samples cut into fragments and reassembled rather than looped whole. Guru's flat, unhurried delivery is a rhythmic instrument in itself.","listenFor":"Premier takes half a second of a jazz record and repitches it across a bar. The source is often unrecognisable, which is the point: he is composing, not quoting.","startTrack":"Take It Personal","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"digable-planets-reachin","genreIds":["jazz-rap"],"tier":"core","artist":"Digable Planets","title":"Reachin' (A New Refutation of Time and Space)","recorded":1992,"released":1993,"label":"Pendulum","catalogNo":"61414-2","personnel":["Butterfly (MC, prod)","Ladybug Mecca (MC)","Doodlebug (MC)"],"whyThisOne":"The most explicitly jazz-referencing rap record of the era, down to the personas and the Art Blakey samples. It won a Grammy and made the aesthetic briefly mainstream.","listenFor":"'Rebirth of Slick' is built on a loop from Art Blakey's 'Stretchin''. The upright bass and the brushed drums are doing the same job they did in 1958.","startTrack":"Rebirth of Slick (Cool Like Dat)","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"guru-jazzmatazz-vol1","genreIds":["jazz-rap"],"tier":"core","artist":"Guru","title":"Jazzmatazz, Volume 1","recorded":1992,"released":1993,"label":"Chrysalis","catalogNo":"F2 21998","personnel":["Guru (MC)","Donald Byrd (tp)","Roy Ayers (vib)","Lonnie Liston Smith (keys)","Branford Marsalis (ts)","N'Dea Davenport (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"The first sustained attempt to put live jazz musicians and a rapper in a studio together rather than sampling them. Donald Byrd, Roy Ayers and Lonnie Liston Smith — the men whose records had been sampled — playing in person.","listenFor":"The difference between a sampled loop and a live rhythm section is immediately audible, and not always in the live section's favour. That tension is the record's subject.","startTrack":"Loungin'","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"pete-rock-cl-smooth-mecca","genreIds":["jazz-rap"],"tier":"core","artist":"Pete Rock & CL Smooth","title":"Mecca and the Soul Brother","recorded":1992,"released":1992,"label":"Elektra","catalogNo":"60948-2","personnel":["Pete Rock (prod, DJ)","CL Smooth (MC)"],"whyThisOne":"The richest horn-sample production of the era, built from soul jazz and hard bop records layered several deep. 'They Reminisce Over You' is an elegy built from a Tom Scott saxophone figure.","listenFor":"Pete Rock stacks multiple horn samples so the result sounds like a section that never existed. It is arranging by other means.","startTrack":"They Reminisce Over You (T.R.O.Y.)","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"robert-glasper-black-radio","genreIds":["jazz-rap","la-beat"],"tier":"core","artist":"Robert Glasper Experiment","title":"Black Radio","recorded":2011,"released":2012,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"509996 06777 2 3","personnel":["Robert Glasper (keys)","Casey Benjamin (as, vocoder)","Derrick Hodge (elb)","Chris Dave (d)","Erykah Badu (voc)","Bilal (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"A jazz pianist made an R&B and hip-hop record with a jazz band's interactivity, and it won a Grammy. It established the template that most of the current jazz-adjacent mainstream works from.","listenFor":"Chris Dave's drumming is the whole argument — an acoustic kit playing with the timing displacement of a programmed Dilla beat, live.","startTrack":"Afro Blue","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"madlib-shades-of-blue","genreIds":["jazz-rap"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Madlib","title":"Shades of Blue","recorded":2002,"released":2003,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"7243 5 39387 2 8","personnel":["Madlib (prod, multi-instruments)"],"whyThisOne":"Blue Note handed a producer its vaults for a second time, and Madlib both remixed the originals and re-recorded material himself playing every instrument. It is a producer's reading of a catalogue.","listenFor":"Some tracks are remixes and some are Madlib playing all the parts under a false band name. Working out which is which is genuinely difficult.","startTrack":"Slim's Return","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"miles-davis-doo-bop","genreIds":["jazz-rap"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Miles Davis","title":"Doo-Bop","recorded":1991,"released":1992,"label":"Warner Bros.","catalogNo":"9 26938-2","personnel":["Miles Davis (tp)","Easy Mo Bee (prod, MC)","J.R. (MC)"],"whyThisOne":"Davis's last album, unfinished at his death and completed by producer Easy Mo Bee from existing trumpet takes. It is not good, and it is historically unavoidable — the last thing he chose to chase was hip-hop.","listenFor":"The trumpet playing is recognisably Davis and the production around it was assembled without him. The seam is audible and worth hearing.","startTrack":"The Doo-Bop Song","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"the-roots-do-you-want-more","genreIds":["jazz-rap"],"tier":"deep","artist":"The Roots","title":"Do You Want More?!!!??!","recorded":1994,"released":1995,"label":"DGC","catalogNo":"DGCD-24708","personnel":["Black Thought (MC)","Questlove (d)","Hub (elb)","Scott Storch (keys)","Rahzel (beatbox)","Cassandra Wilson (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"A rap group with no samples at all, playing everything live, with Cassandra Wilson and Steve Coleman guesting. It inverts the whole jazz rap premise — instead of sampling jazz, they play it.","listenFor":"Questlove's drumming reproduces the feel of a sampled loop on an acoustic kit, deliberately imperfect. This is where the technique that Chris Dave later refined begins.","startTrack":"Proceed","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"nils-petter-molvaer-khmer","genreIds":["nu-jazz"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Nils Petter Molvær","title":"Khmer","recorded":1996,"released":1997,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 1560","personnel":["Nils Petter Molvær (tp, programming)","Eivind Aarset (g)","Rune Arnesen (d)","Ulf Holand (prod)"],"whyThisOne":"ECM's trumpet lyricism placed over drum programming and dub bass, and the record that made electronic jazz a European proposition rather than a novelty. It sold far beyond the label's usual reach.","listenFor":"The trumpet is played sparsely and drenched in delay, so a single note becomes a texture. The electronics are not accompaniment — they are the composition.","startTrack":"Khmer","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bugge-wesseltoft-new-conception","genreIds":["nu-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Bugge Wesseltoft","title":"New Conception of Jazz","recorded":1996,"released":1996,"label":"Jazzland","catalogNo":"JAZZCD 001","personnel":["Bugge Wesseltoft (keys, programming)","Anders Engen (d)","Ingebrigt Håker Flaten (b)"],"whyThisOne":"The record that named the Norwegian project and launched the Jazzland label. Wesseltoft treats the sequencer as a bandmate rather than a backing track, which is the whole difference.","listenFor":"The programmed elements are deliberately allowed to drift and the live players adjust to them. Nothing is locked to a click.","startTrack":"Existence","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"st-germain-tourist","genreIds":["nu-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"St Germain","title":"Tourist","recorded":1999,"released":2000,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"7243 5 25114 2 0","personnel":["Ludovic Navarre (prod)","Edouard Labor (ts, fl)","Pascal Ohsé (tp)","Alexandre Destrez (keys)"],"whyThisOne":"Deep house grooves with live jazz soloing and blues samples, released on Blue Note and selling over two million copies. It is the commercial peak of the whole idea.","listenFor":"'Rose Rouge' loops a Marlena Shaw vocal phrase over a house beat while a live trumpet solos across it. Three eras of Black American music, stacked.","startTrack":"Rose Rouge","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"cinematic-orchestra-motion","genreIds":["nu-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"The Cinematic Orchestra","title":"Motion","recorded":1999,"released":1999,"label":"Ninja Tune","catalogNo":"ZENCD 45","personnel":["Jason Swinscoe (prod)","T. Daniel Howard (d)","Phil France (b)","Tom Chant (ts)"],"whyThisOne":"Sampled and looped material with live improvisation on top, made for a label built on hip-hop production. It is the British answer to the Norwegian project, arrived at independently.","listenFor":"The loops are treated as chord changes to solo over. The saxophone is doing exactly what a jazz soloist does, just over a producer's structure.","startTrack":"Burn Out","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jaga-jazzist-a-livingroom-hush","genreIds":["nu-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Jaga Jazzist","title":"A Livingroom Hush","recorded":2001,"released":2001,"label":"Smalltown Supersound","catalogNo":"STS084","personnel":["Lars Horntveth (multi-instruments, comp)","Martin Horntveth (d)","Line Horntveth (tuba)","Andreas Mjøs (vib)"],"whyThisOne":"A ten-piece Norwegian band writing for horns, vibraphone, tuba and electronics as one integrated machine. It is the most compositionally ambitious thing nu jazz produced.","listenFor":"The arrangements are intricate and largely written, with electronics woven into the ensemble rather than layered on top. This is a big band that grew up on Autechre.","startTrack":"Airborne","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jazzanova-in-between","genreIds":["nu-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Jazzanova","title":"In Between","recorded":2001,"released":2002,"label":"JCR/Sonar Kollektiv","catalogNo":"JCR 019-2","personnel":["Jazzanova (production collective)","Vikter Duplaix (voc)","Ursula Rucker (voc)","Clara Hill (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"The Berlin production team's first album, connecting broken beat, Brazilian material, soul vocals and jazz harmony into something coherent. It is the European production wing at its most assured.","listenFor":"The rhythms are broken beat — displaced, syncopated, never quite settling — and the harmony underneath is straightforwardly jazz. That combination is the genre's core.","startTrack":"That Night","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"eivind-aarset-electronique-noire","genreIds":["nu-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Eivind Aarset","title":"Électronique Noire","recorded":1997,"released":1998,"label":"Jazzland","catalogNo":"537 933-2","personnel":["Eivind Aarset (g, programming)","Nils Petter Molvær (tp)","Bugge Wesseltoft (keys)","Wetle Holte (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Molvær's guitarist as a leader, using the guitar as a sound-design instrument rather than a solo voice. It is more abstract than Khmer and better for it.","listenFor":"There are almost no identifiable guitar sounds. Everything is processed into pads, textures and rhythmic pulses, and the improvisation happens at the level of timbre.","startTrack":"Dark Moisture","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"4hero-two-pages","genreIds":["nu-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"4hero","title":"Two Pages","recorded":1997,"released":1998,"label":"Talkin' Loud","catalogNo":"538 754-2","personnel":["Dego McFarlane (prod)","Marc Mac (prod)","Ursula Rucker (voc)","Carol Crosby (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Drum and bass producers making a double album with a string orchestra, jazz harmony and spoken word. It is the record where the London electronic scene stopped pretending it was not making jazz.","listenFor":"The strings are arranged, not sampled, and the drum programming runs at 170 BPM underneath ballad-tempo melodies. Two time-feels at once.","startTrack":"Star Chasers","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"koop-waltz-for-koop","genreIds":["nu-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Koop","title":"Waltz for Koop","recorded":2001,"released":2001,"label":"JCR/Compost","catalogNo":"JCR 013-2","personnel":["Oscar Simonsson (prod)","Magnus Zingmark (prod)","Cecilia Stalin (voc)","Yukimi Nagano (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"A Swedish duo who built tracks entirely from tiny samples of acoustic jazz records — a single note at a time — and reassembled them into new arrangements. The method is extreme and inaudible.","listenFor":"It sounds like a live jazz band and almost none of it is. Every note was sampled separately from old records and sequenced.","startTrack":"Waltz for Koop","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"kamasi-washington-the-epic","genreIds":["la-beat"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Kamasi Washington","title":"The Epic","recorded":2011,"released":2015,"label":"Brainfeeder","catalogNo":"BF050","personnel":["Kamasi Washington (ts)","Cameron Graves (p)","Miles Mosley (b)","Thundercat (elb)","Ronald Bruner Jr. (d)","Tony Austin (d)","Patrice Quinn (voc)","32-piece orchestra","20-voice choir"],"whyThisOne":"Three hours, seventeen tracks, choir and strings, released on an electronic label and reaching an audience that had not been listening to jazz. The 1970s spiritual jazz ambition returns without a hint of irony.","listenFor":"Two drummers play throughout, panned left and right, playing different parts. The choir functions as an orchestral section, not as a garnish.","startTrack":"Change of the Guard","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"flying-lotus-cosmogramma","genreIds":["la-beat"],"tier":"core","artist":"Flying Lotus","title":"Cosmogramma","recorded":2009,"released":2010,"label":"Warp","catalogNo":"WARPCD195","personnel":["Flying Lotus (prod)","Thundercat (elb)","Ravi Coltrane (ts)","Thom Yorke (voc)","Rebekah Raff (harp)"],"whyThisOne":"The record where the beat scene reached explicitly for jazz — harp, Ravi Coltrane on saxophone, and a structure closer to a suite than to a beat tape. Flying Lotus is Alice Coltrane's great-nephew and the reference is deliberate.","listenFor":"The harp is a direct citation of Alice Coltrane, and the way tracks bleed into one another without pause is a suite structure imported from 1970s Impulse! records.","startTrack":"Zodiac Shit","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"thundercat-drunk","genreIds":["la-beat"],"tier":"core","artist":"Thundercat","title":"Drunk","recorded":2016,"released":2017,"label":"Brainfeeder","catalogNo":"BF061","personnel":["Thundercat (elb, voc)","Flying Lotus (prod)","Kamasi Washington (ts)","Michael McDonald (voc)","Kenny Loggins (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Twenty-three tracks, most under two minutes, with bass playing of real virtuosity underneath deadpan songs about cats and video games. The fusion lineage is worn openly, including hiring Michael McDonald.","listenFor":"The bass playing is technically extraordinary and mixed like a pop record. Stanley Clarke and Jaco Pastorius are the ancestors and Thundercat has said so directly.","startTrack":"Them Changes","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"kendrick-lamar-to-pimp-a-butterfly","genreIds":["la-beat","jazz-rap"],"tier":"core","artist":"Kendrick Lamar","title":"To Pimp a Butterfly","recorded":2014,"released":2015,"label":"Top Dawg/Aftermath/Interscope","catalogNo":"B0022973-02","personnel":["Kendrick Lamar (MC)","Thundercat (elb)","Kamasi Washington (ts)","Terrace Martin (as, keys)","Robert Glasper (p)","Ronald Bruner Jr. (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A mainstream rap album made with a live jazz band, and the record that put the whole West Coast Get Down in front of a global audience. It is where the scene became visible.","listenFor":"'For Free?' is a jazz interlude at bebop tempo with rapping over the top. The band is playing changes and Lamar is phrasing against them.","startTrack":"For Free? (Interlude)","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"terrace-martin-velvet-portraits","genreIds":["la-beat"],"tier":"core","artist":"Terrace Martin","title":"Velvet Portraits","recorded":2015,"released":2016,"label":"Ropeadope","catalogNo":"RAD-352","personnel":["Terrace Martin (as, keys, vocoder)","Robert Glasper (p)","Thundercat (elb)","Ronald Bruner Jr. (d)","Kamasi Washington (ts)"],"whyThisOne":"The producer-musician who assembled the To Pimp a Butterfly sessions making his own record, which is more explicitly jazz than anything he produced for others.","listenFor":"The vocoder is used as a horn section, layered in harmony. It is a G-funk device deployed with jazz voicing.","startTrack":"Mortal Man","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"makaya-mccraven-universal-beings","genreIds":["la-beat","contemporary-creative"],"tier":"core","artist":"Makaya McCraven","title":"Universal Beings","recorded":2017,"released":2018,"label":"International Anthem","catalogNo":"IARC0018","personnel":["Makaya McCraven (d, prod)","Jeff Parker (g)","Shabaka Hutchings (ts)","Tomeka Reid (cello)","Brandee Younger (harp)","Nubya Garcia (ts)"],"whyThisOne":"McCraven records live improvisation and then edits it into composed tracks in the manner of a hip-hop producer. Four sessions in four cities, including London with Shabaka Hutchings and Nubya Garcia.","listenFor":"The tracks are cut from long improvisations, so a groove will be interrupted and resumed in a way live playing never does. The edit is the composition.","startTrack":"Black Lion","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"atwood-ferguson-suite-for-ma-dukes","genreIds":["la-beat"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Miguel Atwood-Ferguson & Carlos Niño","title":"Suite for Ma Dukes: The Music of James Yancey","recorded":2009,"released":2011,"label":"Mochilla","catalogNo":"MOCH005","personnel":["Miguel Atwood-Ferguson (arr, vln, viola)","Carlos Niño (prod)","40-piece orchestra"],"whyThisOne":"J Dilla's beats orchestrated for a forty-piece ensemble, which forced someone to work out what the harmony in those tracks actually was. It is the clearest evidence that the beats were compositions.","listenFor":"The orchestration reveals harmonic movement that the original short loops only implied. Dilla's chord choices turn out to be far from simple.","startTrack":"Antiquity","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"jeff-parker-suite-for-max-brown","genreIds":["la-beat","contemporary-creative"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Jeff Parker & The New Breed","title":"Suite for Max Brown","recorded":2019,"released":2020,"label":"International Anthem / Nonesuch","catalogNo":"IARC0032","personnel":["Jeff Parker (g, prod, keys)","Ruby Parker (voc)","Josh Johnson (as)","Paul Bryan (elb)","Makaya McCraven (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Tortoise's guitarist building a record from his own sampled loops, played mostly by himself, with jazz players dropping in. It is the beat aesthetic applied by someone from the post-rock side.","listenFor":"Parker samples himself, loops it, and then plays over the loop. The 'band' on much of this is one person assembled in layers.","startTrack":"Build a Nest","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"kamaal-williams-the-return","genreIds":["la-beat","uk-jazz-revival"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Kamaal Williams","title":"The Return","recorded":2017,"released":2018,"label":"Black Focus","catalogNo":"BFR002","personnel":["Kamaal Williams (keys)","Pete Martin (elb)","MckNasty (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The London counterpart to the LA scene, built on broken beat and jazz-funk keyboards rather than on spiritual jazz. It shows the two cities working the same territory from different directions.","listenFor":"The drums have the same off-grid feel as the LA records but derive it from UK garage and broken beat rather than from Dilla directly.","startTrack":"Salaam","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"keith-jarrett-koln-concert","genreIds":["ecm-nordic"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Keith Jarrett","title":"The Köln Concert","recorded":1975,"released":1975,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 1064/65","personnel":["Keith Jarrett (p)"],"whyThisOne":"The best-selling solo piano album ever made, improvised in one sitting on a badly out-of-tune house piano that Jarrett nearly refused to play. He worked around the instrument's faults and the record is shaped by them.","listenFor":"He avoids the piano's weak upper register and dead pedals by staying in the middle and using ostinatos. The limitation produced the style.","startTrack":"Part I","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jan-garbarek-afric-pepperbird","genreIds":["ecm-nordic"],"tier":"core","artist":"Jan Garbarek Quartet","title":"Afric Pepperbird","recorded":1970,"released":1970,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 1007","personnel":["Jan Garbarek (ts, bs, fl)","Terje Rypdal (g)","Arild Andersen (b)","Jon Christensen (d)"],"whyThisOne":"ECM's Nordic wing at its most aggressive, before the label's reputation for beauty set in. This is free jazz played by Norwegians who had studied with George Russell, and it is closer to Ayler than to Garbarek's later sound.","listenFor":"The tone is already unmistakable — cold, pure, huge — but the playing is far more violent than what Garbarek became known for a decade later.","startTrack":"Afric Pepperbird","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"keith-jarrett-belonging","genreIds":["ecm-nordic"],"tier":"core","artist":"Keith Jarrett","title":"Belonging","recorded":1974,"released":1974,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 1050","personnel":["Keith Jarrett (p)","Jan Garbarek (ts, ss)","Palle Danielsson (b)","Jon Christensen (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Jarrett's 'European quartet', with tunes that are folk-simple and playing that is anything but. It is the most perfectly balanced record either Jarrett or Garbarek made.","listenFor":"'Long as You Know You're Living Yours' is a two-chord vamp with a melody a child could sing, and the band builds twenty minutes of tension out of refusing to resolve it.","startTrack":"Long as You Know You're Living Yours","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"eberhard-weber-colours-of-chloe","genreIds":["ecm-nordic"],"tier":"core","artist":"Eberhard Weber","title":"The Colours of Chloë","recorded":1973,"released":1974,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 1042","personnel":["Eberhard Weber (b, cello, ocarina)","Rainer Brüninghaus (p)","Ack van Rooyen (flugelhorn)","cello ensemble"],"whyThisOne":"Weber built his own five-string electric upright bass and wrote chamber music for it. The record's sound — enormous, singing, sustained bass over cellos — has no antecedent.","listenFor":"The bass carries the melody for long stretches with a sustain and pitch clarity that an acoustic bass cannot produce. The instrument is the composition's premise.","startTrack":"The Colours of Chloë","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"pat-metheny-bright-size-life","genreIds":["ecm-nordic","fusion"],"tier":"core","artist":"Pat Metheny","title":"Bright Size Life","recorded":1975,"released":1976,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 1073","personnel":["Pat Metheny (g)","Jaco Pastorius (elb)","Bob Moses (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Metheny at twenty-one with Jaco Pastorius, in a trio with no chordal instrument other than the guitar. It brought an American, country-inflected lyricism into ECM's world.","listenFor":"The guitar tone is clean and reverberant with almost no attack, which became one of the most imitated sounds in jazz. Pastorius is playing melody as much as bass.","startTrack":"Bright Size Life","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"tomasz-stanko-litania","genreIds":["ecm-nordic"],"tier":"core","artist":"Tomasz Stańko","title":"Litania: The Music of Krzysztof Komeda","recorded":1997,"released":1997,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 1636","personnel":["Tomasz Stańko (tp)","Bernt Rosengren (ts)","Joakim Milder (ts)","Bobo Stenson (p)","Palle Danielsson (b)","Jon Christensen (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Stańko playing the music of Krzysztof Komeda, the Polish composer and Polanski's film scorer, in whose band he had played as a young man. It is a distinctly Central European strand of the label's aesthetic.","listenFor":"Komeda's melodies are dark and song-like in a way that Nordic material is not — the harmonic language comes from Polish film music, not Norwegian folk.","startTrack":"Svantetic","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"terje-rypdal-odyssey","genreIds":["ecm-nordic"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Terje Rypdal","title":"Odyssey","recorded":1975,"released":1975,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 1067/68","personnel":["Terje Rypdal (g)","Torbjørn Sunde (tb)","Brynjulf Blix (org)","Sveinung Hovensjø (elb)","Svein Christiansen (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Sustained, distorted, orchestral electric guitar inside ECM's acoustic world. Rypdal plays long held notes rather than lines, closer to a string section than to a rock guitarist.","listenFor":"He almost never plays fast. The guitar produces slowly evolving sustained tones, and the volume pedal removes every attack.","startTrack":"Darkness Falls","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"gary-peacock-tales-of-another","genreIds":["ecm-nordic","third-stream"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Gary Peacock","title":"Tales of Another","recorded":1977,"released":1977,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 1101","personnel":["Gary Peacock (b)","Keith Jarrett (p)","Jack DeJohnette (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Nominally Peacock's record, this is the first meeting of the trio that Jarrett later led for thirty years. The interplay is already fully formed and the writing is Peacock's, which changes the character.","listenFor":"Peacock's compositions are more angular than Jarrett's, and the trio treats them more freely than it treated standards later. A different band under the same name.","startTrack":"Vignette","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bobo-stenson-goodbye","genreIds":["ecm-nordic"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Bobo Stenson Trio","title":"Goodbye","recorded":2005,"released":2005,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 1904","personnel":["Bobo Stenson (p)","Anders Jormin (b)","Paul Motian (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The Swedish pianist with Paul Motian, playing material from Ornette Coleman to Cuban song to Alban Berg. It is the label's chamber aesthetic at its most patient and least sentimental.","listenFor":"Motian's drumming has no timekeeping function at all — he is playing colour and shape, and the trio finds the pulse collectively or not at all.","startTrack":"Race Face","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"wynton-marsalis-black-codes","genreIds":["neo-bop"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Wynton Marsalis","title":"Black Codes (From the Underground)","recorded":1985,"released":1985,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"FC 40009","personnel":["Wynton Marsalis (tp)","Branford Marsalis (ts, ss)","Kenny Kirkland (p)","Charnett Moffett (b)","Jeff 'Tain' Watts (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The strongest record of the movement's first phase, made by a working band of contemporaries rather than by a young leader with veteran sidemen. The writing is genuinely difficult and the playing is ferocious.","listenFor":"Jeff 'Tain' Watts plays with Tony Williams' elasticity but hits considerably harder. The Miles quintet is the model and the temperature is completely different.","startTrack":"Black Codes","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"wynton-marsalis-1981-debut","genreIds":["neo-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Wynton Marsalis","title":"Wynton Marsalis","recorded":1981,"released":1982,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"FC 37574","personnel":["Wynton Marsalis (tp)","Branford Marsalis (ts)","Herbie Hancock (p)","Ron Carter (b)","Tony Williams (d)","Clarence Seay (b)","Jeff Watts (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A nineteen-year-old's debut with three-quarters of Miles Davis's second quintet as his rhythm section. The endorsement was the point, and it launched the entire Young Lions marketing apparatus.","listenFor":"Hancock, Carter and Williams play exactly as they did in 1966, and the trumpet in front is the one thing that has changed. The lineage claim is made explicit by personnel.","startTrack":"RJ","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"joshua-redman-moodswing","genreIds":["neo-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Joshua Redman Quartet","title":"MoodSwing","recorded":1994,"released":1994,"label":"Warner Bros.","catalogNo":"9 45643-2","personnel":["Joshua Redman (ts)","Brad Mehldau (p)","Christian McBride (b)","Brian Blade (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The second Young Lions wave, and a band of four musicians who all became major figures. Mehldau and Blade in particular sound nothing like traditionalists here.","listenFor":"Brian Blade's drumming is quieter and more elastic than the 1980s neo-bop model, and Mehldau's comping already refuses to state the harmony plainly.","startTrack":"Sweet Sorrow","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"roy-hargrove-habana","genreIds":["neo-bop","afro-cuban"],"tier":"core","artist":"Roy Hargrove's Crisol","title":"Habana","recorded":1997,"released":1997,"label":"Verve","catalogNo":"537 563-2","personnel":["Roy Hargrove (tp)","Chucho Valdés (p)","David Sánchez (ts)","Russell Malone (g)","Horacio 'El Negro' Hernández (d)","Miguel 'Angá' Díaz (congas)"],"whyThisOne":"Hargrove was the Young Lion least interested in policing boundaries, and this Cuban project won a Grammy while being a genuinely collaborative record rather than a tourist's.","listenFor":"The Cuban rhythm section is not simplified for the American horns. Hargrove had to learn to phrase against clave and you can hear him doing it.","startTrack":"O My Seh Yeh","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"terence-blanchard-malcolm-x-jazz-suite","genreIds":["neo-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Terence Blanchard","title":"The Malcolm X Jazz Suite","recorded":1993,"released":1993,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CK 53599","personnel":["Terence Blanchard (tp)","Sam Newsome (ts)","Bruce Barth (p)","Tarus Mateen (b)","Troy Davis (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Blanchard reworking his own film score into a jazz suite, which is where the neo-bop generation's compositional ambitions actually landed — in extended form and in film.","listenFor":"The writing is programmatic and cinematic, using recurring themes across movements rather than head-solos-head. It is a different project from the quintet records.","startTrack":"Fear","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"marcus-roberts-deep-in-the-shed","genreIds":["neo-bop"],"tier":"core","artist":"Marcus Roberts","title":"Deep in the Shed","recorded":1989,"released":1990,"label":"Novus","catalogNo":"3078-2-N","personnel":["Marcus Roberts (p)","Wynton Marsalis (tp)","Todd Williams (ts)","Wessell Anderson (as)","Reginald Veal (b)","Herlin Riley (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Roberts went furthest back of anyone in the movement, rebuilding stride and blues technique as working equipment. The compositions here are all blues-based and none of them are simple.","listenFor":"Every piece is a blues in some form, and Roberts keeps finding structural variations — different lengths, different harmonic detours — inside that constraint.","startTrack":"The Governor","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"wynton-marsalis-blood-on-the-fields","genreIds":["neo-bop","third-stream"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Wynton Marsalis","title":"Blood on the Fields","recorded":1995,"released":1997,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"C3K 57694","personnel":["Wynton Marsalis (tp, comp)","Jon Hendricks (voc)","Cassandra Wilson (voc)","Miles Griffith (voc)","Lincoln Center Jazz Orchestra"],"whyThisOne":"A three-hour oratorio about slavery for big band and three singers, and the first jazz work to win the Pulitzer Prize for Music, in 1997. Whatever you think of the movement, this is its most serious artefact.","listenFor":"The writing moves through the whole history — field holler, New Orleans polyphony, Ellington, bebop — as narrative rather than as pastiche.","startTrack":"Calling the Indians Out","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jazz-at-lincoln-center-big-train","genreIds":["neo-bop","swing"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Lincoln Center Jazz Orchestra","title":"Big Train","recorded":1998,"released":1999,"label":"Columbia","catalogNo":"CK 69860","personnel":["Wynton Marsalis (tp, comp)","Wycliffe Gordon (tb)","Victor Goines (reeds)","Herlin Riley (d)","Marcus Printup (tp)"],"whyThisOne":"The repertory orchestra playing new material rather than the archive, which is the institution's strongest justification. It is also the clearest sample of what Jazz at Lincoln Center sounds like as a band.","listenFor":"The train imagery is worked out through orchestration — brass imitating whistles, drums imitating wheels. It is an Ellington device applied by a student of Ellington.","startTrack":"Union Pacific Big Boy","difficulty":2,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"brad-mehldau-art-of-the-trio-3","genreIds":["neo-bop","post-bop"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Brad Mehldau Trio","title":"The Art of the Trio, Volume Three: Songs","recorded":1998,"released":1998,"label":"Warner Bros.","catalogNo":"9 47051-2","personnel":["Brad Mehldau (p)","Larry Grenadier (b)","Jorge Rossy (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The Young Lions generation's most harmonically searching pianist, playing standards, Radiohead and his own material with a contrapuntal left hand that has more to do with Brahms than with bebop.","listenFor":"The left hand plays independent melodic lines rather than comping chords, so the trio often has three simultaneous voices with no one stating the harmony.","startTrack":"Song-Song","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"steve-coleman-motherland-pulse","genreIds":["m-base"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Steve Coleman and Five Elements","title":"Motherland Pulse","recorded":1985,"released":1985,"label":"JMT","catalogNo":"JMT 850 001","personnel":["Steve Coleman (as)","Graham Haynes (cor)","Geri Allen (p)","Kelvyn Bell (g)","Lonnie Plaxico (b)","Marvin 'Smitty' Smith (d)","Cassandra Wilson (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"The first Five Elements record and the collective's founding document, with Geri Allen and Cassandra Wilson both present. The rhythmic concept is already fully in place.","listenFor":"The groove is unmistakably funk and you cannot count it in four. Several patterns of different lengths are running at once and the downbeat keeps moving.","startTrack":"Motherland Pulse","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"steve-coleman-black-science","genreIds":["m-base"],"tier":"core","artist":"Steve Coleman and Five Elements","title":"Black Science","recorded":1990,"released":1991,"label":"Novus","catalogNo":"3119-2-N","personnel":["Steve Coleman (as)","Robin Eubanks (tb)","David Gilmore (g)","Reggie Washington (b)","Marvin 'Smitty' Smith (d)","Kokayi (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"The concept at full strength, with rapping, hard funk and rhythmic structures that require the band to count in several ways at once.","listenFor":"The horns phrase across the bar lines constantly, landing on beats that the bass line has already redefined. Nothing lines up where you expect it to.","startTrack":"Black Science","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"greg-osby-season-of-renewal","genreIds":["m-base"],"tier":"core","artist":"Greg Osby","title":"Season of Renewal","recorded":1989,"released":1990,"label":"JMT","catalogNo":"JMT 834 435-2","personnel":["Greg Osby (as, ss)","Michele Rosewoman (keys)","Kevin Bruce Harris (b)","Paul Samuels (d)","Cassandra Wilson (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Osby's version of the concept is more song-shaped than Coleman's and more openly indebted to funk and go-go. It shows how differently two people could apply the same working method.","listenFor":"The alto tone is drier and more nasal than Coleman's, and the lines are shorter and more fragmented. Same idea, different instrument.","startTrack":"Season of Renewal","difficulty":3,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"geri-allen-twylight","genreIds":["m-base"],"tier":"core","artist":"Geri Allen","title":"Twylight","recorded":1989,"released":1989,"label":"Minor Music","catalogNo":"MM 801 013","personnel":["Geri Allen (p, keys)","Robin Eubanks (tb)","Marcus Belgrave (tp)","Lonnie Plaxico (b)","Pheeroan akLaff (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Allen was the collective's most harmonically sophisticated member and the one most connected to the AACM lineage. Her writing here is denser and stranger than the group's usual funk framework.","listenFor":"The piano voicings owe more to Herbie Nichols and Muhal Richard Abrams than to funk. She is applying the rhythmic concept to a very different harmonic language.","startTrack":"Twylight","difficulty":4,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"steve-coleman-rhythm-people","genreIds":["m-base"],"tier":"core","artist":"Steve Coleman and Five Elements","title":"Rhythm People (The Resurrection of Creative Black Civilization)","recorded":1990,"released":1990,"label":"Novus","catalogNo":"3092-2-N","personnel":["Steve Coleman (as)","Graham Haynes (cor)","David Gilmore (g)","Kevin Bruce Harris (b)","Marvin 'Smitty' Smith (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The record where the rhythmic project is stated most explicitly, title included. It is dense, dry and completely uninterested in charming anyone.","listenFor":"The bass and drums are locked to different cycle lengths, so the pattern only realigns every several bars. Count one instrument at a time.","startTrack":"Little Girl I'll Miss You","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"robin-eubanks-mental-images","genreIds":["m-base"],"tier":"core","artist":"Robin Eubanks","title":"Mental Images","recorded":1994,"released":1994,"label":"JMT","catalogNo":"JMT 514 013-2","personnel":["Robin Eubanks (tb)","Steve Coleman (as)","Kevin Eubanks (g)","Michael Cain (keys)","Dave Holland (b)","Mark Mondesir (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Trombone-led M-Base, which is technically brutal — the instrument has no natural facility for this rhythmic language and Eubanks makes it work anyway.","listenFor":"Trombone playing sixteenth-note lines across displaced cycles. The slide makes this far harder than it would be on a valved instrument.","startTrack":"Mental Images","difficulty":4,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"steve-coleman-curves-of-life","genreIds":["m-base"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Steve Coleman and Five Elements","title":"Curves of Life","recorded":1995,"released":1995,"label":"RCA Victor","catalogNo":"09026 68556-2","personnel":["Steve Coleman (as)","Andy Milne (p)","David Gilmore (g)","Reggie Washington (b)","Gene Lake (d)","Kokayi (voc)","Sub-Zero (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Recorded live in Paris across several nights, which is where this music makes most sense — the cycles have room to establish themselves and the band relaxes into them.","listenFor":"Over ten minutes a pattern that seemed impossible to follow becomes obvious. Duration is part of how the rhythmic concept communicates.","startTrack":"Cross-Fade","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"steve-coleman-sonic-language-of-myth","genreIds":["m-base","contemporary-creative"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Steve Coleman and Five Elements","title":"The Sonic Language of Myth","recorded":1998,"released":1999,"label":"RCA Victor","catalogNo":"09026 63316-2","personnel":["Steve Coleman (as)","Ravi Coltrane (ts)","Andy Milne (p)","Anthony Tidd (b)","Sean Rickman (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Coleman's structural thinking applied to Egyptian and Greek mythological schemes, with the rhythmic cycles derived from astronomical and calendrical ratios. It is where the project becomes fully systematic.","listenFor":"The cycle lengths are derived from numbers Coleman took from mythological sources. Whether you believe that matters, the resulting rhythms are unlike anyone else's.","startTrack":"Precession","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"steve-coleman-sign-and-the-seal","genreIds":["m-base","afro-cuban"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Steve Coleman and the Mystic Rhythm Society with AfroCuba de Matanzas","title":"The Sign and the Seal","recorded":1996,"released":1997,"label":"RCA Victor","catalogNo":"09026 68807-2","personnel":["Steve Coleman (as)","AfroCuba de Matanzas (percussion, voc)","Ravi Coltrane (ts)","Anthony Tidd (b)"],"whyThisOne":"Recorded in Matanzas with a folkloric Cuban ensemble whose repertoire comes from Yoruba and Congolese religious practice. It is fieldwork and collaboration rather than a fusion record.","listenFor":"The Cuban ensemble is not adapting to Coleman. He is finding places inside their cycles, which is exactly the relationship his whole rhythmic project describes.","startTrack":"Yorubaland","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"john-zorn-cobra","genreIds":["downtown"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"John Zorn","title":"Cobra","recorded":1985,"released":1987,"label":"hatART","catalogNo":"hatART 2034","personnel":["John Zorn (prompter)","Bill Frisell (g)","Wayne Horvitz (keys)","Elliott Sharp (g)","Anthony Coleman (keys)","Christian Marclay (turntables)","Bobby Previte (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A game piece: no notes are written, only rules governing who plays, when, and in what relationship. A prompter holds up cards and the ensemble responds. It is the downtown method at its purest.","listenFor":"The music changes direction abruptly and completely, over and over. Those are the cues. Once you realise you are hearing a system operating, it stops sounding arbitrary.","startTrack":"Cobra (Studio Version)","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"naked-city-naked-city","genreIds":["downtown"],"tier":"core","artist":"Naked City","title":"Naked City","recorded":1989,"released":1990,"label":"Elektra Nonesuch","catalogNo":"9 79238-2","personnel":["John Zorn (as)","Bill Frisell (g)","Wayne Horvitz (keys)","Fred Frith (b)","Joey Baron (d)","Yamatsuka Eye (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Twenty-six tracks including several under thirty seconds, cutting between hardcore punk, surf, film noir jazz and country with no transitions. The band could execute the cuts at speed, which is why it works.","listenFor":"'Speedfreaks' compresses about eight genres into forty seconds. The changes are written and rehearsed, not improvised — the precision is the joke's delivery.","startTrack":"Batman","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"lounge-lizards-lounge-lizards","genreIds":["downtown"],"tier":"core","artist":"The Lounge Lizards","title":"The Lounge Lizards","recorded":1980,"released":1981,"label":"EG","catalogNo":"EGS 108","personnel":["John Lurie (as)","Evan Lurie (p)","Arto Lindsay (g)","Steve Piccolo (b)","Anton Fier (d)"],"whyThisOne":"'Fake jazz', as Lurie called it: noir themes played deadpan by musicians from the no wave scene, with Arto Lindsay's untuned guitar noise cutting across. It set the downtown tone of ironic seriousness.","listenFor":"Arto Lindsay is playing a twelve-string guitar he does not tune, purely as noise, over what is otherwise a jazz quintet. Neither element concedes.","startTrack":"Incident on South Street","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"john-zorn-masada-alef","genreIds":["downtown"],"tier":"core","artist":"Masada","title":"Alef","recorded":1993,"released":1994,"label":"DIW","catalogNo":"DIW-888","personnel":["John Zorn (as)","Dave Douglas (tp)","Greg Cohen (b)","Joey Baron (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Zorn's Ornette Coleman quartet playing Jewish scales and modes — the Radical Jewish Culture project in its most focused form, and the most straightforwardly beautiful thing he has made.","listenFor":"The instrumentation and the pulse-keeping freedom are lifted from Ornette Coleman's 1959 quartet exactly. The melodic material is klezmer and Middle Eastern modes.","startTrack":"Jair","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"bill-frisell-have-a-little-faith","genreIds":["downtown"],"tier":"core","artist":"Bill Frisell","title":"Have a Little Faith","recorded":1992,"released":1993,"label":"Elektra Nonesuch","catalogNo":"9 79301-2","personnel":["Bill Frisell (g)","Don Byron (cl)","Guy Klucevsek (accordion)","Kermit Driscoll (b)","Joey Baron (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Copland, Sousa, Madonna, Muddy Waters and Bob Dylan played by a downtown quintet with an accordion. The American repertoire treated as one continuous body of material.","listenFor":"The Copland 'Billy the Kid' arrangement is played with total sincerity by a band that also covers 'Live to Tell'. Frisell never signals which one is the joke, because neither is.","startTrack":"Billy the Kid","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"tim-berne-fractured-fairy-tales","genreIds":["downtown"],"tier":"core","artist":"Tim Berne","title":"Fractured Fairy Tales","recorded":1989,"released":1989,"label":"JMT","catalogNo":"JMT 834 431-2","personnel":["Tim Berne (as)","Herb Robertson (tp)","Mark Dresser (b)","Hank Roberts (cello)","Joey Baron (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Berne writes long, intricate, multi-section pieces for working bands and this is his most tightly organised record. He came out of Julius Hemphill's tutelage and it shows in the horn writing.","listenFor":"Pieces run fifteen minutes and never repeat a section. The structures are through-composed with improvisation slotted in, which is closer to the AACM than to downtown's cut-ups.","startTrack":"Fractured Fairy Tales","difficulty":4,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"marc-ribot-rootless-cosmopolitans","genreIds":["downtown"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Marc Ribot","title":"Rootless Cosmopolitans","recorded":1990,"released":1990,"label":"Island/Antilles","catalogNo":"422-848 361-2","personnel":["Marc Ribot (g, voc)","Don Byron (cl)","Anthony Coleman (keys)","Melvin Gibbs (elb)","Roy Nathanson (as)"],"whyThisOne":"Ribot's debut as leader, covering Hendrix and Ellington with equal deliberate awkwardness. His guitar playing is the scene's most abrasive and least virtuosic-sounding, on purpose.","listenFor":"He plays like someone who cannot quite reach the note and does not care. It is a technique, not a limitation, and it is the opposite of every guitar aesthetic around it.","startTrack":"The Wind Cries Mary","difficulty":4,"confidence":"medium"},{"id":"james-white-off-white","genreIds":["downtown","free-funk"],"tier":"deep","artist":"James White and the Blacks","title":"Off White","recorded":1979,"released":1979,"label":"ZE","catalogNo":"ZEA 33-002","personnel":["James Chance (as, voc)","Pat Place (g)","Robert Quine (g)","Don Christensen (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The no wave prehistory of punk jazz: Albert Ayler's saxophone screaming over a James Brown rhythm section, played by people from the New York art-rock scene rather than the jazz one.","listenFor":"The saxophone playing is genuinely free-jazz-derived and the band is playing disco-funk. Neither is a parody of the other, which is what makes it uncomfortable.","startTrack":"Contort Yourself","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"elliott-sharp-carbon-tocsin","genreIds":["downtown"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Elliott Sharp / Carbon","title":"Tocsin","recorded":1989,"released":1990,"label":"SST","catalogNo":"SST 246","personnel":["Elliott Sharp (g, reeds)","David Weinstein (keys)","Zeena Parkins (harp)","Samm Bennett (percussion)"],"whyThisOne":"Sharp built structures from Fibonacci sequences and fractal geometry and then had a band improvise inside them, on a hardcore punk label. It is downtown's most rigorously systematic wing.","listenFor":"Zeena Parkins' electric harp is processed beyond recognition. The harp as a noise instrument is one of the scene's genuinely original contributions.","startTrack":"Tocsin","difficulty":5,"confidence":"low","note":"Elliott Sharp's late-1980s Carbon discography is spread across several small labels and is inconsistently documented."},{"id":"vijay-iyer-historicity","genreIds":["contemporary-creative"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Vijay Iyer Trio","title":"Historicity","recorded":2009,"released":2009,"label":"ACT","catalogNo":"ACT 9489-2","personnel":["Vijay Iyer (p)","Stephan Crump (b)","Marcus Gilmore (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A piano trio playing M.I.A., Stevie Wonder, Andrew Hill and Ronnie Foster with rhythmic thinking derived from South Indian and West African practice. It made contemporary creative music briefly commercially visible.","listenFor":"The trio keeps subdividing the beat into groupings that do not fit the meter, then resolving them. Marcus Gilmore's hi-hat is usually where the real time is.","startTrack":"Historicity","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"steve-lehman-travail-transformation","genreIds":["contemporary-creative"],"tier":"core","artist":"Steve Lehman Octet","title":"Travail, Transformation and Flow","recorded":2009,"released":2009,"label":"Pi Recordings","catalogNo":"PI33","personnel":["Steve Lehman (as)","Jonathan Finlayson (tp)","Tim Albright (tb)","Chris Dingman (vib)","Jose Davila (tuba)","Drew Gress (b)","Tyshawn Sorey (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Spectral harmony — derived from analysing the overtone content of sounds, a technique from French contemporary composition — applied to an improvising octet. It is a genuinely new harmonic resource for jazz.","listenFor":"The vibraphone and tuba voicings produce chords that sound metallic and slightly out of tune. They are tuned to overtone relationships rather than to equal temperament.","startTrack":"Echoes","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"mary-halvorson-illusionary-sea","genreIds":["contemporary-creative"],"tier":"core","artist":"Mary Halvorson Septet","title":"Illusionary Sea","recorded":2013,"released":2013,"label":"Firehouse 12","catalogNo":"FH12-04-01-018","personnel":["Mary Halvorson (g)","Jonathan Finlayson (tp)","Jon Irabagon (as)","Ingrid Laubrock (ts)","Jacob Garchik (tb)","John Hébert (b)","Ches Smith (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Halvorson's writing for larger groups, with a guitar language built from pitch-bending and delay that is instantly identifiable and has no obvious ancestor.","listenFor":"She uses a pedal to drop the pitch of individual notes mid-decay, so lines sag and warp. It sounds like tape damage and it is entirely deliberate.","startTrack":"Smiles of Great Men","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"jason-moran-black-stars","genreIds":["contemporary-creative"],"tier":"core","artist":"Jason Moran","title":"Black Stars","recorded":2001,"released":2001,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"7243 5 32922 2 5","personnel":["Jason Moran (p)","Sam Rivers (ts, ss, fl)","Tarus Mateen (b)","Nasheet Waits (d)"],"whyThisOne":"Moran's trio with Sam Rivers, then in his late seventies, as guest — the loft generation and the current one in the same room. Moran's stride references sit alongside completely free playing.","listenFor":"Moran will drop into a Fats Waller left hand in the middle of an abstract passage. He treats the piano's entire history as available at any moment.","startTrack":"Out Front","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"tyshawn-sorey-verisimilitude","genreIds":["contemporary-creative"],"tier":"core","artist":"Tyshawn Sorey","title":"Verisimilitude","recorded":2016,"released":2017,"label":"Pi Recordings","catalogNo":"PI68","personnel":["Tyshawn Sorey (d, percussion)","Cory Smythe (p)","Chris Tordini (b)"],"whyThisOne":"A trio record where almost nothing happens quickly and the dynamic range runs from silence to barely audible. Sorey works between improvised music and contemporary composition without treating them as separate jobs.","listenFor":"The patience is the point. Events are separated by long stretches of near-silence, and the tension comes from waiting rather than from density.","startTrack":"Contemplating Tranquility","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"ambrose-akinmusire-imagined-savior","genreIds":["contemporary-creative"],"tier":"core","artist":"Ambrose Akinmusire","title":"The Imagined Savior Is Far Easier to Paint","recorded":2013,"released":2014,"label":"Blue Note","catalogNo":"509990 27871 2 8","personnel":["Ambrose Akinmusire (tp)","Walter Smith III (ts)","Sam Harris (p)","Harish Raghavan (b)","Justin Brown (d)","OSSO String Quartet","Becca Stevens (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"A long-form album with a string quartet, three vocalists and a piece named after Trayvon Martin's shooting. It is contemporary jazz taking on subject matter directly rather than through implication.","listenFor":"Akinmusire's trumpet tone cracks, splits and half-valves deliberately. He treats imperfection as expressive material rather than as something to eliminate.","startTrack":"Our Basement (Ed)","difficulty":3,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"craig-taborn-avenging-angel","genreIds":["contemporary-creative","ecm-nordic"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Craig Taborn","title":"Avenging Angel","recorded":2010,"released":2011,"label":"ECM","catalogNo":"ECM 2207","personnel":["Craig Taborn (p)"],"whyThisOne":"Solo piano improvisation with the structural discipline of composition and none of the romanticism ECM solo piano usually carries. Taborn builds each piece from a single cell.","listenFor":"Most tracks start with one small figure and expand outward without ever introducing unrelated material. It is improvisation working like a compositional process.","startTrack":"The Broad Day King","difficulty":4,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"matana-roberts-coin-coin-chapter-one","genreIds":["contemporary-creative","aacm-avant"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Matana Roberts","title":"Coin Coin Chapter One: Gens de Couleur Libres","recorded":2011,"released":2011,"label":"Constellation","catalogNo":"CST081","personnel":["Matana Roberts (as, voc)","16-piece ensemble"],"whyThisOne":"The first instalment of a projected twelve-part work tracing Roberts' own ancestry through American slavery, using AACM structural methods, spoken narrative and a large ensemble.","listenFor":"Roberts sings, screams and speaks as well as playing. The narrative is delivered directly rather than being carried by the music's mood.","startTrack":"Rise","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"henry-threadgill-in-for-a-penny","genreIds":["contemporary-creative","aacm-avant"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Henry Threadgill Zooid","title":"In for a Penny, In for a Pound","recorded":2014,"released":2015,"label":"Pi Recordings","catalogNo":"PI60","personnel":["Henry Threadgill (as, fl, bass fl)","Liberty Ellman (g)","Jose Davila (tb, tuba)","Christopher Hoffman (cello)","Elliot Humberto Kavee (d)"],"whyThisOne":"The record that won the 2016 Pulitzer Prize for Music, written for a band with tuba, cello and acoustic guitar and using a harmonic system Threadgill devised himself.","listenFor":"Threadgill assigns each player a set of intervals rather than chords, so the harmony is generated by what they choose within those sets. Nothing is stated vertically.","startTrack":"In for a Penny, In for a Pound (Opening)","difficulty":5,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"we-out-here-compilation","genreIds":["uk-jazz-revival"],"tier":"gateway","artist":"Various Artists","title":"We Out Here","recorded":2017,"released":2018,"label":"Brownswood","catalogNo":"BWOOD0180","personnel":["Shabaka Hutchings (curator, ts)","Nubya Garcia (ts)","Moses Boyd (d)","Theon Cross (tuba)","Ezra Collective","Joe Armon-Jones (keys)"],"whyThisOne":"Nine tracks recorded in three days with a shared pool of musicians, assembled by Shabaka Hutchings for Gilles Peterson's label. It is the scene introducing itself, deliberately, as a scene.","listenFor":"The same players recur across tracks in different combinations. That overlap is the point — this is a community with a shared rhythmic language, not a compilation of unrelated acts.","startTrack":"Black Skin, Black Masks (Theon Cross)","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"sons-of-kemet-your-queen-is-a-reptile","genreIds":["uk-jazz-revival"],"tier":"core","artist":"Sons of Kemet","title":"Your Queen Is a Reptile","recorded":2017,"released":2018,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"00602567289654","personnel":["Shabaka Hutchings (ts, cl)","Theon Cross (tuba)","Tom Skinner (d)","Eddie Hick (d)","Congo Natty (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Tuba, two drummers and a saxophone, with every track named for a Black woman the band offers as an alternative monarch. The politics are stated in the titles and carried in the rhythm.","listenFor":"Theon Cross's tuba is playing bass lines with dancehall articulation and sub-bass weight. Two drummers play complementary Caribbean patterns rather than doubling each other.","startTrack":"My Queen Is Ada Eastman","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"nubya-garcia-source","genreIds":["uk-jazz-revival"],"tier":"core","artist":"Nubya Garcia","title":"Source","recorded":2019,"released":2020,"label":"Concord Jazz","catalogNo":"CJA00505","personnel":["Nubya Garcia (ts, fl)","Joe Armon-Jones (keys)","Daniel Casimir (b)","Sam Jones (d)","La Perla (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Garcia's debut album for a major label, drawing on her Guyanese and Trinidadian background, dub, cumbia and spiritual jazz without any of it feeling like a checklist.","listenFor":"The title track is over twelve minutes and built on a dub structure — heavy delay, dropouts, and a bass line that anchors everything while the horn floats.","startTrack":"Source","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"ezra-collective-where-im-meant-to-be","genreIds":["uk-jazz-revival"],"tier":"core","artist":"Ezra Collective","title":"Where I'm Meant to Be","recorded":2022,"released":2022,"label":"Partisan","catalogNo":"PTKF2249","personnel":["Femi Koleoso (d)","TJ Koleoso (elb)","Joe Armon-Jones (keys)","James Mollison (ts)","Ife Ogunjobi (tp)","Sampa the Great (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"The record that won the Mercury Prize in 2023, the first jazz album to do so. Afrobeat and reggae rhythms under jazz soloing, made explicitly for people to dance to.","listenFor":"Femi Koleoso's drumming is Afrobeat-derived — the kick and the hi-hat carry the groove and the snare is used sparingly. It is not a jazz drummer playing Afrobeat.","startTrack":"Life Goes On","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"moses-boyd-dark-matter","genreIds":["uk-jazz-revival","nu-jazz"],"tier":"core","artist":"Moses Boyd","title":"Dark Matter","recorded":2019,"released":2020,"label":"Exodus","catalogNo":"EXODUS001","personnel":["Moses Boyd (d, prod)","Theon Cross (tuba)","Nonku Phiri (voc)","Obongjayar (voc)","Joe Armon-Jones (keys)"],"whyThisOne":"Boyd works as a producer and a drummer simultaneously, and this record refuses to choose — grime, Afrobeat, jazz and electronic production, with the drums as the connecting logic.","listenFor":"'Stranger Than Fiction' has an acoustic kit and programmed elements interlocking so tightly that separating them is difficult. That integration is the scene's technical signature.","startTrack":"Stranger Than Fiction","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"comet-is-coming-trust-in-lifeforce","genreIds":["uk-jazz-revival"],"tier":"core","artist":"The Comet Is Coming","title":"Trust in the Lifeforce of the Deep Mystery","recorded":2018,"released":2019,"label":"Impulse!","catalogNo":"00602567981633","personnel":["Shabaka Hutchings (ts)","Danalogue (synths)","Betamax (d)","Kate Tempest (voc)"],"whyThisOne":"Saxophone, analogue synthesizers and drums playing what amounts to psychedelic dance music, signed to Impulse! — the label that released Ascension. The lineage is claimed openly.","listenFor":"The synthesizers do what an organ would have done in 1970 spiritual jazz, and the saxophone builds by overblowing rather than by running changes.","startTrack":"Summon the Fire","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"theon-cross-fyah","genreIds":["uk-jazz-revival"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Theon Cross","title":"Fyah","recorded":2018,"released":2019,"label":"Gearbox","catalogNo":"GB1546","personnel":["Theon Cross (tuba)","Nubya Garcia (ts)","Moses Boyd (d)"],"whyThisOne":"A tuba-led trio record, which should not work and does. Cross treats the instrument as a bass, a rhythm section and a lead voice, with dancehall and grime phrasing.","listenFor":"The tuba is amplified and processed to produce sub-bass frequencies a brass instrument does not naturally have. That is a soundsystem decision, not a jazz one.","startTrack":"Activate","difficulty":2,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"emma-jean-thackray-yellow","genreIds":["uk-jazz-revival"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Emma-Jean Thackray","title":"Yellow","recorded":2020,"released":2021,"label":"Movementt","catalogNo":"MOVE003","personnel":["Emma-Jean Thackray (tp, voc, keys, prod)","Lyle Barton (keys)","Dougal Taylor (d)","Soweto Kinch (as)"],"whyThisOne":"Written, played, produced and mixed largely by one person, drawing on Alice Coltrane, P-Funk and brass band tradition. It is the scene's most complete single-authored statement.","listenFor":"Thackray comes from a Yorkshire brass band background and the horn voicings show it — thick, hymn-like block harmony under a funk rhythm section.","startTrack":"Say Something","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"},{"id":"yussef-kamaal-black-focus","genreIds":["uk-jazz-revival","nu-jazz"],"tier":"deep","artist":"Yussef Kamaal","title":"Black Focus","recorded":2016,"released":2016,"label":"Brownswood","catalogNo":"BWOOD0157","personnel":["Yussef Dayes (d)","Kamaal Williams (keys)","Tom Driessler (b)","Mansur Brown (g)"],"whyThisOne":"The record that connected the London scene to broken beat and West London's electronic history most directly. The duo split shortly afterwards, which makes it a single complete statement.","listenFor":"Yussef Dayes' drumming is a live drum-and-bass and broken beat feel executed on an acoustic kit at extreme speed, with the hi-hat carrying most of the information.","startTrack":"Strings of Light","difficulty":1,"confidence":"high"}],"paths":[{"id":"start-here","name":"Start Here","subtitle":"Twelve records, chronological, nothing difficult","blurb":"One easy record from each stage of the music, in order. Nothing here is demanding; the point is to hear the ground shifting between 1921 and today without anyone shouting at you.","expect":{"count":12,"maxDifficulty":2,"chronological":true},"steps":[{"albumId":"james-p-johnson-carolina-shout","year":1921,"bridge":"Begin with one man and a piano, because that is where the machinery is most visible: a marching left hand and a right hand refusing to land with it."},{"albumId":"louis-armstrong-hot-fives-sevens","year":1925,"bridge":"Johnson had to be his own band; Armstrong had one, and then discovered he did not need it either — this is where the soloist takes over jazz."},{"albumId":"billie-holiday-quintessential-vol3","year":1936,"bridge":"Armstrong showed that a singer could phrase like a horn; Holiday shows what happens when someone takes that permission and uses it on the words."},{"albumId":"ellington-blanton-webster-band","year":1940,"bridge":"Same decade, opposite scale: instead of five players listening to each other, fifteen executing a composer's design, with the improvising rationed."},{"albumId":"clifford-brown-max-roach-1954","year":1954,"bridge":"The big bands have collapsed and the small group is back, but now it plays too fast to dance to and the harmony moves twice as often."},{"albumId":"moanin-1959","year":1958,"bridge":"Bebop had become a specialist's music; this is the correction — the same harmony slowed down with the blues and the church put back into it."},{"albumId":"kind-of-blue","year":1959,"bridge":"Hard bop gives a soloist a chord every two beats. Here Miles Davis takes almost all of them away and finds out what is left to play."},{"albumId":"a-love-supreme","year":1964,"bridge":"Coltrane was on the previous record. Five years later he takes the same reduced harmony and aims it at something other than music."},{"albumId":"miles-davis-in-a-silent-way","year":1969,"bridge":"Davis again, and the instruments have changed: electric piano, electric guitar, and eighth notes that no longer swing."},{"albumId":"herbie-hancock-head-hunters","year":1973,"bridge":"Hancock played electric piano on that record. Four years later he builds a band where the groove is the composition and the solo is a passenger."},{"albumId":"wynton-marsalis-black-codes","year":1985,"bridge":"A generation decides electricity was a wrong turn, goes back to acoustic instruments, and plays them with conservatory precision and considerable force."},{"albumId":"we-out-here-compilation","year":2017,"bridge":"And a generation after that decides the argument was never worth having, and builds jazz out of Afrobeat, dub and grime instead."}]},{"id":"full-arc","name":"The Full Arc","subtitle":"Thirty records, ragtime to now","blurb":"The historical spine in order. Some of it is hard going and that is deliberate — this is the route that shows you how each thing came out of the last one, including the parts that came out of an argument.","expect":{"count":30,"chronological":true},"steps":[{"albumId":"rifkin-piano-rags-joplin","year":1899,"bridge":"Before jazz: composed piano music with the syncopation written in, no improvising, no blues. Everything downstream inherits the rhythmic habit and discards the rest."},{"albumId":"odjb-complete-original-dixieland","year":1917,"bridge":"The first jazz record, made by a white New Orleans band who then claimed to have invented the music. Hold both of those facts at once."},{"albumId":"james-p-johnson-carolina-shout","year":1921,"bridge":"In Harlem, ragtime's left hand gets loosened and swung and turned into a competitive sport at rent parties."},{"albumId":"king-oliver-creole-jazz-band-1923","year":1923,"bridge":"Back to New Orleans, properly this time: three horns improvising simultaneously, and a second cornet player who will not stay in the background much longer."},{"albumId":"louis-armstrong-hot-fives-sevens","year":1925,"bridge":"That second cornet player. Across three years the collective front line stops being the point and the soloist becomes it."},{"albumId":"django-quintette-hot-club-1936","year":1936,"bridge":"The first evidence that this music could be rebuilt somewhere else: Paris, no drums, no horns, and a guitar language from Romani string tradition."},{"albumId":"billie-holiday-quintessential-vol3","year":1936,"bridge":"Meanwhile in New York, a singer and a tenor player work out how to have a conversation across a mediocre pop song."},{"albumId":"count-basie-original-decca","year":1937,"bridge":"Kansas City arrives and changes the floor everyone is standing on — an even four, timekeeping moved to the cymbals, and enormous space."},{"albumId":"ellington-blanton-webster-band","year":1940,"bridge":"The peak of the written tradition, by a composer who wrote for specific people rather than for parts, which is why nobody else can play it properly."},{"albumId":"charlie-parker-savoy-dial","year":1945,"bridge":"And the reaction: the big band abandoned, the dance floor abandoned, the tempo doubled, and the harmony made deliberately harder to follow."},{"albumId":"birth-of-the-cool","year":1949,"bridge":"Four years later the same harmony is played at half the temperature, with written counterpoint and a French horn, by people including a very young Miles Davis."},{"albumId":"machito-afro-cuban-jazz-suite","year":1950,"bridge":"A different reaction to bebop entirely: keep the harmony and the soloing, replace the swing feel with clave, and the whole rhythmic basis changes."},{"albumId":"horace-silver-art-blakey-jazz-messengers-1955","year":1954,"bridge":"Back in New York the correction to cool jazz is heat — gospel chords, a backbeat, and tunes you can actually hum."},{"albumId":"joao-gilberto-chega-de-saudade","year":1958,"bridge":"In Rio, musicians listening to the cool jazz records take the restraint, add samba and much harder harmony, and invent something Brazil still argues about."},{"albumId":"kind-of-blue","year":1959,"bridge":"Davis's answer to hard bop's congestion: take away nearly all the chords and see what a soloist does with the silence."},{"albumId":"ornette-coleman-shape-of-jazz-to-come","year":1959,"bridge":"The same year, in a different city, someone takes away the chords entirely and keeps the tune. The reception was hostile and personal."},{"albumId":"jimmy-smith-back-at-the-chicken-shack","year":1960,"bridge":"None of that argument reached the neighbourhood bars, where the Hammond organ was doing perfectly well and the audience answered out loud."},{"albumId":"wayne-shorter-speak-no-evil","year":1964,"bridge":"Musicians who heard Ornette Coleman and went partway: forms that will not resolve, harmony that will not declare a key, and a rhythm section that argues back."},{"albumId":"a-love-supreme","year":1964,"bridge":"Coltrane in the same year, using the reduced harmony for something else — a suite structured as a religious offering, with a chant where the head should be."},{"albumId":"roscoe-mitchell-sound","year":1966,"bridge":"Chicago's answer to New York's free jazz: a musicians' collective, composed sections, small instruments, and long stretches where almost nothing happens."},{"albumId":"miles-davis-miles-smiles","year":1966,"bridge":"Davis's second quintet holding a pulse while treating the harmony as negotiable — the last acoustic thing he did before plugging in."},{"albumId":"terumasa-hino-hi-nology","year":1969,"bridge":"By now the music has left the country for good: Tokyo takes the modal template and plays it harder, recorded better than almost anything in America."},{"albumId":"bitches-brew","year":1969,"bridge":"Davis plugs in, hires thirteen people, and lets a producer assemble the record out of tape splices afterwards. The argument this started lasted twenty years."},{"albumId":"herbie-hancock-head-hunters","year":1973,"bridge":"One of those thirteen forms a band where the groove is the composition, sells enormously, and gets called a sell-out for it."},{"albumId":"keith-jarrett-koln-concert","year":1975,"bridge":"In Europe an entirely different reaction to the same decade: one man, one out-of-tune piano, no plan, and no volume at all."},{"albumId":"wildflowers-loft-jazz-sessions","year":1976,"bridge":"And in New York the avant-garde, priced out of every club, rents lofts and puts on its own concerts. This is what a weeknight sounded like."},{"albumId":"wynton-marsalis-black-codes","year":1985,"bridge":"The counter-revolution: acoustic instruments, formidable technique, and a public argument that the previous fifteen years had been a mistake."},{"albumId":"tribe-called-quest-low-end-theory","year":1991,"bridge":"While that argument runs, producers in the same city are lifting two-bar loops off the jazz records nobody was buying and building something else out of them."},{"albumId":"kamasi-washington-the-epic","year":2011,"bridge":"Twenty years on, musicians raised on both sides of that split make a three-hour spiritual jazz record with a choir and put it out on an electronic label."},{"albumId":"we-out-here-compilation","year":2017,"bridge":"And in London, a scene that came up through a free youth programme plays jazz for people who are standing up, on rhythms from Lagos, Kingston and Port of Spain."}]},{"id":"deep-end","name":"Into the Deep End","subtitle":"Fifteen records that get progressively harder","blurb":"Free, spiritual and avant-garde music, sequenced so each record teaches your ear what it needs for the next one. Do not start at the bottom of this list. It will not go well.","expect":{"count":15},"steps":[{"albumId":"john-coltrane-my-favorite-things","year":1960,"bridge":"Start with a song you already know, stretched to thirteen minutes over two chords. Nothing here is strange yet; you are just learning to wait."},{"albumId":"art-ensemble-of-chicago-les-stances-a-sophie","year":1970,"bridge":"Now the avant-garde with a bass line and a backbeat, so you can hear that free playing and a groove are not opposites."},{"albumId":"ornette-coleman-shape-of-jazz-to-come","year":1959,"bridge":"Keep the singable melody and the steady pulse, and remove one thing only: the chord sequence. That single subtraction is what all of this is built on."},{"albumId":"a-love-supreme","year":1964,"bridge":"Same reduction, aimed at devotion rather than at freedom — a four-note figure and a mantra holding forty minutes together."},{"albumId":"alice-coltrane-journey-in-satchidananda","year":1970,"bridge":"Now remove the chord changes entirely and replace them with a drone, and add a harp. Your ear stops looking for movement and starts listening to colour."},{"albumId":"pharoah-sanders-karma","year":1969,"bridge":"A gentle pentatonic melody that goes somewhere violent and comes back. This is your first taste of an ecstatic build, with a safe route home."},{"albumId":"eric-dolphy-out-to-lunch","year":1964,"bridge":"Back to a composed record, but everything wobbles — no piano stating the harmony, and a drummer playing around the beat rather than on it."},{"albumId":"ornette-coleman-free-jazz","year":1960,"bridge":"Two quartets improvising at once, one in each speaker. Follow one at a time until you can hear both, then decide what you think about it."},{"albumId":"albert-ayler-spiritual-unity","year":1964,"bridge":"Now the melodies get simpler and the playing gets more extreme. It is a hymn and a howl, and the tune always survives."},{"albumId":"john-coltrane-ascension","year":1965,"bridge":"Eleven players alternating between collective density and single solos. If you can hear that alternation, the wall becomes a structure."},{"albumId":"cecil-taylor-unit-structures","year":1966,"bridge":"A completely different route to freedom: atonal, percussive and organised by written units rather than by feel. Nothing about this comes from the blues."},{"albumId":"roscoe-mitchell-sound","year":1966,"bridge":"Chicago's opposite proposition — silence and space as the material, and a refusal to keep blowing simply because the tape is running."},{"albumId":"peter-brotzmann-machine-gun","year":1968,"bridge":"Europe rejecting all of the above, including the blues and the pulse, at maximum volume for the entire record. There is a written theme; find it."},{"albumId":"evan-parker-monoceros","year":1978,"bridge":"One saxophone, no breathing pauses, producing several simultaneous lines. By now you should be listening to texture rather than for melody."},{"albumId":"coltrane-interstellar-space","year":1967,"bridge":"Finish where there is nothing left to hold: tenor and drums, no bass, no harmony, no meter. Recorded five months before he died."}]}]};
